globalThis.QuestRouteData=[{"chapter":25,"title":"搭配与表达选择","target":"B2","focus":"把常见词组合成自然、准确的表达","grammar":"动词与名词搭配；介词搭配；语境辨义","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"The repair café","text":"When a broken lamp appeared in our community hall, several volunteers offered to take responsibility for it. Nobody promised to make a profit. The purpose of our repair café was to reduce waste and give neighbours an opportunity to exchange practical skills. Before the first session, we carried out a survey. Most residents wanted help with small electrical items, but a few asked for advice on torn clothes.\nOur biggest mistake was to set an unrealistic deadline. We advertised that every repair would be finished within an hour. An experienced engineer pointed out that checking an item safely might take longer than fixing it. We changed the notice and asked visitors to describe their problem in advance. This simple step made a considerable difference to the atmosphere.\nBy the third Saturday, people were bringing stories as well as objects. One woman explained that her radio had belonged to her father. Although its market value was low, replacing it would not have met her needs. We could not guarantee success, but we paid close attention to her explanation. When the radio finally worked, the whole room applauded. I realised that providing a service sometimes means understanding what matters to someone before deciding what action to take."},"sourceB":{"title":"A museum changes its plans","text":"Our local museum wanted to attract a wider audience without losing the trust of regular visitors. The director initially suggested installing large screens in every room. A designer warned that this could draw attention away from the objects themselves. Instead of making an immediate decision, the team agreed to test one small display for six weeks.\nVisitors could listen to a potter explain how she used a bowl similar to the one in the case. The recording offered an insight into everyday work rather than a list of dates. Staff gathered feedback using short interviews near the exit. Younger visitors particularly appreciated the human voice, while several older guests requested a written version because the entrance hall was noisy.\nThe museum took these comments into account. It moved the listening station to a quiet corner and provided a transcript with adjustable print. These changes did not require a substantial investment, yet they improved access for different groups. Attendance rose slightly, but the team avoided claiming that the display had caused the increase. A rainy holiday week might also have played a part. The final report therefore drew a clear distinction between evidence of satisfaction and evidence of long term growth. That careful wording helped the museum maintain public confidence."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Why did the café change its notice?","answer":"Safety checks could take longer than an hour.","options":["Safety checks could take longer than an hour.","Visitors refused to bring lamps.","The hall charged extra rent."],"explanation":"工程师指出安全检查可能比修理更久，原来的承诺不现实。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What mattered most about the radio?","answer":"Its connection with the owner’s father.","options":["Its connection with the owner’s father.","Its high selling price.","Its modern design."],"explanation":"原文明确对比低市场价值与家庭记忆。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What improved preparation?","answer":"Visitors described problems beforehand.","options":["Visitors described problems beforehand.","All visitors bought replacement items.","Volunteers stopped accepting clothes."],"explanation":"提前描述故障让志愿者能安排工作。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why were screens not installed everywhere?","answer":"They might distract from museum objects.","options":["They might distract from museum objects.","The director disliked all technology.","No visitors could use them."],"explanation":"设计者担心屏幕把注意力从展品移开。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did older guests request?","answer":"A written version of the recording.","options":["A written version of the recording.","A louder entrance hall.","Fewer historical objects."],"explanation":"噪声使部分访客需要文字版本。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why was the attendance claim cautious?","answer":"Weather could also have influenced visits.","options":["Weather could also have influenced visits.","Nobody counted visitors.","The display had already been removed."],"explanation":"报告没有把同期增长直接当作因果证据。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"设定期限","en":"We need to set a realistic deadline.","zh":"我们需要设定一个现实的期限。","word":"set a realistic deadline","meaning":"设定期限","tip":"本句的“set a realistic deadline”表达“设定期限”。先结合“我们需要设定一个现实的期限。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"承担责任","en":"She took responsibility for checking the wiring.","zh":"她承担了检查电线的责任。","word":"took responsibility","meaning":"承担责任","tip":"本句的“took responsibility”表达“承担责任”。先结合“她承担了检查电线的责任。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"深入了解","en":"The survey gave us an insight into local needs.","zh":"调查让我们了解了当地需求。","word":"an insight into","meaning":"深入了解","tip":"本句的“an insight into”表达“深入了解”。先结合“调查让我们了解了当地需求。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"注意","en":"Please pay close attention to the safety instructions.","zh":"请仔细注意安全说明。","word":"pay close attention to","meaning":"注意","tip":"本句的“pay close attention to”表达“注意”。先结合“请仔细注意安全说明。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"开展调查","en":"The volunteers carried out a survey before opening.","zh":"志愿者在开办前进行了调查。","word":"carried out a survey","meaning":"开展调查","tip":"本句的“carried out a survey”表达“开展调查”。先结合“志愿者在开办前进行了调查。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"保证成功","en":"We cannot guarantee that every repair will succeed.","zh":"我们不能保证每次修理都成功。","word":"guarantee","meaning":"保证","tip":"本句的“guarantee”表达“保证”。先结合“我们不能保证每次修理都成功。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"产生影响","en":"Your description made a considerable difference to our preparation.","zh":"你的描述对我们的准备产生了相当大的影响。","word":"made a considerable difference","meaning":"产生影响","tip":"本句的“made a considerable difference”表达“产生影响”。先结合“你的描述对我们的准备产生了相当大的影响。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"考虑到","en":"We took the owner’s personal story into account.","zh":"我们考虑了物主的个人故事。","word":"took","meaning":"在本句中与 into account 搭配表示考虑了","tip":"本句的“took”表达“在本句中与 into account 搭配表示考虑了”。先结合“我们考虑了物主的个人故事。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"转移注意力","en":"Repeated questions can draw attention away from a difficult repair.","zh":"反复提问可能转移对困难修理工作的注意力。","word":"draw attention away","meaning":"转移注意力","tip":"本句的“draw attention away”表达“转移注意力”。先结合“反复提问可能转移对困难修理工作的注意力。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"满足需求","en":"A replacement would not have met her needs.","zh":"替代品不会满足她的需求。","word":"met her needs","meaning":"满足需求","tip":"本句的“met her needs”表达“满足需求”。先结合“替代品不会满足她的需求。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"作出区分","en":"We drew a distinction between market value and personal meaning.","zh":"我们区分了市场价值与个人意义。","word":"drew a distinction","meaning":"作出区分","tip":"本句的“drew a distinction”表达“作出区分”。先结合“我们区分了市场价值与个人意义。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"发挥作用","en":"Patient listening played a part in the café’s success.","zh":"耐心倾听是修理咖啡馆成功的因素之一。","word":"played a part","meaning":"发挥作用","tip":"本句的“played a part”表达“发挥作用”。先结合“耐心倾听是修理咖啡馆成功的因素之一。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete with one word: Please pay close ___ to the instructions.","answers":["attention"],"explanation":"固定搭配 pay attention to。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete with one word: We must take the cost into ___.","answers":["account"],"explanation":"take something into account 表示考虑。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use RESPONSIBLE: The manager accepted ___ for the mistake.","answers":["responsibility"],"explanation":"accepted 后需名词 responsibility。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DIFFERENCE in exactly three words: Your advice changed the result considerably. Your advice ___ to the result.","answers":["made a difference"],"explanation":"固定表达 make a difference，过去时为 made。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an article explaining how a community service can meet different people’s needs. Include an example and one limitation.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"A useful community service begins with listening. It is tempting to buy impressive equipment first, but an expensive solution may fail if nobody understands the problem. A short survey and several conversations can give organisers a much clearer picture of local needs.\nFor example, a repair café might discover that visitors care about familiar objects because of their personal history. Volunteers should therefore ask what an item means to its owner as well as what is broken. Clear limits are equally important. Promising to repair everything immediately creates pressure and can lead to unsafe decisions. A realistic booking system allows time for proper checks.\nThere is one important limitation: volunteers cannot replace qualified professionals in every situation. Dangerous electrical work should be referred to an expert. Explaining this boundary honestly helps maintain trust. In my view, the most successful service combines practical help with careful communication, rather than measuring success only by the number of objects repaired."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Describe a useful service in your neighbourhood.","followups":["How could organisers gather reliable feedback?","What should they avoid promising?"],"sample":"I would like a small repair café in our neighbourhood because many people throw away objects that could still be useful. It should offer appointments and explain its safety limits clearly. Volunteers could teach simple skills while specialists check electrical items. I would judge its success by asking whether visitors felt listened to, rather than simply counting repairs. The main difficulty would be finding enough trained volunteers to keep the service reliable."},"project":"为社区服务设计一页方案，使用六个自然搭配；让同伴指出任何夸大承诺。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Hello, this is Maya from the sports centre. You booked court two for Thursday evening, but a damaged light means we cannot use it safely. We can offer court four at the same time, although it is slightly smaller. If that does not suit your group, there is a full size court available on Friday at six. You will not need to pay any extra charge for either option. Please call before noon tomorrow so we can confirm the arrangement. Your equipment booking is unchanged, and you can still collect the rackets at reception when you arrive.","questions":[{"prompt":"Why is the original court unavailable?","answer":"A light is damaged.","options":["A light is damaged.","The floor is being painted.","A competition has taken every court."],"explanation":"明确说明损坏照明带来安全问题。"},{"prompt":"What is different about court four?","answer":"It is slightly smaller.","options":["It is slightly smaller.","It has no rackets.","It is only available Friday."],"explanation":"court four 同时段但更小。"},{"prompt":"What must the listener do?","answer":"Call before noon tomorrow.","options":["Call before noon tomorrow.","Pay an extra charge today.","Collect equipment immediately."],"explanation":"要求明日中午前联系确认。"}]}},{"chapter":26,"title":"构词与词性判断","target":"B2","focus":"利用语法位置和上下文判断词形","grammar":"名词化；否定前缀；形容词与副词","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"A rooftop garden","text":"The roof above our college library used to be an empty concrete surface. Turning it into a garden sounded attractive, but the first proposal was rejected because its financial calculations were incomplete. A student team then worked with an engineer to check the building’s capacity and the accessibility of the stairs. Their revised proposal was less ambitious but much more practical.\nInstead of planting large trees, they selected lightweight containers and vegetables that could survive windy conditions. Participation was voluntary, and beginners were given simple instructions. At first, some plants grew unevenly because different groups watered them without checking the soil. The solution was not stricter punishment but a clear weekly record showing what had already been done.\nThe garden eventually became a quiet place for reading and conversation. Its educational value was unexpected. Students who rarely spoke during lessons confidently explained how compost developed. However, the organisers resisted describing the project as completely sustainable. Delivering soil and replacing damaged containers still used resources. Their end of year report listed both achievements and limitations. This honesty made future funding more likely because supporters could see that the team understood the difference between an attractive idea and a carefully managed activity."},"sourceB":{"title":"When a warning becomes confusing","text":"A town council replaced its old flood notices with a new design intended to improve communication. The original signs contained technical vocabulary and several sentences in small print. Residents often misunderstood which streets were affected. The replacement used a simple map, three colours and a short instruction beside each colour.\nDuring a practice exercise, however, researchers noticed an unexpected problem. Some residents assumed that a green street would remain safe throughout the evening, although the colour described only the current situation. The designers added a visible time and an explanation that conditions could change rapidly. They also avoided relying entirely on colour, since this would disadvantage people with certain visual difficulties.\nThe next exercise showed a considerable improvement in accuracy. Most participants identified both the affected area and the recommended action. Nevertheless, the research team distinguished understanding a notice from following it during a real emergency. Anxiety, poor phone reception and responsibility for relatives could all influence behaviour. The council therefore arranged meetings where residents could discuss practical difficulties. Better wording was an essential improvement, but it was only one part of emergency preparation. Successful communication depended on trust, accessibility and repeated opportunities to ask questions, not merely on producing a visually impressive sign."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Why was the first garden proposal rejected?","answer":"Its financial calculations were incomplete.","options":["Its financial calculations were incomplete.","The library was closing permanently.","Students refused to participate."],"explanation":"第一段直接说明财务测算不完整。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"How did organisers solve uneven watering?","answer":"They introduced a shared weekly record.","options":["They introduced a shared weekly record.","They punished every beginner.","They removed all vegetables."],"explanation":"记录已完成工作解决重复浇水问题。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why did the report mention limitations?","answer":"To show realistic management of the project.","options":["To show realistic management of the project.","To prove gardening was impossible.","To demand that volunteers leave."],"explanation":"如实报告资源消耗有助于支持者判断。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did some people misunderstand?","answer":"Green described the present situation, not a guarantee for later.","options":["Green described the present situation, not a guarantee for later.","Every street was already flooded.","The map showed a different town."],"explanation":"绿色只表示当前情况，部分人把它理解为整晚安全。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why were labels added as well as colours?","answer":"Some users could not reliably distinguish the colours.","options":["Some users could not reliably distinguish the colours.","Printing colours was forbidden.","All maps had been removed."],"explanation":"文本标记照顾视觉识别有困难的人。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What remained uncertain after the exercise?","answer":"Whether people would act similarly during a real emergency.","options":["Whether people would act similarly during a real emergency.","Whether any signs existed.","Whether the council could print maps."],"explanation":"练习中的理解不等于真正紧急时会行动。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"在财务上","en":"The first proposal was financially unrealistic.","zh":"第一份方案在财务上不现实。","word":"financially","meaning":"在财务上","tip":"本句的“financially”表达“在财务上”。先结合“第一份方案在财务上不现实。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"可达性","en":"We checked the accessibility of the roof.","zh":"我们检查了屋顶是否便于到达。","word":"accessibility","meaning":"可达性","tip":"本句的“accessibility”表达“可达性”。先结合“我们检查了屋顶是否便于到达。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"参与","en":"Participation in the garden project is voluntary.","zh":"参与花园项目是自愿的。","word":"participation","meaning":"参与","tip":"本句的“participation”表达“参与”。先结合“参与花园项目是自愿的。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"修订过的","en":"The revised instructions were easier to follow.","zh":"修改后的说明更容易遵循。","word":"revised","meaning":"修订过的","tip":"本句的“revised”表达“修订过的”。先结合“修改后的说明更容易遵循。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"不均匀地","en":"The plants grew unevenly near the wall.","zh":"靠墙的植物长得不均匀。","word":"unevenly","meaning":"不均匀地","tip":"本句的“unevenly”表达“不均匀地”。先结合“靠墙的植物长得不均匀。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"信心","en":"Her confidence increased after teaching other students.","zh":"教过其他学生后，她的信心增强了。","word":"confidence","meaning":"信心","tip":"本句的“confidence”表达“信心”。先结合“教过其他学生后，她的信心增强了。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"困惑","en":"The watering record reduced unnecessary confusion.","zh":"浇水记录减少了不必要的困惑。","word":"confusion","meaning":"困惑","tip":"本句的“confusion”表达“困惑”。先结合“浇水记录减少了不必要的困惑。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"准确地","en":"Students accurately recorded when each container was watered.","zh":"学生准确记录了每个花盆何时浇水。","word":"accurately","meaning":"准确地","tip":"本句的“accurately”表达“准确地”。先结合“学生准确记录了每个花盆何时浇水。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"迅速地","en":"Small plants can dry rapidly in windy conditions.","zh":"小植物在风中可能迅速变干。","word":"rapidly","meaning":"迅速地","tip":"本句的“rapidly”表达“迅速地”。先结合“小植物在风中可能迅速变干。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"不便到达的","en":"The original roof was inaccessible to ordinary visitors.","zh":"原来的屋顶不便普通访客到达。","word":"inaccessible","meaning":"不便到达的","tip":"本句的“inaccessible”表达“不便到达的”。先结合“原来的屋顶不便普通访客到达。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"理解","en":"Understanding the soil helped beginners care for vegetables.","zh":"理解土壤有助于新手照顾蔬菜。","word":"understanding","meaning":"理解","tip":"本句的“understanding”表达“理解”。先结合“理解土壤有助于新手照顾蔬菜。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"准备","en":"Careful preparation made the revised proposal more practical.","zh":"仔细准备使修订方案更实际。","word":"preparation","meaning":"准备","tip":"本句的“preparation”表达“准备”。先结合“仔细准备使修订方案更实际。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use ACCESS: The lift improved the building’s ___ for wheelchair users.","answers":["accessibility"],"explanation":"物主结构后需要表示可达性的名词。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use ACCURATE: The instrument measures temperature ___.","answers":["accurately"],"explanation":"修饰动词 measures 用副词。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use PARTICIPATE: ___ in the survey is voluntary.","answers":["Participation"],"explanation":"作主语，用名词；句首大写。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use EXPECT: The sudden closure was completely ___. Nobody had predicted it.","answers":["unexpected"],"explanation":"Nobody had predicted 排除 expected；用否定形容词。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a report recommending how to organise a college rooftop garden. Discuss practical preparation and volunteer participation.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"This report recommends a small rooftop garden that students can maintain safely and consistently. The proposal should begin with an engineering check rather than a list of attractive plants. The building must support the equipment, and access should be suitable for the intended volunteers.\nOnce these conditions are confirmed, the college should start with lightweight containers and a limited range of vegetables. A modest project would be easier to manage than an ambitious design that depends on constant expert attention. Clear instructions and a shared watering record would help beginners contribute without accidentally repeating one another’s work.\nParticipation should remain voluntary, with short training sessions available to new members. Students could also record plant growth and explain their observations to other classes.\nFinally, the project should publish maintenance costs and difficulties alongside achievements. The garden may offer valuable learning and a pleasant meeting place, but a realistic report should not describe it as completely sustainable without examining the resources it uses."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how to make a student garden practical for beginners.","followups":["What safety checks should happen first?","How could volunteers share responsibility?"],"sample":"I would begin with a small garden rather than fill the whole roof immediately. The building and access would need a professional safety check first. Beginners could then use clear instructions and a shared watering record. This would reduce confusion when several people care for the same plants. I would keep participation voluntary and offer short training sessions. The project should report difficulties honestly, because an attractive idea still needs practical management to remain useful."},"project":"选择一条真实公共通知，重写并标出八个词的词根、词形与句法作用。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I started running a lunch club at my office because colleagues rarely spoke to people from other departments. At first, I sent an enthusiastic invitation asking everyone to bring an interesting dish. Only six people came. Several colleagues later explained that they did not cook confidently and thought homemade food was compulsory. For the next meeting, I changed the invitation to say that bought food was equally welcome and that sharing was optional. More than twenty people attended. The main lesson was that friendly intentions are not enough if the wording accidentally makes participation feel difficult or competitive.","questions":[{"prompt":"Why did the speaker start the club?","answer":"To connect colleagues from different departments.","options":["To connect colleagues from different departments.","To judge cooking skills.","To sell homemade lunches."],"explanation":"目的在于跨部门交流。"},{"prompt":"Why did some people avoid the first meeting?","answer":"They believed they had to cook something themselves.","options":["They believed they had to cook something themselves.","They were forbidden to buy food.","The office was closed."],"explanation":"误认为家制食物强制要求。"},{"prompt":"What helped attendance?","answer":"An invitation clarifying that bought food was welcome.","options":["An invitation clarifying that bought food was welcome.","A higher entry price.","A cooking examination."],"explanation":"澄清参与条件后人数增多。"}]}},{"chapter":27,"title":"复杂句与句型转换","target":"B2","focus":"保留原意，准确表达条件、让步与强调","grammar":"关系从句；让步；虚拟条件；被动语态","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"The postponed expedition","text":"Our geography group had planned to spend a weekend measuring streams in the hills. Although the weather forecast mentioned showers, nobody expected the road to close. Had our tutor not checked the transport website on Friday evening, we would have reached the station with no way to continue. Instead, we stayed in town and redesigned the project.\nSome students felt that a local canal could not provide worthwhile evidence. Our tutor asked us to identify the question we actually wanted to answer. We were investigating how recent rainfall affected water flow, not proving that distant places were more interesting. The canal, which was monitored by a public agency, offered a reliable record for comparison. By the end of Saturday, we had collected more usable observations than our original schedule would have allowed.\nThe revised project still had weaknesses. Since water levels were controlled by gates, we could not attribute every change to rain. We explained this limitation in the report instead of hiding it in a footnote. What impressed our tutor most was not the amount of data but the care with which we interpreted it. The cancelled journey had forced us to distinguish an appealing location from a suitable research method."},"sourceB":{"title":"A flexible working trial","text":"Employees at a small design company were invited to work from home on two days each week. The director agreed to the trial provided that customer response times did not increase. Staff were allowed to choose their days, whereas managers had to ensure that somebody remained available for urgent requests.\nAfter a month, the average response time had fallen. This did not necessarily mean that remote work was responsible. A large project had ended just before the trial, reducing the number of messages. The manager therefore compared similar requests rather than relying on the overall figure. She also interviewed new employees, who reported missing informal advice from experienced colleagues.\nThe final arrangement included shared office mornings for training and optional remote afternoons. Anyone whose home was unsuitable could continue using a desk every day. Had the company required everybody to stay home, it would have disadvantaged some staff. The director concluded that flexibility worked best when clear expectations were combined with genuine choice. Despite the positive results, the company scheduled another review during its busiest season. A system that succeeded in a quiet month might need adjustment when deadlines became more demanding. The trial was treated as useful evidence, rather than a final answer to every working problem."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Why did the group avoid a wasted journey?","answer":"The tutor checked the transport website.","options":["The tutor checked the transport website.","The station phoned every student.","Rain had stopped before Friday."],"explanation":"倒装条件句说明若非导师查询，大家会白跑。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why was the canal suitable?","answer":"It allowed investigation of their actual research question.","options":["It allowed investigation of their actual research question.","It was farther away than the hills.","Its water level never changed."],"explanation":"研究问题是雨水与流量，不是地点是否偏远。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What limited the conclusions?","answer":"Gates also controlled water levels.","options":["Gates also controlled water levels.","No observations were collected.","The tutor banned comparisons."],"explanation":"人工闸门是另一个影响水位的变量。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What condition did the director set?","answer":"Customer response times must not increase.","options":["Customer response times must not increase.","Every desk must be removed.","Staff must work only at night."],"explanation":"provided that 引出试行前提。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why was the average response time insufficient evidence?","answer":"The workload had also decreased.","options":["The workload had also decreased.","No customers sent messages.","The records had been destroyed."],"explanation":"同期项目结束造成工作量下降。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why plan another review?","answer":"Busy periods could create different demands.","options":["Busy periods could create different demands.","The trial had already failed completely.","All staff wanted to resign."],"explanation":"淡季结果未必适用于旺季。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"虽然","en":"Although it rained, we collected useful data.","zh":"虽然下雨，我们仍收集了有用的数据。","word":"although","meaning":"虽然","tip":"本句的“although”表达“虽然”。先结合“虽然下雨，我们仍收集了有用的数据。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"过去未实现条件","en":"If the road had remained open, we would have travelled.","zh":"如果道路当时畅通，我们就会出行。","word":"had remained","meaning":"过去未实现条件","tip":"本句的“had remained”表达“过去未实现条件”。先结合“如果道路当时畅通，我们就会出行。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"非限制性关系从句","en":"The canal, which runs through town, is monitored daily.","zh":"穿过镇子的运河每天都有人监测。","word":"which","meaning":"非限制性关系从句","tip":"本句的“which”表达“非限制性关系从句”。先结合“穿过镇子的运河每天都有人监测。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"主语从句","en":"What matters most is the quality of the evidence.","zh":"最重要的是证据质量。","word":"what matters","meaning":"主语从句","tip":"本句的“what matters”表达“主语从句”。先结合“最重要的是证据质量。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"归因于","en":"We could not attribute every change to rainfall.","zh":"我们不能把每个变化都归因于降雨。","word":"attribute","meaning":"在本句中与 to 搭配表示归因于","tip":"本句的“attribute”表达“在本句中与 to 搭配表示归因于”。先结合“我们不能把每个变化都归因于降雨。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"被动语态","en":"The report was revised after the discussion.","zh":"讨论后报告经过了修改。","word":"was revised","meaning":"被动语态","tip":"本句的“was revised”表达“被动语态”。先结合“讨论后报告经过了修改。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"尽管","en":"Despite the delay, the project was successful.","zh":"尽管延误，项目仍然成功。","word":"despite","meaning":"尽管","tip":"本句的“despite”表达“尽管”。先结合“尽管延误，项目仍然成功。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"直到","en":"We stayed until enough observations had been recorded.","zh":"我们一直待到记录了足够多的观察结果。","word":"until","meaning":"直到","tip":"本句的“until”表达“直到”。先结合“我们一直待到记录了足够多的观察结果。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"省略 if 的倒装","en":"Had I known about the closure, I would have warned you.","zh":"如果我当时知道封路，就会提醒你。","word":"Had I known","meaning":"省略 if 的倒装","tip":"本句的“Had I known”表达“省略 if 的倒装”。先结合“如果我当时知道封路，就会提醒你。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"原因关系从句","en":"The reason why we changed the plan was practical.","zh":"我们改变计划的原因是现实需要。","word":"the reason why","meaning":"原因关系从句","tip":"本句的“the reason why”表达“原因关系从句”。先结合“我们改变计划的原因是现实需要。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"否定前置倒装","en":"Not only did we save time, but we also improved the method.","zh":"我们不仅省了时间，还改进了方法。","word":"not only","meaning":"否定前置倒装","tip":"本句的“not only”表达“否定前置倒装”。先结合“我们不仅省了时间，还改进了方法。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"比较关联结构","en":"The more carefully we check, the fewer errors we miss.","zh":"我们检查得越仔细，漏掉的错误就越少。","word":"the more","meaning":"比较关联结构","tip":"本句的“the more”表达“比较关联结构”。先结合“我们检查得越仔细，漏掉的错误就越少。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DESPITE: Although it rained, we continued. Complete: ___, we continued.","answers":["Despite the rain"],"explanation":"despite 后接名词短语。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use BEEN: Someone has cancelled the trip. Complete: The trip ___.","answers":["has been cancelled"],"explanation":"现在完成时被动语态。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete with one word: The bridge, ___ opened last year, is closed today.","answers":["which"],"explanation":"非限制性定语从句以 which 指代物。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use HAD: If I had known, I would have called. Complete the inverted condition: ___, I would have called.","answers":["Had I known"],"explanation":"第三条件句可省略 if 并倒装 had。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an essay: Is changing a plan a sign of failure? Discuss preparation and flexibility.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"Changing a plan is often treated as evidence that somebody prepared badly. Although poor preparation can cause difficulties, I believe that a sensible change may actually show good judgement.\nCareful planning is valuable because it makes responsibilities and limits clear. A group travelling to collect information should check transport, equipment and safety requirements. Without this preparation, even a simple activity can waste time. However, no plan can predict every change in circumstances. Weather, illness or an unexpected closure may make the original arrangement unsuitable.\nIn these situations, the important question is whether the purpose can still be achieved. If students can investigate the same research question locally, a shorter journey may be the better choice. They should explain any limitations rather than pretend that the revised method is perfect.\nIn conclusion, a plan should guide decisions rather than prevent them. Success depends on combining preparation with the willingness to reconsider a method when the evidence changes."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Describe a time when a group should change its original plan.","followups":["What should remain unchanged?","How can a leader explain the change fairly?"],"sample":"A group should change its plan when the original method becomes unsafe or no longer serves its purpose. For example, a field trip might be replaced by a local investigation if transport is cancelled. The learning goal should remain clear, even when the location changes. A leader needs to explain the evidence and acknowledge disadvantages. Otherwise, people may think that the change was made simply because it was more convenient for one person."},"project":"把一个真实计划改写成原方案与备用方案，使用条件句、让步句及关系从句。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"We had intended to hold the astronomy evening outside, but cloud cover made that impossible. Rather than cancel, we moved into the school hall and projected photographs taken by club members. I expected people to be disappointed, yet the questions were more detailed than usual because everyone could see the same image. We still refunded anyone who had booked specifically to use the telescope. Next time we will advertise both plans in advance, so visitors can decide whether the indoor alternative meets their expectations. The change worked well, although it was not identical to the experience originally promised.","questions":[{"prompt":"Why did the event move indoors?","answer":"Clouds prevented the planned observation.","options":["Clouds prevented the planned observation.","The telescope had been sold.","The school field was flooded."],"explanation":"原文为 cloud cover。"},{"prompt":"Why were questions more detailed?","answer":"Everyone could examine the same projected image.","options":["Everyone could examine the same projected image.","Only experts were allowed inside.","The event lasted all night."],"explanation":"投影提供共同可见材料。"},{"prompt":"Who could receive a refund?","answer":"People who booked specifically to use the telescope.","options":["People who booked specifically to use the telescope.","Every club member automatically.","Only people who brought photographs."],"explanation":"明确限定退款对象。"}]}},{"chapter":28,"title":"主题词汇与精确表达","target":"B2","focus":"在环境、工作与科技话题中解释取舍","grammar":"可数与不可数；限定词；原因与结果","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"Sharing tools instead of buying them","text":"A neighbourhood library began lending drills, sewing machines and gardening equipment alongside books. Its supporters argued that people should not need to buy an expensive tool for a single afternoon’s work. The scheme could reduce household costs and unnecessary production, provided that shared equipment was maintained properly.\nBefore joining, members attended a short safety session. Staff recorded the condition of each item and kept a small supply of replacement parts. The most popular tool was a carpet cleaner, which several families had previously hired from a commercial shop. A booking calendar prevented arguments about availability, although weekends still filled quickly.\nThe organisers discovered that environmental benefits were harder to calculate than expected. If a member drove across town to borrow something inexpensive, the journey might cancel part of the saving. Durable tools that replaced several purchases offered a clearer benefit. The library therefore prioritised reliable equipment and encouraged collection during other planned trips.\nSix months later, the project had not transformed the local economy, but it had changed conversations about ownership. Members began asking whether access mattered more than possession. The team published repair costs as well as borrowing figures, allowing residents to judge both the practical value and the resources needed to keep the service running."},"sourceB":{"title":"Technology on a family farm","text":"A farmer named Lena installed moisture sensors in three fields to help decide when to water her vegetables. The sales brochure promised efficiency, but she wanted evidence from her own soil before expanding the system. One field remained under her usual watering routine so that she could make a rough comparison.\nDuring the first summer, the sensors helped her avoid watering after light rain when moisture below the surface was already sufficient. They also produced an alarming reading in one corner. A technician discovered that a damaged cable, rather than unusually dry soil, was responsible. Lena kept checking plants directly instead of trusting every number on the screen.\nWater use fell, although an unusually cool August made the size of the technology’s contribution uncertain. The equipment also required a yearly subscription and reliable phone coverage. These costs mattered more to a small farm than to the large businesses shown in the advertising photographs.\nLena decided to continue the trial for another season rather than buy sensors for every field immediately. She regarded technology as a source of additional information, not a replacement for experience. Her neighbours found this cautious conclusion more useful than a simple claim that modern farming was either entirely beneficial or entirely harmful."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"What was the main purpose of the tool library?","answer":"To provide access without requiring individual purchases.","options":["To provide access without requiring individual purchases.","To sell new tools at a profit.","To replace every local shop."],"explanation":"借用设备减少偶尔使用也必须购买的负担。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why did the library prioritise durable equipment?","answer":"It could replace several purchases over time.","options":["It could replace several purchases over time.","It always needed no maintenance.","It made booking unnecessary."],"explanation":"耐用设备的资源节约更有依据。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why publish repair costs?","answer":"So residents could assess the service’s full resource needs.","options":["So residents could assess the service’s full resource needs.","To discourage all borrowing.","To hide unpopular tools."],"explanation":"不仅公布借用量，也公开维护成本。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why did Lena leave one field unchanged?","answer":"To provide a rough comparison.","options":["To provide a rough comparison.","Because vegetables cannot grow there.","Because the technician owned it."],"explanation":"保留原方法有助比较。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What caused the alarming reading?","answer":"A damaged cable.","options":["A damaged cable.","A sudden drought everywhere.","Too much fertiliser."],"explanation":"检查发现是电缆故障。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why delay expansion?","answer":"More evidence was needed alongside an assessment of costs.","options":["More evidence was needed alongside an assessment of costs.","The sensors never worked at all.","Her neighbours prohibited technology."],"explanation":"一年试验受天气等因素影响，且费用重要。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"消费","en":"Shared equipment can reduce unnecessary consumption.","zh":"共享设备能减少不必要的消费。","word":"consumption","meaning":"消费","tip":"本句的“consumption”表达“消费”。先结合“共享设备能减少不必要的消费。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"所有权","en":"Access may matter more than ownership.","zh":"使用权可能比所有权更重要。","word":"ownership","meaning":"所有权","tip":"本句的“ownership”表达“所有权”。先结合“使用权可能比所有权更重要。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"维护","en":"We must consider the cost of maintenance.","zh":"我们必须考虑维护成本。","word":"maintenance","meaning":"维护","tip":"本句的“maintenance”表达“维护”。先结合“我们必须考虑维护成本。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"资源","en":"The booking system distributes limited resources fairly.","zh":"预约系统公平分配有限资源。","word":"resources","meaning":"资源","tip":"本句的“resources”表达“资源”。先结合“预约系统公平分配有限资源。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"耐用的","en":"A durable product should remain useful for years.","zh":"耐用产品应该多年仍可使用。","word":"durable","meaning":"耐用的","tip":"本句的“durable”表达“耐用的”。先结合“耐用产品应该多年仍可使用。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"负担得起的","en":"The scheme offers an affordable alternative.","zh":"该计划提供了价格可承受的替代选择。","word":"affordable","meaning":"负担得起的","tip":"本句的“affordable”表达“负担得起的”。先结合“该计划提供了价格可承受的替代选择。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"证据","en":"There is little evidence of a long term benefit yet.","zh":"目前几乎没有长期收益的证据。","word":"evidence","meaning":"证据","tip":"本句的“evidence”表达“证据”。先结合“目前几乎没有长期收益的证据。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"家庭住户","en":"Several households have joined the scheme.","zh":"几个家庭已加入该计划。","word":"households","meaning":"家庭住户","tip":"本句的“households”表达“家庭住户”。先结合“几个家庭已加入该计划。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"可用性","en":"We need reliable information about availability.","zh":"我们需要可靠的可用情况信息。","word":"availability","meaning":"可用性","tip":"本句的“availability”表达“可用性”。先结合“我们需要可靠的可用情况信息。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"影响","en":"The environmental impact depends on how people travel.","zh":"环境影响取决于人们如何出行。","word":"impact","meaning":"影响","tip":"本句的“impact”表达“影响”。先结合“环境影响取决于人们如何出行。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"超过","en":"The benefits should outweigh the practical disadvantages.","zh":"收益应该超过实际弊端。","word":"outweigh","meaning":"超过","tip":"本句的“outweigh”表达“超过”。先结合“收益应该超过实际弊端。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"透明的","en":"Publishing the costs makes the process more transparent.","zh":"公开成本使过程更加透明。","word":"transparent","meaning":"透明的","tip":"本句的“transparent”表达“透明的”。先结合“公开成本使过程更加透明。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete with much or many: We do not have ___ evidence yet.","answers":["much"],"explanation":"evidence 为不可数名词。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use MAINTAIN: Regular ___ keeps the equipment safe.","answers":["maintenance"],"explanation":"作主语，用 maintenance。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete with one word beginning with t: The benefits outweigh the costs; ___, the scheme is worth continuing.","answers":["therefore"],"explanation":"前句给原因，后句给结论；本题要求结果连接词 therefore。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use AFFORD: The fee must remain ___ for low income families.","answers":["affordable"],"explanation":"remain 后需要形容词 affordable。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an essay on whether sharing products is always better than owning them. Discuss cost and environmental impact.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"Sharing products can help people save money and use fewer resources. However, whether it is better than ownership depends on the product and the way the service operates.\nThe financial argument is strongest for equipment that is expensive but rarely needed. A household may use a powerful drill only once a year, so paying a small borrowing fee makes sense. Sharing can also reduce the number of products manufactured. This benefit is greater when the equipment is durable and easy to repair.\nThere are disadvantages, however. Members may need the same item at the same time, particularly during holidays. Travelling a long distance to collect a small object can also reduce the environmental saving. Moreover, somebody must pay for storage and maintenance.\nI therefore support sharing schemes with realistic booking systems and transparent costs. Ownership remains reasonable for frequently used personal items. The best approach is to compare actual needs rather than assume that either sharing or buying is always the responsible choice."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Compare borrowing a tool with buying one.","followups":["When is ownership more practical?","How could a sharing service reduce travel?"],"sample":"Borrowing makes sense when a tool is expensive and I only need it occasionally. I would avoid paying for storage and maintenance. However, I might have to wait for a booking, and collecting it could take time. Buying is more practical if I use the tool every week or need it urgently. A local sharing service could reduce unnecessary journeys by arranging collection alongside existing trips or offering several neighbourhood collection points."},"project":"为一个共享产品方案制作成本与影响比较表，用英文解释至少三个有条件的结论。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Our bakery tested reusable containers for customers buying soup. We thought the main obstacle would be the deposit, but most customers accepted it once we explained that it was refundable. The real difficulty was returning containers during our short opening hours. We arranged a collection box at the nearby community centre, which stays open later. Containers are washed professionally before reuse; customers are asked only to empty them. We are still counting how many returns each container survives, because a reusable product needs to be used repeatedly before we can judge whether it offers a genuine resource saving.","questions":[{"prompt":"What was the main practical difficulty?","answer":"Returning containers during limited opening hours.","options":["Returning containers during limited opening hours.","Understanding the soup menu.","Finding any washing equipment."],"explanation":"非押金而是归还时间。"},{"prompt":"What must customers do before returning containers?","answer":"Empty them.","options":["Empty them.","Wash them professionally.","Buy another soup."],"explanation":"顾客只需倒空，专业清洗由商家处理。"},{"prompt":"Why track repeated returns?","answer":"To evaluate actual resource savings.","options":["To evaluate actual resource savings.","To identify the fastest customer.","To stop the centre opening late."],"explanation":"重复使用次数关系真实节约。"}]}},{"chapter":29,"title":"选择填空与语篇线索","target":"B2","focus":"结合搭配、词义和上下文排除干扰项","grammar":"词汇选择；转折与因果；固定介词","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"A station becomes a workspace","text":"The former railway station stood empty for years before a local group proposed a shared workspace. Rather than assume that everyone needed a private office, the organisers interviewed potential users about their routines. Freelance designers wanted quiet desks, whereas people running small shops mainly needed a place for occasional meetings. These contrasting needs persuaded the group to divide the building into several areas with different booking rules. The ticket office became a silent room, while the waiting hall was suitable for conversations and shared equipment. Despite limited funding, the group invested in good lighting because poor conditions would discourage people from returning. Membership fees covered ordinary running costs, but major repairs still required support from the council and local businesses. At first, several members complained that the booking website allowed some people to reserve too many afternoons. The organisers responded by introducing a weekly limit rather than blaming members for using the existing system. Within three months, bookings were distributed more evenly, although the most convenient times remained particularly popular with commuters. The project’s success depended less on fashionable furniture than on matching practical arrangements to the way members actually worked. Its report recommended regular consultation, recognising that a flexible service must continue adapting as the community and its working habits change."},"sourceB":{"title":"A mobile library route","text":"A county library introduced a van service for villages where the nearest branch was difficult to reach. The first timetable was designed around population figures. Larger villages received longer visits, while smaller settlements were offered brief stops. On paper, this appeared to distribute staff time fairly.\nAfter several weeks, the driver noticed that attendance depended on more than village size. At one busy stop, the van arrived just after the only morning bus had left. Older residents who used that bus were therefore absent. In another village, the chosen location had steps that made access awkward for some readers. The team adjusted both the timetable and the parking places after speaking to residents.\nThe library also allowed users to request books by telephone before a visit. This reduced disappointment when popular titles were unavailable. However, the van could not provide every service offered by a permanent branch. Private internet appointments and large study tables remained beyond its capacity.\nThe librarian’s final recommendation was to judge the service by how well it reached people who faced barriers, rather than by the number of books carried. A smaller collection, delivered at the right time and place, could be more useful than a larger one that residents could not access."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Why were interviews carried out?","answer":"Users had different working routines.","options":["Users had different working routines.","The station was still operating trains.","Furniture had already been ordered."],"explanation":"访谈揭示不同使用需求。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why did the group prioritise lighting?","answer":"Poor conditions could prevent repeat visits.","options":["Poor conditions could prevent repeat visits.","It was required for train signals.","Members wanted a cinema."],"explanation":"灯光影响使用者是否愿意再来。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"How was unfair booking addressed?","answer":"A weekly reservation limit was introduced.","options":["A weekly reservation limit was introduced.","All online bookings were cancelled forever.","Commuters were banned."],"explanation":"规则调整解决资源分配问题。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was wrong with using population figures alone?","answer":"They missed transport and access barriers.","options":["They missed transport and access barriers.","They could not identify any village.","They measured only book prices."],"explanation":"人口无法反映公交时刻及台阶障碍。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why allow advance telephone requests?","answer":"To improve the chance of bringing wanted books.","options":["To improve the chance of bringing wanted books.","To remove the need for the van.","To charge readers extra."],"explanation":"提前申请能降低找不到所需书的概率。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What should determine success?","answer":"Whether the service reaches people facing barriers.","options":["Whether the service reaches people facing barriers.","The size of the van’s engine.","The number of permanent desks aboard."],"explanation":"结尾提出可达性优先于载书数量。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"提议","en":"The former railway station stood empty for years before a local group proposed a shared workspace.","zh":"旧火车站空置多年后，一个当地团体提议把它变成共享工作场所。","word":"proposed","meaning":"提议","tip":"本句的“proposed”表达“提议”。先结合“旧火车站空置多年后，一个当地团体提议把它变成共享工作场所。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"潜在的","en":"Rather than assume that everyone needed a private office, the organisers interviewed potential users about their routines.","zh":"组织者没有假定人人都需要独立办公室，而是访谈潜在使用者的工作习惯。","word":"potential","meaning":"潜在的","tip":"本句的“potential”表达“潜在的”。先结合“组织者没有假定人人都需要独立办公室，而是访谈潜在使用者的工作习惯。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"然而","en":"Freelance designers wanted quiet desks, whereas people running small shops mainly needed a place for occasional meetings.","zh":"自由设计师需要安静书桌，而小店经营者主要需要偶尔开会的地方。","word":"whereas","meaning":"然而","tip":"本句的“whereas”表达“然而”。先结合“自由设计师需要安静书桌，而小店经营者主要需要偶尔开会的地方。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"形成对照的","en":"These contrasting needs persuaded the group to divide the building into several areas with different booking rules.","zh":"这些不同需求促使团队把建筑分成几个预约规则不同的区域。","word":"contrasting","meaning":"形成对照的","tip":"本句的“contrasting”表达“形成对照的”。先结合“这些不同需求促使团队把建筑分成几个预约规则不同的区域。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"适合的","en":"The ticket office became a silent room, while the waiting hall was suitable for conversations and shared equipment.","zh":"售票处变成了安静室，候车厅则适合交流和共享设备。","word":"suitable","meaning":"适合的","tip":"本句的“suitable”表达“适合的”。先结合“售票处变成了安静室，候车厅则适合交流和共享设备。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"使失去意愿","en":"Despite limited funding, the group invested in good lighting because poor conditions would discourage people from returning.","zh":"尽管资金有限，团队仍投入优质照明，因为不好的环境会使人不再来。","word":"discourage","meaning":"使失去意愿","tip":"本句的“discourage”表达“使失去意愿”。先结合“尽管资金有限，团队仍投入优质照明，因为不好的环境会使人不再来。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"支付得起","en":"Membership fees covered ordinary running costs, but major repairs still required support from the council and local businesses.","zh":"会员费承担日常运营成本，但大修仍需议会及当地企业支持。","word":"covered","meaning":"支付得起","tip":"本句的“covered”表达“支付得起”。先结合“会员费承担日常运营成本，但大修仍需议会及当地企业支持。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"预订","en":"At first, several members complained that the booking website allowed some people to reserve too many afternoons.","zh":"起初，几位成员抱怨预约网站允许部分人预订过多下午时段。","word":"reserve","meaning":"预订","tip":"本句的“reserve”表达“预订”。先结合“起初，几位成员抱怨预约网站允许部分人预订过多下午时段。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"回应","en":"The organisers responded by introducing a weekly limit rather than blaming members for using the existing system.","zh":"组织者通过增加每周上限回应，而不是责怪成员使用现有系统。","word":"responded","meaning":"回应","tip":"本句的“responded”表达“回应”。先结合“组织者通过增加每周上限回应，而不是责怪成员使用现有系统。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"均匀地","en":"Within three months, bookings were distributed more evenly, although the most convenient times remained particularly popular with commuters.","zh":"三个月内预约分配更均衡了，不过通勤者仍特别喜欢最方便的时段。","word":"evenly","meaning":"均匀地","tip":"本句的“evenly”表达“均匀地”。先结合“三个月内预约分配更均衡了，不过通勤者仍特别喜欢最方便的时段。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"取决于","en":"The project’s success depended less on fashionable furniture than on matching practical arrangements to the way members actually worked.","zh":"项目成功与时髦家具关系较小，更多取决于实际安排符合成员工作方式。","word":"depended less on","meaning":"取决于","tip":"本句的“depended less on”表达“取决于”。先结合“项目成功与时髦家具关系较小，更多取决于实际安排符合成员工作方式。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"征询意见","en":"Its report recommended regular consultation, recognising that a flexible service must continue adapting as the community and its working habits change.","zh":"报告建议定期征求意见，认识到灵活服务必须随社区和工作习惯变化持续调整。","word":"consultation","meaning":"征询意见","tip":"本句的“consultation”表达“征询意见”。先结合“报告建议定期征求意见，认识到灵活服务必须随社区和工作习惯变化持续调整。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose depend, discuss or consist: Success will ___ on careful planning.","answers":["depend"],"explanation":"depend on 表示取决于；discuss 直接接宾语，consist 常与 of 连用。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose suitable, capable or aware: This room is ___ for quiet study.","answers":["suitable"],"explanation":"suitable for，其他两词常接 of。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use CONSULT: Regular ___ helps us understand users.","answers":["consultation"],"explanation":"形容词 regular 后用名词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DESPITE: Although funds were limited, work continued. Complete: ___, work continued.","answers":["Despite limited funds"],"explanation":"保留让步关系，despite 后名词短语。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an article explaining what makes a shared workplace useful. Discuss facilities and fair access.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"A shared workplace should offer more than an attractive photograph for its website. Its real value comes from helping people do their work comfortably and reliably.\nFacilities matter, but different tasks require different conditions. Somebody editing a long report may need silence, while a small business owner may want a room for a lively meeting. Separating these activities is usually more effective than asking everyone to tolerate the same level of noise. Reliable lighting and internet access are also sensible priorities before expensive decoration.\nFair access is equally important. If a few members can book every convenient afternoon, others may stop paying for the service. Clear reservation limits and a visible cancellation policy help prevent this problem.\nFinally, organisers should consult users regularly. Working habits can change, and a successful arrangement this year may become unsuitable later. A useful workplace is therefore one that combines dependable basic facilities with a willingness to adjust its rules when genuine difficulties appear."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how you would design a shared study space.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"I would divide the space into a quiet area and a separate discussion room. This would avoid expecting people with different tasks to work under identical conditions. I would also prioritise lighting, comfortable desks and reliable internet before decoration. Booking rules should prevent one group from reserving every popular time. After opening, I would ask users about practical problems and make small changes before assuming that the original design was correct."},"project":"制作六题搭配选择题，每个干扰项都解释为什么在该语境不合适。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"When our choir moved rehearsals to a new hall, we assumed the larger room would solve every problem. It did give us more space, but the echo made it difficult to hear neighbouring singers. Thick curtains improved the sound, although they could not be closed across the emergency exit. We eventually moved the chairs into a semicircle at the other end of the room. Members could hear one another better without blocking access. I am glad we tried several arrangements before buying expensive equipment. The most obvious improvement, more space, was not the only condition needed for a useful rehearsal.","questions":[{"prompt":"What problem did the new hall introduce?","answer":"An echo made other singers difficult to hear.","options":["An echo made other singers difficult to hear.","There were too few chairs.","There was no emergency exit."],"explanation":"空间变大却回声大。"},{"prompt":"Why not close curtains everywhere?","answer":"An emergency exit had to remain clear.","options":["An emergency exit had to remain clear.","Curtains were banned in all halls.","Singers wanted direct sunlight."],"explanation":"消防通道限制安排。"},{"prompt":"What finally helped?","answer":"A semicircle arrangement elsewhere in the room.","options":["A semicircle arrangement elsewhere in the room.","Removing every singer except one.","Buying the largest speakers."],"explanation":"重新布置椅子改善互听。"}]}},{"chapter":30,"title":"开放填空与构词综合","target":"B2","focus":"由语法功能确定缺失词，并核对语篇意义","grammar":"冠词；代词；助动词；连接词；派生词","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"A visitor guide written together","text":"Our town’s first walking guide was written by a historian whose knowledge was impressive but whose sentences were extremely long. Although the information was accurate, visitors often stopped reading before they reached the description of the next building. The tourism office invited residents and language learners to help produce a version that would be easier to follow. Each volunteer was asked to explain one location in fewer than a hundred words without removing its main historical significance. This limit forced writers to decide which details were essential and which could be provided separately for interested readers. One contributor replaced an unfamiliar architectural term with a brief description of what visitors could actually see above the door. Another checked whether references such as this and it clearly pointed to the correct building rather than the previous one. The team read the draft aloud because a missing word or awkward connection was often easier to notice by listening. They also walked the route with first time visitors, who revealed that two directions depended on a shop sign that had disappeared. The final guide used permanent landmarks and included enough context for readers to understand why each stop had been selected. It was not simply shorter than the original; its organisation made the relationship between places and explanations much clearer for newcomers. The experience taught the volunteers that successful editing requires attention to grammar, meaning and the reader’s situation at the same time."},"sourceB":{"title":"Learning from an empty noticeboard","text":"A university accommodation office placed all its announcements on an electronic noticeboard near the entrance. Staff believed the large screen would be more noticeable than paper messages. It displayed maintenance dates, social events and instructions for reporting faults. Yet students continued asking questions whose answers had already appeared there.\nAn investigation showed that each announcement remained visible for only eight seconds. A student carrying shopping bags could miss an entire message while opening the door. International students also needed more time to interpret unfamiliar terms. The problem was therefore not simply a lack of interest.\nThe office separated urgent notices from general news. Essential information stayed on screen, while a printed summary was available beside the lift. Every maintenance announcement included a date, an affected location and a contact person. Social events moved to a separate display inside the common room.\nQuestions about maintenance fell over the following month, although staff continued to offer help in person. They had learned that making information available was not the same as making it usable. The redesigned system gave residents enough time and context to act, instead of expecting them to adapt to the limitations of a fashionable display. Staff also scheduled regular checks to remove messages once they were no longer relevant."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Why did readers stop using the original guide?","answer":"Its sentences were too long.","options":["Its sentences were too long.","Its historical facts were all false.","It had no buildings in it."],"explanation":"文章对比信息准确但句子过长。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why read the draft aloud?","answer":"To detect missing words and awkward connections.","options":["To detect missing words and awkward connections.","To record an advertisement.","To avoid checking meaning."],"explanation":"朗读是检查语法和衔接的方法。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why use permanent landmarks?","answer":"A shop sign used in directions had disappeared.","options":["A shop sign used in directions had disappeared.","All visitors knew the town already.","The route was being cancelled."],"explanation":"实走发现可变店招不可靠。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why did residents miss announcements?","answer":"Messages disappeared too quickly during entry.","options":["Messages disappeared too quickly during entry.","The screen was always switched off.","Students were forbidden to stop."],"explanation":"八秒轮换且进门忙碌，不足以读懂。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What changed for urgent information?","answer":"It remained visible instead of rotating away.","options":["It remained visible instead of rotating away.","It moved entirely online.","It was written without dates."],"explanation":"紧急信息持续显示。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What principle did staff learn?","answer":"Available information is not necessarily usable.","options":["Available information is not necessarily usable.","Large screens always improve understanding.","In person help is unnecessary."],"explanation":"末段明确区分 available 与 usable。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"所属关系","en":"Our town’s first walking guide was written by a historian whose knowledge was impressive but whose sentences were extremely long.","zh":"我们镇第一份步行指南由一位历史学家撰写，他知识丰富，但句子很长。","word":"whose","meaning":"所属关系","tip":"本句的“whose”表达“所属关系”。先结合“我们镇第一份步行指南由一位历史学家撰写，他知识丰富，但句子很长。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"虽然","en":"Although the information was accurate, visitors often stopped reading before they reached the description of the next building.","zh":"虽然信息准确，游客常在读到下一栋建筑介绍前就不再读了。","word":"although","meaning":"虽然","tip":"本句的“although”表达“虽然”。先结合“虽然信息准确，游客常在读到下一栋建筑介绍前就不再读了。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"版本","en":"The tourism office invited residents and language learners to help produce a version that would be easier to follow.","zh":"旅游办公室邀请居民和语言学习者帮助制作更易理解的版本。","word":"version","meaning":"版本","tip":"本句的“version”表达“版本”。先结合“旅游办公室邀请居民和语言学习者帮助制作更易理解的版本。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"意义","en":"Each volunteer was asked to explain one location in fewer than a hundred words without removing its main historical significance.","zh":"每位志愿者要用不到一百词解释一个地点，同时保留主要历史意义。","word":"significance","meaning":"意义","tip":"本句的“significance”表达“意义”。先结合“每位志愿者要用不到一百词解释一个地点，同时保留主要历史意义。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"必要的","en":"This limit forced writers to decide which details were essential and which could be provided separately for interested readers.","zh":"这一限制迫使作者判断哪些细节必要，哪些可以另供感兴趣的读者查看。","word":"essential","meaning":"必要的","tip":"本句的“essential”表达“必要的”。先结合“这一限制迫使作者判断哪些细节必要，哪些可以另供感兴趣的读者查看。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"不熟悉的","en":"One contributor replaced an unfamiliar architectural term with a brief description of what visitors could actually see above the door.","zh":"一位投稿者用游客能在门上方看到的景象简短描述替代了生僻建筑术语。","word":"unfamiliar","meaning":"不熟悉的","tip":"本句的“unfamiliar”表达“不熟悉的”。先结合“一位投稿者用游客能在门上方看到的景象简短描述替代了生僻建筑术语。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"指代","en":"Another checked whether references such as this and it clearly pointed to the correct building rather than the previous one.","zh":"另一位检查 this 和 it 等指代是否清楚指向正确建筑而不是上一栋。","word":"references","meaning":"指代","tip":"本句的“references”表达“指代”。先结合“另一位检查 this 和 it 等指代是否清楚指向正确建筑而不是上一栋。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"草稿","en":"The team read the draft aloud because a missing word or awkward connection was often easier to notice by listening.","zh":"团队朗读草稿，因为听的时候往往更容易发现漏词或衔接不顺。","word":"draft","meaning":"草稿","tip":"本句的“draft”表达“草稿”。先结合“团队朗读草稿，因为听的时候往往更容易发现漏词或衔接不顺。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"依赖","en":"They also walked the route with first time visitors, who revealed that two directions depended on a shop sign that had disappeared.","zh":"他们还带首次来访的游客走路线，发现两条指引依赖一块已经消失的店招。","word":"depended","meaning":"依赖","tip":"本句的“depended”表达“依赖”。先结合“他们还带首次来访的游客走路线，发现两条指引依赖一块已经消失的店招。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"地标","en":"The final guide used permanent landmarks and included enough context for readers to understand why each stop had been selected.","zh":"最终指南使用永久地标，并提供足够背景让读者理解每站为何被选中。","word":"landmarks","meaning":"地标","tip":"本句的“landmarks”表达“地标”。先结合“最终指南使用永久地标，并提供足够背景让读者理解每站为何被选中。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"组织","en":"It was not simply shorter than the original; its organisation made the relationship between places and explanations much clearer for newcomers.","zh":"它不只是比原版短，组织方式也让新来者更清楚地点与解释的关系。","word":"organisation","meaning":"组织","tip":"本句的“organisation”表达“组织”。先结合“它不只是比原版短，组织方式也让新来者更清楚地点与解释的关系。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"编辑","en":"The experience taught the volunteers that successful editing requires attention to grammar, meaning and the reader’s situation at the same time.","zh":"这次经历让志愿者明白，成功编辑需要同时注意语法、意义及读者情境。","word":"editing","meaning":"编辑","tip":"本句的“editing”表达“编辑”。先结合“这次经历让志愿者明白，成功编辑需要同时注意语法、意义及读者情境。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word beginning with w: This is the guide ___ we revised yesterday. Use a relative pronoun referring to a thing.","answers":["which"],"explanation":"which 指 guide；明确要求该关系代词，避免零关系代词。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: Neither the map ___ the text was clear.","answers":["nor"],"explanation":"neither...nor。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use SIGNIFICANT: The building has historical ___.","answers":["significance"],"explanation":"形容词 historical 后需要名词。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: The text was so long ___ nobody finished it.","answers":["that"],"explanation":"so...that 结果结构。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a report recommending improvements to a visitor guide. Consider language and directions.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"The current walking guide contains useful historical information, but visitors may find it difficult to follow. Two changes would make it more practical without removing its educational value.\nFirst, the language should be revised. Several sentences contain too many ideas, and technical terms appear without explanation. Shorter sentences would help, but the aim should be clarity rather than a strict word limit. Essential unfamiliar terms could remain if a brief explanation followed them.\nSecond, the directions should rely on permanent landmarks. A reference to a temporary shop sign may become inaccurate within a few months. Street names and the visible features of buildings would be safer choices. Each instruction should also make clear whether visitors need to turn before or after a particular point.\nI recommend testing the revised guide with people who do not know the town. Asking them to walk the route would reveal problems that experienced residents might overlook. Their experience should inform the final version."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Describe how to check whether written directions are clear.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"I would first check that each instruction names a visible, permanent landmark. Then I would ask somebody unfamiliar with the area to follow the route without extra help. If they stopped or turned incorrectly, I would ask which words influenced their decision. This is better than simply asking whether the directions look clear. I would also check references such as it and this, because they can point to more than one place."},"project":"重写一段旅行指引，设计四个开放填空并说明每个空格的语法约束。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"This message is for volunteers helping with Saturday’s beach survey. Please meet at the north car park at nine, not the café entrance mentioned in the first email. You need sturdy shoes and a waterproof jacket, even if the morning looks bright. We will provide gloves and collection bags. The survey is about recording the types of litter, so please do not put everything into one bag before the coordinator has counted it. If strong winds make the beach unsafe, we will notify you by text at seven thirty. Otherwise, assume the session is going ahead as planned.","questions":[{"prompt":"Where should volunteers meet?","answer":"The north car park.","options":["The north car park.","The café entrance.","The south railway station."],"explanation":"消息修正最初地点。"},{"prompt":"Why keep litter separate initially?","answer":"The coordinator needs to count the different types.","options":["The coordinator needs to count the different types.","Volunteers must take it home.","Bags are unavailable."],"explanation":"调查需要分类计数。"},{"prompt":"When will an unsafe weather cancellation be announced?","answer":"At seven thirty by text.","options":["At seven thirty by text.","At noon on the website only.","After everyone reaches the beach."],"explanation":"明确七点半短信通知。"}]}},{"chapter":31,"title":"长文理解与推断","target":"B2","focus":"区分作者主张、证据、推断及过度概括","grammar":"指代链；限定与保留表达；篇章结构","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"The night train returns","text":"When the night train returned to our region, newspaper headlines described it as a complete solution to uncomfortable travel. My first journey suggested a more complicated picture, although I was pleased to reach the city without losing a working day. The compartment was quiet enough for reading, but the movement made deep sleep difficult for passengers unfamiliar with overnight trains. A fellow traveller explained that saving a hotel bill mattered more to her than arriving completely rested for a holiday. For a businessman preparing to give a presentation, however, the same trade off might be much less attractive or practical. Ticket prices were lower when booked early, so comparisons with flights needed to use equivalent dates and booking conditions. The operator advertised reduced environmental impact, but its short publicity leaflet did not explain how the figures had been calculated. This absence did not prove the claim false; it meant that a careful reader needed additional information before accepting it. At the destination, a convenient tram connection helped passengers avoid a costly taxi ride from an airport far outside town. That advantage depended on where travellers actually needed to go, rather than on the station’s central location in itself. I would choose the train again for a relaxed visit, while considering other options before an important morning meeting. The service expands our choices, yet its usefulness cannot be judged accurately without considering the priorities and circumstances of different passengers."},"sourceB":{"title":"What an audience survey missed","text":"A regional theatre surveyed people who had attended at least one performance during the previous year. Most respondents praised its programme and said they were likely to return. The board initially interpreted this as evidence that the theatre was meeting the needs of the whole region.\nOne researcher pointed out that the survey excluded people who had never attended. Their reasons might include high prices, unsuitable times or a belief that the building was not welcoming. Positive responses from existing customers could reveal satisfaction, but they could not explain the absence of everyone else.\nThe theatre commissioned interviews in libraries, workplaces and a youth centre. These conversations produced mixed results. Some residents simply preferred other activities, while others were interested but had difficulty getting home after evening performances. A trial afternoon show attracted several new visitors, although the theatre could not tell whether they would become regular customers.\nThe board retained the original survey because it still provided useful information. It changed the conclusion rather than discarding the evidence. The revised report distinguished the opinions of current audiences from barriers faced by potential audiences. This narrower claim was less impressive as publicity, but more valuable when deciding how to spend a limited budget."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"What does the writer think of the train?","answer":"Its value depends on the traveller’s priorities.","options":["Its value depends on the traveller’s priorities.","It is unsuitable for every passenger.","It always guarantees deep sleep."],"explanation":"作者对不同任务条件作出区分。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What does missing calculation detail mean?","answer":"Further information is needed to evaluate the claim.","options":["Further information is needed to evaluate the claim.","The claim must be false.","Environmental impact does not matter."],"explanation":"缺少证据不能直接推断造假。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why mention the businessman?","answer":"To show a priority different from the holiday traveller’s.","options":["To show a priority different from the holiday traveller’s.","To establish that business travel is banned.","To prove all hotels are unnecessary."],"explanation":"举例说明相同取舍对不同人不同。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was the survey’s main limitation?","answer":"It included only previous attendees.","options":["It included only previous attendees.","It asked no questions about satisfaction.","It interviewed residents from another region."],"explanation":"样本排除了未到访者。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What barrier did later interviews reveal?","answer":"Difficulty travelling home after evening shows.","options":["Difficulty travelling home after evening shows.","A complete lack of interest in any arts.","A ban on afternoon performances."],"explanation":"交通问题是新增具体证据。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why retain the first survey?","answer":"It remained informative about existing audiences.","options":["It remained informative about existing audiences.","It proved the entire region was satisfied.","Its conclusions could never be changed."],"explanation":"缩小结论适用范围而不是全盘丢弃。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"新闻标题","en":"When the night train returned to our region, newspaper headlines described it as a complete solution to uncomfortable travel.","zh":"当夜间列车回到本地区，报纸标题称它是解决出行不适的完整方案。","word":"headlines","meaning":"新闻标题","tip":"本句的“headlines”表达“新闻标题”。先结合“当夜间列车回到本地区，报纸标题称它是解决出行不适的完整方案。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"复杂的","en":"My first journey suggested a more complicated picture, although I was pleased to reach the city without losing a working day.","zh":"首次旅程让我看到更复杂的情况，虽然我很高兴没损失一个工作日就到达城市。","word":"complicated","meaning":"复杂的","tip":"本句的“complicated”表达“复杂的”。先结合“首次旅程让我看到更复杂的情况，虽然我很高兴没损失一个工作日就到达城市。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"隔间","en":"The compartment was quiet enough for reading, but the movement made deep sleep difficult for passengers unfamiliar with overnight trains.","zh":"车厢安静到可以阅读，但晃动让不习惯夜车的乘客难以熟睡。","word":"compartment","meaning":"隔间","tip":"本句的“compartment”表达“隔间”。先结合“车厢安静到可以阅读，但晃动让不习惯夜车的乘客难以熟睡。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"休息充分的","en":"A fellow traveller explained that saving a hotel bill mattered more to her than arriving completely rested for a holiday.","zh":"一位同行旅客解释，对她而言省住宿费比度假时精神饱满地到达更重要。","word":"rested","meaning":"休息充分的","tip":"本句的“rested”表达“休息充分的”。先结合“一位同行旅客解释，对她而言省住宿费比度假时精神饱满地到达更重要。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"取舍","en":"For a businessman preparing to give a presentation, however, the same trade off might be much less attractive or practical.","zh":"但对准备演讲的商务人士，同样的取舍可能远不那么有吸引力或实用。","word":"trade off","meaning":"取舍","tip":"本句的“trade off”表达“取舍”。先结合“但对准备演讲的商务人士，同样的取舍可能远不那么有吸引力或实用。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"等同的","en":"Ticket prices were lower when booked early, so comparisons with flights needed to use equivalent dates and booking conditions.","zh":"提前订票更便宜，因此与飞机比较必须使用相同日期及订票条件。","word":"equivalent","meaning":"等同的","tip":"本句的“equivalent”表达“等同的”。先结合“提前订票更便宜，因此与飞机比较必须使用相同日期及订票条件。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"计算","en":"The operator advertised reduced environmental impact, but its short publicity leaflet did not explain how the figures had been calculated.","zh":"运营方宣传环境影响降低，但简短宣传页没有解释数字如何计算。","word":"calculated","meaning":"计算","tip":"本句的“calculated”表达“计算”。先结合“运营方宣传环境影响降低，但简短宣传页没有解释数字如何计算。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"缺少","en":"This absence did not prove the claim false; it meant that a careful reader needed additional information before accepting it.","zh":"缺少解释并不证明主张错误，而是意味着审慎读者需更多信息再接受它。","word":"absence","meaning":"缺少","tip":"本句的“absence”表达“缺少”。先结合“缺少解释并不证明主张错误，而是意味着审慎读者需更多信息再接受它。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"接驳","en":"At the destination, a convenient tram connection helped passengers avoid a costly taxi ride from an airport far outside town.","zh":"到站后方便的电车接驳让乘客避免从远郊机场搭昂贵出租车。","word":"connection","meaning":"接驳","tip":"本句的“connection”表达“接驳”。先结合“到站后方便的电车接驳让乘客避免从远郊机场搭昂贵出租车。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"优势","en":"That advantage depended on where travellers actually needed to go, rather than on the station’s central location in itself.","zh":"这一优势取决于旅客实际目的地，而不只是车站本身处于市中心。","word":"advantage","meaning":"优势","tip":"本句的“advantage”表达“优势”。先结合“这一优势取决于旅客实际目的地，而不只是车站本身处于市中心。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"选择","en":"I would choose the train again for a relaxed visit, while considering other options before an important morning meeting.","zh":"悠闲旅行我会再选火车，但重要早会前会考虑其他选择。","word":"options","meaning":"选择","tip":"本句的“options”表达“选择”。先结合“悠闲旅行我会再选火车，但重要早会前会考虑其他选择。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"优先事项","en":"The service expands our choices, yet its usefulness cannot be judged accurately without considering the priorities and circumstances of different passengers.","zh":"该服务增加了选择，但不考虑不同乘客的重点与情况就不能准确判断用途。","word":"priorities","meaning":"优先事项","tip":"本句的“priorities”表达“优先事项”。先结合“该服务增加了选择，但不考虑不同乘客的重点与情况就不能准确判断用途。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: Missing evidence does not ___ prove the claim false. Use NECESSARY in its adverb form.","answers":["necessarily"],"explanation":"not necessarily 表示不一定。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use COMPARE: A fair ___ needs equivalent booking dates.","answers":["comparison"],"explanation":"冠词 a 后需要名词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DEPENDS: Different priorities affect usefulness. Complete: Usefulness ___ different priorities.","answers":["depends on"],"explanation":"depend on 表示取决于。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: The train was useful, ___ it did not suit every journey. Use a contrasting conjunction beginning with b.","answers":["but"],"explanation":"but 连接转折。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an essay: Should travel choices be judged mainly by price? Discuss two other considerations.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"Price is an important part of a travel decision, especially for people with a limited budget. Nevertheless, comparing tickets alone can give a misleading picture of which option is best.\nTime should also be considered. A cheap flight may require a long journey to an airport and an early arrival for security checks. A more expensive train could bring a passenger much closer to the final destination. The relevant comparison is therefore the whole journey rather than the advertised travelling time.\nComfort matters differently according to the purpose of the trip. Somebody going on holiday may enjoy an overnight train despite sleeping less well. A person preparing for an important meeting might reasonably prefer a different arrangement.\nIn my view, travellers should begin with their actual priorities and compare options under similar booking conditions. The cheapest ticket can be a good choice, but it is not automatically the most economical or suitable journey overall."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain why two people might judge the same journey differently.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"Two people can have different priorities even when they take the same route. A student visiting friends may accept a long overnight journey to save money. A person attending a morning interview might care more about arriving rested and avoiding delays. Neither choice is automatically unreasonable. I would compare the whole cost, including local transport and accommodation, and then decide which disadvantages I could realistically accept for that particular trip."},"project":"分析一篇宣传短文，标注事实、推断、评价及仍需核实的主张。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Our office tested a four day working week, and several headlines later described the results as proof that every organisation should do the same. I would be more careful. We maintained output and staff reported less stress, but we are a small team with projects that can be scheduled in advance. A hospital or emergency repair service faces different demands. The trial also coincided with a relatively quiet period. I support continuing our arrangement, yet I would recommend that other employers investigate their own conditions instead of copying our timetable and expecting an identical result.","questions":[{"prompt":"How does the speaker view the trial?","answer":"Successful locally but not automatically transferable.","options":["Successful locally but not automatically transferable.","A complete failure in every respect.","Proof that hospitals should close a day."],"explanation":"肯定本地效果但限定外推。"},{"prompt":"What may have helped the result?","answer":"A relatively quiet period.","options":["A relatively quiet period.","A sudden increase in emergencies.","A permanent loss of all projects."],"explanation":"提及淡季因素。"},{"prompt":"What should other employers do?","answer":"Examine their own working conditions.","options":["Examine their own working conditions.","Adopt the same timetable without review.","Ignore all staff feedback."],"explanation":"建议本地调查而非直接复制。"}]}},{"chapter":32,"title":"补句与多重匹配","target":"B2","focus":"用指代、时间和论证关系恢复篇章","grammar":"连接副词；代词回指；主题句与例证","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"Restoring a woodland path","text":"At the first meeting about restoring a woodland path, volunteers disagreed about whether visitors needed more information or fewer signs. The discussion initially seemed to concern appearance, but it soon revealed different ideas about who would use the route. Regular walkers already recognised the turns, whereas families visiting for the first time wanted reassurance that they were not lost. This difference explained why an apparently unnecessary marker could still be valuable to somebody with less local knowledge. Rather than choose one group’s preference, the team walked the path with visitors who had never seen it before. At a fallen tree, everyone paused because the original route continued behind branches that concealed the next turning point. The team cleared those branches first, which solved a navigation problem without adding another object to the landscape. Further along, however, two equally visible paths led in different directions, so a small arrow remained necessary at that junction. Its wording referred to the destination rather than merely saying continue, allowing walkers to connect the instruction with their map. After the changes, visitors made fewer wrong turns, although heavy rain later created a separate problem with the muddy surface. The final maintenance plan therefore separated tasks concerned with understanding the route from tasks concerned with moving along it safely. This distinction helped volunteers agree on priorities, because solving a communication problem would not automatically repair a damaged physical path."},"sourceB":{"title":"Three approaches to learning a craft","text":"Three adults described what helped them learn pottery. Mira preferred demonstrations followed immediately by practice. Watching a complete film at home had seemed convenient, but she repeatedly forgot the early steps by the time she reached her wheel. A short demonstration in the workshop allowed her to try each movement while it was still fresh. She did not mind working slowly, provided she understood why a step mattered.\nDaniel valued written notes. His instructor’s quick hand movements were difficult to follow, especially when several actions happened together. A numbered checklist helped him identify where he had lost control of the clay. He still watched demonstrations, but the written sequence gave him something stable to return to afterwards.\nRosa learned most from comparing two imperfect objects. When a teacher placed a cracked bowl beside a successful one, she could see how uneven thickness affected drying. General praise was less helpful because it did not tell her what to change. She welcomed specific criticism when it included a practical next step.\nAll three appreciated patient teaching, yet their accounts suggest that patience alone was insufficient. Each person needed information in a form that helped them notice and correct a particular difficulty during practice."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"What does “This difference” refer to?","answer":"The contrast between familiar and first time walkers.","options":["The contrast between familiar and first time walkers.","A difference in tree species.","The cost of two maps."],"explanation":"回指前句两类游客的需求差异。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why was an arrow still needed?","answer":"Two visible paths led to different destinations.","options":["Two visible paths led to different destinations.","All branches were still blocking the route.","Nobody had a map."],"explanation":"一个路口仍存在选择歧义。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why distinguish two task types?","answer":"Clear directions do not repair physical damage.","options":["Clear directions do not repair physical damage.","Signs always create mud.","Visitors never read directions."],"explanation":"信息可理解性与路面安全需要不同工作。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Who needed a stable sequence to revisit?","answer":"Daniel.","options":["Daniel.","Mira.","Rosa."],"explanation":"Daniel 使用编号清单重看步骤。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Who valued comparison between objects?","answer":"Rosa.","options":["Rosa.","Daniel.","Mira."],"explanation":"Rosa 比较开裂与成功碗。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Who preferred immediately applying a brief demonstration?","answer":"Mira.","options":["Mira.","Rosa.","Daniel."],"explanation":"Mira 需要动作仍新鲜时马上练习。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"修复","en":"At the first meeting about restoring a woodland path, volunteers disagreed about whether visitors needed more information or fewer signs.","zh":"第一次修复林间小径会议上，志愿者对游客需要更多信息还是更少标牌意见不一。","word":"restoring","meaning":"修复","tip":"本句的“restoring”表达“修复”。先结合“第一次修复林间小径会议上，志愿者对游客需要更多信息还是更少标牌意见不一。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"揭示","en":"The discussion initially seemed to concern appearance, but it soon revealed different ideas about who would use the route.","zh":"讨论起初似乎关于外观，很快却显出大家对路线使用者的不同设想。","word":"revealed","meaning":"揭示","tip":"本句的“revealed”表达“揭示”。先结合“讨论起初似乎关于外观，很快却显出大家对路线使用者的不同设想。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"安心确认","en":"Regular walkers already recognised the turns, whereas families visiting for the first time wanted reassurance that they were not lost.","zh":"常客认识转弯处，而首次来的家庭想确认自己没走丢。","word":"reassurance","meaning":"安心确认","tip":"本句的“reassurance”表达“安心确认”。先结合“常客认识转弯处，而首次来的家庭想确认自己没走丢。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"标记","en":"This difference explained why an apparently unnecessary marker could still be valuable to somebody with less local knowledge.","zh":"这种差异解释了为何看似多余的标记对不熟悉当地的人仍有价值。","word":"marker","meaning":"标记","tip":"本句的“marker”表达“标记”。先结合“这种差异解释了为何看似多余的标记对不熟悉当地的人仍有价值。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"偏好","en":"Rather than choose one group’s preference, the team walked the path with visitors who had never seen it before.","zh":"团队没有只选一组人的偏好，而是和从未见过路线的游客一起实走。","word":"preference","meaning":"偏好","tip":"本句的“preference”表达“偏好”。先结合“团队没有只选一组人的偏好，而是和从未见过路线的游客一起实走。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"遮挡","en":"At a fallen tree, everyone paused because the original route continued behind branches that concealed the next turning point.","zh":"在倒树处大家都停了，因为原路线在遮住下个弯的树枝后延伸。","word":"concealed","meaning":"遮挡","tip":"本句的“concealed”表达“遮挡”。先结合“在倒树处大家都停了，因为原路线在遮住下个弯的树枝后延伸。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"路线辨识","en":"The team cleared those branches first, which solved a navigation problem without adding another object to the landscape.","zh":"团队先清理树枝，不必再向景观增加物品就解决了认路问题。","word":"navigation","meaning":"路线辨识","tip":"本句的“navigation”表达“路线辨识”。先结合“团队先清理树枝，不必再向景观增加物品就解决了认路问题。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"岔口","en":"Further along, however, two equally visible paths led in different directions, so a small arrow remained necessary at that junction.","zh":"但更前方两条同样明显的小路通向不同方向，所以岔口仍需小箭头。","word":"junction","meaning":"岔口","tip":"本句的“junction”表达“岔口”。先结合“但更前方两条同样明显的小路通向不同方向，所以岔口仍需小箭头。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"目的地","en":"Its wording referred to the destination rather than merely saying continue, allowing walkers to connect the instruction with their map.","zh":"标牌写目的地而不只写继续，让步行者能将指引与地图对应。","word":"destination","meaning":"目的地","tip":"本句的“destination”表达“目的地”。先结合“标牌写目的地而不只写继续，让步行者能将指引与地图对应。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"表面","en":"After the changes, visitors made fewer wrong turns, although heavy rain later created a separate problem with the muddy surface.","zh":"修改后游客走错更少，尽管后来大雨又造成泥泞路面这一独立问题。","word":"surface","meaning":"表面","tip":"本句的“surface”表达“表面”。先结合“修改后游客走错更少，尽管后来大雨又造成泥泞路面这一独立问题。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"维护计划","en":"The final maintenance plan therefore separated tasks concerned with understanding the route from tasks concerned with moving along it safely.","zh":"最终维护计划因而把理解路线的任务与安全行走的任务分开。","word":"maintenance plan","meaning":"维护计划","tip":"本句的“maintenance plan”表达“维护计划”。先结合“最终维护计划因而把理解路线的任务与安全行走的任务分开。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"区分","en":"This distinction helped volunteers agree on priorities, because solving a communication problem would not automatically repair a damaged physical path.","zh":"这一区分帮助志愿者确定重点，因为解决沟通问题不会自动修好损坏的路。","word":"distinction","meaning":"区分","tip":"本句的“distinction”表达“区分”。先结合“这一区分帮助志愿者确定重点，因为解决沟通问题不会自动修好损坏的路。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: The path was visible. ___, an arrow was needed because the junction was ambiguous. Use the contrast adverb starting with H.","answers":["However"],"explanation":"However 承接让步后的转折。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use REFER: This difference points back to the previous sentence. Complete: This difference ___ the previous sentence.","answers":["refers to"],"explanation":"refer to 表示指向。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use NAVIGATE: The signs improve ___ for visitors.","answers":["navigation"],"explanation":"动词 improve 后接名词。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose therefore or nevertheless: The branches blocked the turn; ___, we removed them.","answers":["therefore"],"explanation":"前因后果。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an article explaining how to make a walking route easier for newcomers to follow.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"People who know a walking route well often underestimate how confusing it can be for newcomers. Improving a route should therefore begin with observation rather than simply adding more signs.\nFirst, invite someone unfamiliar with the area to follow the path. Notice where they hesitate and ask what information they expected to find. A hidden turning may become clear after a few branches are removed. In that case, an additional sign would create unnecessary clutter.\nAt a genuine choice between two paths, however, a clear marker is useful. It should name a destination that also appears on the map. An instruction such as continue may not help someone who cannot identify the intended direction.\nFinally, organisers must distinguish clear navigation from physical safety. Excellent signs will not repair a muddy surface or a broken bridge. A good maintenance plan addresses both needs separately and checks the route again after changes in weather or vegetation."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how signs and maps can work together.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"Signs and maps are most useful when they use the same destination names. A sign saying continue may be unclear if two paths look equally likely. A named destination lets the walker connect the instruction with a wider route. However, more signs are not always the answer. Removing a visual obstruction might solve the problem more simply. I would test both the map and the signs with somebody unfamiliar with the area."},"project":"把自写文章拆成段落，设计六个衔接线索并解释唯一合理的顺序。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I teach beginners to use sewing machines. One learner preferred watching my hands, while another understood better when I marked the sequence on paper. I originally thought I needed to choose the best method for the whole class. In fact, providing both helped everyone check different aspects of the task. The demonstration showed the movement, and the notes reminded them of the order. We then compared two short seams and discussed why one was uneven. The combination took a little longer at first, but learners needed less individual rescue later. It taught me to match information to the difficulty rather than label each person as one fixed kind of learner.","questions":[{"prompt":"What did the teacher initially assume?","answer":"One method should be selected for the whole class.","options":["One method should be selected for the whole class.","Nobody needed any instructions.","Machines could never be demonstrated."],"explanation":"起初认为必须选唯一最佳方法。"},{"prompt":"What did paper notes help with?","answer":"The order of actions.","options":["The order of actions.","The machine’s electricity bill.","The colour of every fabric."],"explanation":"notes reminded them of order。"},{"prompt":"What was the later benefit?","answer":"Less need for individual rescue.","options":["Less need for individual rescue.","No practice was necessary.","Every seam became identical immediately."],"explanation":"组合方式前期费时后期减少救场。"}]}},{"chapter":33,"title":"听力中的态度与保留","target":"B2","focus":"听出赞同程度、转折重点及说话者意图","grammar":"语气缓和；评价词；重音对应的意义","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"A podcast volunteer reflects","text":"I agreed to help record our neighbourhood podcast because I thought speaking into a microphone would be the easy part. To be honest, I was far less comfortable when I heard my first recording and noticed how quickly I interrupted people. My colleague said the energy was promising, which sounded encouraging until she added that the guests hardly finished a sentence. She was not asking me to sound less interested; she wanted me to leave enough space for a worthwhile answer. At the next interview, I prepared fewer questions and listened for points that could lead naturally to a follow up. There were uncomfortable pauses, although the guest later told me that those moments had helped her collect her thoughts. I would not describe the result as polished, but the conversation was certainly more revealing than our first attempt together. One listener suggested background music, and I can see the attraction, especially when an episode begins with a long introduction. Still, music underneath every answer might make careful listening harder for some people, so we are testing it only between sections. What surprised me most was how much editing depended on understanding the speaker’s intention rather than removing every hesitation automatically. A small pause can signal uncertainty, and cutting it out may make a cautious statement sound stronger than the speaker intended. I am pleased we tried the project, but I now think of interviewing as a listening skill before considering it a performance."},"sourceB":{"title":"An organiser reviews a street festival","text":"People keep asking whether I was delighted with the street festival. Well, seeing the square full of families was wonderful, and the volunteers did an extraordinary amount of work. I would certainly like another festival next year. That does not mean I would repeat every decision we made.\nThe main stage looked impressive, but it faced the market stalls. Traders told us they struggled to hear customers whenever a band played. We had checked the sound from the audience area and forgotten that people elsewhere were trying to hold conversations. Moving the stage would be more useful than simply asking musicians to be quieter.\nI was initially doubtful about the children’s storytelling tent because it seemed rather modest beside the bigger attractions. In fact, it was busy all afternoon. Its success reminded me that an activity need not be spectacular to be worthwhile.\nAs for attendance, the crowd was larger than we expected. I would hesitate to promise the same result next year, though. The weather was unusually kind, and another local event had been cancelled. I am encouraged by the response, but our next budget should be based on a cautious estimate rather than our happiest memory of the day."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"How does the speaker view the first recording?","answer":"It revealed an interviewing weakness.","options":["It revealed an interviewing weakness.","It was technically impossible to hear.","It proved guests had nothing to say."],"explanation":"打断别人是说话者发现的问题。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why retain some pauses?","answer":"They can communicate uncertainty.","options":["They can communicate uncertainty.","They always make recordings shorter.","Listeners requested complete silence."],"explanation":"停顿可能携带态度信息。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What is the view of background music?","answer":"It is worth limited testing, with reservations.","options":["It is worth limited testing, with reservations.","It must accompany every answer.","It has no possible appeal."],"explanation":"承认吸引力但担心影响理解。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What is the organiser’s overall attitude?","answer":"Positive but willing to revise decisions.","options":["Positive but willing to revise decisions.","Completely disappointed with everything.","Certain every decision was ideal."],"explanation":"开头赞扬活动同时提出改进。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What changed the organiser’s view of storytelling?","answer":"Its popularity despite its modest appearance.","options":["Its popularity despite its modest appearance.","A complaint from every child.","Its expensive stage equipment."],"explanation":"起初怀疑，后来发现持续有人。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why avoid promising equal attendance?","answer":"Unusual external conditions helped this year.","options":["Unusual external conditions helped this year.","No one counted any visitors.","The organiser opposes another festival."],"explanation":"天气与其他活动取消影响人数。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"同意","en":"I agreed to help record our neighbourhood podcast because I thought speaking into a microphone would be the easy part.","zh":"我答应帮忙录社区播客，因为我以为对着麦克风说话是容易的部分。","word":"agreed","meaning":"同意","tip":"本句的“agreed”表达“同意”。先结合“我答应帮忙录社区播客，因为我以为对着麦克风说话是容易的部分。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"坦白说","en":"To be honest, I was far less comfortable when I heard my first recording and noticed how quickly I interrupted people.","zh":"说实话，听到第一次录音并发现自己多快打断别人时，我很不自在。","word":"to be honest","meaning":"坦白说","tip":"本句的“to be honest”表达“坦白说”。先结合“说实话，听到第一次录音并发现自己多快打断别人时，我很不自在。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"有潜力的","en":"My colleague said the energy was promising, which sounded encouraging until she added that the guests hardly finished a sentence.","zh":"同事说活力不错，听起来很鼓舞，直到她补充嘉宾几乎说不完一句话。","word":"promising","meaning":"有潜力的","tip":"本句的“promising”表达“有潜力的”。先结合“同事说活力不错，听起来很鼓舞，直到她补充嘉宾几乎说不完一句话。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"值得的","en":"She was not asking me to sound less interested; she wanted me to leave enough space for a worthwhile answer.","zh":"她不是让我听起来没兴趣，而是希望我留出足够空间得到有价值的回答。","word":"worthwhile","meaning":"值得的","tip":"本句的“worthwhile”表达“值得的”。先结合“她不是让我听起来没兴趣，而是希望我留出足够空间得到有价值的回答。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"追问","en":"At the next interview, I prepared fewer questions and listened for points that could lead naturally to a follow up.","zh":"下次访谈我准备更少问题，留意能自然引出追问的细节。","word":"follow up","meaning":"追问","tip":"本句的“follow up”表达“追问”。先结合“下次访谈我准备更少问题，留意能自然引出追问的细节。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"整理思路","en":"There were uncomfortable pauses, although the guest later told me that those moments had helped her collect her thoughts.","zh":"中间有令人不自在的停顿，不过嘉宾后来告诉我那些时刻有助于整理思路。","word":"collect her thoughts","meaning":"整理思路","tip":"本句的“collect her thoughts”表达“整理思路”。先结合“中间有令人不自在的停顿，不过嘉宾后来告诉我那些时刻有助于整理思路。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"成熟精致的","en":"I would not describe the result as polished, but the conversation was certainly more revealing than our first attempt together.","zh":"我不会说结果很成熟，但对话肯定比第一次尝试透露了更多内容。","word":"polished","meaning":"成熟精致的","tip":"本句的“polished”表达“成熟精致的”。先结合“我不会说结果很成熟，但对话肯定比第一次尝试透露了更多内容。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"吸引力","en":"One listener suggested background music, and I can see the attraction, especially when an episode begins with a long introduction.","zh":"一位听众建议背景音乐，我理解其吸引力，尤其开头介绍很长的时候。","word":"attraction","meaning":"吸引力","tip":"本句的“attraction”表达“吸引力”。先结合“一位听众建议背景音乐，我理解其吸引力，尤其开头介绍很长的时候。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"不过","en":"Still, music underneath every answer might make careful listening harder for some people, so we are testing it only between sections.","zh":"不过每个回答下面都有音乐可能让部分人难以细听，所以我们仅在段落间测试。","word":"still","meaning":"不过","tip":"本句的“still”表达“不过”。先结合“不过每个回答下面都有音乐可能让部分人难以细听，所以我们仅在段落间测试。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"意图","en":"What surprised me most was how much editing depended on understanding the speaker’s intention rather than removing every hesitation automatically.","zh":"最让我惊讶的是编辑多么依赖理解说话意图，而不是自动删掉每次犹豫。","word":"intention","meaning":"意图","tip":"本句的“intention”表达“意图”。先结合“最让我惊讶的是编辑多么依赖理解说话意图，而不是自动删掉每次犹豫。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"不确定","en":"A small pause can signal uncertainty, and cutting it out may make a cautious statement sound stronger than the speaker intended.","zh":"小停顿能表示不确定，删掉可能让谨慎陈述听起来比原意更肯定。","word":"uncertainty","meaning":"不确定","tip":"本句的“uncertainty”表达“不确定”。先结合“小停顿能表示不确定，删掉可能让谨慎陈述听起来比原意更肯定。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"表演","en":"I am pleased we tried the project, but I now think of interviewing as a listening skill before considering it a performance.","zh":"我很高兴尝试项目，但现在先把访谈视为倾听技巧，再视为表演。","word":"performance","meaning":"表演","tip":"本句的“performance”表达“表演”。先结合“我很高兴尝试项目，但现在先把访谈视为倾听技巧，再视为表演。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose cautious or certain: “It might work, but we need a trial” expresses a ___ attitude.","answers":["cautious"],"explanation":"might 和 but we need a trial 表示保留。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use CERTAIN with a negative prefix and noun ending: A hesitant pause may communicate ___.","answers":["uncertainty"],"explanation":"本语境强调不能确定，用 uncertainty。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use HESITATE: I would not immediately promise that. Complete: I would ___ promise that.","answers":["hesitate to"],"explanation":"hesitate to do 表示迟疑。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: I can see the attraction. ___, I have concerns. Use S as the first letter.","answers":["Still"],"explanation":"Still 在此承认前项后提出保留。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a review of a community podcast. Mention a strength, a weakness and a practical recommendation.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"Our neighbourhood podcast offers a welcome opportunity to hear local people discuss experiences that rarely appear in the news. Its strongest feature is the range of guests, from shop owners to volunteers restoring public gardens.\nThe most recent episode was particularly interesting because the interviewer allowed the guest to explain why the work mattered personally. These details made the conversation more memorable than a simple list of achievements. Occasional pauses also sounded natural rather than unprofessional.\nHowever, the introduction was too long, and background music sometimes made quieter answers difficult to hear. This could discourage listeners who use small speakers or have difficulty separating speech from other sounds.\nI recommend shortening the opening and keeping music between sections instead of underneath the interview. A written summary with the guest’s main points would improve access as well. Overall, the programme is worth developing, provided that the team continues to put understandable conversation ahead of decorative effects."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Give a balanced opinion about a podcast or interview.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"I enjoy interviews that let guests develop an answer instead of rushing through a list of questions. A natural pause can show that someone is thinking carefully. However, an interviewer still needs to keep the conversation focused. I am less enthusiastic about constant background music because it can hide quieter speech. I would use it briefly between sections and ask several listeners whether the final recording is comfortable to follow."},"project":"录制同一段话的肯定、犹豫和委婉反对版本，请同伴解释听到的态度。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I was rather doubtful when our manager suggested replacing the annual staff dinner with an afternoon workshop. It sounded suspiciously like extra work disguised as a treat. Actually, the ceramic artist who led it made the atmosphere wonderfully relaxed, and I spoke to colleagues I hardly knew. I would happily try something similar again. My only reservation is that the session required everyone to stay until five, which made collection from school difficult for some parents. A successful activity should not create avoidable pressure, so next time I would suggest an earlier finish or a choice of sessions.","questions":[{"prompt":"What was the speaker’s initial attitude?","answer":"Suspicious that the activity was extra work.","options":["Suspicious that the activity was extra work.","Certain it would be the best event ever.","Completely unaware of the plan."],"explanation":"rather doubtful 和 disguised as a treat 表达怀疑。"},{"prompt":"How did the final opinion change?","answer":"The speaker enjoyed the relaxed social contact.","options":["The speaker enjoyed the relaxed social contact.","The speaker refused to attend.","The artist made it competitive."],"explanation":"actually 引出正向体验。"},{"prompt":"What reservation remains?","answer":"The finishing time caused difficulties for some parents.","options":["The finishing time caused difficulties for some parents.","Ceramics cannot be social.","Nobody spoke to colleagues."],"explanation":"保留意见是接孩子时间。"}]}},{"chapter":34,"title":"长篇讲话与访谈笔记","target":"B2","focus":"保持主线，区分例子、修正与最终结论","grammar":"时序线索；总结表达；数字与条件","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"A researcher explains urban shade","text":"When we began mapping shade in the city, our first task was to decide what we actually needed to measure. Air temperature alone was not enough, because standing in direct sunlight can feel very different from standing beneath a tree nearby. We therefore recorded surface temperature, shade coverage and the times when people were most likely to use each public space. At first, volunteers visited every location at noon, which made the timetable easy but missed changes later in the afternoon. After reviewing the early results, we introduced morning and late afternoon visits to capture the movement of shadows across important routes. One school entrance looked comfortable at lunchtime yet offered almost no protection when children were waiting to be collected after lessons. That observation changed our priorities more than the hottest single reading, since it connected a physical condition with a repeated daily need. We considered planting trees, installing shelters and changing where people waited, rather than assuming that one solution would suit every site. Trees offered additional benefits, but they required suitable soil, continued watering and enough time to develop a useful canopy over the pavement. Temporary shelters could work sooner, although their design had to account for wind, maintenance and access for people using wheelchairs. Our final recommendations combined immediate changes with longer term planting and made clear which proposals still needed detailed engineering advice. The study did not produce a single ranking of beautiful places; it identified when practical protection mattered most to the people using them."},"sourceB":{"title":"An interview about a swimming pool","text":"Interviewer: Why did the town review the swimming pool timetable?\nManager: We received requests for more sessions, but they were not all asking for the same thing. Some swimmers wanted quiet exercise before work. Families wanted weekend lessons, and rehabilitation groups needed warm water with easy access to the shallow end.\nInterviewer: Did you simply extend opening hours?\nManager: That was our first idea. However, heating and staffing costs would have risen considerably. We examined existing attendance before making that commitment. The early afternoon lane sessions were often half empty, while family lessons had waiting lists.\nInterviewer: What changed?\nManager: We moved two lessons into those quieter periods and kept one lane available for individual swimmers. We also published the water temperature beside the timetable, since calling a session gentle did not tell rehabilitation users whether conditions would suit them.\nInterviewer: Has the problem been solved?\nManager: Not entirely. Some parents cannot attend in the afternoon, and we still need more qualified instructors. The revised timetable uses the building better, but it does not remove every barrier. Our next review will include people who asked about lessons but never booked, because attendance figures alone cannot explain why somebody stayed away."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Why were additional visit times introduced?","answer":"Shade changed as the day progressed.","options":["Shade changed as the day progressed.","Volunteers could not read thermometers.","The city banned visits at noon."],"explanation":"中午测量漏掉午后阴影变化。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why did the school entrance matter?","answer":"Its lack of shade coincided with a repeated waiting period.","options":["Its lack of shade coincided with a repeated waiting period.","It had the most beautiful trees.","It was closed every afternoon."],"explanation":"需求时段与遮阴不足重合。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why combine solutions?","answer":"Locations had different constraints and timescales.","options":["Locations had different constraints and timescales.","Tree planting was impossible everywhere.","Shelters never needed maintenance."],"explanation":"各方案生效时间及条件不同。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why not simply extend hours?","answer":"It would increase heating and staffing costs.","options":["It would increase heating and staffing costs.","Every current session was full.","The pool could never open mornings."],"explanation":"经理说明延时运营成本高。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why publish water temperature?","answer":"A vague session label did not indicate suitable conditions.","options":["A vague session label did not indicate suitable conditions.","Swimmers were choosing paint colours.","The heating had been removed."],"explanation":"gentle 不说明康复人群所需实际温度。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Whose views will the next review seek?","answer":"People who enquired but never booked.","options":["People who enquired but never booked.","Only the fastest swimmers.","Only existing instructors."],"explanation":"未预约者能解释到访障碍。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"绘制地图","en":"When we began mapping shade in the city, our first task was to decide what we actually needed to measure.","zh":"开始绘制城市遮阴图时，首要任务是决定实际需要测量什么。","word":"mapping","meaning":"绘制地图","tip":"本句的“mapping”表达“绘制地图”。先结合“开始绘制城市遮阴图时，首要任务是决定实际需要测量什么。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"阳光直射","en":"Air temperature alone was not enough, because standing in direct sunlight can feel very different from standing beneath a tree nearby.","zh":"单测气温不够，因为阳光直射下与附近树荫下的感觉可能很不同。","word":"direct sunlight","meaning":"阳光直射","tip":"本句的“direct sunlight”表达“阳光直射”。先结合“单测气温不够，因为阳光直射下与附近树荫下的感觉可能很不同。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"覆盖","en":"We therefore recorded surface temperature, shade coverage and the times when people were most likely to use each public space.","zh":"因此我们记录地表温度、遮阴覆盖以及人们最可能使用各处公共空间的时间。","word":"coverage","meaning":"覆盖","tip":"本句的“coverage”表达“覆盖”。先结合“因此我们记录地表温度、遮阴覆盖以及人们最可能使用各处公共空间的时间。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"在中午","en":"At first, volunteers visited every location at noon, which made the timetable easy but missed changes later in the afternoon.","zh":"起初志愿者中午访问每处地点，排程简单却错过午后变化。","word":"at noon","meaning":"在中午","tip":"本句的“at noon”表达“在中午”。先结合“起初志愿者中午访问每处地点，排程简单却错过午后变化。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"记录","en":"After reviewing the early results, we introduced morning and late afternoon visits to capture the movement of shadows across important routes.","zh":"回顾早期结果后，我们加入早上和傍晚访问，记录重要路线上的影子移动。","word":"capture","meaning":"记录","tip":"本句的“capture”表达“记录”。先结合“回顾早期结果后，我们加入早上和傍晚访问，记录重要路线上的影子移动。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"保护","en":"One school entrance looked comfortable at lunchtime yet offered almost no protection when children were waiting to be collected after lessons.","zh":"一个校门午餐时很舒适，放学后孩子等待接送时却几乎没有遮护。","word":"protection","meaning":"保护","tip":"本句的“protection”表达“保护”。先结合“一个校门午餐时很舒适，放学后孩子等待接送时却几乎没有遮护。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"优先事项","en":"That observation changed our priorities more than the hottest single reading, since it connected a physical condition with a repeated daily need.","zh":"这一观察比单个最高温度更改变重点，因为它将物理条件与每天重复需求联系起来。","word":"priorities","meaning":"优先事项","tip":"本句的“priorities”表达“优先事项”。先结合“这一观察比单个最高温度更改变重点，因为它将物理条件与每天重复需求联系起来。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"遮蔽物","en":"We considered planting trees, installing shelters and changing where people waited, rather than assuming that one solution would suit every site.","zh":"我们考虑种树、装棚和改变等候地点，而非假定一种方案适合所有地点。","word":"shelters","meaning":"遮蔽物","tip":"本句的“shelters”表达“遮蔽物”。先结合“我们考虑种树、装棚和改变等候地点，而非假定一种方案适合所有地点。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"树冠","en":"Trees offered additional benefits, but they required suitable soil, continued watering and enough time to develop a useful canopy over the pavement.","zh":"树有额外好处，但需要合适土壤、持续浇水以及时间形成覆盖路面的树冠。","word":"canopy","meaning":"树冠","tip":"本句的“canopy”表达“树冠”。先结合“树有额外好处，但需要合适土壤、持续浇水以及时间形成覆盖路面的树冠。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"考虑","en":"Temporary shelters could work sooner, although their design had to account for wind, maintenance and access for people using wheelchairs.","zh":"临时棚能更快生效，但设计需考虑风、维护和轮椅通行。","word":"account for","meaning":"考虑","tip":"本句的“account for”表达“考虑”。先结合“临时棚能更快生效，但设计需考虑风、维护和轮椅通行。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"建议","en":"Our final recommendations combined immediate changes with longer term planting and made clear which proposals still needed detailed engineering advice.","zh":"最终建议结合即时改变和长期种植，并明确哪些提议仍需详细工程意见。","word":"recommendations","meaning":"建议","tip":"本句的“recommendations”表达“建议”。先结合“最终建议结合即时改变和长期种植，并明确哪些提议仍需详细工程意见。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"识别","en":"The study did not produce a single ranking of beautiful places; it identified when practical protection mattered most to the people using them.","zh":"研究没有只给美丽地点排名，而是识别实际使用者何时最需要保护。","word":"identified","meaning":"识别","tip":"本句的“identified”表达“识别”。先结合“研究没有只给美丽地点排名，而是识别实际使用者何时最需要保护。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: We recorded temperatures at different times in ___ to detect changes.","answers":["order"],"explanation":"in order to。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use PROTECT: Children need ___ from direct sunlight.","answers":["protection"],"explanation":"need 后需名词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use ACCOUNT: The design must consider wheelchair access. Complete: The design must ___ wheelchair access.","answers":["take into account"],"explanation":"take into account 后接宾语。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose whereas or because: Trees take time to grow, ___ a temporary shelter can be installed quickly.","answers":["whereas"],"explanation":"比较两个方案的时间特点。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a report recommending how to make a busy outdoor waiting area more comfortable in summer.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"This report considers how to improve the outdoor waiting area beside the community centre. The main problem is a lack of shade during the late afternoon, when many families collect children from activities.\nAn immediate improvement would be to move the waiting point closer to the existing covered entrance. Staff should first check that this would not block wheelchair access or emergency routes. Clear signs could then help families find the new location.\nFor the longer term, the centre should investigate planting suitable trees. They could improve comfort and the appearance of the area, but they would require space, watering and several years of growth. A temporary shelter may be necessary while they develop.\nI recommend observing the area at different times before choosing a permanent design. A solution based only on conditions at noon might fail during the actual collection period. The final plan should therefore combine evidence about sunlight with information about how people use the space."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Summarise the main stages of an investigation into a public space.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"First, I would identify who uses the space and when they need it most. Then I would collect observations at several times rather than rely on one visit. The findings should be connected to practical needs, such as waiting safely or moving through an entrance. Finally, I would compare short term and long term solutions, explaining costs and uncertainty. A follow up visit would show whether the change actually helped users."},"project":"听一段三分钟讲话，分别记主张、例子、修正和结论，再做一分钟摘要。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"The archive project has three stages. First, volunteers photograph the old letters using a support that keeps the camera steady. Please do not flatten folded pages forcefully; ask the conservation officer if a page resists opening. Next, another volunteer checks each image against the original and records missing edges or unreadable areas. Only after that check should the file be uploaded to the public catalogue. We considered uploading immediately, but errors were then harder to trace. The aim is reliable access, not the greatest number of files in a single day. Training begins Tuesday, and you do not need previous photography experience.","questions":[{"prompt":"What should volunteers do with a resistant folded page?","answer":"Ask the conservation officer.","options":["Ask the conservation officer.","Force it flat immediately.","Throw it away."],"explanation":"保护原件优先，找保管人员。"},{"prompt":"When may an image be uploaded?","answer":"After comparison with the original.","options":["After comparison with the original.","Before taking the photograph.","Only after a public vote."],"explanation":"必须先检查图像。"},{"prompt":"What is the project’s priority?","answer":"Reliable access to the material.","options":["Reliable access to the material.","Maximum daily file numbers regardless of errors.","Replacing every original letter."],"explanation":"明确可靠而非数量。"}]}},{"chapter":35,"title":"图片比较与推测","target":"B2","focus":"比较两种情境，围绕问题推测而非罗列物品","grammar":"比较级；情态推测；相似与差异","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"Two ways of learning outdoors","text":"Imagine one photograph showing teenagers beside a river with measuring equipment and another showing a family following signs through a nature reserve. Both groups appear to be learning outdoors, although the purpose of their activities and the support available may be rather different. The teenagers might be carrying out a school investigation, since one person is recording figures while another checks an instrument near the water. By contrast, the family seems to be choosing its own pace, with a child pointing towards an illustrated notice beside the path. I cannot tell whether either group is enjoying the experience, but both activities seem to encourage attention. Working with instruments could help the teenagers understand ideas that would remain abstract if they only read about them in a classroom. However, they may have less freedom to follow an unexpected interest because their group probably needs to complete a planned task together. The family’s visit is likely to be more flexible, although a clear explanation would still help them interpret unfamiliar plants or animal behaviour. Rather than describing every visible object, a useful comparison connects a few details with the question of how each group might learn. Words such as probably and perhaps make clear that these are reasonable possibilities rather than facts that the photographs can fully establish. If asked which activity would be more memorable, I would choose the investigation because making a measurement could create a strong personal connection. Someone who prefers exploring freely might choose the family visit, showing that several conclusions can be defensible."},"sourceB":{"title":"Photographs in a local history exhibition","text":"A curator was selecting photographs for an exhibition about work in the town. One image showed a crowded workshop from the nineteen thirties. Another showed a recent employee working alone at a computer. Several visitors to an early preview concluded that modern work must be lonelier.\nThe curator thought the comparison was interesting but incomplete. The old photograph had been taken during a planned visit, when workers were asked to stand together. The recent image showed only one corner of a much larger office. Neither photograph revealed how people communicated throughout a normal day.\nInstead of removing the pictures, she added short accounts from workers. A retired mechanic described the companionship of the workshop but also its noise and strict supervision. A current employee explained that she worked alone on some tasks and collaborated online with colleagues elsewhere on others.\nThe final display invited visitors to compare visible conditions while questioning what remained outside the frame. It did not claim that images were useless. Rather, it demonstrated that photographs are selected moments, and a confident interpretation can become misleading if the viewer forgets how the image was produced. Visitors were encouraged to distinguish observation from assumption before drawing broader conclusions about social change."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"What should a useful comparison focus on?","answer":"Details connected to the question being answered.","options":["Details connected to the question being answered.","Every object regardless of relevance.","A memorised description of any picture."],"explanation":"比较要服务题目而非罗列。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why use “might” and “probably”?","answer":"The image cannot establish every interpretation as fact.","options":["The image cannot establish every interpretation as fact.","The speaker knows all details are false.","Grammar requires uncertainty in every sentence."],"explanation":"视觉证据允许推测但有局限。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What limitation might the teenagers face?","answer":"Less freedom to pursue unplanned interests.","options":["Less freedom to pursue unplanned interests.","No possible way to learn.","A complete absence of equipment."],"explanation":"计划小组任务可能限制自由探索。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why was the initial loneliness conclusion weak?","answer":"The images did not represent complete working days.","options":["The images did not represent complete working days.","The old photograph showed no workers.","Computers were invisible in both images."],"explanation":"拍摄情境及画面范围使推断过度。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why add worker accounts?","answer":"To provide context beyond the visible images.","options":["To provide context beyond the visible images.","To prove photographs have no value.","To replace the exhibition with a textbook."],"explanation":"叙述补充画面看不到的经历。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What distinction did the display encourage?","answer":"Observation versus assumption.","options":["Observation versus assumption.","Colour versus black ink only.","Paid work versus all unpaid activity."],"explanation":"最后一句明确区分看见与假定。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"想象","en":"Imagine one photograph showing teenagers beside a river with measuring equipment and another showing a family following signs through a nature reserve.","zh":"想象一张照片是青少年在河边拿测量设备，另一张是家庭沿标牌参观自然保护区。","word":"imagine","meaning":"想象","tip":"本句的“imagine”表达“想象”。先结合“想象一张照片是青少年在河边拿测量设备，另一张是家庭沿标牌参观自然保护区。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"似乎","en":"Both groups appear to be learning outdoors, although the purpose of their activities and the support available may be rather different.","zh":"两组似乎都在户外学习，但活动目的及可用支持可能很不同。","word":"appear to","meaning":"似乎","tip":"本句的“appear to”表达“似乎”。先结合“两组似乎都在户外学习，但活动目的及可用支持可能很不同。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"可能正在","en":"The teenagers might be carrying out a school investigation, since one person is recording figures while another checks an instrument near the water.","zh":"青少年可能在做学校调查，因为一个人在记数字，另一个在水边检查仪器。","word":"might be","meaning":"可能正在","tip":"本句的“might be”表达“可能正在”。先结合“青少年可能在做学校调查，因为一个人在记数字，另一个在水边检查仪器。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"相比之下","en":"By contrast, the family seems to be choosing its own pace, with a child pointing towards an illustrated notice beside the path.","zh":"相比之下，家庭似乎自己决定速度，一个孩子正指着路边图示牌。","word":"by contrast","meaning":"相比之下","tip":"本句的“by contrast”表达“相比之下”。先结合“相比之下，家庭似乎自己决定速度，一个孩子正指着路边图示牌。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"无法判断","en":"I cannot tell whether either group is enjoying the experience, but both activities seem to encourage attention.","zh":"我不能判断两组是否享受经历，但两项活动似乎都鼓励专注。","word":"cannot tell","meaning":"无法判断","tip":"本句的“cannot tell”表达“无法判断”。先结合“我不能判断两组是否享受经历，但两项活动似乎都鼓励专注。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"抽象的","en":"Working with instruments could help the teenagers understand ideas that would remain abstract if they only read about them in a classroom.","zh":"使用仪器可帮助青少年理解那些只在教室读到时可能仍抽象的概念。","word":"abstract","meaning":"抽象的","tip":"本句的“abstract”表达“抽象的”。先结合“使用仪器可帮助青少年理解那些只在教室读到时可能仍抽象的概念。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"自由","en":"However, they may have less freedom to follow an unexpected interest because their group probably needs to complete a planned task together.","zh":"但他们可能较少自由追随意外兴趣，因为小组可能要共同完成计划任务。","word":"freedom","meaning":"自由","tip":"本句的“freedom”表达“自由”。先结合“但他们可能较少自由追随意外兴趣，因为小组可能要共同完成计划任务。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"理解解释","en":"The family’s visit is likely to be more flexible, although a clear explanation would still help them interpret unfamiliar plants or animal behaviour.","zh":"家庭参观很可能更灵活，但清晰解释仍有助理解陌生植物或动物行为。","word":"interpret","meaning":"理解解释","tip":"本句的“interpret”表达“理解解释”。先结合“家庭参观很可能更灵活，但清晰解释仍有助理解陌生植物或动物行为。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"联系","en":"Rather than describing every visible object, a useful comparison connects a few details with the question of how each group might learn.","zh":"有效比较不是描述每个可见物品，而是把少量细节与每组如何学习这一问题联系。","word":"connects","meaning":"联系","tip":"本句的“connects”表达“联系”。先结合“有效比较不是描述每个可见物品，而是把少量细节与每组如何学习这一问题联系。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"确立","en":"Words such as probably and perhaps make clear that these are reasonable possibilities rather than facts that the photographs can fully establish.","zh":"probably 和 perhaps 等词表明这些是合理可能性，而非照片能充分确定的事实。","word":"establish","meaning":"确立","tip":"本句的“establish”表达“确立”。先结合“probably 和 perhaps 等词表明这些是合理可能性，而非照片能充分确定的事实。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"难忘的","en":"If asked which activity would be more memorable, I would choose the investigation because making a measurement could create a strong personal connection.","zh":"若问哪项更难忘，我会选调查，因为亲自测量可能建立强烈个人联系。","word":"memorable","meaning":"难忘的","tip":"本句的“memorable”表达“难忘的”。先结合“若问哪项更难忘，我会选调查，因为亲自测量可能建立强烈个人联系。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"可辩护的","en":"Someone who prefers exploring freely might choose the family visit, showing that several conclusions can be defensible.","zh":"偏爱自由探索的人可能选家庭参观，说明多种结论都能站得住脚。","word":"defensible","meaning":"可辩护的","tip":"本句的“defensible”表达“可辩护的”。先结合“偏爱自由探索的人可能选家庭参观，说明多种结论都能站得住脚。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: Both pictures show learning, ___ the settings are different. Use A as the first letter.","answers":["although"],"explanation":"although 表示让步关系。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use LIKELY: They will probably be working together. Complete: They ___ be working together.","answers":["are likely to"],"explanation":"be likely to do。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use MEMORY: A practical activity can be particularly ___.","answers":["memorable"],"explanation":"be 后需形容词 memorable。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose must or might: The person ___ be a teacher, but there is not enough evidence to be sure.","answers":["might"],"explanation":"证据不足，不用强推断 must。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an article explaining why photographs can tell powerful but incomplete stories.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"Photographs can communicate a situation immediately. A crowded room or an empty street may attract attention more quickly than a long written explanation. However, an image is a selected moment rather than a complete account.\nThe details inside the frame can support useful observations. We may notice how people are positioned or what equipment they are using. The difficulty begins when we treat an interpretation as a certain fact. A person sitting alone might be concentrating, waiting for a colleague or taking a break. The photograph cannot tell us which explanation is correct.\nContext therefore matters. Knowing why and when an image was taken can change our understanding. Short accounts from the people involved may reveal circumstances that are invisible in the picture.\nIn my view, the best use of photographs combines close observation with cautious questions. Images remain powerful evidence, provided that viewers recognise their limits and seek additional information before making broad claims about other people’s lives."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Compare structured outdoor learning with a self guided visit.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"Both activities can make learning more concrete than reading alone. A structured investigation may be especially useful for understanding a process because participants collect evidence themselves. However, they probably have less freedom to change direction. A self guided visit allows people to follow their own interests, although they may miss important explanations. I would choose according to the goal: an investigation for a specific question, and a flexible visit for general exploration."},"project":"使用自有或获授权的两张图片，完成一分钟比较；回听并标出观察与推测。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"For the exhibition cover, we compared a photograph of an empty workshop with one showing a single apprentice using a tool. The empty room displayed the equipment clearly, but it gave little sense of scale or purpose. The second picture suggested a practical activity, although viewers could not know whether the apprentice was working independently or following instructions outside the frame. We chose it because it invited questions about learning. The caption states what is actually known and avoids claiming that the person felt confident. A facial expression in one photograph would not give us enough evidence for that judgement.","questions":[{"prompt":"What was weak about the empty workshop image?","answer":"It gave little sense of scale or purpose.","options":["It gave little sense of scale or purpose.","It showed no equipment at all.","It was taken in another country."],"explanation":"可见设备但缺使用尺度和目的。"},{"prompt":"Why choose the apprentice image?","answer":"It invited questions about learning.","options":["It invited questions about learning.","It proved the person was working alone.","It showed every instruction clearly."],"explanation":"邀请思考，不等于事实断言。"},{"prompt":"What does the caption avoid claiming?","answer":"That the person felt confident.","options":["That the person felt confident.","That a tool was present.","That the image shows an apprentice."],"explanation":"表情不足证明内心状态。"}]}},{"chapter":36,"title":"协作讨论与共同决策","target":"B2","focus":"回应同伴、比较标准、推进协商","grammar":"建议与回应；委婉异议；条件式同意","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"Choosing a welcome event","text":"Our student committee had to choose a welcome event for new arrivals, and the first suggestions included a concert and a sports afternoon. Instead of voting immediately, we agreed to discuss how each option would help people who had not yet made friends. I suggested a concert because music could give people a shared experience, but my partner wondered whether anyone would actually talk during it. That was a fair point, so I asked whether a short performance followed by small group activities might solve the problem. She thought it could, although she was concerned that the extra equipment would leave too little money for refreshments and accessible transport. We then considered the sports afternoon, distinguishing competitive matches from simple activities that did not require previous skill or expensive clothing. Neither of us wanted less confident students to feel watched or judged while they were still finding their place in the community. My partner proposed an indoor team challenge with practical puzzles, which seemed to combine cooperation with opportunities for informal conversation afterwards. I supported the idea on condition that teams were mixed carefully and instructions were available in clear language for everyone taking part. Before making our decision, we checked it against the same criteria we had used for the concert rather than favouring our newest suggestion automatically. The final proposal was neither person’s original choice, but both could explain why it addressed our main concern. Productive discussion meant using each other’s contributions to build a decision, rather than simply taking turns to speak."},"sourceB":{"title":"A residents’ meeting about a courtyard","text":"Residents of an apartment building had a small budget to improve their shared courtyard. Three proposals were presented: more parking, a children’s play corner and a quiet garden. The meeting became tense when supporters described their own choice as the only practical one.\nThe chair asked each group to identify the problem it wanted to solve. Parking supporters were worried about deliveries rather than long term storage. Parents wanted a safe place to wait after school, not necessarily large climbing equipment. Older residents wanted seating away from the noisy entrance.\nThese explanations revealed that the proposals were not entirely incompatible. A short delivery bay could operate in the morning, while movable planters separated seating from a small open area later in the day. The design would still require clear rules, and nobody claimed it would satisfy every preference.\nBefore approving the plan, residents agreed on a three month trial. They would record access problems and complaints rather than judge success by the appearance of the courtyard alone. The chair’s main contribution was not inventing the final layout. It was helping people move from defending objects to explaining needs, which made a compromise possible without pretending that the original disagreement had never existed."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"What criterion guided the committee?","answer":"Helping newcomers make connections.","options":["Helping newcomers make connections.","Buying the largest possible speakers.","Avoiding all group activities."],"explanation":"迎新目的在于帮助交友。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"How did the speaker respond to criticism?","answer":"By considering a modification to the suggestion.","options":["By considering a modification to the suggestion.","By refusing to listen.","By ending the meeting immediately."],"explanation":"先承认合理性再改进方案。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why recheck the final idea?","answer":"To apply consistent criteria to all options.","options":["To apply consistent criteria to all options.","To prove the newest idea was always best.","To avoid making a decision."],"explanation":"用同一标准避免偏爱新提议。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What made compromise possible?","answer":"Explaining needs behind the proposed objects.","options":["Explaining needs behind the proposed objects.","Increasing the budget without limit.","Cancelling all delivery services."],"explanation":"椅子停车位等背后需求有组合空间。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was the purpose of the trial?","answer":"To gather evidence about practical problems.","options":["To gather evidence about practical problems.","To prevent residents giving feedback.","To make every preference identical."],"explanation":"记录通行和投诉等实际表现。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"How did the chair mainly help?","answer":"By changing the focus of the discussion.","options":["By changing the focus of the discussion.","By personally buying every item.","By hiding the disagreements."],"explanation":"由维护方案转为解释需求。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"委员会","en":"Our student committee had to choose a welcome event for new arrivals, and the first suggestions included a concert and a sports afternoon.","zh":"学生委员会要选迎新活动，最初建议包括音乐会和体育下午活动。","word":"committee","meaning":"委员会","tip":"本句的“committee”表达“委员会”。先结合“学生委员会要选迎新活动，最初建议包括音乐会和体育下午活动。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"达成一致","en":"Instead of voting immediately, we agreed to discuss how each option would help people who had not yet made friends.","zh":"我们没有马上投票，而是先讨论每个选择如何帮助还没交到朋友的人。","word":"agreed","meaning":"达成一致","tip":"本句的“agreed”表达“达成一致”。先结合“我们没有马上投票，而是先讨论每个选择如何帮助还没交到朋友的人。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"提出疑问","en":"I suggested a concert because music could give people a shared experience, but my partner wondered whether anyone would actually talk during it.","zh":"我提议音乐会，因为音乐能创造共同经历，但同伴怀疑演出中是否有人真的会交谈。","word":"wondered","meaning":"提出疑问","tip":"本句的“wondered”表达“提出疑问”。先结合“我提议音乐会，因为音乐能创造共同经历，但同伴怀疑演出中是否有人真的会交谈。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"合理观点","en":"That was a fair point, so I asked whether a short performance followed by small group activities might solve the problem.","zh":"这个观点合理，所以我问短表演后接小组活动是否能解决问题。","word":"fair point","meaning":"合理观点","tip":"本句的“fair point”表达“合理观点”。先结合“这个观点合理，所以我问短表演后接小组活动是否能解决问题。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"担心的","en":"She thought it could, although she was concerned that the extra equipment would leave too little money for refreshments and accessible transport.","zh":"她觉得可以，但担心额外设备会使茶点和无障碍交通预算不足。","word":"concerned","meaning":"担心的","tip":"本句的“concerned”表达“担心的”。先结合“她觉得可以，但担心额外设备会使茶点和无障碍交通预算不足。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"区分","en":"We then considered the sports afternoon, distinguishing competitive matches from simple activities that did not require previous skill or expensive clothing.","zh":"随后我们考虑体育下午活动，区分竞技比赛和不需既有技能或昂贵服装的简单活动。","word":"distinguishing","meaning":"区分","tip":"本句的“distinguishing”表达“区分”。先结合“随后我们考虑体育下午活动，区分竞技比赛和不需既有技能或昂贵服装的简单活动。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"被评判","en":"Neither of us wanted less confident students to feel watched or judged while they were still finding their place in the community.","zh":"我们都不希望不自信的学生在刚融入集体时觉得被围观或评判。","word":"judged","meaning":"被评判","tip":"本句的“judged”表达“被评判”。先结合“我们都不希望不自信的学生在刚融入集体时觉得被围观或评判。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"合作","en":"My partner proposed an indoor team challenge with practical puzzles, which seemed to combine cooperation with opportunities for informal conversation afterwards.","zh":"同伴提议室内团队实用解谜挑战，似乎兼顾合作和之后非正式聊天机会。","word":"cooperation","meaning":"合作","tip":"本句的“cooperation”表达“合作”。先结合“同伴提议室内团队实用解谜挑战，似乎兼顾合作和之后非正式聊天机会。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"以某条件为前提","en":"I supported the idea on condition that teams were mixed carefully and instructions were available in clear language for everyone taking part.","zh":"我支持这个想法，条件是仔细混合组队，且为所有参加者提供清楚说明。","word":"on condition","meaning":"以某条件为前提","tip":"本句的“on condition”表达“以某条件为前提”。先结合“我支持这个想法，条件是仔细混合组队，且为所有参加者提供清楚说明。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"标准","en":"Before making our decision, we checked it against the same criteria we had used for the concert rather than favouring our newest suggestion automatically.","zh":"决定前我们用评价音乐会的相同标准检查，而不是自动偏爱最新建议。","word":"criteria","meaning":"标准","tip":"本句的“criteria”表达“标准”。先结合“决定前我们用评价音乐会的相同标准检查，而不是自动偏爱最新建议。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"提案","en":"The final proposal was neither person’s original choice, but both could explain why it addressed our main concern.","zh":"最终提案不是任何人的最初选择，但双方都能解释为何回应了核心问题。","word":"proposal","meaning":"提案","tip":"本句的“proposal”表达“提案”。先结合“最终提案不是任何人的最初选择，但双方都能解释为何回应了核心问题。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"贡献","en":"Productive discussion meant using each other’s contributions to build a decision, rather than simply taking turns to speak.","zh":"有效讨论意味着利用彼此贡献形成决定，而不只是轮流说话。","word":"contributions","meaning":"贡献","tip":"本句的“contributions”表达“贡献”。先结合“有效讨论意味着利用彼此贡献形成决定，而不只是轮流说话。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: I agree, provided ___ the instructions are clear.","answers":["that"],"explanation":"provided that 引出条件。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use INTO: We should consider your concern. Complete: We should take your concern ___.","answers":["into account"],"explanation":"take into account。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use COOPERATE: The activity should encourage ___.","answers":["cooperation"],"explanation":"encourage 后接名词。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: That is a fair ___. I had not considered the cost.","answers":["point"],"explanation":"a fair point 表示承认对方观点。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a proposal for an event that helps newcomers meet people. Explain your criteria and recommendation.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"The purpose of this proposal is to recommend a welcome event that helps new students start conversations. The activity should be affordable, accessible and comfortable for people who arrive without friends.\nA concert could create a shared experience, but it would offer limited opportunities to speak during the performance. A competitive sports afternoon might encourage teamwork, although students with little confidence in sport could feel excluded.\nI recommend a short indoor team challenge using practical puzzles. Participants would have a reason to communicate without needing specialist knowledge or equipment. Organisers should mix the teams and explain that cooperation matters more than winning. A relaxed refreshment break afterwards would allow conversations to continue naturally.\nClear instructions and suitable access are essential. Before confirming the plan, the committee should ask several incoming students whether the activity sounds welcoming. The event should be judged by how comfortably people can participate, rather than by how impressive it looks in photographs."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Discuss which welcome activity would best help newcomers talk.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"A shared activity could help because people would have something immediate to discuss. I can see the appeal of a concert, but listeners might leave without speaking to anyone. A simple team puzzle seems more suitable, provided that the instructions are clear and winning is not the main focus. I would also include a relaxed break afterwards. Before deciding, I would ask my partner whether this arrangement might still exclude any group."},"project":"与真人同伴讨论五个方案，录制三分钟并检查是否有回应、追问、让步和共同结论。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"We needed to choose a fundraiser for the animal shelter. I favoured a sponsored walk, while Amir preferred an online auction. Instead of arguing about which sounded more exciting, we compared volunteer time, accessibility and likely costs. The walk needed route supervision, and the auction depended on finding suitable donated items. We eventually proposed a small indoor sale with an optional short walk afterwards. It was not either person’s first idea, but it used resources we already had. We agreed to review the plan if the shelter could not provide enough volunteers, rather than promise an event that would overburden its staff.","questions":[{"prompt":"Which option did Amir initially prefer?","answer":"An online auction.","options":["An online auction.","A sponsored walk.","A large concert."],"explanation":"人物与提案对应。"},{"prompt":"What helped the discussion progress?","answer":"Comparing options against shared criteria.","options":["Comparing options against shared criteria.","Repeating the loudest preference.","Ignoring volunteer time."],"explanation":"共同标准促成协商。"},{"prompt":"What condition might require revision?","answer":"Insufficient volunteers from the shelter.","options":["Insufficient volunteers from the shelter.","Too many donated books at home.","A change in the speaker’s birthday."],"explanation":"承诺需视人员条件调整。"}]}},{"chapter":37,"title":"论证写作与修改","target":"B2","focus":"回应问题，发展理由，检查例证是否支持结论","grammar":"主题句；让步反驳；因果限定；结论","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"Should schools set community projects?","text":"A school considering compulsory community projects should begin by asking what students are expected to learn rather than counting volunteer hours. Supporters argue that working with people outside school helps young learners understand needs that textbooks may describe only in general terms. For example, helping to design a clear visitor map could show why accessible communication matters to somebody unfamiliar with an area. This benefit is not automatic, however, because repeating a simple task without explanation may teach little about the community or its difficulties. Students need preparation, appropriate responsibility and time to reflect on what the experience has changed in their understanding of other people. Critics reasonably point out that compulsory service can create pressure for students who already care for relatives or work after lessons. A fair programme would therefore offer flexible schedules and several forms of participation rather than assume that everyone has the same free time. It should also avoid treating community organisations as places that must accept unprepared students simply because the school wants a useful activity. The strongest argument for these projects concerns meaningful cooperation, while the strongest objection concerns unfair demands on participants and their hosts. An effective essay develops both points with explanation instead of presenting a long list of benefits followed by an unrelated personal story. Its conclusion should answer the original question and acknowledge the conditions that determine whether the proposal is likely to work in practice. I would support a well supervised programme with genuine choice, but not a fixed requirement that rewards attendance while ignoring learning and fairness."},"sourceB":{"title":"An essay competition changes its rules","text":"A youth magazine ran an essay competition asking whether cities should provide more space for cycling. In the first year, several entries used impressive vocabulary but barely addressed the question. Some described personal holidays, while others listed environmental problems without explaining how cycling space would help.\nThe editors revised their guidance. They asked writers to state a clear position, develop two relevant reasons and consider one practical objection. They also published an example showing how a simple observation could become an argument. A busy junction was not merely described as dangerous; the writer explained how separating traffic might reduce a particular conflict.\nIn the next competition, entries became more focused. However, a new problem appeared: several writers repeated the model’s structure so closely that their arguments sounded identical. The editors therefore clarified that organisation was a tool, not a script that every writer had to copy.\nThe winning essay supported safer cycling routes but questioned a proposal to remove all delivery access from shopping streets. It distinguished the goal from one possible method and recommended timed access as a compromise. Its success came from relevant reasoning and controlled language, rather than from a large number of unusual expressions. The judges praised its ability to recognise a difficulty without abandoning the central argument."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"What should schools identify first?","answer":"The learning purpose of the project.","options":["The learning purpose of the project.","The longest possible attendance requirement.","The most impressive project photograph."],"explanation":"首句强调学习目标。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why might a fixed requirement be unfair?","answer":"Students have different responsibilities outside school.","options":["Students have different responsibilities outside school.","All students dislike helping others.","Community groups never need help."],"explanation":"照护和工作负担不同。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What should the conclusion do?","answer":"Answer the question with relevant conditions.","options":["Answer the question with relevant conditions.","Introduce an unrelated personal story.","Repeat every sentence word for word."],"explanation":"结论回应原问题并作有条件判断。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was weak in early entries?","answer":"They often failed to address the actual question.","options":["They often failed to address the actual question.","They contained no vocabulary.","They were all too short to read."],"explanation":"用词华丽但论证偏题。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What risk did the model create?","answer":"Writers copied its structure too mechanically.","options":["Writers copied its structure too mechanically.","Nobody understood any example.","The competition became a speaking test."],"explanation":"模型被当作脚本照搬。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why did the winning essay succeed?","answer":"It supported a goal while evaluating a practical method.","options":["It supported a goal while evaluating a practical method.","It rejected all cycling improvements.","It used the rarest possible words."],"explanation":"区分目标与具体禁行方法，提出妥协。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"强制的","en":"A school considering compulsory community projects should begin by asking what students are expected to learn rather than counting volunteer hours.","zh":"学校考虑强制社区项目时，应先问预期学习成果而非只数志愿时数。","word":"compulsory","meaning":"强制的","tip":"本句的“compulsory”表达“强制的”。先结合“学校考虑强制社区项目时，应先问预期学习成果而非只数志愿时数。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"支持者","en":"Supporters argue that working with people outside school helps young learners understand needs that textbooks may describe only in general terms.","zh":"支持者认为校外合作帮助年轻学习者理解课本可能只泛泛描述的需求。","word":"supporters","meaning":"支持者","tip":"本句的“supporters”表达“支持者”。先结合“支持者认为校外合作帮助年轻学习者理解课本可能只泛泛描述的需求。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"便于理解的","en":"For example, helping to design a clear visitor map could show why accessible communication matters to somebody unfamiliar with an area.","zh":"例如帮助设计清晰导览图，可说明方便理解的沟通为何对不熟悉地区的人重要。","word":"accessible","meaning":"便于理解的","tip":"本句的“accessible”表达“便于理解的”。先结合“例如帮助设计清晰导览图，可说明方便理解的沟通为何对不熟悉地区的人重要。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"自动发生的","en":"This benefit is not automatic, however, because repeating a simple task without explanation may teach little about the community or its difficulties.","zh":"然而该收益不是自动发生，因为无解释地重复简单任务可能几乎学不到社区及其困难。","word":"automatic","meaning":"自动发生的","tip":"本句的“automatic”表达“自动发生的”。先结合“然而该收益不是自动发生，因为无解释地重复简单任务可能几乎学不到社区及其困难。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"反思","en":"Students need preparation, appropriate responsibility and time to reflect on what the experience has changed in their understanding of other people.","zh":"学生需要准备、合适责任及时间反思经历如何改变其对他人的理解。","word":"reflect","meaning":"反思","tip":"本句的“reflect”表达“反思”。先结合“学生需要准备、合适责任及时间反思经历如何改变其对他人的理解。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"批评者","en":"Critics reasonably point out that compulsory service can create pressure for students who already care for relatives or work after lessons.","zh":"批评者合理指出，强制服务会让已照顾亲属或课后工作的人承压。","word":"critics","meaning":"批评者","tip":"本句的“critics”表达“批评者”。先结合“批评者合理指出，强制服务会让已照顾亲属或课后工作的人承压。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"灵活的","en":"A fair programme would therefore offer flexible schedules and several forms of participation rather than assume that everyone has the same free time.","zh":"因此公平项目应提供灵活时间和多种参与方式，而非假定每个人空闲相同。","word":"flexible","meaning":"灵活的","tip":"本句的“flexible”表达“灵活的”。先结合“因此公平项目应提供灵活时间和多种参与方式，而非假定每个人空闲相同。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"未准备好的","en":"It should also avoid treating community organisations as places that must accept unprepared students simply because the school wants a useful activity.","zh":"还应避免把社区机构当成仅因学校需要活动就必须接受未准备学生的场所。","word":"unprepared","meaning":"未准备好的","tip":"本句的“unprepared”表达“未准备好的”。先结合“还应避免把社区机构当成仅因学校需要活动就必须接受未准备学生的场所。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"反对意见","en":"The strongest argument for these projects concerns meaningful cooperation, while the strongest objection concerns unfair demands on participants and their hosts.","zh":"支持项目的最强理由关乎有意义合作，最强反对关乎对参与者及接待方不公平要求。","word":"objection","meaning":"反对意见","tip":"本句的“objection”表达“反对意见”。先结合“支持项目的最强理由关乎有意义合作，最强反对关乎对参与者及接待方不公平要求。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"展开","en":"An effective essay develops both points with explanation instead of presenting a long list of benefits followed by an unrelated personal story.","zh":"有效文章解释发展双方观点，而不是列长串好处后接无关个人故事。","word":"develops","meaning":"展开","tip":"本句的“develops”表达“展开”。先结合“有效文章解释发展双方观点，而不是列长串好处后接无关个人故事。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"承认","en":"Its conclusion should answer the original question and acknowledge the conditions that determine whether the proposal is likely to work in practice.","zh":"结论应回答原问题并承认决定提议实际是否可行的条件。","word":"acknowledge","meaning":"承认","tip":"本句的“acknowledge”表达“承认”。先结合“结论应回答原问题并承认决定提议实际是否可行的条件。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"受监督的","en":"I would support a well supervised programme with genuine choice, but not a fixed requirement that rewards attendance while ignoring learning and fairness.","zh":"我支持有监督和真正选择的项目，不支持只奖励出席而忽视学习公平的固定要求。","word":"supervised","meaning":"受监督的","tip":"本句的“supervised”表达“受监督的”。先结合“我支持有监督和真正选择的项目，不支持只奖励出席而忽视学习公平的固定要求。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: ___ example, a project could improve local maps.","answers":["For"],"explanation":"For example 引例。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use RELEVANT: Check the ___ of each supporting detail.","answers":["relevance"],"explanation":"the 后接名词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use UNLESS: A project is useful only if students reflect. Complete: A project is not useful ___.","answers":["unless students reflect"],"explanation":"unless 等于 if not，保留条件逻辑。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose therefore or although: Students have different responsibilities; ___, schedules should be flexible.","answers":["therefore"],"explanation":"原因到建议结论。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an essay: Should community projects be a required part of school education? Discuss learning and fairness.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"Community projects can make education more meaningful by connecting classroom ideas with real situations. However, I believe that schools should require thoughtful participation rather than impose identical hours on every student.\nThe main advantage is the opportunity to understand other people’s needs. For example, helping a local organisation improve its information can show students why clear communication matters. This learning is more likely when teachers provide preparation and ask students to reflect afterwards. Repeating a task without understanding its purpose is less valuable.\nFairness is another important consideration. Some students already work or care for relatives after school. A rigid timetable could punish them for responsibilities they cannot avoid. Schools should therefore offer several suitable activities and flexible ways to complete them.\nIn conclusion, I support community learning when it is carefully supervised and accessible. The programme should be judged by the quality of cooperation and learning, rather than simply by a total of hours recorded on a form."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Give a reasoned opinion about compulsory community projects.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"I support community projects if students understand their purpose and receive proper supervision. They can reveal needs that remain abstract in a textbook. However, a fixed number of hours may be unfair to students with jobs or caring responsibilities. I would offer several activities and flexible schedules. Students should explain what they learned afterwards, because attendance alone does not show that meaningful learning or useful cooperation actually took place."},"project":"独立写一篇论说文，再删去每个不能支持中心观点的句子，并保留修改记录。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"When I read students’ essays, I look first for an answer to the actual question. A paragraph can contain accurate grammar and still contribute little if its example is unrelated. I ask writers to underline the sentence that states each paragraph’s main point, then explain how the next details support it. If they cannot explain the connection, they may need a different example or a clearer reason. This is not a demand that every essay look identical. Different structures can work, provided the reader can follow the argument and see why the conclusion is justified by what came before.","questions":[{"prompt":"What does the speaker check first?","answer":"Whether the essay answers the question.","options":["Whether the essay answers the question.","Whether every word is rare.","Whether all essays look identical."],"explanation":"先看任务回应。"},{"prompt":"Why underline a main point?","answer":"To check how supporting details connect to it.","options":["To check how supporting details connect to it.","To decorate the page.","To remove all examples."],"explanation":"检查论点与支持关系。"},{"prompt":"What does the speaker say about structure?","answer":"Different structures can work if the reasoning is clear.","options":["Different structures can work if the reasoning is clear.","Only one structure is acceptable.","Structure never affects understanding."],"explanation":"不要求统一模板。"}]}},{"chapter":38,"title":"面向读者的多体裁写作","target":"B2","focus":"根据目的选择邮件、文章、评论或报告的结构与语域","grammar":"语域；礼貌请求；推荐；客观报告","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"One event, four different texts","text":"After organising a weekend book exchange, Elena discovered that writing about the event required different choices for different readers and purposes. Her email to a volunteer began with thanks and asked politely whether the borrowed tables could remain until Monday morning. Because the recipient already knew the event, she did not waste space explaining its history or describing every activity in detail. The article for a community magazine needed a more inviting opening, so she began with a child finding a favourite story unexpectedly. She used that moment to introduce the wider question of how sharing books might bring neighbours together beyond a single weekend. Her review of the venue was more evaluative, comparing the convenient location with the difficulty of hearing announcements inside the crowded hall. A useful recommendation followed from those observations: the hall suited informal exchanges, but organisers would need a better system for spoken instructions. The report for the council used clear sections on attendance, access and costs, with a short summary of the changes proposed for next time. Although all four texts concerned the same event, copying identical paragraphs into each would have ignored what their readers actually needed to know. Elena checked whether every request was explicit, every judgement had support and every important piece of information appeared in an appropriate place. She also replaced exaggerated praise with specific details, recognising that a credible recommendation can include a weakness without becoming an entirely negative review. Her final revisions helped each intended reader understand, decide or respond, rather than merely making the language impressive."},"sourceB":{"title":"A complaint that received a useful reply","text":"Tariq bought a weekend photography workshop as a birthday gift for his sister. The website described a small outdoor group with individual feedback. On the day, heavy rain forced the class indoors, and the instructor taught eighteen participants in a single room. Tariq understood the weather problem but felt that the promised feedback had disappeared.\nHis first draft of a complaint called the whole experience terrible. Before sending it, he replaced that judgement with specific facts: the advertised group size, the number who attended and the absence of individual discussion. He acknowledged that rain was outside the company’s control and explained that his concern was the difference between the advertised teaching and what was delivered.\nThe company replied with an apology and offered his sister a place on a smaller feedback session. It did not refund the entire workshop because instruction had still been provided. Tariq accepted the offer after confirming that the new session would involve no extra payment.\nThe exchange did not prove that polite language always produces a satisfactory result. It showed how a clear account of a problem, supported by relevant details and a reasonable requested remedy, can make it easier for another person to respond constructively rather than defend against a vague accusation."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Why was the email brief about background?","answer":"The recipient already knew the event.","options":["The recipient already knew the event.","Emails cannot include any explanation.","The event had no history."],"explanation":"信息选择依据读者已有知识。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What made the venue review useful?","answer":"Its recommendation followed from specific observations.","options":["Its recommendation followed from specific observations.","It contained only praise.","It repeated the attendance table."],"explanation":"判断与证据相连。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What guided final revision?","answer":"The intended reader’s needs.","options":["The intended reader’s needs.","Using the same paragraphs everywhere.","Choosing the longest possible words."],"explanation":"目标读者与交际目的决定写作。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was Tariq’s main complaint?","answer":"The promised individual feedback was missing.","options":["The promised individual feedback was missing.","The company had caused the rain.","His sister could not use a camera."],"explanation":"他区分天气与交付教学的差距。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What remedy was offered?","answer":"A place in a smaller feedback session.","options":["A place in a smaller feedback session.","An entirely free camera.","A refund for every participant automatically."],"explanation":"公司提供小班反馈课。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did the example demonstrate?","answer":"Specific facts can support a constructive response.","options":["Specific facts can support a constructive response.","Politeness guarantees any demand.","Complaints should avoid requested remedies."],"explanation":"结尾谨慎限定结论。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"目的","en":"After organising a weekend book exchange, Elena discovered that writing about the event required different choices for different readers and purposes.","zh":"组织周末换书活动后，Elena 发现面向不同读者目的写作需要不同选择。","word":"purposes","meaning":"目的","tip":"本句的“purposes”表达“目的”。先结合“组织周末换书活动后，Elena 发现面向不同读者目的写作需要不同选择。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"礼貌地","en":"Her email to a volunteer began with thanks and asked politely whether the borrowed tables could remain until Monday morning.","zh":"她给志愿者的邮件先致谢，礼貌询问借来的桌子能否留到周一早晨。","word":"politely","meaning":"礼貌地","tip":"本句的“politely”表达“礼貌地”。先结合“她给志愿者的邮件先致谢，礼貌询问借来的桌子能否留到周一早晨。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"收件人","en":"Because the recipient already knew the event, she did not waste space explaining its history or describing every activity in detail.","zh":"因为收件人已了解活动，她没有浪费篇幅解释历史或细述每项活动。","word":"recipient","meaning":"收件人","tip":"本句的“recipient”表达“收件人”。先结合“因为收件人已了解活动，她没有浪费篇幅解释历史或细述每项活动。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"吸引人的","en":"The article for a community magazine needed a more inviting opening, so she began with a child finding a favourite story unexpectedly.","zh":"社区杂志文章需要更吸引人的开头，所以她以孩子意外找到喜爱故事开篇。","word":"inviting","meaning":"吸引人的","tip":"本句的“inviting”表达“吸引人的”。先结合“社区杂志文章需要更吸引人的开头，所以她以孩子意外找到喜爱故事开篇。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"引出","en":"She used that moment to introduce the wider question of how sharing books might bring neighbours together beyond a single weekend.","zh":"她用该时刻引出更广问题：分享书如何让邻居在一个周末之后仍保持联系。","word":"introduce","meaning":"引出","tip":"本句的“introduce”表达“引出”。先结合“她用该时刻引出更广问题：分享书如何让邻居在一个周末之后仍保持联系。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"评价性的","en":"Her review of the venue was more evaluative, comparing the convenient location with the difficulty of hearing announcements inside the crowded hall.","zh":"她的场地评论更重评价，对比位置方便与拥挤大厅难听广播。","word":"evaluative","meaning":"评价性的","tip":"本句的“evaluative”表达“评价性的”。先结合“她的场地评论更重评价，对比位置方便与拥挤大厅难听广播。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"推荐建议","en":"A useful recommendation followed from those observations: the hall suited informal exchanges, but organisers would need a better system for spoken instructions.","zh":"实用建议来自观察：大厅适合非正式交换，但组织者需更好的口头指引系统。","word":"recommendation","meaning":"推荐建议","tip":"本句的“recommendation”表达“推荐建议”。先结合“实用建议来自观察：大厅适合非正式交换，但组织者需更好的口头指引系统。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"小节","en":"The report for the council used clear sections on attendance, access and costs, with a short summary of the changes proposed for next time.","zh":"给议会的报告分出人数、通行及费用小节，简述下次建议修改。","word":"sections","meaning":"小节","tip":"本句的“sections”表达“小节”。先结合“给议会的报告分出人数、通行及费用小节，简述下次建议修改。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"完全相同的","en":"Although all four texts concerned the same event, copying identical paragraphs into each would have ignored what their readers actually needed to know.","zh":"虽然四文都关乎同一活动，复制相同段落会忽视读者实际需知。","word":"identical","meaning":"完全相同的","tip":"本句的“identical”表达“完全相同的”。先结合“虽然四文都关乎同一活动，复制相同段落会忽视读者实际需知。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"明确的","en":"Elena checked whether every request was explicit, every judgement had support and every important piece of information appeared in an appropriate place.","zh":"Elena 检查请求是否明确、判断是否有支持、重要信息位置是否合适。","word":"explicit","meaning":"明确的","tip":"本句的“explicit”表达“明确的”。先结合“Elena 检查请求是否明确、判断是否有支持、重要信息位置是否合适。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"可信的","en":"She also replaced exaggerated praise with specific details, recognising that a credible recommendation can include a weakness without becoming an entirely negative review.","zh":"她把夸张赞美换成具体细节，认识到可信推荐可含缺点而非全负面评论。","word":"credible","meaning":"可信的","tip":"本句的“credible”表达“可信的”。先结合“她把夸张赞美换成具体细节，认识到可信推荐可含缺点而非全负面评论。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"目标读者","en":"Her final revisions helped each intended reader understand, decide or respond, rather than merely making the language impressive.","zh":"她的最终修改帮助每个目标读者理解、决定或回应，而不只是让语言华丽。","word":"intended reader","meaning":"目标读者","tip":"本句的“intended reader”表达“目标读者”。先结合“她的最终修改帮助每个目标读者理解、决定或回应，而不只是让语言华丽。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use MIND: Can we keep the tables until Monday? Complete: Would you ___ until Monday?","answers":["mind if we kept the tables"],"explanation":"Would you mind if + 过去式表示礼貌。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use RECOMMEND: The report ends with a practical ___.","answers":["recommendation"],"explanation":"冠词 a 后用名词。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: I am writing ___ request further details.","answers":["to"],"explanation":"目的不定式。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DESPITE: The location was convenient, but the hall was noisy. Complete: ___, the hall was noisy.","answers":["Despite the convenient location"],"explanation":"让步结构保持原意。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a polite email to a venue manager. Explain a specific problem at an event, acknowledge a positive feature and request a reasonable solution.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"Dear Ms Chen,\nThank you for allowing our reading group to use the community hall last Saturday. The central location was convenient, and your staff helped us arrange the tables before visitors arrived.\nI am writing about the sound system. We had booked the hall on the understanding that announcements would be audible throughout the room. Unfortunately, the microphone stopped working after the first announcement, and several visitors missed the explanation of how to exchange books. We informed the duty assistant, but no replacement was available.\nWe would like to hold another event next month. Would you please confirm whether the equipment can be checked in advance and whether a backup microphone could be provided? As part of the agreed service was unavailable, we would also appreciate a modest reduction in the next booking fee.\nWe hope to continue using the hall and would welcome a practical arrangement. Thank you for considering these requests.\nKind regards,\nElena Morris"},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how the audience changes the way you write about an event.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"The same event can require very different texts. In an email to a volunteer, I would make a clear request and avoid repeating background they already know. A magazine article needs an inviting opening and a reason for readers to care. A report should make findings and recommendations easy to locate. In every case, I would choose relevant details and support judgements rather than rely on exaggerated praise or vague complaints."},"project":"围绕同一真实活动写邮件、评论、文章、报告各一段，解释语域与信息选择差异。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"The hotel replied quickly to my email, partly because I described the problem precisely. I did not complain that everything was awful. I explained that we had booked an accessible room, but the bathroom doorway was too narrow for my father’s wheelchair. I attached the booking confirmation and asked for a suitable room or help finding an alternative nearby. The receptionist arranged a transfer to another hotel without an extra charge. It was inconvenient, and I would rather the original booking had been correct. Still, a specific request gave the staff a clear action to consider instead of leaving them to guess what response I wanted.","questions":[{"prompt":"What was the specific problem?","answer":"The bathroom doorway was too narrow for a wheelchair.","options":["The bathroom doorway was too narrow for a wheelchair.","The hotel had no restaurant.","The room was on the wrong date."],"explanation":"明确无障碍预订与实际通行不符。"},{"prompt":"What supporting document was included?","answer":"The booking confirmation.","options":["The booking confirmation.","A restaurant review.","A driving licence application."],"explanation":"附上预订确认。"},{"prompt":"Why was the request useful?","answer":"It identified practical actions staff could take.","options":["It identified practical actions staff could take.","It guaranteed the speaker would enjoy moving.","It avoided describing the problem."],"explanation":"提出可操作解决方法。"}]}},{"chapter":39,"title":"听读整合与任务迁移","target":"B2","focus":"比较不同来源，将信息转化为自己的建议","grammar":"转述；信息归属；综合与限定","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"Planning a quieter neighbourhood","text":"Our project group received a residents’ survey and a short interview with a transport planner about noise on a busy shopping street. The survey showed that evening deliveries disturbed several households, while the interview explained why shops depended on flexible access for vehicles. At first, one student wanted to choose whichever source seemed more convincing, but this would have treated different questions as direct contradictions. The residents described their experience, whereas the planner discussed practical constraints that a proposed change would need to address before being introduced. We therefore made separate notes on the problem, the people affected and the conditions under which each possible solution might be workable. A complete ban on deliveries sounded simple, yet it might force businesses to close or transfer journeys to nearby streets with similar problems. Restricting the noisiest activities during late hours appeared more balanced, although the available evidence did not specify which vehicles caused the greatest disturbance. Our recommendation was to collect that missing information during a short trial rather than pretend the existing sources already answered every relevant question. When writing the summary, we identified whose view each claim represented and avoided presenting a small survey as the opinion of every resident. We also paraphrased the main ideas instead of copying striking phrases that sounded impressive but did not fit the purpose of our proposal. The presentation acknowledged disagreement, while showing that protecting rest and supporting shops were not necessarily incompatible goals. Integrating information meant building a reasoned response, not attaching summaries without explaining their relationship."},"sourceB":{"title":"A school chooses its lunch policy","text":"A school board reviewed a proposal to introduce one vegetarian lunch day each week. A supplier estimated that several suitable dishes would cost less than the current meat options. A student survey showed interest in environmental issues, but it did not ask whether pupils would actually choose the proposed meals.\nThe catering manager recommended a tasting session before a permanent change. She was concerned that a cheaper meal would not save resources if much of it was discarded. Parents also raised questions about allergies and portion sizes. These concerns did not directly challenge the environmental aim; they identified conditions the menu would need to satisfy.\nDuring a four week trial, staff recorded how many portions were selected and weighed the leftovers. Two dishes were popular, while a third produced considerably more waste. Students said its unfamiliar name had not explained what ingredients it contained.\nThe board approved a revised menu and clearer descriptions, but postponed claims about long term savings until a full term of records was available. It used the supplier’s estimate, the survey and the trial for different purposes. The decision was stronger because no single piece of evidence was expected to answer every question about cost, acceptance and environmental impact."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Why were the sources not simple contradictions?","answer":"They addressed experiences and practical constraints respectively.","options":["They addressed experiences and practical constraints respectively.","They were both written by the same person.","Neither mentioned the street."],"explanation":"来源讨论不同层面。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What information was missing?","answer":"Which vehicles caused most disturbance.","options":["Which vehicles caused most disturbance.","Whether any shops existed.","The name of the project group."],"explanation":"作者明确承认噪声来源细分数据缺失。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"What makes the summary responsible?","answer":"It identifies whose view each claim represents.","options":["It identifies whose view each claim represents.","It treats every survey as unanimous opinion.","It copies memorable phrases without context."],"explanation":"标明信息归属与样本限制。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why recommend tasting before a permanent change?","answer":"Cost savings might disappear if food was wasted.","options":["Cost savings might disappear if food was wasted.","No vegetarian food can contain nutrients.","Students had already rejected every dish."],"explanation":"食物接受度决定真实资源效果。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why change one dish’s description?","answer":"Its unfamiliar name did not explain ingredients.","options":["Its unfamiliar name did not explain ingredients.","It was too long to fit a plate.","The supplier refused all labels."],"explanation":"名称不透明影响选择。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why delay savings claims?","answer":"A longer record was needed.","options":["A longer record was needed.","All records had been banned.","The trial could never yield useful information."],"explanation":"四周证据不足以声称长期收益。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"居民","en":"Our project group received a residents’ survey and a short interview with a transport planner about noise on a busy shopping street.","zh":"项目组收到居民调查及交通规划师关于繁忙购物街噪声的简短访谈。","word":"residents","meaning":"居民","tip":"本句的“residents”表达“居民”。先结合“项目组收到居民调查及交通规划师关于繁忙购物街噪声的简短访谈。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"依赖","en":"The survey showed that evening deliveries disturbed several households, while the interview explained why shops depended on flexible access for vehicles.","zh":"调查显示晚间配送打扰一些家庭，访谈则解释商店为何依赖灵活车辆通行。","word":"depended on","meaning":"依赖","tip":"本句的“depended on”表达“依赖”。先结合“调查显示晚间配送打扰一些家庭，访谈则解释商店为何依赖灵活车辆通行。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"矛盾","en":"At first, one student wanted to choose whichever source seemed more convincing, but this would have treated different questions as direct contradictions.","zh":"起初一位学生想选更可信来源，但这会把不同问题当成直接矛盾。","word":"contradictions","meaning":"矛盾","tip":"本句的“contradictions”表达“矛盾”。先结合“起初一位学生想选更可信来源，但这会把不同问题当成直接矛盾。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"限制","en":"The residents described their experience, whereas the planner discussed practical constraints that a proposed change would need to address before being introduced.","zh":"居民描述经历，规划师讨论的是变更实施前必须处理的实际限制。","word":"constraints","meaning":"限制","tip":"本句的“constraints”表达“限制”。先结合“居民描述经历，规划师讨论的是变更实施前必须处理的实际限制。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"可行的","en":"We therefore made separate notes on the problem, the people affected and the conditions under which each possible solution might be workable.","zh":"因此我们分别记录问题、受影响者及各可能方案可行的条件。","word":"workable","meaning":"可行的","tip":"本句的“workable”表达“可行的”。先结合“因此我们分别记录问题、受影响者及各可能方案可行的条件。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"转移","en":"A complete ban on deliveries sounded simple, yet it might force businesses to close or transfer journeys to nearby streets with similar problems.","zh":"完全禁配送看似简单，却可能迫使商家关闭或把交通转移到有类似问题的邻街。","word":"transfer","meaning":"转移","tip":"本句的“transfer”表达“转移”。先结合“完全禁配送看似简单，却可能迫使商家关闭或把交通转移到有类似问题的邻街。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"干扰","en":"Restricting the noisiest activities during late hours appeared more balanced, although the available evidence did not specify which vehicles caused the greatest disturbance.","zh":"限制深夜最吵活动似乎更平衡，但现有证据未说明哪些车辆干扰最大。","word":"disturbance","meaning":"干扰","tip":"本句的“disturbance”表达“干扰”。先结合“限制深夜最吵活动似乎更平衡，但现有证据未说明哪些车辆干扰最大。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"试验","en":"Our recommendation was to collect that missing information during a short trial rather than pretend the existing sources already answered every relevant question.","zh":"我们建议短期试验收集缺失信息，而不假装已有来源回答所有相关问题。","word":"trial","meaning":"试验","tip":"本句的“trial”表达“试验”。先结合“我们建议短期试验收集缺失信息，而不假装已有来源回答所有相关问题。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"代表","en":"When writing the summary, we identified whose view each claim represented and avoided presenting a small survey as the opinion of every resident.","zh":"写摘要时我们标明主张属于谁，避免把小调查当成全体居民意见。","word":"represented","meaning":"代表","tip":"本句的“represented”表达“代表”。先结合“写摘要时我们标明主张属于谁，避免把小调查当成全体居民意见。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"改述","en":"We also paraphrased the main ideas instead of copying striking phrases that sounded impressive but did not fit the purpose of our proposal.","zh":"我们还转述主要意思，而不复制听起来出色却不适合提案目的的醒目措辞。","word":"paraphrased","meaning":"改述","tip":"本句的“paraphrased”表达“改述”。先结合“我们还转述主要意思，而不复制听起来出色却不适合提案目的的醒目措辞。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"承认","en":"The presentation acknowledged disagreement, while showing that protecting rest and supporting shops were not necessarily incompatible goals.","zh":"展示承认分歧，同时表明保护休息和支持商店并非必然不相容的目标。","word":"acknowledged","meaning":"承认","tip":"本句的“acknowledged”表达“承认”。先结合“展示承认分歧，同时表明保护休息和支持商店并非必然不相容的目标。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"整合","en":"Integrating information meant building a reasoned response, not attaching summaries without explaining their relationship.","zh":"整合信息意味着形成有理由回应，而非不解释关系就拼接摘要。","word":"integrating","meaning":"整合","tip":"本句的“integrating”表达“整合”。先结合“整合信息意味着形成有理由回应，而非不解释关系就拼接摘要。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: According ___ the survey, evening noise is a concern.","answers":["to"],"explanation":"according to。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use NECESSARILY: Different accounts do not always contradict each other. Complete: Different accounts do not ___ each other.","answers":["necessarily contradict"],"explanation":"not necessarily 限定判断。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use PRACTICE: The proposal faces several ___ constraints.","answers":["practical"],"explanation":"修饰名词 constraints。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: More data is needed before we can ___ a conclusion. Use the verb beginning with d.","answers":["draw"],"explanation":"draw a conclusion。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a proposal for reducing street noise while maintaining access for local shops. Identify missing information.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"This proposal aims to reduce late evening noise without preventing shops from receiving essential deliveries. Residents’ accounts show that disturbance is a genuine concern, while the transport interview explains why unrestricted closure could create difficulties for businesses.\nI recommend a short trial limiting the noisiest deliveries during late hours. Before setting exact times, the council should identify which vehicles cause the most disturbance and consult shops about realistic alternatives. Otherwise, the policy might simply move the problem to a neighbouring street.\nDuring the trial, residents could record the timing of significant noise, and businesses could report delays or additional costs. These records would help distinguish an effective change from one that only appears successful.\nThe current evidence supports further action, but it does not justify promising that one restriction will solve every problem. A carefully monitored trial would allow the council to protect rest while adjusting the arrangements in response to practical difficulties."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how you would combine a survey with an expert interview.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"I would first identify what each source can actually tell me. A residents’ survey may describe experiences, while an expert interview may explain constraints or possible methods. They do not have to disagree simply because they emphasise different issues. I would note where the sources support each other and where information is missing. My recommendation would then acknowledge uncertainty and explain what additional evidence would help choose between the remaining options."},"project":"综合两份独立来源，写一页方案，逐条标明证据出处、限制与待验证问题。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"We compared a bus company’s timetable with interviews from hospital patients. The timetable showed a direct service every hour, so at first the route appeared convenient. Patients pointed out that appointments often finished just after a departure, leaving a long wait with no sheltered seating. The two sources were not contradictory: one described the service, and the other described how it fitted actual journeys. We recommended checking appointment patterns before changing bus frequency. A small adjustment to departure times might help, but we needed information from other passengers too. Improving one group’s connection should not accidentally create a larger problem elsewhere on the route.","questions":[{"prompt":"Why did the service look convenient initially?","answer":"The timetable showed an hourly direct route.","options":["The timetable showed an hourly direct route.","Patients all owned cars.","Buses left every minute."],"explanation":"时刻表表面便利。"},{"prompt":"What did interviews reveal?","answer":"Appointments often ended just after a bus departed.","options":["Appointments often ended just after a bus departed.","No patient ever used the route.","Sheltered seats were always empty."],"explanation":"实际就医时间错配。"},{"prompt":"Why consult other passengers?","answer":"A change could disadvantage people elsewhere on the route.","options":["A change could disadvantage people elsewhere on the route.","Their views would guarantee cheaper buses.","Hospital patients must not be consulted."],"explanation":"需考虑全路线影响。"}]}},{"chapter":40,"title":"B2 阶段复核与自主学习","target":"B2","focus":"在陌生材料与独立表达中检验迁移能力","grammar":"错误模式分析；任务策略；自我修订","prerequisite":"完成前一章的陌生材料练习，并能说明答案依据；初次进入本阶段者先完成 B1 综合诊断。","sourceA":{"title":"What a course completion badge means","text":"When I finished a long language course, the completion badge felt satisfying, but an unexpected conversation soon revealed gaps that the familiar lessons had hidden. A visitor asked me to compare two travel options, and I understood the individual words without immediately seeing which details mattered to the decision. Instead of repeating my strongest memorised sentence, I asked about her priorities and discovered that arriving rested mattered more than paying the lowest fare. Useful assessment includes unfamiliar situations where learners decide what to say instead of recognising a rehearsed answer. I began keeping a record of misunderstandings, separating missing vocabulary from problems with following an argument or organising my response under pressure. This made revision more focused, because another list of words would not necessarily solve a difficulty caused by weak connections between ideas. For listening practice, I chose new speakers and topics, then checked whether I could explain the main point without seeing a transcript first. For writing, I completed a timed task and asked another reader to identify places where my intended meaning was unclear or insufficiently supported. Speaking with a partner was especially revealing, since an unexpected question required me to clarify a view rather than deliver a prepared performance unchanged. I repeated checks after a delay, because immediate success could reflect temporary familiarity with particular material. The resulting profile showed stronger reading than spontaneous speaking, which was more useful for planning practice than a single flattering score without explanation. The badge marked an achievement, yet independent evidence about understanding and communication guided my next learning goals."},"sourceB":{"title":"A tour guide’s practice plan","text":"Nora wanted to become a volunteer guide at a science centre. She already knew the exhibition facts and could deliver a prepared introduction smoothly. During a practice visit, however, a child asked why one experiment produced a different result when repeated. Nora repeated the official explanation without addressing the question.\nHer supervisor suggested a new practice plan. Instead of memorising longer scripts, Nora would explain the same process to three different audiences: a young child, an interested adult and a visitor with limited scientific vocabulary. A partner would interrupt with questions that were not supplied in advance.\nThe first sessions were uncomfortable. Nora sometimes lost her place, but she began noticing when a listener needed an example rather than another technical term. She also practised saying that she needed to check an answer instead of inventing a confident explanation.\nAfter a month, the supervisor repeated the exercise using an unfamiliar exhibit. Nora was less fluent than with her rehearsed material, but she asked useful questions, checked understanding and explained her uncertainty clearly. The supervisor judged this a meaningful improvement. The aim was not flawless performance on a familiar script, but dependable communication when the situation changed and the visitor’s needs could not be predicted beforehand."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Why ask about the traveller’s priorities?","answer":"To identify which information mattered to her decision.","options":["To identify which information mattered to her decision.","To avoid speaking any English.","To repeat a memorised fare advertisement."],"explanation":"交流服务实际决策而非背句。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why classify misunderstandings?","answer":"Different problems need different practice.","options":["Different problems need different practice.","All errors have identical causes.","Vocabulary should never be studied."],"explanation":"缺词与衔接问题需不同干预。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Why use delayed checks?","answer":"Immediate success may reflect temporary familiarity.","options":["Immediate success may reflect temporary familiarity.","All immediate answers are wrong.","Learners should never review."],"explanation":"延迟与陌生材料更能检验迁移。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was Nora’s initial weakness?","answer":"She did not adapt an explanation to an unexpected question.","options":["She did not adapt an explanation to an unexpected question.","She knew no exhibition facts.","She refused all practice."],"explanation":"准备稿流畅不代表即时回应。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why explain to different audiences?","answer":"To practise adapting communication to listeners.","options":["To practise adapting communication to listeners.","To increase the length of a memorised script.","To avoid explaining the process."],"explanation":"受众不同要求表达调整。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What counted as improvement?","answer":"Reliable communication despite an unfamiliar situation.","options":["Reliable communication despite an unfamiliar situation.","Perfect recitation of the original introduction.","Never admitting uncertainty."],"explanation":"陌生展品中提问确认和解释限制是进步。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"完成","en":"When I finished a long language course, the completion badge felt satisfying, but an unexpected conversation soon revealed gaps that the familiar lessons had hidden.","zh":"完成漫长语言课程时，结业徽章让我满足，但意外对话很快暴露熟悉课程掩盖的不足。","word":"completion","meaning":"完成","tip":"本句的“completion”表达“完成”。先结合“完成漫长语言课程时，结业徽章让我满足，但意外对话很快暴露熟悉课程掩盖的不足。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"单独的","en":"A visitor asked me to compare two travel options, and I understood the individual words without immediately seeing which details mattered to the decision.","zh":"游客让我比较两种旅行选择，我懂单词却未立即看出哪些细节影响决定。","word":"individual","meaning":"单独的","tip":"本句的“individual”表达“单独的”。先结合“游客让我比较两种旅行选择，我懂单词却未立即看出哪些细节影响决定。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"重点","en":"Instead of repeating my strongest memorised sentence, I asked about her priorities and discovered that arriving rested mattered more than paying the lowest fare.","zh":"我没重复最熟的背诵句，而问她重点，发现精神饱满到达比最低票价重要。","word":"priorities","meaning":"重点","tip":"本句的“priorities”表达“重点”。先结合“我没重复最熟的背诵句，而问她重点，发现精神饱满到达比最低票价重要。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"排练过的","en":"Useful assessment includes unfamiliar situations where learners decide what to say instead of recognising a rehearsed answer.","zh":"有效评估包含陌生情境，让学习者决定说什么而非认出排练答案。","word":"rehearsed","meaning":"排练过的","tip":"本句的“rehearsed”表达“排练过的”。先结合“有效评估包含陌生情境，让学习者决定说什么而非认出排练答案。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"区分","en":"I began keeping a record of misunderstandings, separating missing vocabulary from problems with following an argument or organising my response under pressure.","zh":"我开始记误解，把缺词与跟不上论证或压力下组织回答的问题分开。","word":"separating","meaning":"区分","tip":"本句的“separating”表达“区分”。先结合“我开始记误解，把缺词与跟不上论证或压力下组织回答的问题分开。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"有针对性的","en":"This made revision more focused, because another list of words would not necessarily solve a difficulty caused by weak connections between ideas.","zh":"这使复习更集中，因为再背词表不一定解决思想衔接薄弱的问题。","word":"focused","meaning":"有针对性的","tip":"本句的“focused”表达“有针对性的”。先结合“这使复习更集中，因为再背词表不一定解决思想衔接薄弱的问题。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"文字稿","en":"For listening practice, I chose new speakers and topics, then checked whether I could explain the main point without seeing a transcript first.","zh":"听力练习我选新说话者和主题，先不看文字检查能否解释主旨。","word":"transcript","meaning":"文字稿","tip":"本句的“transcript”表达“文字稿”。先结合“听力练习我选新说话者和主题，先不看文字检查能否解释主旨。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"不足地","en":"For writing, I completed a timed task and asked another reader to identify places where my intended meaning was unclear or insufficiently supported.","zh":"写作我限时完成并请另一读者指出原意不清或支持不足处。","word":"insufficiently","meaning":"不足地","tip":"本句的“insufficiently”表达“不足地”。先结合“写作我限时完成并请另一读者指出原意不清或支持不足处。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"澄清","en":"Speaking with a partner was especially revealing, since an unexpected question required me to clarify a view rather than deliver a prepared performance unchanged.","zh":"同伴对话尤其能揭示问题，因为意外问题要求澄清观点而非原封不动演准备内容。","word":"clarify","meaning":"澄清","tip":"本句的“clarify”表达“澄清”。先结合“同伴对话尤其能揭示问题，因为意外问题要求澄清观点而非原封不动演准备内容。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"暂时的","en":"I repeated checks after a delay, because immediate success could reflect temporary familiarity with particular material.","zh":"我延时重复检查，因为即时成功可能反映对特定材料暂时熟悉。","word":"temporary","meaning":"暂时的","tip":"本句的“temporary”表达“暂时的”。先结合“我延时重复检查，因为即时成功可能反映对特定材料暂时熟悉。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"能力画像","en":"The resulting profile showed stronger reading than spontaneous speaking, which was more useful for planning practice than a single flattering score without explanation.","zh":"结果显示阅读强于即兴口语，比一个没有解释的好看总分更助规划。","word":"profile","meaning":"能力画像","tip":"本句的“profile”表达“能力画像”。先结合“结果显示阅读强于即兴口语，比一个没有解释的好看总分更助规划。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"},{"title":"独立的","en":"The badge marked an achievement, yet independent evidence about understanding and communication guided my next learning goals.","zh":"徽章标志成就，但关于理解沟通的独立证据指引我的下一学习目标。","word":"independent","meaning":"独立的","tip":"本句的“independent”表达“独立的”。先结合“徽章标志成就，但关于理解沟通的独立证据指引我的下一学习目标。”理解它的对象或条件，再换一个具体对象造句；不要只背中文词义。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use DEPEND: We need ___ evidence of progress.","answers":["independent"],"explanation":"修饰 evidence，用 independent。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"One word: Ask a question to ___ the meaning clear.","answers":["make"],"explanation":"make something clear。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use RATHER: Practise adapting instead of only memorising. Complete: Practise adapting ___ only memorising.","answers":["rather than"],"explanation":"rather than 与 instead of 对应。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use CLEAR: The reader requested further ___.","answers":["clarification"],"explanation":"further 后需名词 clarification。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a learning plan based on stronger reading and weaker spontaneous speaking. Include ways to verify improvement.","minimumWords":140,"sample":"My recent practice suggests that I understand written arguments more confidently than I respond to unexpected spoken questions. My next plan will therefore focus on spontaneous communication while maintaining regular reading.\nTwice a week, I will discuss an unfamiliar topic with a partner. We will exchange short materials beforehand, but we will not prepare exact questions. During the conversation, I will practise asking for clarification, explaining reasons and checking whether my meaning is clear.\nAfter each session, I will identify one recurring difficulty rather than attempt to correct everything immediately. If I lack a useful phrase, I will practise it in several new situations. If my ideas are disorganised, I will work on clearer connections instead of simply learning more vocabulary.\nTo verify improvement, I will repeat a similar task with different material after two weeks and ask an independent listener for feedback. Progress will mean communicating more reliably across new situations, not merely delivering a familiar answer more smoothly."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how you would check whether course learning transfers to real communication.","followups":["What evidence supports your view?","What would make you change your mind?"],"sample":"I would use unfamiliar materials and questions rather than repeat the lesson exactly. For speaking, a partner should ask follow up questions without warning. For listening, I should explain the main point before checking a transcript. I would also repeat the assessment after a delay. A useful result should identify strengths and weaknesses separately, because a high reading score does not automatically mean that spontaneous conversation is equally strong."},"project":"完成独立四项评估并形成证据档案；把课程完成度与实际能力结论分开记录。","reviewChecklist":["能指出至少两处原文证据，并解释干扰项为什么不成立。","不看范文完成写作，检查任务覆盖、组织与语言后修改。","独立录制口语，再回答追问；请同伴反馈表达是否清楚。","至少间隔七天，用陌生情境重做迁移任务，而不是背诵原答案。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I used to judge my speaking progress by how smoothly I could deliver a prepared answer. My tutor suggested a different check: explain a new problem to a partner, then respond to questions I had not seen. The first recording sounded less polished, but it showed whether I could clarify, give reasons and repair a misunderstanding. We repeated the task two weeks later with a different topic. I still paused, yet the listener needed fewer explanations. That was useful evidence of progress. It did not mean every speaking skill was now strong, but it helped us choose what to practise next.","questions":[{"prompt":"What did the old progress check measure?","answer":"Smooth delivery of a prepared answer.","options":["Smooth delivery of a prepared answer.","Understanding all accents.","Independent writing accuracy."],"explanation":"旧检查针对排练回答。"},{"prompt":"Why use unexpected questions?","answer":"To test adaptation and clarification.","options":["To test adaptation and clarification.","To prevent the partner speaking.","To memorise a longer script."],"explanation":"即时回应检验交流能力。"},{"prompt":"What improved at the later check?","answer":"The listener needed fewer explanations.","options":["The listener needed fewer explanations.","The speaker never paused.","Every skill was proven strong."],"explanation":"进步证据限定为更少澄清需求。"}]}},{"chapter":41,"title":"精确搭配：把建议变成可执行承诺","target":"C1","focus":"用搭配区分承诺、证据和限制","grammar":"动词与名词搭配；限定语","prerequisite":"完成第40章独立B2阶段复核；能处理陌生听读并给出有理由的观点。","sourceA":{"title":"An evening service with conditions","text":"A fictional town library is considering two additional evening openings each week. Its manager wants a proposal that distinguishes a useful idea from an affordable commitment. Staff have collected comments, but a popular suggestion is not automatically a workable timetable. The following briefing explains how the team should discuss its evidence and responsibilities. The service is intended for residents who cannot visit during normal working hours, including carers and people on rotating shifts.\n\nThe pilot could begin on one evening rather than two, allowing the rota to be checked under ordinary conditions. Comments from visitors should be considered alongside attendance records. Otherwise, a busy first night might be mistaken for evidence that the same level of demand will continue throughout the year.\n\nWe must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours. The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast. The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing. We need to take account of staff working consecutive evenings. Extended hours could meet the needs of people on rotating shifts. A limited trial would provide evidence without creating an indefinite obligation.\n\nWe should draw a distinction between attendance and satisfaction. The council must bear the cost of additional security staff. Early results may shed light on which evening is busiest. A written rota would reduce uncertainty about individual responsibilities. We cannot rule out lower attendance during winter storms. The final report should set out the conditions for continuing."},"sourceB":{"title":"A workshop changes its booking policy","text":"The Cedar bicycle workshop used to promise same-day repairs whenever a customer arrived before noon. That phrase sounded reassuring, but it concealed an important condition: specialist parts were not always available. Customers planned their journeys around an expectation that the mechanics could not consistently meet. The owner therefore replaced the promise with a diagnosis appointment and a written estimate of completion time. Diagnosis is now offered on the day of arrival; completion depends on the fault and the supply of parts.\n\nA customer group welcomed the clearer distinction, although some members feared that the new wording would excuse unnecessary delays. In response, the workshop began recording the time between diagnosis and completion, separating delays within its control from those caused by unavailable parts. It publishes the median waiting time for each category, rather than combining every repair into one attractive average. This does not eliminate disappointment, but it makes explanations more specific.\n\nThe mechanics also changed their telephone language. Instead of saying that a repair was almost finished, they describe the remaining task and the next agreed update. The policy has a cost: estimates take time, and unexpected faults still require negotiation. Nevertheless, the owner considers a qualified commitment more useful than confident language that cannot be supported. The customer group will review the records after three months before deciding whether the new approach has improved reliability."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"We must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours.","options":["We must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours.","It is not true that we must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“We must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast.","options":["The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast.","It is not true that the survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing.","options":["The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing.","It is not true that the manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What is guaranteed on the arrival day?","answer":"A diagnosis appointment","options":["A diagnosis appointment","Completion of every repair","Delivery of specialist parts"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A workshop changes its booking policy”支持“A diagnosis appointment”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which statistic is published?","answer":"Median waiting time by delay category","options":["Median waiting time by delay category","Only the fastest repair","One average for all customers"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A workshop changes its booking policy”支持“Median waiting time by delay category”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"When will the customer group review the records?","answer":"After three months","options":["After three months","After one week","At the end of each day"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A workshop changes its booking policy”支持“After three months”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 拨出资金","en":"We must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours.","zh":"我们必须先拨出资金，再宣传延长开放时间。","word":"allocate funds","meaning":"拨出资金","tip":"此句用“allocate funds”（拨出资金）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. ……的迹象","en":"The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast.","zh":"调查反映了需求迹象，并非完整预测。","word":"an indication of","meaning":"……的迹象","tip":"此句用“an indication of”（……的迹象）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 作出明确承诺","en":"The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing.","zh":"经理应在核实人员安排后才作出明确承诺。","word":"make a firm commitment","meaning":"作出明确承诺","tip":"此句用“make a firm commitment”（作出明确承诺）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 考虑","en":"We need to take account of staff working consecutive evenings.","zh":"我们需要考虑连续上晚班的员工。","word":"take account of","meaning":"考虑","tip":"此句用“take account of”（考虑）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 满足需要","en":"Extended hours could meet the needs of people on rotating shifts.","zh":"延长开放时间可能满足轮班人群的需要。","word":"meet the needs","meaning":"满足需要","tip":"此句用“meet the needs”（满足需要）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 提供证据","en":"A limited trial would provide evidence without creating an indefinite obligation.","zh":"有限试行能提供证据，又不会造成无限期义务。","word":"provide evidence","meaning":"提供证据","tip":"此句用“provide evidence”（提供证据）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 作出区分","en":"We should draw a distinction between attendance and satisfaction.","zh":"我们应区分到访人数和满意度。","word":"draw a distinction","meaning":"作出区分","tip":"此句用“draw a distinction”（作出区分）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 承担费用","en":"The council must bear the cost of additional security staff.","zh":"委员会必须承担额外安保人员的费用。","word":"bear the cost","meaning":"承担费用","tip":"此句用“bear the cost”（承担费用）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 帮助说明","en":"Early results may shed light on which evening is busiest.","zh":"初步结果可能揭示哪个晚上最忙。","word":"shed light on","meaning":"帮助说明","tip":"此句用“shed light on”（帮助说明）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 减少不确定性","en":"A written rota would reduce uncertainty about individual responsibilities.","zh":"书面轮班表能减少个人职责的不确定性。","word":"reduce uncertainty","meaning":"减少不确定性","tip":"此句用“reduce uncertainty”（减少不确定性）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 排除","en":"We cannot rule out lower attendance during winter storms.","zh":"我们不能排除冬季暴风雨期间到访人数下降的可能。","word":"rule out","meaning":"排除","tip":"此句用“rule out”（排除）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 列明","en":"The final report should set out the conditions for continuing.","zh":"最终报告应列明继续运行的条件。","word":"set out","meaning":"列明","tip":"此句用“set out”（列明）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (拨出资金): We must ___ before advertising additional opening hours.","answers":["allocate funds"],"explanation":"原句用“allocate funds”表达“拨出资金”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (提供证据): A limited trial would ___ without creating an indefinite obligation.","answers":["provide evidence"],"explanation":"原句用“provide evidence”表达“提供证据”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of ALLOCATE: Careful ___ of funds must precede advertising.","answers":["allocation"],"explanation":"空格需要“allocation”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: Only if staffing is confirmed will the library extend its hours. Required opening: The library will extend its …","answers":["The library will extend its hours provided that staffing is confirmed."],"explanation":"参考答案：The library will extend its hours provided that staffing is confirmed. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a recommendation to the library manager. Distinguish a trial from a permanent commitment, specify evidence to collect, and explain one staffing safeguard.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"I recommend a bounded evening-opening trial, with staffing and security confirmed before publicity. The purpose is to test a service residents can use, rather than announce a promise that the library cannot sustain. The pilot could begin on one evening rather than two, allowing the rota to be checked under ordinary conditions. Comments from visitors should be considered alongside attendance records. Otherwise, a busy first night might be mistaken for evidence that the same level of demand will continue throughout the year.\n\nWe must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours. The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast. The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing. We need to take account of staff working consecutive evenings. Extended hours could meet the needs of people on rotating shifts. A limited trial would provide evidence without creating an indefinite obligation.\n\nWe should draw a distinction between attendance and satisfaction. The council must bear the cost of additional security staff. Early results may shed light on which evening is busiest. A written rota would reduce uncertainty about individual responsibilities. We cannot rule out lower attendance during winter storms. The final report should set out the conditions for continuing.\n\nAfter the trial, the manager should publish the findings and explain whether continuing is justified. A clear stopping point protects staff while leaving open the possibility of a useful long-term service."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “An evening service with conditions”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I would start with a limited evening-opening trial, because the library needs to know whether demand and staff capacity fit together. We must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours. The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast. The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing. We need to take account of staff working consecutive evenings. I would also ask staff what the rota means in practice. After a defined trial, the decision should depend on the evidence collected, rather than pressure to defend the original announcement."},"project":"完成“An evening service with conditions”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Here is an update for museum volunteers. Saturday's orientation will begin at ten in the education room, rather than in the entrance hall. Please arrive fifteen minutes early if you still need an identity badge. The first session is about helping visitors find their way; training on the new ticket machines has been postponed until next month because the demonstration software is not ready. At eleven, the coordinator will assign pairs to practise a short route through the building. Nobody will be asked to guide a public group during this session. Bring comfortable shoes and your volunteer handbook. If stairs are difficult for you, email the coordinator by Thursday so that a step-free practice route can be arranged. The café will be closed, so please bring water.","questions":[{"prompt":"Where is orientation held?","answer":"In the education room","options":["In the education room","In the entrance hall","In the café"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“In the education room”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What has been postponed?","answer":"Ticket-machine training","options":["Ticket-machine training","Badge collection","Route practice"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Ticket-machine training”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"Who should email by Thursday?","answer":"Volunteers needing a step-free route","options":["Volunteers needing a step-free route","Everyone bringing water","Only experienced guides"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Volunteers needing a step-free route”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":42,"title":"近义词与分寸：准确解释变化","target":"C1","focus":"区分证据、推断与程度","grammar":"近义词；模态动词与程度副词","prerequisite":"完成第41章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"What a shorter queue really means","text":"The fictional Brookside advice centre has reorganised its reception system. Managers want to explain the early figures without exaggerating what they establish. Queue length, waiting time and the quality of advice are related, but they are not identical measures. Some appointments now take place by telephone, which changes who appears in the reception data. The centre has prepared language for a cautious progress briefing rather than a celebratory announcement.\n\nA person receiving detailed advice may need a longer appointment, so faster service is not always better service. The centre should examine whether clients understand their next step. It should also check whether telephone users and people visiting in person face different barriers before describing the system as an improvement for everyone.\n\nA shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service. The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause. Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline. The reduction was modest rather than dramatic. We should distinguish an accurate count from a representative sample. Some clients described the new reception process as convenient.\n\nConvenience alone does not establish that the advice was appropriate. The manager acknowledged limitations without dismissing the positive results. A plausible explanation still needs independent checking. The evidence is consistent with a benefit, although alternatives remain. We need comparable figures from a similar period. The final statement should reflect the uncertainty in the findings."},"sourceB":{"title":"A quiet carriage experiment","text":"A regional train operator reserved one carriage on six weekday services for passengers who preferred a quieter journey. The signs asked people to keep conversations brief and use headphones. They did not require complete silence. After four weeks, complaints about telephone conversations fell on those services, while the total number of complaints across the route remained broadly unchanged.\n\nThe operator's first internal summary described the policy as effective. A passenger representative asked for a more precise statement. Fewer complaints on selected trains might reflect improved behaviour, but passengers might also have moved to other carriages or stopped reporting incidents. Moreover, the trial services were chosen because they had unusually frequent complaints in the preceding month. A subsequent decline could partly reflect a return to more typical conditions.\n\nThe revised summary says that the results are encouraging and consistent with a useful local effect. It does not claim that the policy has reduced noise across the whole network. The next trial will include comparable services without a designated quiet carriage, and observers will record noise at fixed points in the journey. Observers cannot measure whether a passenger feels relaxed merely by counting conversations. The operator therefore plans a short anonymous survey as well. The passenger representative supports continuing the experiment, provided that the distinction between reduced complaints and reduced disturbance remains explicit."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"A shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service.","options":["A shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service.","It is not true that a shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“A shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause.","options":["The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause.","It is not true that the figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline.","options":["Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline.","It is not true that telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did the signs request?","answer":"Brief conversations and headphones","options":["Brief conversations and headphones","Complete silence","No electronic devices"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A quiet carriage experiment”支持“Brief conversations and headphones”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why might complaints have declined anyway?","answer":"The selected services previously had unusually high complaints","options":["The selected services previously had unusually high complaints","The route had closed","Every passenger received compensation"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A quiet carriage experiment”支持“The selected services previously had unusually high complaints”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What will supplement observation?","answer":"An anonymous survey","options":["An anonymous survey","A fare increase","A ban on all conversations"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A quiet carriage experiment”支持“An anonymous survey”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 意味着","en":"A shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service.","zh":"队伍变短并不一定意味着每个人得到更快的服务。","word":"imply","meaning":"意味着","tip":"此句用“imply”（意味着）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 提示","en":"The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause.","zh":"这些数字提示有所改善，却不能证明原因。","word":"suggest","meaning":"提示","tip":"此句用“suggest”（提示）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 表面的","en":"Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline.","zh":"电话预约可能解释部分表面下降。","word":"apparent","meaning":"表面的","tip":"此句用“apparent”（表面的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 不大的","en":"The reduction was modest rather than dramatic.","zh":"降幅不大，而非十分显著。","word":"modest","meaning":"不大的","tip":"此句用“modest”（不大的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 有代表性的","en":"We should distinguish an accurate count from a representative sample.","zh":"我们应区分准确计数与有代表性的样本。","word":"representative","meaning":"有代表性的","tip":"此句用“representative”（有代表性的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 方便的","en":"Some clients described the new reception process as convenient.","zh":"一些来访者认为新接待流程很方便。","word":"convenient","meaning":"方便的","tip":"此句用“convenient”（方便的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 恰当的","en":"Convenience alone does not establish that the advice was appropriate.","zh":"仅有便利并不能证明建议恰当。","word":"appropriate","meaning":"恰当的","tip":"此句用“appropriate”（恰当的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 承认","en":"The manager acknowledged limitations without dismissing the positive results.","zh":"经理承认局限，同时没有否定积极结果。","word":"acknowledge","meaning":"承认","tip":"此句用“acknowledge”（承认）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 看似合理的","en":"A plausible explanation still needs independent checking.","zh":"合理的解释仍需独立核查。","word":"plausible","meaning":"看似合理的","tip":"此句用“plausible”（看似合理的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 与……相符","en":"The evidence is consistent with a benefit, although alternatives remain.","zh":"证据与产生益处的解释相符，但仍存在其他解释。","word":"consistent with","meaning":"与……相符","tip":"此句用“consistent with”（与……相符）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 可比的","en":"We need comparable figures from a similar period.","zh":"我们需要相近时期的可比数据。","word":"comparable","meaning":"可比的","tip":"此句用“comparable”（可比的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 体现","en":"The final statement should reflect the uncertainty in the findings.","zh":"最终声明应体现研究结果中的不确定性。","word":"reflect","meaning":"体现","tip":"此句用“reflect”（体现）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (意味着): A shorter queue does not necessarily ___ faster individual service.","answers":["imply"],"explanation":"原句用“imply”表达“意味着”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (方便的): Some clients described the new reception process as ___.","answers":["convenient"],"explanation":"原句用“convenient”表达“方便的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of CERTAIN: The report should state the remaining ___ clearly.","answers":["uncertainty"],"explanation":"空格需要“uncertainty”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The figures suggest improvement, but they do not prove its cause. Required opening: Although the figures suggest improvement, …","answers":["Although the figures suggest improvement, they do not prove its cause."],"explanation":"参考答案：Although the figures suggest improvement, they do not prove its cause. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a cautious briefing about the advice centre. Explain why queue length is insufficient, propose two additional measures, and distinguish a finding from an explanation.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The reception changes are encouraging, but the evidence does not yet justify a broad claim about service quality. The centre should explain what has improved and what remains uncertain. A person receiving detailed advice may need a longer appointment, so faster service is not always better service. The centre should examine whether clients understand their next step. It should also check whether telephone users and people visiting in person face different barriers before describing the system as an improvement for everyone.\n\nA shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service. The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause. Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline. The reduction was modest rather than dramatic. We should distinguish an accurate count from a representative sample. Some clients described the new reception process as convenient.\n\nConvenience alone does not establish that the advice was appropriate. The manager acknowledged limitations without dismissing the positive results. A plausible explanation still needs independent checking. The evidence is consistent with a benefit, although alternatives remain. We need comparable figures from a similar period. The final statement should reflect the uncertainty in the findings.\n\nThe next review should combine comparable waiting figures with evidence of whether clients understood the advice. That would support a more informative judgement than treating a shorter visible queue as success in itself."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “What a shorter queue really means”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I would describe the reception results as promising, while being careful about which part of the service the figures actually measure. A shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service. The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause. Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline. The reduction was modest rather than dramatic. I would add a check of whether clients understood their next step. If the apparent improvement mainly reflected movement to telephone appointments, the public explanation should acknowledge that rather than exaggerate it."},"project":"完成“What a shorter queue really means”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"A quick note about next week's orchestra rehearsal: we will use the upstairs studio because repairs are being carried out in the main hall. The lift is working, and large instruments can be taken up before six thirty, when the public classes begin. We will rehearse the second movement first, so please do not assume that the printed running order is still correct. Our guest conductor will arrive at seven, but the section leaders will start warm-up exercises at six forty-five. The rehearsal is expected to finish at nine. That is an estimate, not a promise that every passage will be covered. If you cannot stay until the end, tell your section leader before the session. Sheet music will be provided; you only need to bring a pencil and your instrument.","questions":[{"prompt":"Where will the rehearsal take place?","answer":"The upstairs studio","options":["The upstairs studio","The main hall","A public classroom"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The upstairs studio”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What will be rehearsed first?","answer":"The second movement","options":["The second movement","The printed opening item","Only warm-up exercises"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The second movement”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What should early leavers do?","answer":"Tell their section leader beforehand","options":["Tell their section leader beforehand","Email the guest conductor afterwards","Leave without notifying anyone"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Tell their section leader beforehand”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":43,"title":"语域与语气：同一信息面向不同读者","target":"C1","focus":"正式、友好与坚定表达的切换","grammar":"间接请求；语域；被动语态","prerequisite":"完成第42章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Rewriting a housing notice","text":"A fictional housing cooperative needs access to residents' kitchens to inspect shared water pipes. Its first notice sounded abrupt and gave no way to discuss individual circumstances. A revised version must remain clear about the inspection while respecting privacy and making the required action easy to identify. The team is preparing both a formal letter and a short reminder message. Each version must preserve the same deadline and contact arrangements.\n\nPrivacy is central to residents' response. The letter should explain what staff will inspect, how identity will be checked and how a different appointment can be requested. A shorter reminder can omit background discussion, but it must not drop the action that residents are expected to take.\n\nWe would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window. Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable. The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available. Residents are requested to reply by Thursday afternoon. For clarity, the visit does not involve opening personal cupboards. A member of staff will show identification before entering.\n\nCould you let us know whether anyone requires additional support? The reminder should retain the deadline stated in the formal letter. Friendly wording must not obscure what residents need to do. We apologise for the inconvenience caused by the inspection. If you have already replied, no further action is needed. A firm request can remain respectful and specific."},"sourceB":{"title":"A theatre explains a cancellation","text":"A small theatre cancelled a Sunday matinee when an electrical inspection identified a fault affecting the stage lighting. The fault did not affect the building's emergency exits, but repairing it while preparing the performance would have created an unacceptable risk. The first message to ticket holders contained a long technical description and placed the refund instructions at the bottom. Several readers thought that they were being asked to return to the venue before receiving their money.\n\nThe communications manager rewrote the notice in a simpler order. It opens with the cancellation and an apology, then explains that refunds will be made automatically to the original payment method within ten working days. People who paid in cash should telephone the box office to arrange a refund. Tickets cannot be transferred to the following weekend because that performance is already fully booked.\n\nA separate message to the visiting production company contains the technical details and the revised maintenance schedule. The public notice does not include them, since they do not help ticket holders decide what to do. Nevertheless, both messages identify the same reason for cancellation. The manager rejected a suggestion to describe it merely as an operational issue, considering that phrase unnecessarily evasive. A brief follow-up will be sent once performances resume. The theatre wants to sound considerate without implying that an alternative seat can be offered when none is available."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"We would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window.","options":["We would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window.","It is not true that we would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“We would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable.","options":["Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable.","It is not true that please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available.","options":["The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available.","It is not true that the inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"How are card payments refunded?","answer":"Automatically to the original payment method","options":["Automatically to the original payment method","Only after a visit","Through a ticket transfer"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A theatre explains a cancellation”支持“Automatically to the original payment method”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why can tickets not transfer to next weekend?","answer":"That performance is fully booked","options":["That performance is fully booked","The theatre is closing permanently","Transfers are legally prohibited"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A theatre explains a cancellation”支持“That performance is fully booked”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why were technical details omitted publicly?","answer":"They did not help ticket holders act","options":["They did not help ticket holders act","They contradicted the real reason","They were unavailable to staff"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A theatre explains a cancellation”支持“They did not help ticket holders act”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 希望得到","en":"We would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window.","zh":"希望您能在检查时段允许我们进入厨房。","word":"would appreciate","meaning":"希望得到","tip":"此句用“would appreciate”（希望得到）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 不合适的","en":"Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable.","zh":"如果建议时间不合适，请联系办公室。","word":"unsuitable","meaning":"不合适的","tip":"此句用“unsuitable”（不合适的）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 其他预约时间","en":"The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available.","zh":"检查是必要的，但可以另约时间。","word":"alternative appointment","meaning":"其他预约时间","tip":"此句用“alternative appointment”（其他预约时间）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 被请予以","en":"Residents are requested to reply by Thursday afternoon.","zh":"请住户在星期四下午前回复。","word":"are requested to","meaning":"被请予以","tip":"此句用“are requested to”（被请予以）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 为说明清楚","en":"For clarity, the visit does not involve opening personal cupboards.","zh":"为说明清楚，这次上门不涉及打开私人橱柜。","word":"for clarity","meaning":"为说明清楚","tip":"此句用“for clarity”（为说明清楚）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 身份证明","en":"A member of staff will show identification before entering.","zh":"工作人员会在进入前出示证件。","word":"identification","meaning":"身份证明","tip":"此句用“identification”（身份证明）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 额外协助","en":"Could you let us know whether anyone requires additional support?","zh":"能否告知我们是否有人需要额外协助？","word":"additional support","meaning":"额外协助","tip":"此句用“additional support”（额外协助）提出澄清或协商问题，让对方能具体回应。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 保留","en":"The reminder should retain the deadline stated in the formal letter.","zh":"提醒信息应保留正式信件中规定的截止时间。","word":"retain","meaning":"保留","tip":"此句用“retain”（保留）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 使不清楚","en":"Friendly wording must not obscure what residents need to do.","zh":"友好措辞不能掩盖住户需要采取的行动。","word":"obscure","meaning":"使不清楚","tip":"此句用“obscure”（使不清楚）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 不便","en":"We apologise for the inconvenience caused by the inspection.","zh":"对于检查造成的不便，我们表示歉意。","word":"inconvenience","meaning":"不便","tip":"此句用“inconvenience”（不便）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 进一步行动","en":"If you have already replied, no further action is needed.","zh":"如果您已经回复，则无需进一步操作。","word":"further action","meaning":"进一步行动","tip":"此句用“further action”（进一步行动）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 尊重他人的","en":"A firm request can remain respectful and specific.","zh":"明确坚定的请求仍可以尊重且具体。","word":"respectful","meaning":"尊重他人的","tip":"此句用“respectful”（尊重他人的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (希望得到): We ___ access to your kitchen during the inspection window.","answers":["would appreciate"],"explanation":"原句用“would appreciate”表达“希望得到”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (身份证明): A member of staff will show ___ before entering.","answers":["identification"],"explanation":"原句用“identification”表达“身份证明”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of IDENTIFY: Staff will show ___ before entering.","answers":["identification"],"explanation":"空格需要“identification”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: If you have already replied, you do not need to act again. Required opening: No further action is needed …","answers":["No further action is needed if you have already replied."],"explanation":"参考答案：No further action is needed if you have already replied. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write the cooperative’s formal notice, then explain how you would adapt it into a brief reminder. Preserve all essential actions and offer a practical route for exceptions.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"Dear residents, we are arranging an inspection of shared kitchen water pipes. Please read the access request carefully and contact the office if your household needs an alternative appointment or additional support. Privacy is central to residents' response. The letter should explain what staff will inspect, how identity will be checked and how a different appointment can be requested. A shorter reminder can omit background discussion, but it must not drop the action that residents are expected to take.\n\nWe would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window. Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable. The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available. Residents are requested to reply by Thursday afternoon. For clarity, the visit does not involve opening personal cupboards. A member of staff will show identification before entering.\n\nCould you let us know whether anyone requires additional support? The reminder should retain the deadline stated in the formal letter. Friendly wording must not obscure what residents need to do. We apologise for the inconvenience caused by the inspection. If you have already replied, no further action is needed. A firm request can remain respectful and specific.\n\nPlease reply by Thursday afternoon if you need to discuss access. The shorter reminder should retain that deadline and contact route. Thank you for helping us arrange the inspection clearly and respectfully."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Rewriting a housing notice”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I think the notice should put residents' required action first, while giving enough detail to make the request feel legitimate and respectful. We would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window. Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable. The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available. Residents are requested to reply by Thursday afternoon. A short reminder can sound friendlier, but it must preserve the deadline and contact route. I would check the two versions together to ensure that neither creates a different obligation."},"project":"完成“Rewriting a housing notice”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Before we finish, a reminder about Friday's research club. The meeting is open to everyone, but only members who have submitted a project outline will receive individual feedback. You can still attend and listen if your outline is unfinished. Please send the document to the club secretary by Wednesday evening, using the title of your project as the file name. We have moved the start time from four to four thirty so that students attending the laboratory induction can join us. Each presentation has a six-minute limit, followed by four minutes for questions. Do not prepare a full slide deck; one diagram or a single page of notes will be sufficient. We are discussing plans, not judging completed research. The aim is to identify a practical next step.","questions":[{"prompt":"Who receives individual feedback?","answer":"Members who submitted an outline","options":["Members who submitted an outline","Only laboratory students","Every visitor automatically"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Members who submitted an outline”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"When does the meeting start?","answer":"Four thirty","options":["Four thirty","Four o’clock","Five thirty"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Four thirty”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What presentation material is sufficient?","answer":"One diagram or one page of notes","options":["One diagram or one page of notes","A complete slide deck","A finished research report"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“One diagram or one page of notes”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":44,"title":"错误修复：从正确到自然准确","target":"C1","focus":"识别系统性错误并解释修改理由","grammar":"主谓一致；平行结构；指代与时态","prerequisite":"完成第43章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Editing a volunteer handbook","text":"An environmental charity is updating its fictional volunteer handbook. Earlier versions were assembled from messages written by several coordinators. The information is useful, but inconsistent terms, unclear pronouns and mixed time references make some instructions hard to follow. The editor wants volunteers to understand the revised text without needing to guess the writer's intention. Corrections should preserve the policy rather than silently change it.\n\nVolunteers may read the handbook while preparing equipment, with little time to resolve ambiguous instructions. A correction therefore needs to be checked against the actual procedure by someone familiar with it. The editor should record any uncertainty and ask the coordinator, rather than substitute a fluent sentence with a different meaning.\n\nEach volunteer is responsible for checking the equipment before departure. The guide explains how to collect, label and store samples. When Maya returned, she gave the keys to the coordinator. Last month the team recorded faults; this month it is testing repairs. The equipment has been checked, but the route has not been approved. Fewer volunteers attended than the coordinator had expected.\n\nThe revised instructions distinguish advice from compulsory requirements. Changing a word can unintentionally change the scope of a rule. A clear correction should preserve the original factual meaning. The editor queried the figure instead of inventing a replacement. A consistent term helps readers recognise the same procedure. We should explain why a revision improves accuracy or clarity."},"sourceB":{"title":"A food cooperative corrects its report","text":"The annual report of the Meadow food cooperative stated that deliveries had increased by a quarter. During proofreading, an employee noticed that the calculation referred to the number of delivery days, not the number of orders. The cooperative had added a Friday service, while the total number of orders had risen only slightly. The error was therefore more than an awkward choice of words: it changed the apparent scale of growth.\n\nThe editor asked the accounts team to verify both figures before making a correction. She also found a sentence saying that drivers had discussed complaints with customers after they returned. Since the pronoun could refer to either group, the report now states that drivers discussed complaints after returning to the depot. That edit clarified timing without claiming that complaints had been resolved.\n\nThe revised report includes a short correction note explaining the delivery-day measure. The chair initially worried that drawing attention to the mistake would damage confidence. Other members argued that a visible correction would be less damaging than leaving a misleading claim in circulation. They agreed to retain the original publication date and add the date of the correction, so that readers can distinguish the two versions. For future reports, each numerical claim will have a named source and a second checker. The editor emphasised that fluent prose is not a substitute for checking what a sentence actually asserts."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"Each volunteer is responsible for checking the equipment before departure.","options":["Each volunteer is responsible for checking the equipment before departure.","It is not true that each volunteer is responsible for checking the equipment before departure.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Each volunteer is responsible for checking the equipment before departure.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The guide explains how to collect, label and store samples.","options":["The guide explains how to collect, label and store samples.","It is not true that the guide explains how to collect, label and store samples.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The guide explains how to collect, label and store samples.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"When Maya returned, she gave the keys to the coordinator.","options":["When Maya returned, she gave the keys to the coordinator.","It is not true that when Maya returned, she gave the keys to the coordinator.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“When Maya returned, she gave the keys to the coordinator.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What actually increased by a quarter?","answer":"The number of delivery days","options":["The number of delivery days","The number of orders","The number of drivers"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A food cooperative corrects its report”支持“The number of delivery days”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did the pronoun edit clarify?","answer":"When drivers discussed complaints","options":["When drivers discussed complaints","Whether complaints were resolved","Which customers received refunds"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A food cooperative corrects its report”支持“When drivers discussed complaints”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"How will versions be distinguished?","answer":"By retaining publication and adding correction dates","options":["By retaining publication and adding correction dates","By deleting all dates","By changing the cooperative’s name"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A food cooperative corrects its report”支持“By retaining publication and adding correction dates”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 每一名","en":"Each volunteer is responsible for checking the equipment before departure.","zh":"每名志愿者都有责任在出发前检查设备。","word":"each","meaning":"每一名","tip":"此句用“each”（每一名）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 收集、标记和储存","en":"The guide explains how to collect, label and store samples.","zh":"指南说明如何收集、标记和储存样本。","word":"collect, label and store","meaning":"收集、标记和储存","tip":"此句用“collect, label and store”（收集、标记和储存）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 当玛雅回来时","en":"When Maya returned, she gave the keys to the coordinator.","zh":"玛雅回来后，把钥匙交给了协调员。","word":"When Maya returned","meaning":"当玛雅回来时","tip":"此句用“When Maya returned”（当玛雅回来时）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 上个月","en":"Last month the team recorded faults; this month it is testing repairs.","zh":"上个月团队记录故障，本月正在测试维修结果。","word":"Last month","meaning":"上个月","tip":"此句用“Last month”（上个月）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 已经检查","en":"The equipment has been checked, but the route has not been approved.","zh":"设备已经检查，但路线尚未获批。","word":"has been checked","meaning":"已经检查","tip":"此句用“has been checked”（已经检查）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 更少的","en":"Fewer volunteers attended than the coordinator had expected.","zh":"到场志愿者比协调员预期的更少。","word":"fewer","meaning":"更少的","tip":"此句用“fewer”（更少的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 强制的","en":"The revised instructions distinguish advice from compulsory requirements.","zh":"修改后的说明区分建议与强制要求。","word":"compulsory","meaning":"强制的","tip":"此句用“compulsory”（强制的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 范围","en":"Changing a word can unintentionally change the scope of a rule.","zh":"修改一个词可能无意中改变规则的适用范围。","word":"scope","meaning":"范围","tip":"此句用“scope”（范围）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 保留","en":"A clear correction should preserve the original factual meaning.","zh":"清楚的修改应保留原来的事实含义。","word":"preserve","meaning":"保留","tip":"此句用“preserve”（保留）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 提出疑问","en":"The editor queried the figure instead of inventing a replacement.","zh":"编辑对数字提出疑问，而没有编造替代数字。","word":"queried","meaning":"提出疑问","tip":"此句用“queried”（提出疑问）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 一致的","en":"A consistent term helps readers recognise the same procedure.","zh":"一致的术语有助于读者识别同一流程。","word":"consistent","meaning":"一致的","tip":"此句用“consistent”（一致的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 修改","en":"We should explain why a revision improves accuracy or clarity.","zh":"我们应解释修改为何提高了准确性或清晰度。","word":"revision","meaning":"修改","tip":"此句用“revision”（修改）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (每一名): ___ volunteer is responsible for checking the equipment before departure.","answers":["each"],"explanation":"原句用“each”表达“每一名”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (更少的): ___ volunteers attended than the coordinator had expected.","answers":["fewer"],"explanation":"原句用“fewer”表达“更少的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of CONSISTENT: Terminological ___ helps volunteers follow the handbook.","answers":["consistency"],"explanation":"空格需要“consistency”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The instructions are clear, but the figure still needs checking. Required opening: Although the instructions are clear, …","answers":["Although the instructions are clear, the figure still needs checking."],"explanation":"参考答案：Although the instructions are clear, the figure still needs checking. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an editing policy for the charity. Explain how to handle unclear references, inconsistent terminology and doubtful facts, using examples from the handbook briefing.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The charity should adopt an editing process that checks meaning before polishing style. The handbook's purpose is to guide volunteers accurately, so each change must be checked against the actual procedure. Volunteers may read the handbook while preparing equipment, with little time to resolve ambiguous instructions. A correction therefore needs to be checked against the actual procedure by someone familiar with it. The editor should record any uncertainty and ask the coordinator, rather than substitute a fluent sentence with a different meaning.\n\nEach volunteer is responsible for checking the equipment before departure. The guide explains how to collect, label and store samples. When Maya returned, she gave the keys to the coordinator. Last month the team recorded faults; this month it is testing repairs. The equipment has been checked, but the route has not been approved. Fewer volunteers attended than the coordinator had expected.\n\nThe revised instructions distinguish advice from compulsory requirements. Changing a word can unintentionally change the scope of a rule. A clear correction should preserve the original factual meaning. The editor queried the figure instead of inventing a replacement. A consistent term helps readers recognise the same procedure. We should explain why a revision improves accuracy or clarity.\n\nThe coordinator should approve any change that might alter a requirement. Keeping a record of factual queries will help future editors distinguish a verified correction from a stylistic preference."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Editing a volunteer handbook”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"My priority would be preserving the handbook's meaning. A fluent correction is unhelpful if it changes the procedure volunteers are meant to follow. Each volunteer is responsible for checking the equipment before departure. The guide explains how to collect, label and store samples. When Maya returned, she gave the keys to the coordinator. Last month the team recorded faults; this month it is testing repairs. I would ask the coordinator about doubtful facts and keep the query visible. This is especially important when an instruction affects equipment or timing, because guessing could change what volunteers actually do."},"project":"完成“Editing a volunteer handbook”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"There is one correction to the trip information sent yesterday. The coach will leave from the sports centre entrance, not from the main school gate. The departure time is unchanged: eight fifteen. Please arrive by eight so that the register can be checked. Students should bring a packed lunch, although drinking water will be available at the field centre. We will be outdoors for most of the morning, and the path can be muddy even when the weather is dry, so sturdy shoes are important. The return is scheduled for four forty-five. If traffic causes a delay of more than twenty minutes, the school office will text the contact number on your consent form. You do not need to call the coach company. Please check that the school has the correct contact number before tomorrow afternoon.","questions":[{"prompt":"Where does the coach leave from?","answer":"The sports centre entrance","options":["The sports centre entrance","The main school gate","The field centre"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The sports centre entrance”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What has not changed?","answer":"The departure time","options":["The departure time","The meeting entrance","The published location"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The departure time”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"Who sends a substantial delay notice?","answer":"The school office","options":["The school office","The coach company","The field instructor"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The school office”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":45,"title":"讲座听记：抓住结构与保留条件","target":"C1","focus":"跟踪讲座论证并区分例子与结论","grammar":"篇章标记；名词化；让步从句","prerequisite":"完成第44章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"A lecture on urban shade","text":"In a fictional public lecture, landscape researcher Elena explains why a city should not judge its tree programme by planting totals alone. A young tree offers little shade immediately, and survival depends on soil, watering and protection. Her lecture moves from the problem of summer heat to the practical choices a maintenance team must make. The briefing below preserves that sequence so that listeners can reconstruct the argument rather than merely remember isolated statistics.\n\nFor a school route, the location of shade can matter more than a distant increase in canopy cover. Elena therefore asks the audience to connect measurement with the intended benefit. Her practical conclusion is to care for existing trees while choosing new sites in relation to people's daily movements.\n\nThe speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading. She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive. The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument. Although mature trees provide shade, benefits develop over many years. Maintenance should be treated as part of the investment. The proposal depends on reliable summer watering.\n\nA survival rate tells us more than an initial planting count. The lecturer qualifies her recommendation rather than withdrawing it. In this context, shade distribution matters as much as total cover. The conclusion returns to the neighbourhoods with the least protection. My notes separate the central claim from supporting examples. A useful summary preserves the conditions attached to the recommendation."},"sourceB":{"title":"Why a wetland lecture changed direction","text":"At a college lecture on flood management, the speaker began with a photograph of a restored wetland. Several students expected a simple argument that natural landscapes should replace engineered defences. Instead, the lecturer described the wetland as one component of a wider system. Its shallow basins could hold runoff after ordinary rainfall, but during an exceptional storm their capacity would be exceeded. Walls and drainage channels remained necessary near the hospital downstream.\n\nThe speaker then compared two possible measures of success: the amount of water stored and the number of properties protected. These measures were connected, yet neither could stand alone. A large storage volume in the wrong location might do little for the neighbourhood at greatest risk. Conversely, protecting a small number of critical buildings could be especially valuable. The lecturer used the hospital to illustrate this second point, rather than to claim that every town should prioritise the same type of building.\n\nA student asked whether the restored habitat justified the project even without a flood benefit. The lecturer regarded that as a legitimate separate question. Bird counts could inform an ecological assessment, but they could not establish the reliability of flood protection. Her conclusion was deliberately conditional: restoration deserved consideration where its capacity, position and maintenance fitted the wider plan. She asked students to retain those conditions when summarising the talk."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading.","options":["The speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading.","It is not true that the speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive.","options":["She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive.","It is not true that she then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument.","options":["The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument.","It is not true that the shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why was the hospital mentioned?","answer":"To illustrate the value of protecting critical buildings","options":["To illustrate the value of protecting critical buildings","To claim every town has identical priorities","To show walls are unnecessary"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Why a wetland lecture changed direction”支持“To illustrate the value of protecting critical buildings”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What could bird counts assess?","answer":"Ecological effects","options":["Ecological effects","Flood-defence reliability by themselves","The cost of drainage repairs"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Why a wetland lecture changed direction”支持“Ecological effects”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was the conclusion?","answer":"Restoration must fit the wider protection plan","options":["Restoration must fit the wider protection plan","Wetlands should replace every wall","Storage volume alone determines success"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Why a wetland lecture changed direction”支持“Restoration must fit the wider protection plan”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 说明确立","en":"The speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading.","zh":"讲者首先说明植树总数为何可能误导。","word":"establishes","meaning":"说明确立","tip":"此句用“establishes”（说明确立）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 转向","en":"She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive.","zh":"接着她转向幼树存活所需的条件。","word":"turns to","meaning":"转向","tip":"此句用“turns to”（转向）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 例证","en":"The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument.","zh":"有树荫的操场是例子，而非完整论证。","word":"illustration","meaning":"例证","tip":"此句用“illustration”（例证）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 虽然","en":"Although mature trees provide shade, benefits develop over many years.","zh":"成熟树木虽能遮阴，益处却需多年形成。","word":"although","meaning":"虽然","tip":"此句用“although”（虽然）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 维护","en":"Maintenance should be treated as part of the investment.","zh":"维护应被视为投资的一部分。","word":"maintenance","meaning":"维护","tip":"此句用“maintenance”（维护）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 取决于","en":"The proposal depends on reliable summer watering.","zh":"该方案取决于可靠的夏季浇水。","word":"depends on","meaning":"取决于","tip":"此句用“depends on”（取决于）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 存活率","en":"A survival rate tells us more than an initial planting count.","zh":"存活率比最初种植数量提供更多信息。","word":"survival rate","meaning":"存活率","tip":"此句用“survival rate”（存活率）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 限定","en":"The lecturer qualifies her recommendation rather than withdrawing it.","zh":"讲者对建议加上条件，而非撤回建议。","word":"qualifies","meaning":"限定","tip":"此句用“qualifies”（限定）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 分布","en":"In this context, shade distribution matters as much as total cover.","zh":"在此情境下，树荫分布与总覆盖同样重要。","word":"distribution","meaning":"分布","tip":"此句用“distribution”（分布）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 回到","en":"The conclusion returns to the neighbourhoods with the least protection.","zh":"结论重新关注防护最少的社区。","word":"returns to","meaning":"回到","tip":"此句用“returns to”（回到）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 核心主张","en":"My notes separate the central claim from supporting examples.","zh":"我的笔记区分核心主张和支持例子。","word":"central claim","meaning":"核心主张","tip":"此句用“central claim”（核心主张）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 附带于","en":"A useful summary preserves the conditions attached to the recommendation.","zh":"有用的摘要会保留建议所附带的条件。","word":"attached to","meaning":"附带于","tip":"此句用“attached to”（附带于）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (说明确立): The speaker first ___ why planting totals can be misleading.","answers":["establishes"],"explanation":"原句用“establishes”表达“说明确立”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (取决于): The proposal ___ reliable summer watering.","answers":["depends on"],"explanation":"原句用“depends on”表达“取决于”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of MAINTAIN: Tree ___ forms part of the investment.","answers":["maintenance"],"explanation":"空格需要“maintenance”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: Do not summarise the recommendation without its conditions. Required opening: The conditions must be retained …","answers":["The conditions must be retained when summarising the recommendation."],"explanation":"参考答案：The conditions must be retained when summarising the recommendation. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a structured summary of Elena’s lecture for a council member. Explain the problem, the proposed response, the example and the conditions for success.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"Elena's lecture recommends evaluating an urban tree programme through survival, location and maintenance, rather than through planting totals alone. The proposal connects the physical benefits of trees with the way people use neighbourhood space. For a school route, the location of shade can matter more than a distant increase in canopy cover. Elena therefore asks the audience to connect measurement with the intended benefit. Her practical conclusion is to care for existing trees while choosing new sites in relation to people's daily movements.\n\nThe speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading. She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive. The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument. Although mature trees provide shade, benefits develop over many years. Maintenance should be treated as part of the investment. The proposal depends on reliable summer watering.\n\nA survival rate tells us more than an initial planting count. The lecturer qualifies her recommendation rather than withdrawing it. In this context, shade distribution matters as much as total cover. The conclusion returns to the neighbourhoods with the least protection. My notes separate the central claim from supporting examples. A useful summary preserves the conditions attached to the recommendation.\n\nThe council should therefore connect funding to a maintenance plan and meaningful measures. This preserves the lecture's conditional recommendation while allowing practical action in neighbourhoods that currently have little shade."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “A lecture on urban shade”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"The lecture's most useful point is that planting a tree and obtaining useful shade are not the same event. Maintenance connects the two. The speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading. She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive. The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument. Although mature trees provide shade, benefits develop over many years. My summary would keep that condition rather than say that the speaker simply recommended more planting. If asked about a particular site, I would need information about use, soil and maintenance."},"project":"完成“A lecture on urban shade”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Before today's lecture begins, there are two changes to the rest of the series. Next Tuesday's session on materials will be delivered online because the guest speaker cannot travel. The start remains six o'clock, and the recording will be available to registered participants the following morning. The workshop on Thursday will still take place in person, but it has moved to room twelve on the ground floor. Please bring one object whose design you would like to discuss; a photograph is acceptable if the object is fragile or too large. We will analyse how a design works, rather than estimate its financial value. You do not need specialist knowledge. Places are limited, so registration for the workshop closes at noon on Monday, even if you have already booked the lecture series.","questions":[{"prompt":"Which event moves online?","answer":"Tuesday’s materials session","options":["Tuesday’s materials session","Thursday’s workshop","Every remaining session"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Tuesday’s materials session”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"When is the recording available?","answer":"The following morning","options":["The following morning","During Monday registration","Immediately before the lecture"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The following morning”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What should workshop participants bring?","answer":"An object or photograph for design discussion","options":["An object or photograph for design discussion","A financial valuation","A specialist certificate"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“An object or photograph for design discussion”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":46,"title":"访谈中的隐含意思","target":"C1","focus":"根据修正、保留与上下文判断态度","grammar":"委婉否定；语用推断；引述","prerequisite":"完成第45章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"An organiser reflects on a crowded festival","text":"In this fictional interview, an arts organiser discusses a neighbourhood festival that attracted more people than expected. The interviewer asks whether the event should simply become larger next year. The organiser welcomes interest but is cautious about treating attendance as the only sign of success. Her comments concern staff capacity, access and the experience of nearby residents. Listeners should distinguish what she says directly from what her careful qualifications allow us to infer.\n\nThe organiser wants residents to remain partners in the festival rather than become an audience for a decision already made. Her cautious phrases keep that possibility open. A reliable interpretation must retain both her pleasure at public interest and her concern that a larger programme would require stronger support.\n\nThe organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion. Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success. She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding. However, she questions whether the volunteer team could sustain it. The interviewer interprets hesitation as opposition, which is too strong. Her answer implies a preference for improving access before expanding.\n\nShe does not claim that nearby residents opposed every performance. The qualification concerns evening noise rather than daytime attendance. A warm tone alone does not establish agreement with a proposal. We should support an inference with specific words and context. She recommends consultation before making next year’s programme public. Her position is conditional support, not an outright refusal."},"sourceB":{"title":"A designer declines to celebrate early","text":"A radio interviewer asked furniture designer Ravi whether a large advance order meant his new chair was already a commercial success. Ravi replied that it was certainly a welcome start. He then explained that an order was not the same thing as a completed delivery, and that some buyers had requested modifications. His response acknowledged the good news while resisting the interviewer’s stronger conclusion.\n\nWhen asked whether the chair was revolutionary, Ravi laughed and said that comfortable seating had existed for rather a long time. He preferred to describe the improvement precisely: the chair could be repaired with ordinary tools, and individual components could be replaced. This mattered to the community centres that had ordered it because they maintained furniture themselves. He did not suggest that his design was the first repairable chair ever made.\n\nThe interviewer also raised a critical online review of the prototype. Ravi said that the reviewer had identified a real weakness in the original back support. The current version had been altered, although he would like to see longer trials before claiming that the problem was solved for every user. His willingness to discuss the criticism suggested confidence in the development process rather than indifference to negative feedback. At the end, he asked listeners to judge the chair after sustained use. The interview therefore offered grounds for interest, but not an unconditional endorsement of its long-term performance."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion.","options":["The organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion.","It is not true that the organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success.","options":["Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success.","It is not true that her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding.","options":["She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding.","It is not true that she concedes that a larger event could attract more funding.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"How does Ravi view the advance order?","answer":"Encouraging but insufficient to establish success","options":["Encouraging but insufficient to establish success","Proof of complete commercial success","An unwelcome administrative burden"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A designer declines to celebrate early”支持“Encouraging but insufficient to establish success”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What precise advantage does he emphasise?","answer":"Repair using ordinary tools","options":["Repair using ordinary tools","Being the first chair ever invented","Having no replaceable components"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A designer declines to celebrate early”支持“Repair using ordinary tools”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What does he acknowledge about the criticism?","answer":"It identified a real prototype weakness","options":["It identified a real prototype weakness","It concerned an unrelated product","It was entirely invented"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A designer declines to celebrate early”支持“It identified a real prototype weakness”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 不安的","en":"The organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion.","zh":"组织者对人数感到高兴，却对扩张不安。","word":"uneasy","meaning":"不安的","tip":"此句用“uneasy”（不安的）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 没有进一步到","en":"Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success.","zh":"她说“有益的开端”，尚未宣布成功。","word":"stops short of","meaning":"没有进一步到","tip":"此句用“stops short of”（没有进一步到）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 承认","en":"She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding.","zh":"她承认更大的活动可能吸引更多资金。","word":"concede","meaning":"承认","tip":"此句用“concede”（承认）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 持续承担","en":"However, she questions whether the volunteer team could sustain it.","zh":"然而，她质疑志愿者团队能否持续承担。","word":"sustain","meaning":"持续承担","tip":"此句用“sustain”（持续承担）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 犹豫","en":"The interviewer interprets hesitation as opposition, which is too strong.","zh":"采访者把犹豫解读成反对，程度过重。","word":"hesitation","meaning":"犹豫","tip":"此句用“hesitation”（犹豫）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 暗示","en":"Her answer implies a preference for improving access before expanding.","zh":"她的回答暗示应先改善可及性再扩张。","word":"implies","meaning":"暗示","tip":"此句用“implies”（暗示）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 声称","en":"She does not claim that nearby residents opposed every performance.","zh":"她没有声称附近居民反对每场演出。","word":"claim","meaning":"声称","tip":"此句用“claim”（声称）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 限定意见","en":"The qualification concerns evening noise rather than daytime attendance.","zh":"限定意见涉及晚间噪声，而非日间人数。","word":"qualification","meaning":"限定意见","tip":"此句用“qualification”（限定意见）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 语气","en":"A warm tone alone does not establish agreement with a proposal.","zh":"仅凭亲切语气不能认定赞同方案。","word":"tone","meaning":"语气","tip":"此句用“tone”（语气）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 推断","en":"We should support an inference with specific words and context.","zh":"我们应以具体措辞和上下文支持推断。","word":"inference","meaning":"推断","tip":"此句用“inference”（推断）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 协商","en":"She recommends consultation before making next year’s programme public.","zh":"她建议先协商，再公布明年的安排。","word":"consultation","meaning":"协商","tip":"此句用“consultation”（协商）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 断然拒绝","en":"Her position is conditional support, not an outright refusal.","zh":"她的立场是有条件支持，而非断然拒绝。","word":"outright refusal","meaning":"断然拒绝","tip":"此句用“outright refusal”（断然拒绝）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (不安的): The organiser sounds pleased with attendance but ___ about expansion.","answers":["uneasy"],"explanation":"原句用“uneasy”表达“不安的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (暗示): Her answer ___ a preference for improving access before expanding.","answers":["implies"],"explanation":"原句用“implies”表达“暗示”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of RESERVE: Her main ___ concerns volunteer capacity.","answers":["reservation"],"explanation":"空格需要“reservation”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: Her support depends on consultation with nearby residents. Required opening: She supports the plan provided …","answers":["She supports the plan provided that nearby residents are consulted."],"explanation":"参考答案：She supports the plan provided that nearby residents are consulted. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an interview analysis. Separate the organiser’s explicit statements from two supported inferences, and explain one interpretation the evidence does not justify.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The organiser welcomes the festival's popularity but does not endorse unlimited expansion. Her careful answers show a preference for consultation and practical support before next year's programme is made larger. The organiser wants residents to remain partners in the festival rather than become an audience for a decision already made. Her cautious phrases keep that possibility open. A reliable interpretation must retain both her pleasure at public interest and her concern that a larger programme would require stronger support.\n\nThe organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion. Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success. She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding. However, she questions whether the volunteer team could sustain it. The interviewer interprets hesitation as opposition, which is too strong. Her answer implies a preference for improving access before expanding.\n\nShe does not claim that nearby residents opposed every performance. The qualification concerns evening noise rather than daytime attendance. A warm tone alone does not establish agreement with a proposal. We should support an inference with specific words and context. She recommends consultation before making next year’s programme public. Her position is conditional support, not an outright refusal.\n\nIt would be too strong to describe her as opposed to the festival or committed to expansion. Conditional support best captures the balance between public interest and the capacity to deliver responsibly."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “An organiser reflects on a crowded festival”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I hear the organiser as cautiously supportive. She welcomes interest in the festival but wants the practical conditions for expansion to be discussed. The organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion. Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success. She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding. However, she questions whether the volunteer team could sustain it. I would support that inference with her qualifications, not just her tone. If challenged, I would distinguish what she explicitly states from what the surrounding context makes plausible but not certain."},"project":"完成“An organiser reflects on a crowded festival”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Thanks for agreeing to join the recording on Thursday. We are aiming for a conversation rather than a prepared speech, so please do not write out every answer. It would help if you could send two examples from your own work by Tuesday, especially one occasion when your first approach did not succeed. That is not a request to identify colleagues who made mistakes; you can remove names and sensitive details. The recording starts at eleven, but we will meet online at ten forty-five to check sound levels. Allow an hour altogether, even though the edited programme will last about twenty minutes. You will be able to correct factual errors before publication, but we cannot offer a full rewrite of the discussion. Let me know now if there are topics you are unable to discuss.","questions":[{"prompt":"What should the guest send?","answer":"Two work examples","options":["Two work examples","A complete scripted speech","Colleagues’ confidential records"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Two work examples”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"When is the sound check?","answer":"Ten forty-five","options":["Ten forty-five","Eleven fifteen","At publication"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Ten forty-five”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What review is offered?","answer":"Correction of factual errors","options":["Correction of factual errors","A complete rewrite","Automatic removal of all difficult questions"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Correction of factual errors”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":47,"title":"多人讨论：跟踪立场与共同决定","target":"C1","focus":"区分提议、修正、保留和共识","grammar":"转述立场；条件句；轮次衔接","prerequisite":"完成第46章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"A team negotiates a shared workspace","text":"Four members of a fictional cooperative discuss converting a storeroom into a shared workspace. The finance coordinator favours a small pilot, the designer wants flexible furniture, the access representative raises doorway clearance, and the caretaker explains maintenance constraints. Their first preferences differ, but the conversation is not a competition to find a single winning speaker. The aim is to identify a proposal everyone can accept without pretending that every concern has disappeared.\n\nA plan can look acceptable on a drawing while leaving a practical problem unresolved. For example, movable furniture still needs a safe storage position. The manager should ask participants to test the proposed arrangement in the room itself and report whether access, cleaning and rehearsal can coexist.\n\nThe finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion. The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks. The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed. The caretaker accepts the layout provided that cleaning remains practical. Nobody has agreed to remove the storage shelves permanently. The group distinguishes a temporary arrangement from a lasting commitment.\n\nI take your point about cost, but access is also essential. Could we combine the pilot with a review after six weeks? That would address maintenance without ruling out later improvements. The minutes should identify unresolved questions as well as agreements. Silence should not automatically be recorded as consent. Before closing, the chair checks who will carry out each action."},"sourceB":{"title":"Choosing a community cinema programme","text":"The programme group at a neighbourhood cinema met to choose a new monthly strand. Amira proposed recent international films, arguing that existing screenings offered little opportunity to hear other languages. Ben supported the idea but pointed out that some older members found small subtitles difficult to read. He was not objecting to international films; he wanted the presentation to remain accessible.\n\nClara suggested showing only films dubbed into English. Amira felt that this would remove part of the reason for creating the strand. Dev then proposed testing a larger subtitle display at one screening and collecting feedback afterwards. The technician confirmed that the display could be adjusted, although doing so would require an extra afternoon of preparation. Clara accepted the trial once transport home for the technician had been included in the budget.\n\nThe group agreed to start with three screenings, each followed by an optional discussion. They did not agree on a permanent programme or on the claim that every subtitled film would be suitable. Ben offered to invite members who had previously reported reading difficulties, ensuring that their experience informed the review. Amira would choose possible titles, but the whole group would make the final selection. The minutes recorded both the shared decision and its limits. This prevented later readers from mistaking agreement to a trial for approval of every detail."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion.","options":["The finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion.","It is not true that the finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks.","options":["The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks.","It is not true that the designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed.","options":["The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed.","It is not true that the access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was Ben’s concern?","answer":"Subtitle readability","options":["Subtitle readability","Opposition to other languages","The discussion being optional"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Choosing a community cinema programme”支持“Subtitle readability”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What compromise was accepted?","answer":"A larger-display trial","options":["A larger-display trial","Only dubbed films forever","Immediate permanent expansion"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Choosing a community cinema programme”支持“A larger-display trial”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Who makes the final title selection?","answer":"The whole group","options":["The whole group","Amira alone","The technician alone"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Choosing a community cinema programme”支持“The whole group”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 试点","en":"The finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion.","zh":"财务协调员支持试点，而非全面改造。","word":"pilot","meaning":"试点","tip":"此句用“pilot”（试点）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 在……基础上发展","en":"The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks.","zh":"设计师在此基础上提出可移动书桌。","word":"builds on","meaning":"在……基础上发展","tip":"此句用“builds on”（在……基础上发展）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 无阻挡的","en":"The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed.","zh":"无障碍代表询问门口是否保持畅通。","word":"unobstructed","meaning":"无阻挡的","tip":"此句用“unobstructed”（无阻挡的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 前提是","en":"The caretaker accepts the layout provided that cleaning remains practical.","zh":"管理员接受布局，前提是清洁仍然方便。","word":"provided that","meaning":"前提是","tip":"此句用“provided that”（前提是）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 永久地","en":"Nobody has agreed to remove the storage shelves permanently.","zh":"没有人同意永久拆除储物架。","word":"permanently","meaning":"永久地","tip":"此句用“permanently”（永久地）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 长期承诺","en":"The group distinguishes a temporary arrangement from a lasting commitment.","zh":"小组区分临时安排与长期承诺。","word":"lasting commitment","meaning":"长期承诺","tip":"此句用“lasting commitment”（长期承诺）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 理解你的观点","en":"I take your point about cost, but access is also essential.","zh":"我理解你关于费用的观点，但通行同样必要。","word":"take your point","meaning":"理解你的观点","tip":"此句用“take your point”（理解你的观点）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 结合","en":"Could we combine the pilot with a review after six weeks?","zh":"我们能否把试点与六周后复查结合？","word":"combine","meaning":"结合","tip":"此句用“combine”（结合）提出澄清或协商问题，让对方能具体回应。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 处理","en":"That would address maintenance without ruling out later improvements.","zh":"这能解决维护问题，同时不排除后续改善。","word":"address","meaning":"处理","tip":"此句用“address”（处理）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 未解决的","en":"The minutes should identify unresolved questions as well as agreements.","zh":"会议纪要应同时列明未解决问题和共识。","word":"unresolved","meaning":"未解决的","tip":"此句用“unresolved”（未解决的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 同意","en":"Silence should not automatically be recorded as consent.","zh":"沉默不应自动被记录为同意。","word":"consent","meaning":"同意","tip":"此句用“consent”（同意）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 执行","en":"Before closing, the chair checks who will carry out each action.","zh":"结束前，主持人核实每项行动的负责人。","word":"carry out","meaning":"执行","tip":"此句用“carry out”（执行）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (试点): The finance coordinator supports a ___ rather than full conversion.","answers":["pilot"],"explanation":"原句用“pilot”表达“试点”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (长期承诺): The group distinguishes a temporary arrangement from a ___.","answers":["lasting commitment"],"explanation":"原句用“lasting commitment”表达“长期承诺”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of AGREE: The minutes should record the limits of the ___.","answers":["agreement"],"explanation":"空格需要“agreement”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The caretaker accepts the layout only if cleaning remains practical. Required opening: The caretaker accepts the layout …","answers":["The caretaker accepts the layout provided that cleaning remains practical."],"explanation":"参考答案：The caretaker accepts the layout provided that cleaning remains practical. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write minutes and a short recommendation for the workspace discussion. Preserve each person’s concern and distinguish agreed actions from unresolved decisions.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The workspace discussion produced a possible pilot rather than agreement to permanent conversion. A useful record must preserve the finance, design, access and maintenance concerns that shaped the proposal. A plan can look acceptable on a drawing while leaving a practical problem unresolved. For example, movable furniture still needs a safe storage position. The manager should ask participants to test the proposed arrangement in the room itself and report whether access, cleaning and rehearsal can coexist.\n\nThe finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion. The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks. The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed. The caretaker accepts the layout provided that cleaning remains practical. Nobody has agreed to remove the storage shelves permanently. The group distinguishes a temporary arrangement from a lasting commitment.\n\nI take your point about cost, but access is also essential. Could we combine the pilot with a review after six weeks? That would address maintenance without ruling out later improvements. The minutes should identify unresolved questions as well as agreements. Silence should not automatically be recorded as consent. Before closing, the chair checks who will carry out each action.\n\nThe group should test the furniture arrangement in the room and review it after six weeks. The final minutes should assign actions without turning a provisional layout into an irreversible commitment."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “A team negotiates a shared workspace”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I would summarise the workspace discussion as agreement to explore a pilot, not consent to every proposed detail or a permanent conversion. The finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion. The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks. The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed. The caretaker accepts the layout provided that cleaning remains practical. The next step is to test the layout in the actual room. I would confirm the responsibilities and review date, while recording any concern that participants still consider unresolved."},"project":"完成“A team negotiates a shared workspace”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Let me check that we have the same understanding before we finish. We will test the new delivery route on Tuesday and Thursday, but we are not changing the permanent timetable yet. Sara will notify the drivers; Joel will contact the two customers whose collection times may move. The rest of the customer list does not need a message at this stage. We agreed to record late arrivals and the time spent loading, since a shorter distance does not necessarily mean a quicker round. Please enter the figures on the shared sheet before leaving work each day. We will meet again on Friday morning. If either trial clashes with an urgent order, call me rather than cancelling the test yourself. I will decide whether we can move it without affecting the comparison.","questions":[{"prompt":"Which days are used for the trial?","answer":"Tuesday and Thursday","options":["Tuesday and Thursday","Monday and Friday","Every weekday permanently"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Tuesday and Thursday”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What will Joel do?","answer":"Contact two affected customers","options":["Contact two affected customers","Notify all drivers","Change the permanent timetable"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Contact two affected customers”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What should happen if an urgent order clashes?","answer":"Call the team leader","options":["Call the team leader","Cancel the test independently","Ignore the urgent order"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Call the team leader”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":48,"title":"听力综合：重建信息而非照抄","target":"C1","focus":"综合讲座、访谈与讨论的结构","grammar":"摘要；转述；不确定信息标记","prerequisite":"完成第47章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Reviewing a shared repair scheme","text":"A fictional adult-learning centre is reviewing its repair scheme through a short briefing, an organiser’s interview and a planning discussion. Learners must combine the central points without suggesting that every speaker used identical language. Some statements describe what has already happened; others propose changes. A sound account should preserve that distinction, acknowledge gaps and make the next decision clear to a reader who did not attend the meetings.\n\nThe repair scheme may appear successful when completed jobs are displayed without records of items that could not be fixed. Reviewers need to know what was counted and why. Their next recommendation should connect the project's educational purpose with the practical resources needed to supervise volunteers safely.\n\nThe briefing reports participation, while the interview discusses practical limits. The planning discussion proposes a change that has not yet been approved. I would group the material by issue rather than by speaking order. The organiser’s reservation concerns staffing, not the purpose of repair. A summary should not turn an estimate into an observed result. The records show completed repairs but omit unsuccessful attempts.\n\nWe therefore cannot calculate success from these records alone. The centre needs a common definition of a completed repair. Different speakers agree on the aim but differ on the method. The next decision depends on the number of trained supervisors available. I would mark the missing information explicitly rather than guess. The recommendation should identify both a responsible person and a deadline."},"sourceB":{"title":"A neighbourhood energy meeting","text":"Residents at a housing block heard three contributions about replacing communal corridor lights. The building engineer explained that the existing fittings used unnecessary power, but cautioned that brighter replacements could cause glare at bedroom doors. A supplier then described a motion-sensitive system. Its demonstration showed how lights could dim when corridors were empty; it did not establish the annual saving in this particular building.\n\nDuring the discussion, one resident asked for immediate installation because bills had risen. Another worried about a delay before the lights reached full brightness. The chair summarised both concerns and proposed a trial on one floor. A resident with limited mobility asked to be included in testing, since walking speed might affect how the sensors responded. The engineer agreed that the trial should include different users and times of day.\n\nThe final decision was to obtain a written quotation and test the system for a fortnight. No contract for the entire building was signed. The minutes distinguished the supplier's estimate from a measured saving and assigned the engineer responsibility for checking glare and response time. Residents would receive a summary before voting on installation. A later newsletter incorrectly described the meeting as approval of the new system. The chair corrected that sentence, noting that approval to investigate an option was not approval to purchase it. The distinction mattered."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The briefing reports participation, while the interview discusses practical limits.","options":["The briefing reports participation, while the interview discusses practical limits.","It is not true that the briefing reports participation, while the interview discusses practical limits.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The briefing reports participation, while the interview discusses practical limits.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The planning discussion proposes a change that has not yet been approved.","options":["The planning discussion proposes a change that has not yet been approved.","It is not true that the planning discussion proposes a change that has not yet been approved.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The planning discussion proposes a change that has not yet been approved.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"I would group the material by issue rather than by speaking order.","options":["I would group the material by issue rather than by speaking order.","It is not true that i would group the material by issue rather than by speaking order.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“I would group the material by issue rather than by speaking order.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did the supplier’s demonstration establish?","answer":"How the dimming feature worked","options":["How the dimming feature worked","The building’s actual annual saving","That every resident approved"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A neighbourhood energy meeting”支持“How the dimming feature worked”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Who asked to join testing?","answer":"A resident with limited mobility","options":["A resident with limited mobility","Only the supplier’s staff","A visitor from another town"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A neighbourhood energy meeting”支持“A resident with limited mobility”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was actually approved?","answer":"A quotation and a two-week trial","options":["A quotation and a two-week trial","A whole-building contract","Immediate permanent installation"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A neighbourhood energy meeting”支持“A quotation and a two-week trial”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 而","en":"The briefing reports participation, while the interview discusses practical limits.","zh":"简报报告参与情况，访谈讨论实际限制。","word":"while","meaning":"而","tip":"此句用“while”（而）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 提出","en":"The planning discussion proposes a change that has not yet been approved.","zh":"规划讨论提出尚未获批的改变。","word":"propose","meaning":"提出","tip":"此句用“propose”（提出）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 按议题整理材料","en":"I would group the material by issue rather than by speaking order.","zh":"我会按议题，而非发言顺序整理材料。","word":"group the material by issue","meaning":"按议题整理材料","tip":"此句用“group the material by issue”（按议题整理材料）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 保留意见","en":"The organiser’s reservation concerns staffing, not the purpose of repair.","zh":"组织者的保留意见涉及人员，而非维修宗旨。","word":"reservation","meaning":"保留意见","tip":"此句用“reservation”（保留意见）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 估计","en":"A summary should not turn an estimate into an observed result.","zh":"摘要不应把估计写成观察结果。","word":"estimate","meaning":"估计","tip":"此句用“estimate”（估计）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 遗漏","en":"The records show completed repairs but omit unsuccessful attempts.","zh":"记录显示已完成维修，却遗漏失败尝试。","word":"omit","meaning":"遗漏","tip":"此句用“omit”（遗漏）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 仅凭","en":"We therefore cannot calculate success from these records alone.","zh":"因此，仅凭这些记录无法计算成功率。","word":"alone","meaning":"仅凭","tip":"此句用“alone”（仅凭）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 定义","en":"The centre needs a common definition of a completed repair.","zh":"中心需要对完成维修作出共同定义。","word":"definition","meaning":"定义","tip":"此句用“definition”（定义）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 在……上有分歧","en":"Different speakers agree on the aim but differ on the method.","zh":"不同发言者赞同目标，却对方法有分歧。","word":"differ on","meaning":"在……上有分歧","tip":"此句用“differ on”（在……上有分歧）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 可用的","en":"The next decision depends on the number of trained supervisors available.","zh":"下一项决定取决于可用的合格督导人数。","word":"available","meaning":"可用的","tip":"此句用“available”（可用的）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 明确地","en":"I would mark the missing information explicitly rather than guess.","zh":"我会明确标出缺失信息，而非猜测。","word":"explicitly","meaning":"明确地","tip":"此句用“explicitly”（明确地）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 负责的","en":"The recommendation should identify both a responsible person and a deadline.","zh":"建议应同时明确负责人和截止时间。","word":"responsible","meaning":"负责的","tip":"此句用“responsible”（负责的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (而): The briefing reports participation, ___ the interview discusses practical limits.","answers":["while"],"explanation":"原句用“while”表达“而”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (遗漏): The records show completed repairs but ___ unsuccessful attempts.","answers":["omit"],"explanation":"原句用“omit”表达“遗漏”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of INTERPRET: An ___ should not be presented as an observed result.","answers":["interpretation"],"explanation":"空格需要“interpretation”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The discussion proposed expansion, but it did not approve it. Required opening: Although the discussion proposed expansion, …","answers":["Although the discussion proposed expansion, it did not approve it."],"explanation":"参考答案：Although the discussion proposed expansion, it did not approve it. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a review of the repair scheme using the briefing. Separate results, interpretations and proposals; state what additional information is needed before expansion.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The repair scheme should be reviewed against its educational purpose and the resources needed for supervision. Existing accounts provide useful starting points, but they do not all measure the same outcome. The repair scheme may appear successful when completed jobs are displayed without records of items that could not be fixed. Reviewers need to know what was counted and why. Their next recommendation should connect the project's educational purpose with the practical resources needed to supervise volunteers safely.\n\nThe briefing reports participation, while the interview discusses practical limits. The planning discussion proposes a change that has not yet been approved. I would group the material by issue rather than by speaking order. The organiser’s reservation concerns staffing, not the purpose of repair. A summary should not turn an estimate into an observed result. The records show completed repairs but omit unsuccessful attempts.\n\nWe therefore cannot calculate success from these records alone. The centre needs a common definition of a completed repair. Different speakers agree on the aim but differ on the method. The next decision depends on the number of trained supervisors available. I would mark the missing information explicitly rather than guess. The recommendation should identify both a responsible person and a deadline.\n\nBefore expansion, the centre should agree a definition of completion and record unsuccessful attempts as well. This would make the next review more useful without pretending that current gaps can be filled by guesswork."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Reviewing a shared repair scheme”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"The repair review needs to distinguish what happened, what people inferred and what they now propose. Otherwise, the next decision rests on confusion. The briefing reports participation, while the interview discusses practical limits. The planning discussion proposes a change that has not yet been approved. I would group the material by issue rather than by speaking order. The organiser’s reservation concerns staffing, not the purpose of repair. I would improve the records before expansion, especially the definition of completion. That gives the centre something testable and avoids treating a confident summary as a substitute for missing evidence."},"project":"完成“Reviewing a shared repair scheme”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"For tomorrow's archive visit, meet inside the reception area at nine twenty. The building opens at nine, but the researcher will not be available until nine thirty, so there is no advantage in arriving extremely early. Bags must be left in lockers; pencils and a laptop are allowed in the reading room. Please do not bring a pen to the document tables. We originally planned to examine newspapers, but those volumes are undergoing conservation. Instead, we will compare photographs with council minutes from the same decade. Photography is permitted only after staff have checked the document's condition and rights status. Ask before taking a picture, even if another item was approved. The session ends at twelve. Anyone needing extra research time can book an afternoon place separately, subject to availability.","questions":[{"prompt":"What material is replacing newspapers?","answer":"Photographs and council minutes","options":["Photographs and council minutes","Only modern magazines","Private audio recordings"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Photographs and council minutes”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What writing tool is allowed?","answer":"A pencil","options":["A pencil","A pen at document tables","Any permanent marker"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“A pencil”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"When may a photograph be taken?","answer":"After staff approve that item","options":["After staff approve that item","Whenever another item was approved","Only after buying every document"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“After staff approve that item”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":49,"title":"长篇评论：辨认论点、证据与修辞","target":"C1","focus":"识别作者立场并评价支持力度","grammar":"让步与反驳；修辞问题；论证连接","prerequisite":"完成第48章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Who owns the public square?","text":"A fictional local commentary examines a plan to lease part of a public square to a café. The author recognises that seating and maintenance could improve the space, but questions whether commercial activity should become the default test of public value. Readers should follow the argument beyond the opening contrast: the article considers practical arrangements as well as principles. Its purpose is to invite a better agreement rather than to reject every partnership with a business.\n\nThe central question is whether people retain a right to occupy the square without buying anything. A lease could address that explicitly through marked public seating and enforceable hours. The author asks readers to consider these terms, rather than assume that every improvement funded by a business necessarily benefits every user.\n\nThe author concedes that café income could support maintenance. Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself. The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource. A rhetorical question invites readers to reconsider an assumption. The example illustrates a risk without proving it will occur everywhere. The proposed agreement would protect seating for non-customers.\n\nPublic value cannot be reduced to the amount of money spent. Nevertheless, funding constraints deserve a practical response. The conclusion qualifies the opening criticism rather than abandoning it. I find the access argument stronger than the prediction about noise. The prediction needs evidence about hours, layout and expected use. A fair response should engage with the author’s strongest reason."},"sourceB":{"title":"The price of a convenient museum visit","text":"A city museum is considering timed entry for every visitor. Supporters argue that advance reservations would reduce queues and make staffing more predictable. Those benefits are real, especially for visitors who cannot stand for long periods. Yet convenience for one group can create a new barrier for another. Someone without reliable internet access may find an apparently free museum harder to enter.\n\nThe strongest case against universal booking is therefore not nostalgia for an untidy queue. It is the possibility that an administrative improvement will quietly change who feels welcome. A visitor who decides to enter while walking past should not automatically be treated as a planning failure. Museums serve curiosity as well as organised educational visits. On the other hand, unlimited walk-in entry cannot be promised when a building has a safe occupancy limit.\n\nA reasonable compromise would reserve some capacity for advance bookings and retain a clearly advertised walk-in allocation. The proportions should be reviewed using actual patterns of demand, including the experience of visitors who were turned away. Simply counting successful bookings would conceal that group. The museum should also provide a telephone route for reservations, although a telephone service alone does not remove every obstacle. A policy deserves to be called convenient only after asking whose time and opportunities it improves. The aim should be fairer access within a real capacity constraint, rather than a beautifully orderly booking system considered in isolation."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The author concedes that café income could support maintenance.","options":["The author concedes that café income could support maintenance.","It is not true that the author concedes that café income could support maintenance.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The author concedes that café income could support maintenance.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself.","options":["Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself.","It is not true that her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource.","options":["The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource.","It is not true that the opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What is the central objection to universal booking?","answer":"It may exclude some potential visitors","options":["It may exclude some potential visitors","Queues are always educational","Museums should ignore safe capacity"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“The price of a convenient museum visit”支持“It may exclude some potential visitors”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What compromise is proposed?","answer":"Advance booking plus a walk-in allocation","options":["Advance booking plus a walk-in allocation","Paid entry only","No occupancy limit"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“The price of a convenient museum visit”支持“Advance booking plus a walk-in allocation”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Whose experience should the review include?","answer":"People who were turned away","options":["People who were turned away","Only successful online bookers","Only museum staff"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“The price of a convenient museum visit”支持“People who were turned away”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 承认","en":"The author concedes that café income could support maintenance.","zh":"作者承认咖啡馆收入可能支持维护。","word":"concedes","meaning":"承认","tip":"此句用“concedes”（承认）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 排他的","en":"Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself.","zh":"她反对的是排他控制，而非咖啡本身。","word":"exclusive","meaning":"排他的","tip":"此句用“exclusive”（排他的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 界定","en":"The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource.","zh":"开篇对比把广场界定为共享资源。","word":"frame","meaning":"界定","tip":"此句用“frame”（界定）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 修辞问句","en":"A rhetorical question invites readers to reconsider an assumption.","zh":"修辞问句促使读者重新考虑假设。","word":"rhetorical question","meaning":"修辞问句","tip":"此句用“rhetorical question”（修辞问句）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 说明","en":"The example illustrates a risk without proving it will occur everywhere.","zh":"例子说明风险，却不能证明它处处发生。","word":"illustrate","meaning":"说明","tip":"此句用“illustrate”（说明）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 非顾客","en":"The proposed agreement would protect seating for non-customers.","zh":"建议中的协议会保障非顾客的座位。","word":"non-customers","meaning":"非顾客","tip":"此句用“non-customers”（非顾客）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 被简化为","en":"Public value cannot be reduced to the amount of money spent.","zh":"公共价值不能简化为消费金额。","word":"be reduced to","meaning":"被简化为","tip":"此句用“be reduced to”（被简化为）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 尽管如此","en":"Nevertheless, funding constraints deserve a practical response.","zh":"尽管如此，资金限制仍需实际回应。","word":"nevertheless","meaning":"尽管如此","tip":"此句用“nevertheless”（尽管如此）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 限定","en":"The conclusion qualifies the opening criticism rather than abandoning it.","zh":"结论限定开头的批评，而非放弃批评。","word":"qualifies","meaning":"限定","tip":"此句用“qualifies”（限定）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 预测","en":"I find the access argument stronger than the prediction about noise.","zh":"我认为可及性论点强于噪声预测。","word":"prediction","meaning":"预测","tip":"此句用“prediction”（预测）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 预计使用情况","en":"The prediction needs evidence about hours, layout and expected use.","zh":"该预测需要营业时间、布局和预计使用情况的证据。","word":"expected use","meaning":"预计使用情况","tip":"此句用“expected use”（预计使用情况）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 认真回应","en":"A fair response should engage with the author’s strongest reason.","zh":"公允回应应处理作者最有力的理由。","word":"engage with","meaning":"认真回应","tip":"此句用“engage with”（认真回应）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (承认): The author ___ that café income could support maintenance.","answers":["concedes"],"explanation":"原句用“concedes”表达“承认”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (非顾客): The proposed agreement would protect seating for ___.","answers":["non-customers"],"explanation":"原句用“non-customers”表达“非顾客”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of EXCLUSIVE: The author questions the ___ of commercial control.","answers":["exclusivity"],"explanation":"空格需要“exclusivity”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The author recognises the funding benefit but questions exclusive control. Required opening: Although the author recognises the …","answers":["Although the author recognises the funding benefit, she questions exclusive control."],"explanation":"参考答案：Although the author recognises the funding benefit, she questions exclusive control. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a critical response to the square commentary. Reconstruct the strongest argument, evaluate its evidence and propose a condition under which a café partnership could be acceptable.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The commentary's strongest argument concerns continued public access to a shared square. I support a café partnership only if the agreement protects the interests of people who do not buy anything. The central question is whether people retain a right to occupy the square without buying anything. A lease could address that explicitly through marked public seating and enforceable hours. The author asks readers to consider these terms, rather than assume that every improvement funded by a business necessarily benefits every user.\n\nThe author concedes that café income could support maintenance. Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself. The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource. A rhetorical question invites readers to reconsider an assumption. The example illustrates a risk without proving it will occur everywhere. The proposed agreement would protect seating for non-customers.\n\nPublic value cannot be reduced to the amount of money spent. Nevertheless, funding constraints deserve a practical response. The conclusion qualifies the opening criticism rather than abandoning it. I find the access argument stronger than the prediction about noise. The prediction needs evidence about hours, layout and expected use. A fair response should engage with the author’s strongest reason.\n\nThe lease should therefore specify access, seating and enforcement. If these protections cannot be maintained, the funding benefit would not by itself justify transferring control over a public resource."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Who owns the public square?”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I support improving the square, but the agreement should protect people who want to use it without becoming customers of the café. The author concedes that café income could support maintenance. Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself. The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource. A rhetorical question invites readers to reconsider an assumption. I would ask for enforceable public seating and clear hours. If those protections could not be delivered, the promise of maintenance funding would not settle the access objection for me."},"project":"完成“Who owns the public square?”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Our next book-group meeting will focus on the writer's argument rather than on whether we agree with the conclusion. Please bring one passage that you think contains a strong reason and one that you find less convincing. You do not have to finish the final chapter to take part, although it will help you understand the author's response to criticism. We will meet at the usual library table at seven on Wednesday. The discussion leader is away, so I will keep time and invite people to speak. Please limit your opening comment to two minutes so that everyone can contribute. We are not voting for the best book this month. Instead, we will produce a short list of questions to send to the visiting author before her talk in December.","questions":[{"prompt":"What should members bring?","answer":"A strong and a less convincing passage","options":["A strong and a less convincing passage","A vote for the best book","A complete written review"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“A strong and a less convincing passage”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"Who will keep time?","answer":"The speaker of the invitation","options":["The speaker of the invitation","The absent discussion leader","The visiting author"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The speaker of the invitation”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What will the group produce?","answer":"Questions for the author","options":["Questions for the author","A sales ranking","A new final chapter"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Questions for the author”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":50,"title":"科普阅读：理解机制与研究限制","target":"C1","focus":"解释因果链并区分相关与因果","grammar":"因果连接；被动结构；谨慎归因","prerequisite":"完成第49章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Why stored rainwater needs monitoring","text":"An original fictional science briefing explains a school rainwater project. The school collects roof runoff for garden irrigation, not for drinking. The article follows the path from roof to storage tank and identifies where leaves, sediment and sunlight could affect water quality. Its educational aim is to connect a physical mechanism with an appropriate monitoring plan. No health claim is made about untested water, and the case should not be treated as engineering advice.\n\nMonitoring should follow the route the water actually takes, rather than rely on its appearance at one outlet. The school can use the project to explain filtration, storage and measurement limits. Any description of results must remain within the intended irrigation use and must not imply that visual clarity establishes safety.\n\nLeaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank. Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear. An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water. A plausible mechanism must be distinguished from a measured effect. The tank supplies irrigation water rather than drinking water. The team records rainfall because weather affects the observations.\n\nA single clear sample cannot establish quality throughout the tank. Repeated measurements reveal variation over time. The explanation follows the sequence from collection to storage. We should identify which link in the causal chain was tested. The conclusion remains limited to the conditions actually observed. Further testing is required before any wider claim is justified."},"sourceB":{"title":"Measuring how a green roof behaves","text":"A college installed a planted roof above a low teaching building and compared its summer temperatures with those of a nearby bare roof. On sunny afternoons, the planted surface was often cooler. Soil and vegetation can influence temperature through shading, stored moisture and evaporation. However, those possible mechanisms did not mean that the small comparison had isolated the contribution of each one.\n\nThe buildings differed in age and insulation, and their roof surfaces were not monitored at exactly the same height. These differences mattered when interpreting indoor temperatures. A cooler roof surface did not automatically imply that students used less cooling energy inside. The facilities team therefore resisted a headline claiming that the installation had cut the college's energy bill. No separate energy measurement for the two buildings was available.\n\nFor the next summer, sensors would be placed at standard heights and checked against each other before installation. The team also planned to record soil moisture, rainfall and maintenance activity. Those records might explain why the temperature difference changed from week to week. They would not turn the existing buildings into perfectly matched experimental units. The students writing the project report decided to distinguish an observed pattern, a plausible mechanism and an untested wider benefit. Their recommendation was to improve measurement while continuing routine care of the plants, rather than claim that one striking afternoon established the long-term value of all green roofs."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"Leaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank.","options":["Leaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank.","It is not true that leaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Leaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear.","options":["Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear.","It is not true that sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water.","options":["An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water.","It is not true that an opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What pattern was observed?","answer":"The planted surface was often cooler on sunny afternoons","options":["The planted surface was often cooler on sunny afternoons","Every indoor room used less energy","The roofs had identical insulation"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Measuring how a green roof behaves”支持“The planted surface was often cooler on sunny afternoons”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why was an energy-saving headline rejected?","answer":"Separate energy measurements were unavailable","options":["Separate energy measurements were unavailable","Plants cannot affect temperature","The roof had no sensors at all"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Measuring how a green roof behaves”支持“Separate energy measurements were unavailable”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What will be standardised next summer?","answer":"Sensor heights and pre-installation checks","options":["Sensor heights and pre-installation checks","The age of both buildings","Every weather condition"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Measuring how a green roof behaves”支持“Sensor heights and pre-installation checks”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 被拦截","en":"Leaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank.","zh":"水进入储水箱前，树叶会被拦截。","word":"intercepted","meaning":"被拦截","tip":"此句用“intercepted”（被拦截）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 沉积物","en":"Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear.","zh":"即使流入的水看起来清澈，沉积物仍可能累积。","word":"sediment","meaning":"沉积物","tip":"此句用“sediment”（沉积物）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 不透明的","en":"An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water.","zh":"不透明盖子限制照入水中的光。","word":"opaque","meaning":"不透明的","tip":"此句用“opaque”（不透明的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 机制","en":"A plausible mechanism must be distinguished from a measured effect.","zh":"合理机制必须与测得的效果区分。","word":"mechanism","meaning":"机制","tip":"此句用“mechanism”（机制）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 灌溉","en":"The tank supplies irrigation water rather than drinking water.","zh":"水箱供应灌溉水，而非饮用水。","word":"irrigation","meaning":"灌溉","tip":"此句用“irrigation”（灌溉）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 观察结果","en":"The team records rainfall because weather affects the observations.","zh":"团队记录降雨，因为天气影响观察结果。","word":"observations","meaning":"观察结果","tip":"此句用“observations”（观察结果）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 遍及","en":"A single clear sample cannot establish quality throughout the tank.","zh":"单个清澈样本无法证明整个水箱的水质。","word":"throughout","meaning":"遍及","tip":"此句用“throughout”（遍及）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 变化","en":"Repeated measurements reveal variation over time.","zh":"重复测量揭示随时间发生的变化。","word":"variation","meaning":"变化","tip":"此句用“variation”（变化）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 顺序","en":"The explanation follows the sequence from collection to storage.","zh":"解释按从收集到储存的顺序展开。","word":"sequence","meaning":"顺序","tip":"此句用“sequence”（顺序）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 因果链","en":"We should identify which link in the causal chain was tested.","zh":"我们应明确因果链中的哪个环节接受了测试。","word":"causal chain","meaning":"因果链","tip":"此句用“causal chain”（因果链）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 限于","en":"The conclusion remains limited to the conditions actually observed.","zh":"结论仍限于实际观察的条件。","word":"limited to","meaning":"限于","tip":"此句用“limited to”（限于）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 有依据的","en":"Further testing is required before any wider claim is justified.","zh":"在更广泛主张成立前，还需进一步测试。","word":"justified","meaning":"有依据的","tip":"此句用“justified”（有依据的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (被拦截): Leaves are ___ before water enters the storage tank.","answers":["intercepted"],"explanation":"原句用“intercepted”表达“被拦截”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (观察结果): The team records rainfall because weather affects the ___.","answers":["observations"],"explanation":"原句用“observations”表达“观察结果”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of VARY: Repeated measurements help identify ___ over time.","answers":["variation"],"explanation":"空格需要“variation”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The water is used for irrigation, not for drinking. Required opening: The water is used for …","answers":["The water is used for irrigation rather than drinking."],"explanation":"参考答案：The water is used for irrigation rather than drinking. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an explanatory article about the rainwater case. Trace a mechanism, identify what should be measured and distinguish observed evidence from a possible benefit.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The rainwater project offers a useful way to explain collection, storage and monitoring. Its scope must remain clear: the school intends to irrigate its garden, not demonstrate that collected water is drinkable. Monitoring should follow the route the water actually takes, rather than rely on its appearance at one outlet. The school can use the project to explain filtration, storage and measurement limits. Any description of results must remain within the intended irrigation use and must not imply that visual clarity establishes safety.\n\nLeaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank. Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear. An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water. A plausible mechanism must be distinguished from a measured effect. The tank supplies irrigation water rather than drinking water. The team records rainfall because weather affects the observations.\n\nA single clear sample cannot establish quality throughout the tank. Repeated measurements reveal variation over time. The explanation follows the sequence from collection to storage. We should identify which link in the causal chain was tested. The conclusion remains limited to the conditions actually observed. Further testing is required before any wider claim is justified.\n\nThe educational value lies partly in learning what measurements cannot establish. The final account should retain those limits and avoid extending observations about irrigation storage into unsupported claims about safety."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Why stored rainwater needs monitoring”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"The rainwater case is useful because it connects a physical route with measurement. It also shows why an attractive appearance is not enough. Leaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank. Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear. An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water. A plausible mechanism must be distinguished from a measured effect. The monitoring plan should follow the water's route and record changes over time. I would make clear that this educational irrigation project does not establish whether untreated water is safe to drink."},"project":"完成“Why stored rainwater needs monitoring”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"During tomorrow's induction, we will demonstrate the water-testing equipment, but you will not be collecting field samples yet. Meet at laboratory three at two o'clock, wearing closed shoes. Coats will be provided. The first demonstration uses clean practice water so that you can learn the procedure without dealing with unknown material. Label the container before taking a reading, and record the unit as well as the number. If the display changes unexpectedly, ask the technician to check the setup instead of repeating measurements until a preferred result appears. We will discuss field collection next week, after everyone has completed the safety briefing. The worksheet must be handed in before you leave, but it is a practice record, not a graded examination. You may work in pairs; each person should nevertheless keep their own notes.","questions":[{"prompt":"What water is used first?","answer":"Clean practice water","options":["Clean practice water","Unknown field samples","Drinking water from volunteers"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Clean practice water”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What must accompany the number?","answer":"The measurement unit","options":["The measurement unit","A preferred conclusion","Another person’s grade"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The measurement unit”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What should an unexpected display prompt?","answer":"A technician’s check","options":["A technician’s check","Repeated measurement until a desired result","Immediate field collection"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“A technician’s check”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":51,"title":"比较来源：追踪定义与利益相关方","target":"C1","focus":"比较范围、方法、指标和立场","grammar":"比较结构；归因；范围限定","prerequisite":"完成第50章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Two ways to describe a cycle route","text":"A fictional transport team is preparing a public briefing on a protected cycle route. It has a rider survey and a set of junction observations. The sources answer different questions: the survey describes reported experience, while the observations record behaviour at selected locations and times. Neither source is useless, but neither should be asked to establish everything. The briefing needs to show what can be compared fairly and where uncertainty remains.\n\nThe team should begin by writing the precise question above each result. A statement about comfort, a count of riders and an observation of turning behaviour can all be valuable without measuring the same thing. Understanding that difference prevents an attractive headline from replacing a careful account of the evidence.\n\nThe rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk. Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week. Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators. A source may be accurate within a narrow scope. Different definitions can create an apparent contradiction. The team should attribute each claim to the relevant evidence.\n\nAn interested organisation can still provide useful data. Its role is a reason for scrutiny, not automatic dismissal. The two sources overlap without being interchangeable. Missing evening observations limit conclusions about night-time use. We should report uncertainty instead of forcing agreement. A stronger briefing combines findings while preserving their boundaries."},"sourceB":{"title":"Why two apprenticeship reports disagree","text":"A training provider announced that almost all participants in its apprenticeship programme had completed successfully. An independent community group published a much lower completion figure. At first glance, the reports appeared irreconcilable. Examination of their definitions showed that they counted different populations. The provider included people who reached the final assessment; the community group included everyone who enrolled at the start of the year.\n\nThe provider's figure could describe assessment success among the final candidates, but it could not reveal how many apprentices had left earlier. The community group's broader figure addressed retention, yet it combined voluntary departures with cases in which employers had closed. That distinction mattered when identifying what the training provider could reasonably improve. Neither report alone established the quality of teaching or the experience of apprentices who remained.\n\nA joint meeting produced a more transparent reporting plan. Future tables would show initial enrolment, departures with recorded reasons, candidates reaching assessment and final passes. Where a reason was unknown, the table would say so rather than assign blame. Both organisations would also invite anonymous accounts from current and former apprentices. Those accounts could suggest problems worth investigating, although they would not constitute a representative satisfaction measure without further sampling. The disagreement proved useful once the reports were treated as partial views of a process rather than rival slogans. A clear denominator was more informative than deciding which headline sounded more impressive."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk.","options":["The rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk.","It is not true that the rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week.","options":["Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week.","It is not true that junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators.","options":["Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators.","It is not true that before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why did completion figures differ?","answer":"They counted different populations","options":["They counted different populations","One counted only employers","Both used identical definitions"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Why two apprenticeship reports disagree”支持“They counted different populations”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did the provider’s figure describe?","answer":"Passes among final assessment candidates","options":["Passes among final assessment candidates","Retention of every enrollee","Teaching quality by itself"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Why two apprenticeship reports disagree”支持“Passes among final assessment candidates”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"How will unknown departure reasons be reported?","answer":"Explicitly as unknown","options":["Explicitly as unknown","Automatically as teaching failures","Automatically as employer closures"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Why two apprenticeship reports disagree”支持“Explicitly as unknown”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 自报体验","en":"The rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk.","zh":"骑行者调查衡量自报体验，而非碰撞风险。","word":"reported experience","meaning":"自报体验","tip":"此句用“reported experience”（自报体验）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 选定的","en":"Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week.","zh":"路口观察覆盖选定时段，而非整周。","word":"selected","meaning":"选定的","tip":"此句用“selected”（选定的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 分母","en":"Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators.","zh":"比较百分比前，我们需要查看分母。","word":"denominator","meaning":"分母","tip":"此句用“denominator”（分母）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 范围","en":"A source may be accurate within a narrow scope.","zh":"来源在狭窄范围内也可能准确。","word":"scope","meaning":"范围","tip":"此句用“scope”（范围）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 矛盾","en":"Different definitions can create an apparent contradiction.","zh":"不同定义可能造成表面矛盾。","word":"contradiction","meaning":"矛盾","tip":"此句用“contradiction”（矛盾）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 归于","en":"The team should attribute each claim to the relevant evidence.","zh":"团队应将每项主张归于相应证据。","word":"attribute","meaning":"归于","tip":"此句用“attribute”（归于）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 有利益关系的组织","en":"An interested organisation can still provide useful data.","zh":"有利益关系的组织仍可提供有用数据。","word":"interested organisation","meaning":"有利益关系的组织","tip":"此句用“interested organisation”（有利益关系的组织）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 仔细审查","en":"Its role is a reason for scrutiny, not automatic dismissal.","zh":"其角色是审查理由，而非自动否定的理由。","word":"scrutiny","meaning":"仔细审查","tip":"此句用“scrutiny”（仔细审查）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 可互换的","en":"The two sources overlap without being interchangeable.","zh":"两个来源有交集，但不能互换。","word":"interchangeable","meaning":"可互换的","tip":"此句用“interchangeable”（可互换的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 限制","en":"Missing evening observations limit conclusions about night-time use.","zh":"缺少晚间观察限制了对夜间使用的结论。","word":"limit","meaning":"限制","tip":"此句用“limit”（限制）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 强求一致","en":"We should report uncertainty instead of forcing agreement.","zh":"我们应报告不确定性，而非强求一致。","word":"forcing agreement","meaning":"强求一致","tip":"此句用“forcing agreement”（强求一致）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 边界","en":"A stronger briefing combines findings while preserving their boundaries.","zh":"更好的简报整合结果，同时保留各自边界。","word":"boundaries","meaning":"边界","tip":"此句用“boundaries”（边界）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (自报体验): The rider survey measures ___ rather than collision risk.","answers":["reported experience"],"explanation":"原句用“reported experience”表达“自报体验”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (归于): The team should ___ each claim to the relevant evidence.","answers":["attribute"],"explanation":"原句用“attribute”表达“归于”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of COMPARE: A fair ___ requires compatible definitions.","answers":["comparison"],"explanation":"空格需要“comparison”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The sources overlap, but they cannot be exchanged freely. Required opening: Although the sources overlap, they …","answers":["Although the sources overlap, they are not interchangeable."],"explanation":"参考答案：Although the sources overlap, they are not interchangeable. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a source-evaluation briefing for the cycle-route team. Explain which questions each source can answer and design an additional check for a remaining gap.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The cycle-route briefing should present the rider survey and junction observations as complementary sources. Their different scopes are not a reason to discard either, but they prevent a simple merging of conclusions. The team should begin by writing the precise question above each result. A statement about comfort, a count of riders and an observation of turning behaviour can all be valuable without measuring the same thing. Understanding that difference prevents an attractive headline from replacing a careful account of the evidence.\n\nThe rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk. Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week. Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators. A source may be accurate within a narrow scope. Different definitions can create an apparent contradiction. The team should attribute each claim to the relevant evidence.\n\nAn interested organisation can still provide useful data. Its role is a reason for scrutiny, not automatic dismissal. The two sources overlap without being interchangeable. Missing evening observations limit conclusions about night-time use. We should report uncertainty instead of forcing agreement. A stronger briefing combines findings while preserving their boundaries.\n\nI would collect evening observations and clarify the survey's coverage before making a wider claim. The final recommendation can draw on both sources while stating which questions remain unanswered."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Two ways to describe a cycle route”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I would keep the two cycle-route sources separate at first, asking exactly which question each can answer before trying to combine their findings. The rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk. Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week. Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators. A source may be accurate within a narrow scope. After that comparison, I would look for the most important gap, such as evening use. The final judgement should preserve each source's limits instead of forcing them to support one headline."},"project":"完成“Two ways to describe a cycle route”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"When you interview market visitors on Saturday, please use the revised opening question on the blue sheet. The old version asked whether people liked the market; the new one first asks how often they visit. That distinction is important because we want to compare regular visitors with occasional ones. Do not interview only the people waiting at the coffee stall. Move between the three marked locations according to the rota, even if one seems less busy. If someone declines, thank them and record the refusal without guessing their age or reason. Each interview should take about four minutes. We are collecting opinions, not selling memberships, so leave the promotional leaflets at the information desk. At the end, return both completed forms and the sheet showing the number of people approached.","questions":[{"prompt":"What does the opening question now ask?","answer":"How often people visit","options":["How often people visit","Whether they want membership","Their exact age"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“How often people visit”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"Why follow the location rota?","answer":"To avoid interviewing only one convenient group","options":["To avoid interviewing only one convenient group","To sell more coffee","To finish without recording refusals"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“To avoid interviewing only one convenient group”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What must accompany completed forms?","answer":"The count of people approached","options":["The count of people approached","Promotional leaflets","Guessed reasons for every refusal"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The count of people approached”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":52,"title":"多来源综合：形成自己的有据判断","target":"C1","focus":"合并证据并明确推论边界","grammar":"综合摘要；逻辑衔接；间接引语","prerequisite":"完成第51章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"A balanced plan for a learning garden","text":"A fictional college wants to turn unused land into a learning garden. Its planning dossier combines students' requests, a caretaker's maintenance estimate and a teacher's ideas for practical lessons. The writer must develop a recommendation from these different perspectives without inventing consensus. A garden could serve several purposes, but the plan has to make trade-offs visible. The report will go to a committee that has not read the original discussion notes.\n\nThe garden proposal should state which activities have priority when space or supervision is limited. Access to a raised bed means little if nobody is available to support a planned class. A phased arrangement allows the committee to investigate real demand while avoiding a promise that every requested activity will begin immediately.\n\nThe requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity. The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds. That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved. The teacher proposes practical lessons rather than unrestricted public access. These purposes can coexist if responsibilities are clearly assigned. I recommend a smaller first phase with accessible raised beds.\n\nThe report should acknowledge the opportunity cost of using the land. A synthesis explains relationships between sources instead of listing summaries. Where accounts differ, I would identify the underlying question. The committee needs a decision rule for continuing the project. My conclusion is provisional because summer staffing remains uncertain. The next report should show what happened as well as what was planned."},"sourceB":{"title":"Combining evidence about a late bus","text":"A neighbourhood forum wanted to know whether the final bus should leave the station later. An online petition described passengers stranded after evening shifts. Ticket records, however, showed few people using the current final departure. Some councillors treated the figures as a reason to reject the request. The forum pointed out that people finishing work after that departure could not appear in its passenger count.\n\nA short employer survey found several workplaces with shifts ending late, but managers could not say how many employees would choose the bus. A trial was therefore proposed on two evenings each week. The transport operator warned that a later departure would require an additional paid driver period, rather than merely moving an empty vehicle. A grant could support the trial but would not fund the service indefinitely.\n\nThe joint report recommended a six-week test with a published end date and clear measures: passenger numbers, operating cost, connection reliability and users' reported alternatives. It also proposed contacting workers through employers without requiring them to disclose personal travel histories publicly. The petition established that a problem was being raised, not its exact scale. Existing ticket records described current use, not demand for a service that did not yet exist. By preserving these distinctions, the report supported an experiment while declining to promise a permanent bus. The committee asked for the trial criteria to be agreed before publicity began."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity.","options":["The requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity.","It is not true that the requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds.","options":["The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds.","It is not true that the caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved.","options":["That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved.","It is not true that that assumption should be checked before the budget is approved.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why were current ticket figures insufficient?","answer":"They excluded people finishing after the final departure","options":["They excluded people finishing after the final departure","They included imaginary passengers","They measured only private cars"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Combining evidence about a late bus”支持“They excluded people finishing after the final departure”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What additional cost was identified?","answer":"An extra paid driver period","options":["An extra paid driver period","A new railway station","A permanent new bus depot"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Combining evidence about a late bus”支持“An extra paid driver period”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"When should trial criteria be agreed?","answer":"Before publicity begins","options":["Before publicity begins","Only after deciding to retain the bus","After the grant ends"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Combining evidence about a late bus”支持“Before publicity begins”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 能力","en":"The requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity.","zh":"请求说明理想用途，但未证明维护能力。","word":"capacity","meaning":"能力","tip":"此句用“capacity”（能力）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 假设","en":"The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds.","zh":"管理员的估算假设志愿者会给种植床浇水。","word":"assume","meaning":"假设","tip":"此句用“assume”（假设）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 假设","en":"That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved.","zh":"预算获批前应核实该假设。","word":"assumption","meaning":"假设","tip":"此句用“assumption”（假设）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 无限制的","en":"The teacher proposes practical lessons rather than unrestricted public access.","zh":"教师建议开展实践课，而非无限制公众开放。","word":"unrestricted","meaning":"无限制的","tip":"此句用“unrestricted”（无限制的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 共存","en":"These purposes can coexist if responsibilities are clearly assigned.","zh":"如果职责分配清楚，这些用途可以共存。","word":"coexist","meaning":"共存","tip":"此句用“coexist”（共存）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 第一阶段","en":"I recommend a smaller first phase with accessible raised beds.","zh":"我建议第一阶段规模较小，采用便于使用的高架种植床。","word":"first phase","meaning":"第一阶段","tip":"此句用“first phase”（第一阶段）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 机会成本","en":"The report should acknowledge the opportunity cost of using the land.","zh":"报告应承认使用这块地的机会成本。","word":"opportunity cost","meaning":"机会成本","tip":"此句用“opportunity cost”（机会成本）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 综合分析","en":"A synthesis explains relationships between sources instead of listing summaries.","zh":"综合分析解释来源之间的关系，而非罗列摘要。","word":"synthesis","meaning":"综合分析","tip":"此句用“synthesis”（综合分析）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 背后的","en":"Where accounts differ, I would identify the underlying question.","zh":"当说法不一致时，我会找出背后的问题。","word":"underlying","meaning":"背后的","tip":"此句用“underlying”（背后的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 决策规则","en":"The committee needs a decision rule for continuing the project.","zh":"委员会需要决定是否继续项目的规则。","word":"decision rule","meaning":"决策规则","tip":"此句用“decision rule”（决策规则）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 暂定的","en":"My conclusion is provisional because summer staffing remains uncertain.","zh":"我的结论是暂定的，因为暑期人员仍不确定。","word":"provisional","meaning":"暂定的","tip":"此句用“provisional”（暂定的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 以及","en":"The next report should show what happened as well as what was planned.","zh":"下一份报告既应说明计划，也应说明实际情况。","word":"as well as","meaning":"以及","tip":"此句用“as well as”（以及）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (能力): The requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance ___.","answers":["capacity"],"explanation":"原句用“capacity”表达“能力”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (第一阶段): I recommend a smaller ___ with accessible raised beds.","answers":["first phase"],"explanation":"原句用“first phase”表达“第一阶段”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of RECOMMEND: The committee needs a clearly justified ___.","answers":["recommendation"],"explanation":"空格需要“recommendation”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The first phase can begin only if maintenance responsibilities are assigned. Required opening: The first phase can begin …","answers":["The first phase can begin provided that maintenance responsibilities are assigned."],"explanation":"参考答案：The first phase can begin provided that maintenance responsibilities are assigned. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a recommendation for the garden committee. Integrate the different perspectives, identify an assumption requiring verification and specify conditions for a manageable first phase.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"I recommend a limited first phase for the learning garden, with responsibility for summer care confirmed before construction. The proposal should integrate student preferences, teaching aims and the caretaker's practical estimate. The garden proposal should state which activities have priority when space or supervision is limited. Access to a raised bed means little if nobody is available to support a planned class. A phased arrangement allows the committee to investigate real demand while avoiding a promise that every requested activity will begin immediately.\n\nThe requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity. The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds. That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved. The teacher proposes practical lessons rather than unrestricted public access. These purposes can coexist if responsibilities are clearly assigned. I recommend a smaller first phase with accessible raised beds.\n\nThe report should acknowledge the opportunity cost of using the land. A synthesis explains relationships between sources instead of listing summaries. Where accounts differ, I would identify the underlying question. The committee needs a decision rule for continuing the project. My conclusion is provisional because summer staffing remains uncertain. The next report should show what happened as well as what was planned.\n\nThe committee should review actual use and maintenance after the first phase. Expansion would then depend on evidence and resources, rather than on treating every initial request as a commitment already made."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “A balanced plan for a learning garden”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I favour a smaller garden first phase. It could test the idea while making maintenance and teaching responsibilities clear before promises become too broad. The requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity. The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds. That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved. The teacher proposes practical lessons rather than unrestricted public access. The proposal also needs an agreed priority when uses clash. I would review the first phase before expansion, so that enthusiasm becomes evidence of practical use rather than an unlimited obligation."},"project":"完成“A balanced plan for a learning garden”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I have read your draft report on the campus seating project. The observations are useful, but the recommendation currently appears before the reader knows what problem you are trying to solve. Start by explaining the shortage near the teaching rooms, then compare the two locations you inspected. Please keep the caretaker's cost estimate separate from the students' preferences; they are different kinds of evidence. You do not need another survey this week. What you do need is confirmation that the proposed area is not an emergency access route. Ask the facilities office about that before revising the final paragraph. Bring the new draft to our meeting on Monday at ten. I will comment on structure and evidence, but I will not write the recommendation for you. That judgement should remain your own.","questions":[{"prompt":"What should the report explain first?","answer":"The seating shortage","options":["The seating shortage","The final recommendation without context","The supervisor’s timetable"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The seating shortage”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What new check is needed?","answer":"Whether the area is an emergency access route","options":["Whether the area is an emergency access route","Another preference survey","The price of a new classroom"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Whether the area is an emergency access route”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"Who should formulate the recommendation?","answer":"The student writer","options":["The student writer","The supervisor alone","The survey respondents collectively"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The student writer”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":53,"title":"即兴长答：有结构地持续表达","target":"C1","focus":"无稿组织观点、例证和限定","grammar":"话语标记；自我修正；举例","prerequisite":"完成第52章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Speaking about a change at work","text":"In an original speaking workshop, learners prepare a short unscripted account of a fictional workplace change: a community centre is replacing paper room bookings with a shared calendar. They should not memorise an impressive speech. Instead, they need to identify the purpose, explain a practical example and respond when a listener asks about exceptions. The briefing models language for a coherent long turn while leaving room to adjust the answer.\n\nA listener who has never used the centre should still understand why the booking change matters. The speaker can illustrate an overlapping request and explain how the shared record helps. If asked about people without digital access, the answer must develop the exception instead of simply praising the software again.\n\nThe main change was introducing a calendar that every coordinator could see. To put that in context, paper requests sometimes overlapped. One practical example is a class that needs extra setup time. The benefit is clearer visibility rather than automatic conflict resolution. I should qualify that: not every volunteer uses a smartphone. A telephone booking option would therefore remain necessary.\n\nIf I understand the question correctly, you are asking about exceptions. Let me distinguish an urgent request from a routine booking. I would not claim that the new system eliminates every mistake. What it does provide is a shared record of agreed arrangements. Returning to my main point, training matters as much as software. The next step would be a short trial with regular feedback."},"sourceB":{"title":"An interview candidate changes course","text":"During a practice interview, Lina was asked to describe a time she had changed a plan. She began with a long explanation of her former employer's organisation. After a minute, the tutor interrupted and asked which decision she personally had made. Lina realised that she had supplied background without reaching the event the listener needed to understand.\n\nIn a second attempt, she opened with the decision: moving a public workshop from an outdoor yard into a smaller indoor room when heavy rain was forecast. She explained the constraint, described how she reduced group size and acknowledged that some participants had to attend a later session. This account was shorter but more informative. It also gave the interviewer clear opportunities to ask about fairness and communication.\n\nThe tutor then challenged her claim that everyone had been satisfied. Lina corrected herself: no formal satisfaction check had been conducted, although several participants had thanked the organisers. The correction did not weaken the whole answer. It made the boundary of her knowledge explicit and demonstrated that she could respond to a question rather than defend an exaggerated phrase. The tutor advised her to practise several different experiences using a flexible structure, not to memorise the revised wording. A strong unscripted answer needs enough detail to be credible and enough room for the conversation to develop."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The main change was introducing a calendar that every coordinator could see.","options":["The main change was introducing a calendar that every coordinator could see.","It is not true that the main change was introducing a calendar that every coordinator could see.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The main change was introducing a calendar that every coordinator could see.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"To put that in context, paper requests sometimes overlapped.","options":["To put that in context, paper requests sometimes overlapped.","It is not true that to put that in context, paper requests sometimes overlapped.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“To put that in context, paper requests sometimes overlapped.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"One practical example is a class that needs extra setup time.","options":["One practical example is a class that needs extra setup time.","It is not true that one practical example is a class that needs extra setup time.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“One practical example is a class that needs extra setup time.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why was the first answer interrupted?","answer":"It supplied background without reaching her decision","options":["It supplied background without reaching her decision","It was too brief to contain any information","It discussed a completely different employer"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An interview candidate changes course”支持“It supplied background without reaching her decision”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What decision did Lina explain?","answer":"Moving a workshop indoors because of forecast rain","options":["Moving a workshop indoors because of forecast rain","Cancelling all future workshops","Increasing the outdoor group size"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An interview candidate changes course”支持“Moving a workshop indoors because of forecast rain”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"How did she correct the satisfaction claim?","answer":"She acknowledged there had been no formal check","options":["She acknowledged there had been no formal check","She invented a survey","She refused to answer"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An interview candidate changes course”支持“She acknowledged there had been no formal check”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 主要变化","en":"The main change was introducing a calendar that every coordinator could see.","zh":"主要变化是引入每位协调员都能看到的日历。","word":"main change","meaning":"主要变化","tip":"此句用“main change”（主要变化）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 结合背景","en":"To put that in context, paper requests sometimes overlapped.","zh":"说明背景，纸质申请有时会重叠。","word":"in context","meaning":"结合背景","tip":"此句用“in context”（结合背景）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 布置时间","en":"One practical example is a class that needs extra setup time.","zh":"一个实际例子是需要额外布置时间的课程。","word":"setup time","meaning":"布置时间","tip":"此句用“setup time”（布置时间）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 可见清晰程度","en":"The benefit is clearer visibility rather than automatic conflict resolution.","zh":"益处是安排更清楚，而非自动解决冲突。","word":"visibility","meaning":"可见清晰程度","tip":"此句用“visibility”（可见清晰程度）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 加以限定","en":"I should qualify that: not every volunteer uses a smartphone.","zh":"我应加以限定：并非每位志愿者都用智能手机。","word":"qualify","meaning":"加以限定","tip":"此句用“qualify”（加以限定）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 仍有必要","en":"A telephone booking option would therefore remain necessary.","zh":"因此，电话预约渠道仍有必要。","word":"remain necessary","meaning":"仍有必要","tip":"此句用“remain necessary”（仍有必要）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 如果我理解问题正确","en":"If I understand the question correctly, you are asking about exceptions.","zh":"如果我理解正确，你是在问例外情况。","word":"If I understand the question correctly","meaning":"如果我理解问题正确","tip":"此句用“If I understand the question correctly”（如果我理解问题正确）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 区分","en":"Let me distinguish an urgent request from a routine booking.","zh":"让我区分紧急请求和常规预约。","word":"distinguish","meaning":"区分","tip":"此句用“distinguish”（区分）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 消除","en":"I would not claim that the new system eliminates every mistake.","zh":"我不会声称新系统消除了所有错误。","word":"eliminate","meaning":"消除","tip":"此句用“eliminate”（消除）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 共享记录","en":"What it does provide is a shared record of agreed arrangements.","zh":"它确实提供了约定安排的共享记录。","word":"shared record","meaning":"共享记录","tip":"此句用“shared record”（共享记录）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 回到","en":"Returning to my main point, training matters as much as software.","zh":"回到主要观点，培训与软件同样重要。","word":"returning to","meaning":"回到","tip":"此句用“returning to”（回到）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 定期反馈","en":"The next step would be a short trial with regular feedback.","zh":"下一步是开展定期收集反馈的短期试行。","word":"regular feedback","meaning":"定期反馈","tip":"此句用“regular feedback”（定期反馈）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (主要变化): The ___ was introducing a calendar that every coordinator could see.","answers":["main change"],"explanation":"原句用“main change”表达“主要变化”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (仍有必要): A telephone booking option would therefore ___.","answers":["remain necessary"],"explanation":"原句用“remain necessary”表达“仍有必要”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of VISIBLE: The calendar improves ___ of agreed bookings.","answers":["visibility"],"explanation":"空格需要“visibility”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The system provides a shared record, but it does not eliminate all mistakes. Required opening: Although the system provides a …","answers":["Although the system provides a shared record, it does not eliminate all mistakes."],"explanation":"参考答案：Although the system provides a shared record, it does not eliminate all mistakes. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a reflective account of the booking-calendar change. Explain the problem, the limits of the solution and how you would adapt your explanation to a sceptical colleague.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The shared booking calendar is a reasonable response to overlapping room requests, provided that it remains accessible to volunteers who need a different route. Training and exception handling should accompany the software. A listener who has never used the centre should still understand why the booking change matters. The speaker can illustrate an overlapping request and explain how the shared record helps. If asked about people without digital access, the answer must develop the exception instead of simply praising the software again.\n\nThe main change was introducing a calendar that every coordinator could see. To put that in context, paper requests sometimes overlapped. One practical example is a class that needs extra setup time. The benefit is clearer visibility rather than automatic conflict resolution. I should qualify that: not every volunteer uses a smartphone. A telephone booking option would therefore remain necessary.\n\nIf I understand the question correctly, you are asking about exceptions. Let me distinguish an urgent request from a routine booking. I would not claim that the new system eliminates every mistake. What it does provide is a shared record of agreed arrangements. Returning to my main point, training matters as much as software. The next step would be a short trial with regular feedback.\n\nThe trial should record conflicts, exceptions and users' difficulties. If it improves the shared record without excluding volunteers, the centre would have a practical reason to continue and refine the arrangement."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Speaking about a change at work”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"The calendar could help coordinators see each other's arrangements, but it should not be presented as a solution to every booking difficulty. The main change was introducing a calendar that every coordinator could see. To put that in context, paper requests sometimes overlapped. One practical example is a class that needs extra setup time. The benefit is clearer visibility rather than automatic conflict resolution. I would keep telephone access and test exceptions during a short trial. In a follow-up, I would explain that the goal is a better shared record, not the removal of human judgement."},"project":"完成“Speaking about a change at work”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"In this practice session you will have one minute to prepare and two minutes to speak. You may make brief notes, but please do not write a full script and read it aloud. The first prompt concerns a change to a shared facility. You can use a real experience or a clearly identified imagined example; you will not be assessed on specialist knowledge. After your long answer, your partner will ask one question that requires clarification rather than a simple yes or no. Then exchange roles. The observer should record one moment when the structure was clear and one moment when more detail would have helped. Do not interrupt merely to correct a small grammatical slip. We will discuss language accuracy after the speaker has finished and responded to the follow-up.","questions":[{"prompt":"How long is preparation?","answer":"One minute","options":["One minute","Two minutes","Five minutes"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“One minute”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What follow-up is required?","answer":"A clarification question","options":["A clarification question","Only a yes-or-no question","A specialist factual quiz"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“A clarification question”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"When should minor language slips be discussed?","answer":"After the answer and follow-up","options":["After the answer and follow-up","Immediately during every sentence","Before the prompt is given"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“After the answer and follow-up”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":54,"title":"反驳与回应：处理最强的不同意见","target":"C1","focus":"公允重述对方理由再回应","grammar":"让步；条件反驳；论证限定","prerequisite":"完成第53章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Should a club remove late fees?","text":"A fictional community sports club is debating whether to remove late cancellation fees. Supporters say fees discourage people facing unpredictable caring duties. Opponents argue that empty spaces prevent others from joining. The workshop asks learners to engage with both concerns rather than label one side selfish. A persuasive response should identify the purpose of the current rule, test its assumptions and offer a workable alternative with limits.\n\nThe club should record when notice is given and whether a place is subsequently filled. Those measures would help distinguish unavoidable last-minute changes from a pattern that a different booking rule might address. A fair discussion should avoid demanding private details from carers merely to establish that their responsibilities can change.\n\nThe strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity. That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee. I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven. People with caring duties may face changes beyond their control. Removing the fee need not mean removing every consequence. A waiting list could fill some places if notice is prompt.\n\nThe alternative must be judged against the same practical objective. One example does not establish how frequently the problem occurs. We should avoid treating a disagreement as a personal failing. If repeat absences increase, the trial may require adjustment. My support is conditional on keeping a clear attendance record. A fair reply addresses the concern rather than attacking its speaker."},"sourceB":{"title":"A debate about library fines","text":"At a public meeting, one speaker argued that overdue fines were necessary because books needed to circulate. Another replied that the fines could discourage people from returning at all, particularly after a debt had accumulated. Their disagreement initially sounded absolute, but both wanted materials to return and access to remain fair.\n\nA librarian explained that removing fines would not mean abandoning reminders or allowing unlimited loans. The proposed policy would send notices, suspend additional borrowing after a specified overdue period and restore access when items were returned. Lost materials would still be handled through a separate replacement process, with a route for discussing hardship. This distinction addressed the claim that the proposal offered no incentive to return books.\n\nA critic then asked whether reminders would cost more in staff time. That was a relevant question not answered by the general argument about access. The committee agreed to compare administration time before and after a trial, as well as return rates and membership use. It rejected a suggestion to measure success only by the money collected, since a policy intended to improve circulation should also be judged on circulation. The final recommendation acknowledged that removing fines might not improve every indicator. Its case rested on a testable alternative system, not on the assumption that rules were inherently unkind or that every overdue reader was irresponsible."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity.","options":["The strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity.","It is not true that the strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee.","options":["That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee.","It is not true that that concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven.","options":["I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven.","It is not true that i agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What common aim did both speakers have?","answer":"Returning materials and maintaining fair access","options":["Returning materials and maintaining fair access","Collecting as much money as possible","Ending all borrowing rules"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A debate about library fines”支持“Returning materials and maintaining fair access”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What happens under the proposed alternative?","answer":"Additional borrowing is suspended after a defined overdue period","options":["Additional borrowing is suspended after a defined overdue period","All reminders end","Lost items are ignored"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A debate about library fines”支持“Additional borrowing is suspended after a defined overdue period”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What additional cost question was raised?","answer":"Staff time spent on reminders","options":["Staff time spent on reminders","The price of every new building","Travel expenses for the meeting"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A debate about library fines”支持“Staff time spent on reminders”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 反对意见","en":"The strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity.","zh":"最有力的反对意见是空位剥夺了他人的机会。","word":"objection","meaning":"反对意见","tip":"此句用“objection”（反对意见）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 合理的","en":"That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee.","zh":"即使反对现有费用，这一担忧仍然合理。","word":"legitimate","meaning":"合理的","tip":"此句用“legitimate”（合理的）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 不均等的","en":"I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven.","zh":"我赞同取消有成本，但负担并不均等。","word":"uneven","meaning":"不均等的","tip":"此句用“uneven”（不均等的）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 超出他们的控制","en":"People with caring duties may face changes beyond their control.","zh":"承担照护责任的人可能面临无法控制的变化。","word":"beyond their control","meaning":"超出他们的控制","tip":"此句用“beyond their control”（超出他们的控制）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 不一定需要","en":"Removing the fee need not mean removing every consequence.","zh":"取消费用不一定意味着取消一切后果。","word":"need not","meaning":"不一定需要","tip":"此句用“need not”（不一定需要）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 候补名单","en":"A waiting list could fill some places if notice is prompt.","zh":"若及时通知，候补名单可以填补部分空位。","word":"waiting list","meaning":"候补名单","tip":"此句用“waiting list”（候补名单）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 目标","en":"The alternative must be judged against the same practical objective.","zh":"替代方案必须按同一实际目标评判。","word":"objective","meaning":"目标","tip":"此句用“objective”（目标）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 频繁地","en":"One example does not establish how frequently the problem occurs.","zh":"一个例子无法证明问题出现的频率。","word":"frequently","meaning":"频繁地","tip":"此句用“frequently”（频繁地）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 个人缺点","en":"We should avoid treating a disagreement as a personal failing.","zh":"我们应避免把分歧当成个人缺点。","word":"personal failing","meaning":"个人缺点","tip":"此句用“personal failing”（个人缺点）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 调整","en":"If repeat absences increase, the trial may require adjustment.","zh":"如果反复缺席增加，试行可能需要调整。","word":"adjustment","meaning":"调整","tip":"此句用“adjustment”（调整）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 有条件的","en":"My support is conditional on keeping a clear attendance record.","zh":"我的支持以保留清晰出勤记录为条件。","word":"conditional","meaning":"有条件的","tip":"此句用“conditional”（有条件的）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 回应处理","en":"A fair reply addresses the concern rather than attacking its speaker.","zh":"公允回应处理担忧，而非攻击发言者。","word":"address","meaning":"回应处理","tip":"此句用“address”（回应处理）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (反对意见): The strongest ___ is that unused places deny others an opportunity.","answers":["objection"],"explanation":"原句用“objection”表达“反对意见”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (候补名单): A ___ could fill some places if notice is prompt.","answers":["waiting list"],"explanation":"原句用“waiting list”表达“候补名单”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of ATTEND: The trial needs an accurate ___ record.","answers":["attendance"],"explanation":"空格需要“attendance”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: Removing the fee does not necessarily require removing all consequences. Required opening: Removing the fee need not …","answers":["Removing the fee need not mean removing all consequences."],"explanation":"参考答案：Removing the fee need not mean removing all consequences. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an argument for a revised cancellation policy. State the strongest objection fairly, respond with a practical alternative and identify a condition that could change your recommendation.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The club should test a revised cancellation policy rather than assume that either the existing fee or complete freedom from consequences is the only option. The aim is fair access to limited places. The club should record when notice is given and whether a place is subsequently filled. Those measures would help distinguish unavoidable last-minute changes from a pattern that a different booking rule might address. A fair discussion should avoid demanding private details from carers merely to establish that their responsibilities can change.\n\nThe strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity. That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee. I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven. People with caring duties may face changes beyond their control. Removing the fee need not mean removing every consequence. A waiting list could fill some places if notice is prompt.\n\nThe alternative must be judged against the same practical objective. One example does not establish how frequently the problem occurs. We should avoid treating a disagreement as a personal failing. If repeat absences increase, the trial may require adjustment. My support is conditional on keeping a clear attendance record. A fair reply addresses the concern rather than attacking its speaker.\n\nA waiting-list process and clear records would make the trial testable. If repeated absences increased, the club should adjust the policy rather than protect it simply because its intentions were fair."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Should a club remove late fees?”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I would judge the cancellation rule against access to places, not merely against the amount collected in fees or the popularity of removing them. The strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity. That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee. I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven. People with caring duties may face changes beyond their control. A trial should record notice, refilled places and repeated absences. I would adjust my view if those results showed that the alternative failed to protect access for other members."},"project":"完成“Should a club remove late fees?”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"For tonight's exercise, you will defend the position on your card for the first round, even if it differs from your personal view. Start by summarising the opposing case in a way its supporter would recognise. Your partner may ask you to revise that summary before you respond. Each opening contribution has a three-minute limit. In the second round, you can change your position, but explain which reason or piece of evidence led you to do so. We are not choosing a winner by applause. The observer will note whether speakers addressed the actual argument, distinguished claims from examples and acknowledged a genuine limitation. Please avoid using personal information about other members as evidence. The topic is the club's future booking policy, not the behaviour of particular individuals.","questions":[{"prompt":"What must speakers do first?","answer":"Summarise the opposing case fairly","options":["Summarise the opposing case fairly","State their personal view only","Identify an individual to blame"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Summarise the opposing case fairly”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"Can speakers change position?","answer":"Yes, with an explanation in round two","options":["Yes, with an explanation in round two","Never","Only after an applause vote"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Yes, with an explanation in round two”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What does the observer assess?","answer":"Engagement with the actual argument","options":["Engagement with the actual argument","Popularity of each speaker","Private details about club members"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Engagement with the actual argument”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":55,"title":"谈判与协商：把分歧变成条件方案","target":"C1","focus":"区分立场、利益、底线和可交换条件","grammar":"条件交易；假设表达；确认条款","prerequisite":"完成第54章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Negotiating use of a rehearsal room","text":"Two fictional groups want the same community rehearsal room on Thursday evenings. A choir needs stable weekly practice, while a youth theatre needs occasional longer sessions before performances. The building manager cannot create another room or ignore the closing time. Learners will negotiate around the interests behind the requests, making proposals specific enough to check. Agreement should not rely on a vague promise that somebody will somehow be flexible later.\n\nThe building manager can help by identifying the calendar dates that genuinely create a clash. Once those are known, the groups may trade a small number of sessions instead of disputing an entire term. The written agreement should preserve that limited scope so that an occasional exception does not become an assumed permanent entitlement.\n\nThe choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule. The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday. Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions? We could accept that provided the dates are agreed in advance. The closing time is a fixed constraint rather than a bargaining point. Let us separate what is essential from what is merely preferred.\n\nA tentative agreement still needs confirmation from both coordinators. Who would notify members when the usual timetable changes? We should record a procedure for handling an unforeseen clash. I cannot commit the other group without consulting its organiser. To confirm, the exception applies only to the two named dates. A workable agreement makes responsibilities and review dates explicit."},"sourceB":{"title":"Agreeing a market delivery window","text":"Shopkeepers and a delivery company met to discuss a pedestrian street. The shopkeepers wanted goods before customers arrived; drivers wanted a reliable unloading space instead of circling the block. Residents opposed engines running beneath bedroom windows early in the morning. Initially, each group presented a preferred time as if it were the only acceptable option.\n\nA facilitator asked what each request was intended to protect. That revealed room for a conditional arrangement. Deliveries could enter between seven thirty and eight thirty, provided that vehicles switched off engines while waiting and used the marked bay. Shops needing unusually large deliveries would book a separate supervised slot twice a month. The company could not promise that every supplier would join the arrangement, so the council retained responsibility for informing other operators.\n\nThe written agreement named who would keep the booking record, how missed slots would be reported and when the arrangement would be reviewed. It also stated that a booked slot was permission to unload, not permission to park all morning. Residents accepted a six-week trial but did not withdraw their concerns about noise. A sound-level review and a contact route for complaints remained part of the deal. The facilitator described the result as a workable test of shared conditions. Nobody had obtained their original preference in full, and the agreement would fail if its practical responsibilities were left implicit."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule.","options":["The choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule.","It is not true that the choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday.","options":["The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday.","It is not true that the theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions?","options":["Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions?","It is not true that would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions?","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions?”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did drivers primarily need?","answer":"Reliable unloading space","options":["Reliable unloading space","All-day parking permission","Permission to run engines continuously"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Agreeing a market delivery window”支持“Reliable unloading space”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What condition accompanied the delivery window?","answer":"Switching off engines while waiting","options":["Switching off engines while waiting","Ignoring marked bays","Cancelling all large deliveries"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Agreeing a market delivery window”支持“Switching off engines while waiting”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did residents accept?","answer":"A six-week trial with monitoring","options":["A six-week trial with monitoring","Permanent approval without review","Removal of the complaint route"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“Agreeing a market delivery window”支持“A six-week trial with monitoring”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 背后的利益","en":"The choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule.","zh":"合唱团背后的利益是可预测的每周安排。","word":"underlying interest","meaning":"背后的利益","tip":"此句用“underlying interest”（背后的利益）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 额外时间","en":"The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday.","zh":"剧团需要演出前加时，而非每个星期四。","word":"extra time","meaning":"额外时间","tip":"此句用“extra time”（额外时间）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 交换","en":"Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions?","zh":"你愿意用更早开始换取两次较长排练吗？","word":"in exchange for","meaning":"交换","tip":"此句用“in exchange for”（交换）提出澄清或协商问题，让对方能具体回应。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 前提是","en":"We could accept that provided the dates are agreed in advance.","zh":"我们可以接受，前提是日期提前商定。","word":"provided","meaning":"前提是","tip":"此句用“provided”（前提是）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 限制","en":"The closing time is a fixed constraint rather than a bargaining point.","zh":"关门时间是固定限制，而非讨价还价点。","word":"constraint","meaning":"限制","tip":"此句用“constraint”（限制）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 必要的","en":"Let us separate what is essential from what is merely preferred.","zh":"让我们区分必要条件和偏好。","word":"essential","meaning":"必要的","tip":"此句用“essential”（必要的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 暂定的","en":"A tentative agreement still needs confirmation from both coordinators.","zh":"暂定协议仍需双方协调员确认。","word":"tentative","meaning":"暂定的","tip":"此句用“tentative”（暂定的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 通知","en":"Who would notify members when the usual timetable changes?","zh":"惯常时间表变化时由谁通知成员？","word":"notify","meaning":"通知","tip":"此句用“notify”（通知）提出澄清或协商问题，让对方能具体回应。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 意外的","en":"We should record a procedure for handling an unforeseen clash.","zh":"我们应记录处理意外冲突的流程。","word":"unforeseen","meaning":"意外的","tip":"此句用“unforeseen”（意外的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 使承担承诺","en":"I cannot commit the other group without consulting its organiser.","zh":"未经咨询组织者，我不能代表另一组承诺。","word":"commit","meaning":"使承担承诺","tip":"此句用“commit”（使承担承诺）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 例外","en":"To confirm, the exception applies only to the two named dates.","zh":"确认一下，例外仅适用于列明的两个日期。","word":"exception","meaning":"例外","tip":"此句用“exception”（例外）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 可行的","en":"A workable agreement makes responsibilities and review dates explicit.","zh":"可行协议会明确职责和复查日期。","word":"workable","meaning":"可行的","tip":"此句用“workable”（可行的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (背后的利益): The choir’s ___ is a predictable weekly schedule.","answers":["underlying interest"],"explanation":"原句用“underlying interest”表达“背后的利益”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (必要的): Let us separate what is ___ from what is merely preferred.","answers":["essential"],"explanation":"原句用“essential”表达“必要的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of CONFIRM: The tentative arrangement requires ___ from both groups.","answers":["confirmation"],"explanation":"空格需要“confirmation”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: We can accept longer sessions only if the dates are agreed beforehand. Required opening: We can accept longer sessions …","answers":["We can accept longer sessions provided that the dates are agreed beforehand."],"explanation":"参考答案：We can accept longer sessions provided that the dates are agreed beforehand. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a proposed agreement for the rehearsal room. Explain each group’s underlying interest, offer a conditional exchange and record responsibilities and a review process.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"A rehearsal agreement should protect the choir's predictable practice and the theatre's occasional need for longer sessions. Both interests can be considered without ignoring the building's fixed closing time. The building manager can help by identifying the calendar dates that genuinely create a clash. Once those are known, the groups may trade a small number of sessions instead of disputing an entire term. The written agreement should preserve that limited scope so that an occasional exception does not become an assumed permanent entitlement.\n\nThe choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule. The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday. Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions? We could accept that provided the dates are agreed in advance. The closing time is a fixed constraint rather than a bargaining point. Let us separate what is essential from what is merely preferred.\n\nA tentative agreement still needs confirmation from both coordinators. Who would notify members when the usual timetable changes? We should record a procedure for handling an unforeseen clash. I cannot commit the other group without consulting its organiser. To confirm, the exception applies only to the two named dates. A workable agreement makes responsibilities and review dates explicit.\n\nBoth coordinators should confirm dates, notifications and the procedure for an unforeseen clash. The agreement would then describe a genuine exchange of conditions rather than a hope that goodwill will solve every conflict."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Negotiating use of a rehearsal room”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"The rehearsal groups need an agreement based on their actual interests. Discussing specific dates may create more room than arguing over every Thursday. The choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule. The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday. Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions? We could accept that provided the dates are agreed in advance. I would put the agreed exceptions and notifications in writing. That allows each group to check what it has accepted and avoids a later dispute about an informal promise to remain flexible."},"project":"完成“Negotiating use of a rehearsal room”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I can confirm that we can offer your group the small hall on the first and third Fridays next month. The large hall is unavailable because the floor is being repaired. Your requested six o'clock start is possible if your group sets out its own chairs; our caretaker will be dealing with another event at that time. We can provide thirty chairs and a projector, but you will need to bring a laptop and the correct connector. Please finish clearing the room by nine thirty, rather than ending the programme at that time. The price includes heating and normal cleaning, not removal of decorations fixed to the walls. Send confirmation by Monday so that I can hold both dates. If you need to cancel one session, tell us separately which date you no longer require.","questions":[{"prompt":"Which dates are offered?","answer":"The first and third Fridays","options":["The first and third Fridays","Every Friday indefinitely","Only the second Friday"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The first and third Fridays”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What permits the six o’clock start?","answer":"The group setting out its own chairs","options":["The group setting out its own chairs","A caretaker arriving early","Using the large hall"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The group setting out its own chairs”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What must be completed by nine thirty?","answer":"Clearing the room","options":["Clearing the room","Starting the programme","Confirming the booking"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Clearing the room”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":56,"title":"讨论复核：回应追问与修正立场","target":"C1","focus":"持续互动并依据新信息调整","grammar":"澄清；归纳共识；修正断言","prerequisite":"完成第55章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Reviewing a trial without defending every decision","text":"A fictional neighbourhood association has trialled a tool-sharing cupboard. At a review meeting, participants must discuss late returns, missing safety instructions and uneven demand. Some members want to continue immediately; others want to suspend lending. Learners should demonstrate that fluent discussion includes listening, checking interpretations and revising a claim when evidence changes. The task is to reach a defensible next step, not to protect the original proposal from every criticism.\n\nA suspended scheme and an unchanged scheme are not the only options. The association could pause lending of items lacking instructions while continuing other loans. Explaining that distinction would let the discussion respond to the safety concern without claiming that every tool or borrower presented the same difficulty.\n\nCould you clarify whether late returns affected all tools equally? I may have overstated the problem when I described it as constant. The new figures suggest that two popular items account for most delays. That changes my view about suspending the entire scheme. We still need a clear procedure for replacing missing instructions. Your concern appears to be safe use rather than lending itself.\n\nHave I represented your position fairly? We seem to agree on continuing with narrower conditions. I would reserve judgement until the replacement instructions are checked. The minutes should record why the recommendation changed. A follow-up question can expose an assumption without sounding hostile. Let us confirm the decision and the evidence required for review."},"sourceB":{"title":"A school council revisits its proposal","text":"The school council had proposed replacing printed newsletters with email. At the review meeting, the chair described the trial as universally successful because most families had opened at least one message. A parent representative asked whether opening one message meant that important information had actually reached each household. The chair accepted that the measure was too broad.\n\nA teacher then reported that several families shared a single device and sometimes missed attachments. This did not show that email was unsuitable for everyone. It did reveal a need for an alternative route and clearer message design. A student suggested short text reminders for urgent items, but the office manager warned that maintaining several contact lists could create errors unless responsibility was assigned.\n\nThe group revised its position. Email would remain the main route, while families could request a printed summary without explaining their circumstances publicly. Urgent notices would use a separate agreed process. The office would verify contact details once a term and record undelivered messages. Members did not set a target for eliminating paper, since access to information was the actual objective. At the end, the chair restated the decision and invited corrections. The parent representative confirmed that the revised policy addressed the central concern, while asking for a review after one term. The discussion demonstrated progress through a changed judgement, rather than through a unanimous defence of the original wording."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"Could you clarify whether late returns affected all tools equally?","options":["Could you clarify whether late returns affected all tools equally?","It is not true that could you clarify whether late returns affected all tools equally?","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Could you clarify whether late returns affected all tools equally?”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"I may have overstated the problem when I described it as constant.","options":["I may have overstated the problem when I described it as constant.","It is not true that i may have overstated the problem when I described it as constant.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“I may have overstated the problem when I described it as constant.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The new figures suggest that two popular items account for most delays.","options":["The new figures suggest that two popular items account for most delays.","It is not true that the new figures suggest that two popular items account for most delays.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The new figures suggest that two popular items account for most delays.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why was universal success questioned?","answer":"Opening one message did not establish reliable access to important information","options":["Opening one message did not establish reliable access to important information","No emails had been sent","Every family opposed email"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A school council revisits its proposal”支持“Opening one message did not establish reliable access to important information”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What alternative will remain?","answer":"A requested printed summary","options":["A requested printed summary","Only a shared social-media account","No alternative"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A school council revisits its proposal”支持“A requested printed summary”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What objective replaced eliminating paper?","answer":"Access to information","options":["Access to information","Reducing the number of families","Increasing attachment size"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A school council revisits its proposal”支持“Access to information”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 澄清","en":"Could you clarify whether late returns affected all tools equally?","zh":"能否说明延迟归还是否同样影响所有工具？","word":"clarify","meaning":"澄清","tip":"此句用“clarify”（澄清）提出澄清或协商问题，让对方能具体回应。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 夸大","en":"I may have overstated the problem when I described it as constant.","zh":"我把问题描述成持续存在，可能说得过重。","word":"overstate","meaning":"夸大","tip":"此句用“overstate”（夸大）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 占","en":"The new figures suggest that two popular items account for most delays.","zh":"新数字显示两种热门工具占了大部分延迟。","word":"account for","meaning":"占","tip":"此句用“account for”（占）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 改变我的看法","en":"That changes my view about suspending the entire scheme.","zh":"这改变了我对暂停整个计划的看法。","word":"changes my view","meaning":"改变我的看法","tip":"此句用“changes my view”（改变我的看法）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 流程","en":"We still need a clear procedure for replacing missing instructions.","zh":"我们仍需明确补充遗失说明书的流程。","word":"procedure","meaning":"流程","tip":"此句用“procedure”（流程）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 似乎","en":"Your concern appears to be safe use rather than lending itself.","zh":"你的担忧似乎是安全使用，而非借用本身。","word":"appears to","meaning":"似乎","tip":"此句用“appears to”（似乎）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 表述","en":"Have I represented your position fairly?","zh":"我是否公允地表述了你的立场？","word":"represent","meaning":"表述","tip":"此句用“represent”（表述）提出澄清或协商问题，让对方能具体回应。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 更具体的条件","en":"We seem to agree on continuing with narrower conditions.","zh":"我们似乎同意附加更具体条件后继续。","word":"narrower conditions","meaning":"更具体的条件","tip":"此句用“narrower conditions”（更具体的条件）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 暂缓判断","en":"I would reserve judgement until the replacement instructions are checked.","zh":"在核查补充说明书前，我会暂缓判断。","word":"reserve judgement","meaning":"暂缓判断","tip":"此句用“reserve judgement”（暂缓判断）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 建议","en":"The minutes should record why the recommendation changed.","zh":"纪要应记录建议改变的原因。","word":"recommendation","meaning":"建议","tip":"此句用“recommendation”（建议）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 敌对的","en":"A follow-up question can expose an assumption without sounding hostile.","zh":"追问可以揭示假设，而不显得敌对。","word":"hostile","meaning":"敌对的","tip":"此句用“hostile”（敌对的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 确认","en":"Let us confirm the decision and the evidence required for review.","zh":"让我们确认决定及复查所需证据。","word":"confirm","meaning":"确认","tip":"此句用“confirm”（确认）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (澄清): Could you ___ whether late returns affected all tools equally?","answers":["clarify"],"explanation":"原句用“clarify”表达“澄清”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (似乎): Your concern ___ be safe use rather than lending itself.","answers":["appears to"],"explanation":"原句用“appears to”表达“似乎”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of CLARIFY: A request for ___ can reveal an inaccurate assumption.","answers":["clarification"],"explanation":"空格需要“clarification”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The new figures changed my view of suspension. Required opening: I changed my view of …","answers":["I changed my view of suspension because of the new figures."],"explanation":"参考答案：I changed my view of suspension because of the new figures. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a review of the tool-sharing trial. Show how clarification changes an initial judgement, then propose a limited continuation with clear safeguards.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The tool-sharing trial should continue only within conditions that address the actual problems. Clarifying which items were returned late allows the association to respond proportionately rather than suspend everything automatically. A suspended scheme and an unchanged scheme are not the only options. The association could pause lending of items lacking instructions while continuing other loans. Explaining that distinction would let the discussion respond to the safety concern without claiming that every tool or borrower presented the same difficulty.\n\nCould you clarify whether late returns affected all tools equally? I may have overstated the problem when I described it as constant. The new figures suggest that two popular items account for most delays. That changes my view about suspending the entire scheme. We still need a clear procedure for replacing missing instructions. Your concern appears to be safe use rather than lending itself.\n\nHave I represented your position fairly? We seem to agree on continuing with narrower conditions. I would reserve judgement until the replacement instructions are checked. The minutes should record why the recommendation changed. A follow-up question can expose an assumption without sounding hostile. Let us confirm the decision and the evidence required for review.\n\nThe association should document why its judgement changed and review the safeguards later. Revising a position in response to better information is responsible discussion, not a failure to defend the original idea."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Reviewing a trial without defending every decision”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"The new figures make me less inclined to suspend all lending. I would focus the response on the items and procedures causing difficulty. Could you clarify whether late returns affected all tools equally? I may have overstated the problem when I described it as constant. The new figures suggest that two popular items account for most delays. That changes my view about suspending the entire scheme. I would still insist on clear instructions and a review date. Changing my view does not mean abandoning safety; it means matching the response more accurately to the problem the evidence identifies."},"project":"完成“Reviewing a trial without defending every decision”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"For next week's discussion review, listen to your recording before choosing an extract. Select ninety seconds in which somebody asks for clarification or changes a view. Do not simply choose your longest uninterrupted speech. Bring a short transcript of that section and mark any place where the written words alone fail to show the speaker's attitude. We will compare the transcript with the audio, so keep pauses and brief self-corrections rather than polishing them away. You should remove personal names and any sensitive details before sharing. During the workshop, the group will identify how the discussion moved forward. We are not awarding points for sounding certain. A justified change of mind can be stronger evidence of effective interaction than repeating a confident claim. Please send the extract title, but not the whole recording, by Thursday.","questions":[{"prompt":"What extract should learners choose?","answer":"Clarification or a change of view","options":["Clarification or a change of view","Their longest speech automatically","Only grammatically perfect sentences"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Clarification or a change of view”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What should the transcript preserve?","answer":"Pauses and self-corrections","options":["Pauses and self-corrections","Only polished conclusions","Real names and sensitive details"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Pauses and self-corrections”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What is sent by Thursday?","answer":"The extract title","options":["The extract title","The entire recording","A confidence score"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The extract title”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":57,"title":"正式报告：让发现支持建议","target":"C1","focus":"按目的组织发现、限制和行动","grammar":"报告语域；非人称表达；建议结构","prerequisite":"完成第56章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Reporting on a study-room trial","text":"A fictional college committee has asked for a report on reservable study rooms. The writer has booking records, staff observations and comments from students who could not find a place. A useful report must distinguish the questions these materials answer and make practical recommendations. Headings should guide a busy reader without becoming a substitute for connected argument. The report is intended to support a decision, not merely demonstrate that data have been collected.\n\nThe committee needs to know whether a proposed change addresses empty reservations, lack of space or both. The report should not assume that visible empty rooms are available for immediate use without checking their status. A practical recommendation can begin with the booking rule while leaving broader capacity questions open.\n\nThe purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial. The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt. It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved. Student comments indicate frustration, although their frequency is uncertain. These findings should be interpreted within the limited observation period. An automatic release rule could reduce unused reservations.\n\nIt is recommended that the rule be tested before wider adoption. The facilities team should be responsible for monitoring room availability. The report separates immediate actions from questions requiring further study. A finding should be traceable to the material that supports it. Headings help the reader locate evidence and recommendations. The conclusion should not introduce an unsupported new claim."},"sourceB":{"title":"An inspection report on changing facilities","text":"A leisure centre commissioned a report on its changing facilities after receiving complaints. The inspector visited during two busy evening sessions and one quiet morning. She recorded damaged fittings, observed queues and interviewed a small number of users who volunteered to speak. The resulting report distinguished direct observations from those users' accounts and explained that the visits did not cover every opening period.\n\nThe most urgent finding concerned a faulty lock on an accessible cubicle. The inspector recommended immediate repair rather than waiting for a complete refurbishment. She also noted that the signs directing visitors to family facilities were difficult to see from reception. Several interviewees said they had used the main changing area because they did not know an alternative existed. Better signs could address that problem, but would not establish whether there were enough cubicles at peak times.\n\nThe report therefore separated immediate maintenance, low-cost communication changes and a longer capacity review. It did not recommend a major extension on the basis of three visits. For the review, it proposed counting waiting times across different days and consulting users who needed assistance. The centre manager initially asked for one simple satisfaction score. The inspector explained that such a score would conceal a critical fault alongside more general preferences. Her final summary identified which actions could be taken now and which required stronger evidence, with a responsible department named for each."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial.","options":["The purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial.","It is not true that the purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt.","options":["The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt.","It is not true that the records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved.","options":["It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved.","It is not true that it was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which action was most urgent?","answer":"Repairing the accessible cubicle lock","options":["Repairing the accessible cubicle lock","Building a major extension","Replacing every satisfaction question"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An inspection report on changing facilities”支持“Repairing the accessible cubicle lock”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What could better signs address?","answer":"Lack of awareness of family facilities","options":["Lack of awareness of family facilities","All peak-time capacity constraints","The faulty lock itself"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An inspection report on changing facilities”支持“Lack of awareness of family facilities”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why was one satisfaction score insufficient?","answer":"It could conceal a critical fault among general preferences","options":["It could conceal a critical fault among general preferences","No user had any opinion","The centre had no changing rooms"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An inspection report on changing facilities”支持“It could conceal a critical fault among general preferences”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 目的","en":"The purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial.","zh":"本报告旨在评估预约试行。","word":"purpose","meaning":"目的","tip":"此句用“purpose”（目的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 覆盖","en":"The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt.","zh":"记录覆盖已完成预约，却不包含每次失败尝试。","word":"cover","meaning":"覆盖","tip":"此句用“cover”（覆盖）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 观察发现","en":"It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved.","zh":"观察发现一些房间被预约后仍然空置。","word":"it was observed","meaning":"观察发现","tip":"此句用“it was observed”（观察发现）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 表明","en":"Student comments indicate frustration, although their frequency is uncertain.","zh":"学生意见反映不满，但其频率尚不确定。","word":"indicate","meaning":"表明","tip":"此句用“indicate”（表明）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 解读","en":"These findings should be interpreted within the limited observation period.","zh":"应在有限观察期内解读这些发现。","word":"interpret","meaning":"解读","tip":"此句用“interpret”（解读）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 释放规则","en":"An automatic release rule could reduce unused reservations.","zh":"自动释放规则可能减少闲置预约。","word":"release rule","meaning":"释放规则","tip":"此句用“release rule”（释放规则）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 建议","en":"It is recommended that the rule be tested before wider adoption.","zh":"建议先测试该规则，再扩大采用。","word":"it is recommended","meaning":"建议","tip":"此句用“it is recommended”（建议）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 监测","en":"The facilities team should be responsible for monitoring room availability.","zh":"设施团队应负责监测房间可用性。","word":"monitor","meaning":"监测","tip":"此句用“monitor”（监测）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 立即的","en":"The report separates immediate actions from questions requiring further study.","zh":"报告区分立即行动与需进一步研究的问题。","word":"immediate","meaning":"立即的","tip":"此句用“immediate”（立即的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 可追溯的","en":"A finding should be traceable to the material that supports it.","zh":"发现应能追溯到支持它的材料。","word":"traceable","meaning":"可追溯的","tip":"此句用“traceable”（可追溯的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 标题","en":"Headings help the reader locate evidence and recommendations.","zh":"标题帮助读者定位证据与建议。","word":"heading","meaning":"标题","tip":"此句用“heading”（标题）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 无依据的","en":"The conclusion should not introduce an unsupported new claim.","zh":"结论不应引入无依据的新主张。","word":"unsupported","meaning":"无依据的","tip":"此句用“unsupported”（无依据的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (目的): The ___ of this report is to evaluate the booking trial.","answers":["purpose"],"explanation":"原句用“purpose”表达“目的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (释放规则): An automatic ___ could reduce unused reservations.","answers":["release rule"],"explanation":"原句用“release rule”表达“释放规则”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of AVAILABLE: The facilities team will monitor room ___.","answers":["availability"],"explanation":"空格需要“availability”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The rule should be tested before it is adopted more widely. Required opening: Wider adoption should follow a …","answers":["Wider adoption should follow a test of the rule."],"explanation":"参考答案：Wider adoption should follow a test of the rule. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a formal report on the study-room trial. Include purpose, findings, limitations and prioritised recommendations with named responsibilities.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"This report evaluates the study-room booking trial and recommends a limited test of an automatic release rule. It also identifies evidence gaps that prevent a reliable judgement about overall room capacity. The committee needs to know whether a proposed change addresses empty reservations, lack of space or both. The report should not assume that visible empty rooms are available for immediate use without checking their status. A practical recommendation can begin with the booking rule while leaving broader capacity questions open.\n\nThe purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial. The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt. It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved. Student comments indicate frustration, although their frequency is uncertain. These findings should be interpreted within the limited observation period. An automatic release rule could reduce unused reservations.\n\nIt is recommended that the rule be tested before wider adoption. The facilities team should be responsible for monitoring room availability. The report separates immediate actions from questions requiring further study. A finding should be traceable to the material that supports it. Headings help the reader locate evidence and recommendations. The conclusion should not introduce an unsupported new claim.\n\nThe facilities team should report the effect of the rule before wider adoption. A separate review can then examine whether demand exceeds space, using evidence beyond the current booking records."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Reporting on a study-room trial”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"The study-room report should identify the problem precisely. Empty reservations and insufficient overall capacity are related issues, but they require different evidence. The purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial. The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt. It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved. Student comments indicate frustration, although their frequency is uncertain. I would recommend testing the release rule first and investigating wider demand separately. The report should name who monitors the result and avoid claiming that a small trial proves every proposed change."},"project":"完成“Reporting on a study-room trial”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I would like your report on the reception area by Friday afternoon. Please focus on how visitors find the correct desk and how staff handle queues. We are not reviewing the whole building at this stage. You may observe the entrance on Tuesday morning and Wednesday lunchtime, but ask the duty manager before speaking to visitors. Do not include names or descriptions that could identify an individual. The report should separate what you saw from what staff told you and from your own recommendations. Photographs are unnecessary. A simple layout sketch would be more useful if it helps explain a sign's position. Please put urgent safety concerns in a separate message immediately; do not wait until the report is complete. We will discuss possible changes at Monday's staff meeting.","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the report’s scope?","answer":"Reception navigation and queues","options":["Reception navigation and queues","The entire building structure","Only the staff lunch menu"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Reception navigation and queues”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What should replace unnecessary photographs?","answer":"A useful layout sketch","options":["A useful layout sketch","Identifiable visitor portraits","An advertising leaflet"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“A useful layout sketch”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"When should urgent safety concerns be reported?","answer":"Immediately in a separate message","options":["Immediately in a separate message","Only in Friday’s final report","After Monday’s meeting"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Immediately in a separate message”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":58,"title":"建议书：明确收益、成本与落实条件","target":"C1","focus":"写出面向决策者的可执行提案","grammar":"预测与条件；建议；成本效益表达","prerequisite":"完成第57章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Proposing a practical mentoring scheme","text":"A fictional adult college is considering peer mentoring for new students. The proposal should explain who would benefit, what mentors could realistically provide and where professional support remains necessary. Enthusiasm alone cannot establish capacity. The writer must specify recruitment, training, supervision and a modest first phase. The intended reader is a budget committee deciding whether the scheme is ready for a trial, not a publicity team seeking a slogan.\n\nA mentor's value may lie in helping a student locate the right service, not in answering every question personally. Training should therefore include how to recognise a concern outside the mentor's role. The committee can authorise a bounded trial while requiring a coordinator to check the quality of referrals and contact.\n\nThe proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life. Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers. A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable. The budget must include training as well as publicity. Potential benefits depend on consistent contact and clear boundaries. Participation should be voluntary for both mentors and new students.\n\nWe should specify how a mentor can refer a difficult concern. The proposal identifies a coordinator rather than assuming shared responsibility. A review after one term would inform any expansion. The alternative is to improve existing induction sessions first. A persuasive proposal acknowledges what it cannot yet establish. Funding should be released only when the training plan is agreed."},"sourceB":{"title":"A proposal for a travelling repair desk","text":"A residents' association proposed a travelling desk where people could bring small household objects for assessment. Its purpose was to help residents decide whether an item could be repaired, not to guarantee that volunteers would fix everything on the spot. The distinction mattered because electrical testing required trained personnel and suitable equipment. The proposal excluded hazardous materials and items too large to carry safely.\n\nInstead of buying a vehicle, the association suggested a three-month trial at existing community venues. Each venue would provide a table and a power supply that had been checked by its manager. A qualified supervisor would decide which tasks volunteers could undertake. Residents would book a brief appointment, receive an explanation of the likely next step and be told when a professional service was needed. Donation requests would be separate from access to advice, so nobody felt obliged to pay before asking a question.\n\nThe budget included transport for tools, insurance, replacement consumables and supervision time. It did not count every volunteer hour as a cash saving, since volunteers might otherwise have contributed to different activities. Success would be assessed through attendance, safe referrals, completed simple repairs and feedback about the clarity of advice. The association proposed reviewing demand at each venue before expanding. A cautious pilot could establish whether the service was useful; it could not justify purchasing a vehicle merely because a travelling project sounded attractive."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life.","options":["The proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life.","It is not true that the proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers.","options":["Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers.","It is not true that mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable.","options":["A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable.","It is not true that a small first cohort would keep supervision manageable.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What does the desk guarantee?","answer":"Assessment and advice about next steps","options":["Assessment and advice about next steps","Repair of every item immediately","Free replacement of all appliances"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A proposal for a travelling repair desk”支持“Assessment and advice about next steps”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What trial arrangement is proposed?","answer":"Existing venues for three months","options":["Existing venues for three months","Immediate vehicle purchase","Permanent daily visits everywhere"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A proposal for a travelling repair desk”支持“Existing venues for three months”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why not count all volunteer hours as cash savings?","answer":"They could have supported other activities","options":["They could have supported other activities","Volunteers were forbidden","No time would be spent"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A proposal for a travelling repair desk”支持“They could have supported other activities”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 拟议计划","en":"The proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life.","zh":"拟议计划将帮助新生适应日常校园生活。","word":"proposed scheme","meaning":"拟议计划","tip":"此句用“proposed scheme”（拟议计划）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 替代","en":"Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers.","zh":"导师提供指引，而非替代专业顾问。","word":"replace","meaning":"替代","tip":"此句用“replace”（替代）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 同批群体","en":"A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable.","zh":"首批人数较少有助于管理督导。","word":"cohort","meaning":"同批群体","tip":"此句用“cohort”（同批群体）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 宣传","en":"The budget must include training as well as publicity.","zh":"预算必须包括培训及宣传。","word":"publicity","meaning":"宣传","tip":"此句用“publicity”（宣传）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 边界","en":"Potential benefits depend on consistent contact and clear boundaries.","zh":"潜在益处取决于稳定联系和明确边界。","word":"boundaries","meaning":"边界","tip":"此句用“boundaries”（边界）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 自愿的","en":"Participation should be voluntary for both mentors and new students.","zh":"导师和新生都应自愿参加。","word":"voluntary","meaning":"自愿的","tip":"此句用“voluntary”（自愿的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 转介","en":"We should specify how a mentor can refer a difficult concern.","zh":"我们应明确导师如何转介棘手问题。","word":"refer","meaning":"转介","tip":"此句用“refer”（转介）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 协调员","en":"The proposal identifies a coordinator rather than assuming shared responsibility.","zh":"提案明确协调员，而非假定共同负责。","word":"coordinator","meaning":"协调员","tip":"此句用“coordinator”（协调员）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 为……提供依据","en":"A review after one term would inform any expansion.","zh":"一学期后复查将为扩展提供依据。","word":"inform","meaning":"为……提供依据","tip":"此句用“inform”（为……提供依据）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 入学介绍","en":"The alternative is to improve existing induction sessions first.","zh":"另一选择是先改善现有入学介绍。","word":"induction","meaning":"入学介绍","tip":"此句用“induction”（入学介绍）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 确定","en":"A persuasive proposal acknowledges what it cannot yet establish.","zh":"有说服力的提案会承认尚不能确定之处。","word":"establish","meaning":"确定","tip":"此句用“establish”（确定）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 拨付","en":"Funding should be released only when the training plan is agreed.","zh":"只有培训计划获同意后才应拨付资金。","word":"released","meaning":"拨付","tip":"此句用“released”（拨付）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (拟议计划): The ___ would help new students navigate routine college life.","answers":["proposed scheme"],"explanation":"原句用“proposed scheme”表达“拟议计划”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (自愿的): Participation should be ___ for both mentors and new students.","answers":["voluntary"],"explanation":"原句用“voluntary”表达“自愿的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of SUPERVISE: A small mentoring cohort makes ___ manageable.","answers":["supervision"],"explanation":"空格需要“supervision”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: Funding will be released only if the training plan is agreed. Required opening: Funding will be released provided …","answers":["Funding will be released provided that the training plan is agreed."],"explanation":"参考答案：Funding will be released provided that the training plan is agreed. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a funding proposal for the mentoring scheme. Define its scope, costs, supervision, alternatives and criteria for deciding whether to continue after one term.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"I propose a small peer-mentoring trial for new students, with a named coordinator and supervised referrals. The scheme should help students navigate college life while keeping professional advice within the appropriate services. A mentor's value may lie in helping a student locate the right service, not in answering every question personally. Training should therefore include how to recognise a concern outside the mentor's role. The committee can authorise a bounded trial while requiring a coordinator to check the quality of referrals and contact.\n\nThe proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life. Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers. A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable. The budget must include training as well as publicity. Potential benefits depend on consistent contact and clear boundaries. Participation should be voluntary for both mentors and new students.\n\nWe should specify how a mentor can refer a difficult concern. The proposal identifies a coordinator rather than assuming shared responsibility. A review after one term would inform any expansion. The alternative is to improve existing induction sessions first. A persuasive proposal acknowledges what it cannot yet establish. Funding should be released only when the training plan is agreed.\n\nFunding should depend on the training and supervision plan being ready. A one-term review can assess whether the limited scheme is useful before the college recruits a larger group of mentors."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Proposing a practical mentoring scheme”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"A small mentoring scheme seems worthwhile if the college provides supervision and keeps the role clear. Mentors should guide students toward suitable support. The proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life. Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers. A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable. The budget must include training as well as publicity. The review should examine referrals and contact, not just recruitment totals. If mentors were regularly being asked to solve specialist problems, I would revise the training or scope before expanding."},"project":"完成“Proposing a practical mentoring scheme”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Applicants presenting proposals on Monday should bring a one-page budget and a brief implementation timetable. We do not need a polished promotional video. You will have five minutes to explain the problem, your proposed response and the evidence that a trial is justified. The panel will then ask questions for another five minutes. Please distinguish confirmed contributions from support you have merely requested. If a partner has not yet agreed to provide a venue, say so and explain your alternative. The maximum grant is three thousand pounds, but requesting the maximum is not a requirement. Projects may begin in January only after the award letter and conditions have been accepted. Do not make purchases now on the assumption that the application will succeed. A short written decision will be sent within two weeks.","questions":[{"prompt":"What materials are requested?","answer":"A one-page budget and timetable","options":["A one-page budget and timetable","A promotional video only","Proof that every purchase is complete"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“A one-page budget and timetable”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"How should unconfirmed partner support appear?","answer":"Clearly as requested rather than confirmed","options":["Clearly as requested rather than confirmed","As guaranteed income","Not mentioned even if essential"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Clearly as requested rather than confirmed”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"When may funded purchases begin?","answer":"After award conditions are accepted","options":["After award conditions are accepted","Before the panel meeting","Whenever an applicant expects success"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“After award conditions are accepted”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":59,"title":"论证与修改：提升结构和精确性","target":"C1","focus":"用反馈重组论证而非只改拼写","grammar":"段落推进；反证；修订说明","prerequisite":"完成第58章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Revising an argument about public classes","text":"A fictional essay argues that a town should offer more free evening classes. Its first draft lists benefits enthusiastically but overlooks who can attend and what the budget would displace. The revision workshop asks learners to preserve a defensible position while making its reasoning more precise. Effective editing may require moving paragraphs, narrowing claims or acknowledging a serious objection. A longer essay is not automatically a stronger one.\n\nA revised essay should make clear which classes deserve initial funding and why. It may support free provision while recognising that timetable, transport and support determine who can benefit. This is a stronger position than praising education generally and leaving the reader unable to identify a decision the town should actually make.\n\nThe original claim is broader than the evidence can support. I would narrow it to classes with demonstrable local demand. The access paragraph belongs before the proposed timetable. A counterargument should influence the recommendation, not merely decorate it. The budget creates a trade-off with other community services. An example of one successful class cannot represent every subject.\n\nThis paragraph repeats the opening without advancing the reasoning. The revision should preserve the useful distinction between price and access. Free tuition does not remove transport or childcare barriers. My change log explains why I replaced an absolute claim. I retained the conclusion but made its conditions explicit. A final accuracy check comes after the argument has been reorganised."},"sourceB":{"title":"An editorial team revises a transport article","text":"A student magazine article originally argued that every commuter should cycle. The editor asked the writer to test the word every against readers with mobility limitations, long journeys or caring responsibilities. The writer first added a brief sentence acknowledging exceptions but left the headline and conclusion unchanged. The inconsistency remained: the qualification did not actually alter the argument.\n\nIn the next draft, the central claim became that the college should make short, feasible cycling journeys easier. This narrower claim supported concrete proposals about secure parking, route information and changing facilities. It also allowed the article to explain why bus access and accessible parking still mattered. The writer replaced a dramatic story about one journey with evidence about facilities that students had reported using. He clearly identified the limits of that informal information rather than presenting it as a complete travel survey.\n\nThe revised article was less sweeping but more persuasive. Its paragraphs moved from the practical problem to the proposed response, then considered who might remain excluded. The conclusion returned to that defined purpose instead of introducing a new argument about national transport policy. In a revision note, the writer explained that he had changed the scope of the claim, reorganised the evidence and retained the main commitment to making active travel possible. The editor approved the draft while requesting a final check of the source links and the accuracy of the facility descriptions."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The original claim is broader than the evidence can support.","options":["The original claim is broader than the evidence can support.","It is not true that the original claim is broader than the evidence can support.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The original claim is broader than the evidence can support.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"I would narrow it to classes with demonstrable local demand.","options":["I would narrow it to classes with demonstrable local demand.","It is not true that i would narrow it to classes with demonstrable local demand.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“I would narrow it to classes with demonstrable local demand.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The access paragraph belongs before the proposed timetable.","options":["The access paragraph belongs before the proposed timetable.","It is not true that the access paragraph belongs before the proposed timetable.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The access paragraph belongs before the proposed timetable.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why did the first qualification fail?","answer":"The headline and conclusion still made the universal claim","options":["The headline and conclusion still made the universal claim","It removed the entire topic","It supplied too much numerical evidence"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An editorial team revises a transport article”支持“The headline and conclusion still made the universal claim”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What was the revised central claim?","answer":"Make short feasible cycling journeys easier","options":["Make short feasible cycling journeys easier","Require every commuter to cycle","Remove bus access"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An editorial team revises a transport article”支持“Make short feasible cycling journeys easier”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What final checks were requested?","answer":"Sources and facility descriptions","options":["Sources and facility descriptions","Only spelling of the title","A new national transport survey"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An editorial team revises a transport article”支持“Sources and facility descriptions”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 更广的","en":"The original claim is broader than the evidence can support.","zh":"原主张比证据能支持的范围更广。","word":"broader","meaning":"更广的","tip":"此句用“broader”（更广的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 收窄","en":"I would narrow it to classes with demonstrable local demand.","zh":"我会把它收窄到当地有可证需求的课程。","word":"narrow","meaning":"收窄","tip":"此句用“narrow”（收窄）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 应置于","en":"The access paragraph belongs before the proposed timetable.","zh":"可及性段落应位于建议时间表之前。","word":"belong","meaning":"应置于","tip":"此句用“belong”（应置于）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 反方论点","en":"A counterargument should influence the recommendation, not merely decorate it.","zh":"反方论点应影响建议，而非仅作装饰。","word":"counterargument","meaning":"反方论点","tip":"此句用“counterargument”（反方论点）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 取舍","en":"The budget creates a trade-off with other community services.","zh":"预算与其他社区服务形成取舍。","word":"trade-off","meaning":"取舍","tip":"此句用“trade-off”（取舍）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 代表","en":"An example of one successful class cannot represent every subject.","zh":"一门成功课程的例子不能代表所有科目。","word":"represent","meaning":"代表","tip":"此句用“represent”（代表）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 推进","en":"This paragraph repeats the opening without advancing the reasoning.","zh":"这一段重复开头，没有推进推理。","word":"advancing","meaning":"推进","tip":"此句用“advancing”（推进）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 区分","en":"The revision should preserve the useful distinction between price and access.","zh":"修改应保留价格与可及性的有用区分。","word":"distinction","meaning":"区分","tip":"此句用“distinction”（区分）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 障碍","en":"Free tuition does not remove transport or childcare barriers.","zh":"免学费并不能消除交通或托育障碍。","word":"barrier","meaning":"障碍","tip":"此句用“barrier”（障碍）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 绝对主张","en":"My change log explains why I replaced an absolute claim.","zh":"我的修改记录解释为何替换绝对主张。","word":"absolute claim","meaning":"绝对主张","tip":"此句用“absolute claim”（绝对主张）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 保留","en":"I retained the conclusion but made its conditions explicit.","zh":"我保留结论，但明确其条件。","word":"retain","meaning":"保留","tip":"此句用“retain”（保留）连接让步或转折，保留前后两个观点的关系。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 重组","en":"A final accuracy check comes after the argument has been reorganised.","zh":"论证重组之后，再进行最终准确性检查。","word":"reorganise","meaning":"重组","tip":"此句用“reorganise”（重组）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (更广的): The original claim is ___ than the evidence can support.","answers":["broader"],"explanation":"原句用“broader”表达“更广的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (代表): An example of one successful class cannot ___ every subject.","answers":["represent"],"explanation":"原句用“represent”表达“代表”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of REVISE: The change log explains the purpose of each ___.","answers":["revision"],"explanation":"空格需要“revision”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: Free tuition does not remove childcare barriers. Required opening: Childcare barriers remain even when …","answers":["Childcare barriers remain even when tuition is free."],"explanation":"参考答案：Childcare barriers remain even when tuition is free. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a revised argument for selected free evening classes. Address access and opportunity cost, then add a revision note explaining two substantive choices.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The town should fund selected evening classes where demand and access arrangements justify them. Free tuition can be valuable, but it is not a complete answer to the barriers potential learners face. A revised essay should make clear which classes deserve initial funding and why. It may support free provision while recognising that timetable, transport and support determine who can benefit. This is a stronger position than praising education generally and leaving the reader unable to identify a decision the town should actually make.\n\nThe original claim is broader than the evidence can support. I would narrow it to classes with demonstrable local demand. The access paragraph belongs before the proposed timetable. A counterargument should influence the recommendation, not merely decorate it. The budget creates a trade-off with other community services. An example of one successful class cannot represent every subject.\n\nThis paragraph repeats the opening without advancing the reasoning. The revision should preserve the useful distinction between price and access. Free tuition does not remove transport or childcare barriers. My change log explains why I replaced an absolute claim. I retained the conclusion but made its conditions explicit. A final accuracy check comes after the argument has been reorganised.\n\nThis narrower argument preserves support for learning while recognising real trade-offs. The next draft should check the demand evidence and explain which access measures are essential to the initial programme."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Revising an argument about public classes”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I would preserve the essay's support for evening learning but narrow its recommendation. The town needs to know which action the writer actually favours. The original claim is broader than the evidence can support. I would narrow it to classes with demonstrable local demand. The access paragraph belongs before the proposed timetable. A counterargument should influence the recommendation, not merely decorate it. My revision would address transport and caring barriers as well as fees. I would explain why the claim changed and check whether the selected evidence really supports the recommendation that remains."},"project":"完成“Revising an argument about public classes”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I have returned your essay with comments on the argument, not just corrections to individual sentences. For the next draft, choose two comments that require a substantial change and explain how you responded. You do not have to accept every suggestion, but give a reason if you retain a passage I questioned. The paragraph about cost should appear before the final recommendation, because the reader needs that information to judge your proposal. Please verify the quotation in paragraph three; if you cannot locate the original, remove the quotation marks and either paraphrase accurately with a source or omit the claim. Do not add extra examples simply to reach a higher word count. Bring the revised version and a short change log next Wednesday. We will compare how the reasoning develops.","questions":[{"prompt":"What should the change log explain?","answer":"Responses to two substantial comments","options":["Responses to two substantial comments","Every keystroke","Only a new word count"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Responses to two substantial comments”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"Where should the cost paragraph appear?","answer":"Before the final recommendation","options":["Before the final recommendation","After all references","Only in the change log"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Before the final recommendation”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What should happen to an unverifiable quotation?","answer":"It must be checked, accurately sourced or omitted","options":["It must be checked, accurately sourced or omitted","It should remain unchanged","It should be attributed to the tutor"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“It must be checked, accurately sourced or omitted”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":60,"title":"C1综合项目：从资料到答辩","target":"C1","focus":"独立完成综合报告并回应质疑","grammar":"综合论证；准确归因；答辩澄清","prerequisite":"完成第59章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Designing an accessible community learning weekend","text":"The original integrated project concerns a fictional learning weekend combining practical workshops, talks and peer support. Learners act as an advisory team, not as event promoters. They must compare demand with capacity, identify access requirements and propose a limited programme with an evaluation plan. The project brings together reading, explanation, negotiation and revision. Completing it provides evidence for review but does not by itself certify C1 proficiency.\n\nA single weekend cannot demonstrate every long-term educational outcome. It can nevertheless provide evidence about access, participation and the feasibility of activities. The advisory team should identify which findings would support repetition, which would require redesign and which questions would remain unanswered even if visitors enjoyed the event.\n\nOur central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance. The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators. We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support. The access plan includes a route for requesting individual adjustments. Demand remains uncertain because the survey reached existing members only. The project can proceed in a limited form while that gap is investigated.\n\nI would respond to that challenge by narrowing the claim. The proposed measure captures participation, not learning by itself. A review should include work produced as well as participant comments. Our report records the reasoning behind each significant trade-off. The final presentation should invite questions about uncertain assumptions. Independent language review remains necessary before claiming a C1 level."},"sourceB":{"title":"A panel reviews a regional exchange project","text":"A regional association invited a team to defend its proposal for an exchange between two adult-learning centres. The written submission described shared classes, staff visits and a digital resource collection. Panel members asked which of these activities was essential to the project's purpose and which could wait. The team initially presented every activity as equally important, making the budget difficult to assess.\n\nDuring questions, the team clarified that the central purpose was to develop joint teaching materials through staff collaboration. Student visits were desirable but not necessary for the first phase. This distinction allowed a smaller proposal with clear outputs: two jointly designed modules, recorded trial feedback and a revision meeting. The panel also asked how the team would avoid interpreting polite praise as proof of educational benefit. In response, the team proposed examining learner work before and after each module, while acknowledging that a small project would not establish broad causal effects.\n\nThe panel requested a revised timetable and explicit consent arrangements for sharing anonymised learner work. It did not reject the collaboration or approve the original full budget. The team would receive a decision after those conditions were checked. In its reflection, the team noted that a defensible project could change during questioning without losing its core purpose. Evidence of effective advanced communication included accurate explanation, recognition of uncertainty and a coherent response to challenge, not simply long answers delivered with confidence."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"Our central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance.","options":["Our central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance.","It is not true that our central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Our central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators.","options":["The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators.","It is not true that the first programme should match the number of trained facilitators.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support.","options":["We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support.","It is not true that we have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What became the first-phase central purpose?","answer":"Joint teaching-material development through staff collaboration","options":["Joint teaching-material development through staff collaboration","Immediate large-scale student travel","A publicity campaign only"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A panel reviews a regional exchange project”支持“Joint teaching-material development through staff collaboration”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"How would educational benefit be explored?","answer":"By examining learner work around each module","options":["By examining learner work around each module","By counting polite praise alone","By assuming every participant improved"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A panel reviews a regional exchange project”支持“By examining learner work around each module”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What did the panel request?","answer":"A revised timetable and consent arrangements","options":["A revised timetable and consent arrangements","Cancellation of all collaboration","Immediate spending of the full budget"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A panel reviews a regional exchange project”支持“A revised timetable and consent arrangements”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 核心目的","en":"Our central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance.","zh":"我们的核心目的是可及学习，而非最大人数。","word":"central purpose","meaning":"核心目的","tip":"此句用“central purpose”（核心目的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 匹配","en":"The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators.","zh":"首期安排应与受训引导员人数相匹配。","word":"match","meaning":"匹配","tip":"此句用“match”（匹配）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 已确认的","en":"We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support.","zh":"我们已经区分确认资源和申请中的支持。","word":"confirmed","meaning":"已确认的","tip":"此句用“confirmed”（已确认的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 调整","en":"The access plan includes a route for requesting individual adjustments.","zh":"可及性计划包含申请个别调整的渠道。","word":"adjustment","meaning":"调整","tip":"此句用“adjustment”（调整）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 触及","en":"Demand remains uncertain because the survey reached existing members only.","zh":"需求仍不确定，因为调查仅触及现有会员。","word":"reach","meaning":"触及","tip":"此句用“reach”（触及）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 有限形式","en":"The project can proceed in a limited form while that gap is investigated.","zh":"调查该缺口期间，项目可以有限形式开展。","word":"limited form","meaning":"有限形式","tip":"此句用“limited form”（有限形式）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 质疑","en":"I would respond to that challenge by narrowing the claim.","zh":"我会通过收窄主张来回应这一质疑。","word":"challenge","meaning":"质疑","tip":"此句用“challenge”（质疑）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 衡量体现","en":"The proposed measure captures participation, not learning by itself.","zh":"拟议指标衡量参与，而非学习本身。","word":"capture","meaning":"衡量体现","tip":"此句用“capture”（衡量体现）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 产出作品","en":"A review should include work produced as well as participant comments.","zh":"复核应包括产出作品及参与者意见。","word":"work produced","meaning":"产出作品","tip":"此句用“work produced”（产出作品）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 推理理由","en":"Our report records the reasoning behind each significant trade-off.","zh":"我们的报告记录每项重要取舍背后的理由。","word":"reasoning","meaning":"推理理由","tip":"此句用“reasoning”（推理理由）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 假设","en":"The final presentation should invite questions about uncertain assumptions.","zh":"最终展示应接受对不确定假设的提问。","word":"assumption","meaning":"假设","tip":"此句用“assumption”（假设）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 独立语言评估","en":"Independent language review remains necessary before claiming a C1 level.","zh":"在声称达到C1前，仍需独立语言评估。","word":"Independent language review","meaning":"独立语言评估","tip":"此句用“Independent language review”（独立语言评估）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (核心目的): Our ___ is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance.","answers":["central purpose"],"explanation":"原句用“central purpose”表达“核心目的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (有限形式): The project can proceed in a ___ while that gap is investigated.","answers":["limited form"],"explanation":"原句用“limited form”表达“有限形式”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of PARTICIPATE: Attendance records measure ___ rather than learning alone.","answers":["participation"],"explanation":"空格需要“participation”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The project can continue only if access arrangements are agreed. Required opening: The project can continue provided …","answers":["The project can continue provided that access arrangements are agreed."],"explanation":"参考答案：The project can continue provided that access arrangements are agreed. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write the advisory report for the learning weekend. Define scope, evidence, access, budget responsibilities and evaluation, then prepare responses to two serious objections.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"The learning weekend should begin with a manageable programme designed around access and available facilitation. Its evaluation must distinguish participation, satisfaction and evidence of learning rather than treat them as interchangeable. A single weekend cannot demonstrate every long-term educational outcome. It can nevertheless provide evidence about access, participation and the feasibility of activities. The advisory team should identify which findings would support repetition, which would require redesign and which questions would remain unanswered even if visitors enjoyed the event.\n\nOur central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance. The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators. We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support. The access plan includes a route for requesting individual adjustments. Demand remains uncertain because the survey reached existing members only. The project can proceed in a limited form while that gap is investigated.\n\nI would respond to that challenge by narrowing the claim. The proposed measure captures participation, not learning by itself. A review should include work produced as well as participant comments. Our report records the reasoning behind each significant trade-off. The final presentation should invite questions about uncertain assumptions. Independent language review remains necessary before claiming a C1 level.\n\nThe advisory team should defend its recommendation but remain willing to revise assumptions during questions. Independent language review should evaluate that interaction separately from the attractiveness of the event proposal."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Designing an accessible community learning weekend”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"The weekend project should put accessible learning ahead of a headline attendance figure. Its scale has to fit the people available to facilitate it. Our central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance. The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators. We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support. The access plan includes a route for requesting individual adjustments. The evaluation should include what participants produce and how access works. I would welcome questions that reveal a weak assumption, since revising the proposal may make it more defensible."},"project":"完成“Designing an accessible community learning weekend”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Your project review will take place on Tuesday afternoon. Submit the report by noon on Monday so that the reviewers have time to read it. Begin your presentation with the problem and recommendation; you do not need to repeat the whole report. After six minutes, the reviewers will ask questions for another six minutes. One question will concern an alternative explanation or a practical limitation. You may refer to your source notes, but the answers must be your own. If you discover a factual error during discussion, correct it clearly rather than defending it. After the review, you will have forty-eight hours to submit a short reflection explaining any changes to your proposal. The review is a source of learning evidence, not an official language certificate. Language and project reasoning will be recorded separately.","questions":[{"prompt":"When is the report due?","answer":"Noon on Monday","options":["Noon on Monday","Tuesday evening","Forty-eight hours after the review"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Noon on Monday”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What should the presentation begin with?","answer":"The problem and recommendation","options":["The problem and recommendation","Every page of the report","Only a source list"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The problem and recommendation”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What is submitted after the review?","answer":"A reflection within forty-eight hours","options":["A reflection within forty-eight hours","An official certificate","A memorised transcript"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“A reflection within forty-eight hours”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":61,"title":"雅思听读：把综合能力转成答题流程","target":"C1／雅思专项","focus":"定位、改述、核查与限时管理","grammar":"同义改述；判断信息；指代","prerequisite":"完成第60章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Reading a training-centre policy precisely","text":"This original practice briefing describes a fictional training centre, not an official IELTS test. Learners practise identifying scope words, recognising paraphrase and checking whether a claim is stated, contradicted or simply absent. Accurate reading matters more than matching an isolated word. The listening practice will use an unrelated notice so that familiarity with this text cannot substitute for understanding new information. Exam-specific performance should later be checked against authorised official practice material.\n\nUnder time pressure, learners sometimes choose an answer because it repeats a striking word. A more reliable method is to identify the claim, locate the relevant section and test every important condition. Listening adds the need to follow corrections, while reading allows deliberate return to a passage for verification.\n\nThe centre welcomes applicants with varied educational backgrounds. An applicant must still demonstrate readiness for the chosen course. A recommendation is not the same as a compulsory requirement. The word “normally” leaves room for an exception. We should not infer a fee waiver merely because none is mentioned. A statement can be absent without being contradicted.\n\nThe relevant detail may be expressed through a paraphrase. After locating the passage, I would read the surrounding qualification. An answer must satisfy the stated word or number limit. If the audio corrects a detail, the final version usually matters. I would leave time to check spelling and singular or plural forms. Practice scores should be interpreted using the relevant test guidance."},"sourceB":{"title":"A research-residency application guide","text":"The Harbour Research Centre offers short residencies to people developing projects about coastal communities. Applicants do not need a university affiliation, but they must explain how their work would use the centre's archive or local contacts. A general wish to work beside the sea is not sufficient. The programme provides a desk and archive access; accommodation is not included, although a small travel allowance may be available to selected applicants.\n\nApplications are assessed first for relevance and feasibility. A panel then considers how the projects would contribute to discussion among residents. Previous publications can support an application, but they are not compulsory. The centre does not publish a fixed preference for either academic or creative work. Applicants should submit a proposal of no more than eight hundred words and a separate timetable. Material exceeding the limit will not be read beyond the stated maximum.\n\nSuccessful applicants must attend an induction before using original archive documents. They may arrange remote meetings before arrival, but remote participation does not replace the residency itself. Each resident will give an informal talk near the end of the visit. The centre will not claim ownership of resulting work; acknowledgement of its support is required in any related publication. Candidates whose projects change substantially after acceptance should contact the coordinator before making travel commitments. A place is offered for the approved project, not an unrestricted entitlement to use every facility."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"The centre welcomes applicants with varied educational backgrounds.","options":["The centre welcomes applicants with varied educational backgrounds.","It is not true that the centre welcomes applicants with varied educational backgrounds.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“The centre welcomes applicants with varied educational backgrounds.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"An applicant must still demonstrate readiness for the chosen course.","options":["An applicant must still demonstrate readiness for the chosen course.","It is not true that an applicant must still demonstrate readiness for the chosen course.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“An applicant must still demonstrate readiness for the chosen course.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"A recommendation is not the same as a compulsory requirement.","options":["A recommendation is not the same as a compulsory requirement.","It is not true that a recommendation is not the same as a compulsory requirement.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“A recommendation is not the same as a compulsory requirement.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What is not included automatically?","answer":"Accommodation","options":["Accommodation","A desk","Archive access"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A research-residency application guide”支持“Accommodation”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Are previous publications compulsory?","answer":"No, although they may support an application","options":["No, although they may support an application","Yes, for every applicant","Only for creative applicants"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A research-residency application guide”支持“No, although they may support an application”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What must precede handling original archive documents?","answer":"An induction","options":["An induction","Publication of a final book","Payment for accommodation"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A research-residency application guide”支持“An induction”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 多样的","en":"The centre welcomes applicants with varied educational backgrounds.","zh":"中心欢迎教育背景不同的申请者。","word":"varied","meaning":"多样的","tip":"此句用“varied”（多样的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 准备程度","en":"An applicant must still demonstrate readiness for the chosen course.","zh":"申请者仍须证明已为所选课程做好准备。","word":"readiness","meaning":"准备程度","tip":"此句用“readiness”（准备程度）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 强制的","en":"A recommendation is not the same as a compulsory requirement.","zh":"建议与强制要求并不相同。","word":"compulsory","meaning":"强制的","tip":"此句用“compulsory”（强制的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 通常","en":"The word “normally” leaves room for an exception.","zh":"“通常”一词保留了例外空间。","word":"normally","meaning":"通常","tip":"此句用“normally”（通常）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 费用减免","en":"We should not infer a fee waiver merely because none is mentioned.","zh":"不能仅因未提及便推断有费用减免。","word":"fee waiver","meaning":"费用减免","tip":"此句用“fee waiver”（费用减免）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 被否定","en":"A statement can be absent without being contradicted.","zh":"一项说法可以未被提及，而非被否定。","word":"contradicted","meaning":"被否定","tip":"此句用“contradicted”（被否定）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 改述","en":"The relevant detail may be expressed through a paraphrase.","zh":"相关细节可能以改述方式表达。","word":"paraphrase","meaning":"改述","tip":"此句用“paraphrase”（改述）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 限定条件","en":"After locating the passage, I would read the surrounding qualification.","zh":"定位段落后，我会阅读周围的限定条件。","word":"qualification","meaning":"限定条件","tip":"此句用“qualification”（限定条件）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 限制","en":"An answer must satisfy the stated word or number limit.","zh":"答案必须满足规定的词数或数字限制。","word":"limit","meaning":"限制","tip":"此句用“limit”（限制）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 修正","en":"If the audio corrects a detail, the final version usually matters.","zh":"如果录音修正细节，最终版本通常重要。","word":"corrects","meaning":"修正","tip":"此句用“corrects”（修正）说明结论成立的条件。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 单数","en":"I would leave time to check spelling and singular or plural forms.","zh":"我会留时间检查拼写及单复数。","word":"singular","meaning":"单数","tip":"此句用“singular”（单数）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 指导","en":"Practice scores should be interpreted using the relevant test guidance.","zh":"练习分数应按对应考试指导解读。","word":"guidance","meaning":"指导","tip":"此句用“guidance”（指导）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (多样的): The centre welcomes applicants with ___ educational backgrounds.","answers":["varied"],"explanation":"原句用“varied”表达“多样的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (被否定): A statement can be absent without being ___.","answers":["contradicted"],"explanation":"原句用“contradicted”表达“被否定”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of READY: Course completion alone cannot establish examination ___.","answers":["readiness"],"explanation":"空格需要“readiness”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: The source does not mention a fee waiver. Required opening: No fee waiver is mentioned …","answers":["No fee waiver is mentioned in the source."],"explanation":"参考答案：No fee waiver is mentioned in the source. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a study plan explaining how you will practise unfamiliar IELTS listening and reading material. Identify the errors you will log, the checks you will apply and the evidence needed before estimating readiness.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"My preparation plan will combine unfamiliar listening and reading tasks with a record of recurring errors. The aim is to improve accurate interpretation under time pressure, rather than memorise a familiar set of answers. Under time pressure, learners sometimes choose an answer because it repeats a striking word. A more reliable method is to identify the claim, locate the relevant section and test every important condition. Listening adds the need to follow corrections, while reading allows deliberate return to a passage for verification.\n\nThe centre welcomes applicants with varied educational backgrounds. An applicant must still demonstrate readiness for the chosen course. A recommendation is not the same as a compulsory requirement. The word “normally” leaves room for an exception. We should not infer a fee waiver merely because none is mentioned. A statement can be absent without being contradicted.\n\nThe relevant detail may be expressed through a paraphrase. After locating the passage, I would read the surrounding qualification. An answer must satisfy the stated word or number limit. If the audio corrects a detail, the final version usually matters. I would leave time to check spelling and singular or plural forms. Practice scores should be interpreted using the relevant test guidance.\n\nI will review spelling, limits and the reasons for wrong choices, then retest using different material. Any readiness estimate should draw on appropriately assessed writing and speaking as well as listening and reading."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Reading a training-centre policy precisely”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"My preparation would focus on unfamiliar material and the reasons for errors. Repeating a known task may improve fluency without establishing examination readiness. The centre welcomes applicants with varied educational backgrounds. An applicant must still demonstrate readiness for the chosen course. A recommendation is not the same as a compulsory requirement. The word “normally” leaves room for an exception. I would log the interpretation that led to each mistake and test the same skill with new material later. A final readiness judgement needs the other skills reviewed appropriately too."},"project":"完成“Reading a training-centre policy precisely”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Hello, I am returning your message about the evening photography course. There are still places in the Thursday group, which starts on the twelfth of November. The Tuesday group is full, but I can add your name to its waiting list if Thursday is impossible. Each session runs from six thirty to eight thirty, with a short break. You do not need to buy a camera before the first class; the tutor will explain the equipment options and the centre has a few loan cameras. The fee is ninety-six pounds for the six-session course, and printing costs are extra. Please complete the online form by Friday, but wait for our confirmation before making payment. If you have difficulty with the form, come to reception between ten and four and we can help.","questions":[{"prompt":"Which group has places?","answer":"The Thursday group","options":["The Thursday group","The Tuesday group","Only a weekend group"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The Thursday group”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What should the applicant do before payment?","answer":"Wait for confirmation","options":["Wait for confirmation","Buy a camera","Pay printing costs"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Wait for confirmation”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What is extra to the course fee?","answer":"Printing","options":["Printing","The first class","The short break"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Printing”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":62,"title":"雅思学术写作Task 1：准确描述数据","target":"C1／雅思专项","focus":"概括主要特征并作准确比较","grammar":"比例与变化；概述；数据限定","prerequisite":"完成第61章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"How library visits changed","text":"The following table is fictional practice data. It reports annual visits to three branches of a library network, in thousands. Each column uses the same counting method; the figures count visits, not unique people. No information is provided about changes in opening hours, population or promotion. Therefore, a description may compare the figures but must not invent reasons for the differences.\n\n| Branch | 2020 | 2022 | 2024 |\n| Riverside | 40 | 50 | 65 |\n| Hilltop | 60 | 55 | 50 |\n| Central | 100 | 110 | 125 |\n\nUse the table rather than outside knowledge. Write a clear overview and select comparisons that help the reader see the main patterns.\n\nThe difference between an increase by a stated amount and an increase to a final value is essential here. In addition, Central's larger total does not mean its proportional growth was greatest. A concise response can show both comparisons while acknowledging that the table supplies no causal explanation.\n\nCentral recorded the highest number of visits in all three years. Visits to Riverside rose from forty thousand to sixty-five thousand. Hilltop was the only branch to record a decline. Central gained twenty-five thousand visits over the period. Riverside also increased by twenty-five thousand visits. The equal numerical increases represented different proportional changes.\n\nHilltop fell from sixty thousand visits to fifty thousand. The network total rose from two hundred thousand to two hundred forty thousand. By 2024, Central exceeded Riverside by sixty thousand visits. Riverside overtook Hilltop between the two final observations. The table does not explain the reasons for these changes. An overview should highlight growth in two branches and decline in one."},"sourceB":{"title":"A comparison of training participation","text":"A fictional adult college reports enrolments in three evening subjects for 2021 and 2024. Digital skills rose from eighty to one hundred and forty enrolments, languages increased from one hundred to one hundred and twenty, and art remained at sixty. These are enrolments rather than individual learners; a person taking two subjects appears twice in the total. The college has not supplied information about course fees, advertising or the number of classes offered.\n\nThe total rose from two hundred and forty to three hundred and twenty enrolments. Digital skills accounted for the largest numerical increase and became the biggest subject by the later year. Languages had been the largest category at the start. Art's number was unchanged, but its share of the larger total declined. A description that calls art participation a numerical decline would therefore be inaccurate.\n\nThe figures support a comparison of scale and change across the subjects. They do not establish why people enrolled or whether the courses improved their skills. Nor do two observations establish a steady year-by-year trend. The college's communication officer was asked to write a concise factual summary with an overview, selecting important contrasts rather than repeating every number separately. She also checked that a rise of sixty enrolments was described as an absolute increase, not confused with sixty percent. Accurate reporting required attention to the unit and the denominator as well as fluent language."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"Central recorded the highest number of visits in all three years.","options":["Central recorded the highest number of visits in all three years.","It is not true that central recorded the highest number of visits in all three years.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Central recorded the highest number of visits in all three years.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Visits to Riverside rose from forty thousand to sixty-five thousand.","options":["Visits to Riverside rose from forty thousand to sixty-five thousand.","It is not true that visits to Riverside rose from forty thousand to sixty-five thousand.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Visits to Riverside rose from forty thousand to sixty-five thousand.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Hilltop was the only branch to record a decline.","options":["Hilltop was the only branch to record a decline.","It is not true that hilltop was the only branch to record a decline.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Hilltop was the only branch to record a decline.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which subject was largest in 2024?","answer":"Digital skills","options":["Digital skills","Languages","Art"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A comparison of training participation”支持“Digital skills”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What happened to art enrolments?","answer":"The number was unchanged but its share declined","options":["The number was unchanged but its share declined","The number fell to zero","The number doubled"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A comparison of training participation”支持“The number was unchanged but its share declined”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What cannot be established from two observations?","answer":"A steady year-by-year trend","options":["A steady year-by-year trend","The total at each reported year","Which subject increased most numerically"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A comparison of training participation”支持“A steady year-by-year trend”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 记录了","en":"Central recorded the highest number of visits in all three years.","zh":"中央分馆在三个年份的到访量均最高。","word":"recorded","meaning":"记录了","tip":"此句用“recorded”（记录了）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 从……上升","en":"Visits to Riverside rose from forty thousand to sixty-five thousand.","zh":"河畔分馆到访量从四万升至六万五千。","word":"rose from","meaning":"从……上升","tip":"此句用“rose from”（从……上升）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 下降","en":"Hilltop was the only branch to record a decline.","zh":"山顶分馆是唯一记录下降的分馆。","word":"decline","meaning":"下降","tip":"此句用“decline”（下降）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 增加","en":"Central gained twenty-five thousand visits over the period.","zh":"中央分馆在此期间增加了两万五千次到访。","word":"gain","meaning":"增加","tip":"此句用“gain”（增加）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 增加了","en":"Riverside also increased by twenty-five thousand visits.","zh":"河畔分馆也增加了两万五千次到访。","word":"increased by","meaning":"增加了","tip":"此句用“increased by”（增加了）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 比例的","en":"The equal numerical increases represented different proportional changes.","zh":"相同的数量增幅代表不同的比例变化。","word":"proportional","meaning":"比例的","tip":"此句用“proportional”（比例的）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 从……下降","en":"Hilltop fell from sixty thousand visits to fifty thousand.","zh":"山顶分馆从六万次降至五万次到访。","word":"fell from","meaning":"从……下降","tip":"此句用“fell from”（从……下降）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 总计","en":"The network total rose from two hundred thousand to two hundred forty thousand.","zh":"网络总到访量从二十万升至二十四万。","word":"total","meaning":"总计","tip":"此句用“total”（总计）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 超过","en":"By 2024, Central exceeded Riverside by sixty thousand visits.","zh":"到2024年，中央分馆比河畔多六万次到访。","word":"exceed","meaning":"超过","tip":"此句用“exceed”（超过）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 超过","en":"Riverside overtook Hilltop between the two final observations.","zh":"在最后两次观测之间，河畔分馆超过山顶分馆。","word":"overtook","meaning":"超过","tip":"此句用“overtook”（超过）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 解释","en":"The table does not explain the reasons for these changes.","zh":"表格没有解释这些变化的原因。","word":"explain","meaning":"解释","tip":"此句用“explain”（解释）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 概述","en":"An overview should highlight growth in two branches and decline in one.","zh":"概述应突出两个分馆增长、一个下降。","word":"overview","meaning":"概述","tip":"此句用“overview”（概述）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (记录了): Central ___ the highest number of visits in all three years.","answers":["recorded"],"explanation":"原句用“recorded”表达“记录了”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (比例的): The equal numerical increases represented different ___ changes.","answers":["proportional"],"explanation":"原句用“proportional”表达“比例的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of COMPARE: The overview should identify a meaningful ___ between branches.","answers":["comparison"],"explanation":"空格需要“comparison”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: Hilltop was the only branch whose visits declined. Required opening: Visits declined at Hilltop but …","answers":["Visits declined at Hilltop but not at the other two branches."],"explanation":"参考答案：Visits declined at Hilltop but not at the other two branches. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"The table gives annual library visits, in thousands: Riverside 40/50/65, Hilltop 60/55/50, Central 100/110/125 for 2020/2022/2024 respectively. Summarise the main features and make relevant comparisons. Write at least 150 words. Do not invent causes.","minimumWords":150,"sample":"The table compares annual visits, measured in thousands, to Riverside, Hilltop and Central libraries in 2020, 2022 and 2024. Overall, Central remained the busiest branch throughout, while visits increased at Central and Riverside but declined at Hilltop. The network as a whole recorded growth over the period.\n\nCentral's visits rose from 100,000 in 2020 to 110,000 in 2022 and then to 125,000 in 2024. Riverside followed an upward pattern, increasing from 40,000 to 50,000 and finally 65,000. Both branches therefore gained 25,000 visits, although Riverside's increase was proportionally larger because its starting figure was lower.\n\nBy contrast, Hilltop fell by 5,000 visits in each interval, from 60,000 to 55,000 and then 50,000. Riverside was initially below Hilltop, but by 2024 it exceeded Hilltop by 15,000 visits. Total visits across the three branches increased from 200,000 to 215,000 and then 240,000. The figures describe visits rather than unique visitors, and the table provides no information about the reasons for these changes."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “How library visits changed”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"The main pattern is that Central remained busiest, while Riverside grew and Hilltop declined. I would select comparisons rather than repeat the whole table. Central recorded the highest number of visits in all three years. Visits to Riverside rose from forty thousand to sixty-five thousand. Hilltop was the only branch to record a decline. Central gained twenty-five thousand visits over the period. The figures count visits, not different people. I would avoid guessing why the patterns occurred, and I would check that changes by an amount were not confused with final values."},"project":"完成“How library visits changed”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"For today's writing task, use only the table on the worksheet. It shows the number of journeys made by three transport modes in two separate years. Begin by identifying the unit and the years, then look for the largest categories and the most noticeable changes. You should not add a reason for a change unless the source actually supplies one. The overview does not need to include every number; it should convey the main pattern. Use selected figures later to support that overview. Remember that a percentage-point change differs from a percentage change. Before handing in your work, check that each comparison has a clear reference and that your verbs match the time period. You have twenty minutes for this practice, including checking. There is no need to write a personal opinion about which transport mode is best.","questions":[{"prompt":"What should writers avoid inventing?","answer":"Reasons not supplied by the source","options":["Reasons not supplied by the source","The stated unit","The reported years"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Reasons not supplied by the source”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What belongs in the overview?","answer":"The main pattern","options":["The main pattern","Every number separately","A personal transport preference"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“The main pattern”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"How long is the practice?","answer":"Twenty minutes including checking","options":["Twenty minutes including checking","Thirty minutes before checking","An unlimited period"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Twenty minutes including checking”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":63,"title":"雅思Task 2与口语：发展观点而非背模板","target":"C1／雅思专项","focus":"完成论证并灵活回应个人与抽象话题","grammar":"论点发展；例证；让步与概括","prerequisite":"完成第62章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Who should decide local learning priorities?","text":"This original practice topic asks whether local authorities should determine all publicly funded adult-learning priorities or give residents a stronger role. It is designed for argument development, not for memorising a universal essay. A convincing answer should address the extent of agreement, explain why its reasons matter and use a plausible example without presenting invented figures as research. Related speaking practice asks learners to move from a personal experience to a broader social question.\n\nA strong essay should explain how resident participation could improve decisions and how its limitations would be managed. The writer can then connect that balance to an explicit position. In speech, the same reasoning should develop naturally from a familiar example into a broader discussion rather than emerge as a memorised essay.\n\nResidents should influence priorities without being expected to design every course. Local knowledge can reveal needs that a standard survey overlooks. However, vocal participants may not represent the whole community. Authorities remain responsible for access, quality and public spending. A combined approach would use consultation within transparent criteria. For example, carers may need shorter sessions at predictable times.\n\nThe example illustrates a mechanism rather than proving a national trend. I agree to a considerable extent, but not without safeguards. My experience is relevant, although it cannot represent every learner. At a broader level, the issue is whose needs become visible. I would develop fewer reasons fully rather than list many briefly. A clear position should remain consistent throughout the answer."},"sourceB":{"title":"An essay workshop on public spaces","text":"An examination-preparation class discussed whether cities should devote more land to parks even when housing is scarce. The tutor did not ask learners to reproduce a model opinion. Instead, she asked each writer to define the decision being considered. Protecting an existing park, converting unused land and replacing occupied housing were different proposals with different consequences.\n\nOne draft claimed that parks always improved public health. The tutor suggested narrowing the claim and explaining the mechanism: accessible green space may support exercise and relaxation, but design, safety and maintenance affect whether residents use it. Another draft argued that housing should take priority because everyone needed somewhere to live. That reason was relevant, yet it did not answer whether a particular parcel of land was suitable for housing or whether the district already lacked recreation space.\n\nThe revised essays were expected to state a position and develop it through linked reasoning. Acknowledging the competing need did not require dividing the essay into two equally enthusiastic halves. Writers could favour one priority while specifying the circumstances under which the other deserved greater weight. The tutor also warned against invented statistics and memorised openings that added no meaning. In oral follow-up, learners compared a local place they knew with the wider planning problem. Effective preparation involved adapting language to the question and responding to challenge, rather than delivering the same polished answer regardless of the prompt."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"Residents should influence priorities without being expected to design every course.","options":["Residents should influence priorities without being expected to design every course.","It is not true that residents should influence priorities without being expected to design every course.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Residents should influence priorities without being expected to design every course.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Local knowledge can reveal needs that a standard survey overlooks.","options":["Local knowledge can reveal needs that a standard survey overlooks.","It is not true that local knowledge can reveal needs that a standard survey overlooks.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Local knowledge can reveal needs that a standard survey overlooks.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"However, vocal participants may not represent the whole community.","options":["However, vocal participants may not represent the whole community.","It is not true that however, vocal participants may not represent the whole community.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“However, vocal participants may not represent the whole community.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why distinguish different land decisions?","answer":"They have different consequences","options":["They have different consequences","They require identical answers","The question contains no trade-offs"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An essay workshop on public spaces”支持“They have different consequences”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"How should the health claim improve?","answer":"By narrowing it and explaining conditions","options":["By narrowing it and explaining conditions","By adding an invented percentage","By repeating the word always"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An essay workshop on public spaces”支持“By narrowing it and explaining conditions”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"What should oral follow-up demonstrate?","answer":"Adaptation from personal example to broader discussion","options":["Adaptation from personal example to broader discussion","An unchanged memorised answer","Only a list of technical planning terms"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“An essay workshop on public spaces”支持“Adaptation from personal example to broader discussion”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 影响","en":"Residents should influence priorities without being expected to design every course.","zh":"居民应影响优先事项，但不必设计每门课程。","word":"influence","meaning":"影响","tip":"此句用“influence”（影响）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 遗漏","en":"Local knowledge can reveal needs that a standard survey overlooks.","zh":"本地知识可揭示标准调查遗漏的需求。","word":"overlook","meaning":"遗漏","tip":"此句用“overlook”（遗漏）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 积极发声的","en":"However, vocal participants may not represent the whole community.","zh":"然而，积极发言者可能并不代表整个社区。","word":"vocal","meaning":"积极发声的","tip":"此句用“vocal”（积极发声的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 对……负责","en":"Authorities remain responsible for access, quality and public spending.","zh":"主管部门仍负责可及性、质量和公共支出。","word":"responsible for","meaning":"对……负责","tip":"此句用“responsible for”（对……负责）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 透明的","en":"A combined approach would use consultation within transparent criteria.","zh":"综合方法会在透明标准内开展协商。","word":"transparent","meaning":"透明的","tip":"此句用“transparent”（透明的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 可预测的","en":"For example, carers may need shorter sessions at predictable times.","zh":"例如，照护者可能需要时间稳定的短课。","word":"predictable","meaning":"可预测的","tip":"此句用“predictable”（可预测的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 说明","en":"The example illustrates a mechanism rather than proving a national trend.","zh":"例子说明机制，而非证明全国趋势。","word":"illustrate","meaning":"说明","tip":"此句用“illustrate”（说明）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 在相当程度上","en":"I agree to a considerable extent, but not without safeguards.","zh":"我在相当程度上赞同，但需要保障。","word":"to a considerable extent","meaning":"在相当程度上","tip":"此句用“to a considerable extent”（在相当程度上）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 代表","en":"My experience is relevant, although it cannot represent every learner.","zh":"我的经历相关，但不能代表每位学习者。","word":"represent","meaning":"代表","tip":"此句用“represent”（代表）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 更广层面","en":"At a broader level, the issue is whose needs become visible.","zh":"从更广层面看，问题是谁的需求被看见。","word":"broader level","meaning":"更广层面","tip":"此句用“broader level”（更广层面）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 展开","en":"I would develop fewer reasons fully rather than list many briefly.","zh":"我会充分展开较少理由，而非简列许多理由。","word":"develop","meaning":"展开","tip":"此句用“develop”（展开）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 一致的","en":"A clear position should remain consistent throughout the answer.","zh":"清楚的立场应贯穿回答并保持一致。","word":"consistent","meaning":"一致的","tip":"此句用“consistent”（一致的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (影响): Residents should ___ priorities without being expected to design every course.","answers":["influence"],"explanation":"原句用“influence”表达“影响”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (可预测的): For example, carers may need shorter sessions at ___ times.","answers":["predictable"],"explanation":"原句用“predictable”表达“可预测的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of CONSULT: Resident ___ should operate within transparent criteria.","answers":["consultation"],"explanation":"空格需要“consultation”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: Residents should contribute, but authorities retain responsibility. Required opening: Although residents should contribute, authorities …","answers":["Although residents should contribute, authorities retain responsibility."],"explanation":"参考答案：Although residents should contribute, authorities retain responsibility. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Some people believe local authorities should decide all priorities for publicly funded adult learning. Others think residents should have a stronger role. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. Write at least 250 words, developing reasons rather than listing slogans.","minimumWords":250,"sample":"Local authorities must be accountable for publicly funded adult education, but this does not mean that they should make every decision without residents. I favour a system in which public bodies set transparent standards and budgets while communities help identify needs and evaluate whether provision is useful.\n\nThe argument for central responsibility is substantial. Authorities can compare needs across neighbourhoods, maintain quality requirements and protect courses whose value is not immediately popular. If decisions were made only by whoever attended a public meeting, confident participants could secure resources while people with less time or influence remained unheard. Financial responsibility also requires someone to explain why one service has been funded instead of another.\n\nNevertheless, residents possess practical knowledge that a general plan may overlook. A course advertised as accessible may be impossible for carers to attend if it runs at unpredictable times. Workers may need short evening sessions rather than a full daytime programme. Consultation can expose these barriers before money is committed, provided that it reaches beyond existing students and organised groups.\n\nA combined approach would ask residents about problems and preferences, then assess proposals against published criteria such as access, demand, cost and educational value. A small trial could test an uncertain proposal before expansion. Feedback should influence subsequent decisions, but enthusiasm should not be mistaken for proof of learning.\n\nIn conclusion, authorities should retain responsibility for fairness and public expenditure while giving residents a meaningful role. The aim is not to transfer every technical decision to a public vote, but to ensure that professional planning remains informed by the people it is intended to serve."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Who should decide local learning priorities?”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"I think residents should have a meaningful voice, but public authorities should remain accountable for access, quality and the use of limited funding. Residents should influence priorities without being expected to design every course. Local knowledge can reveal needs that a standard survey overlooks. However, vocal participants may not represent the whole community. Authorities remain responsible for access, quality and public spending. Consultation should reach people beyond established groups and operate through transparent criteria. That balance lets local knowledge improve decisions without assuming that the loudest participants represent every potential learner."},"project":"完成“Who should decide local learning priorities?”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"For tomorrow's practice, please choose a place where you learned something useful outside school. In the first part I will ask a few short questions about your everyday habits. Then you will have a minute to prepare a longer answer about that place. You can use brief notes, but I will change the follow-up questions, so a memorised paragraph will not be enough. The final discussion will move to the wider question of how communities provide learning opportunities. You do not need to know government statistics. Explain your view, support it with a relevant example and acknowledge a limitation if necessary. We will record the session only with your agreement. Afterwards, we will listen for whether the answers developed naturally and remained relevant, rather than count how many unusual words you used.","questions":[{"prompt":"What should the learner choose?","answer":"A place of useful learning outside school","options":["A place of useful learning outside school","A government statistical report","A memorised paragraph"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“A place of useful learning outside school”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What will the final discussion concern?","answer":"Community learning opportunities","options":["Community learning opportunities","Only the learner’s daily timetable","The recording equipment"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Community learning opportunities”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What will the review prioritise?","answer":"Natural development and relevance","options":["Natural development and relevance","The number of unusual words alone","Perfect recall of fixed questions"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Natural development and relevance”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":64,"title":"独立评估：用证据决定下一步","target":"C1／雅思专项","focus":"分开课程完成、实际能力和考试准备","grammar":"自我评估；证据限定；行动计划","prerequisite":"完成第63章核心任务；能在无范例提示时解释观点并回应追问。","sourceA":{"title":"Building a defensible language portfolio","text":"The final original briefing concerns a fictional learner preparing an independent review. A portfolio can show completed tasks and responses to feedback, but familiar work alone does not establish how the learner handles new material. The review therefore includes unseen listening and reading, independent writing and interaction with another person. It reports strengths and limitations separately. Neither course completion nor a local transcription score is treated as an official CEFR or IELTS result.\n\nA reviewer needs enough information to distinguish a stable ability from a successful rehearsed performance. The learner should therefore label assistance, revisions and repeated tasks honestly. The resulting profile can guide further practice even when the evidence is too limited to support a single confident level for all four skills.\n\nMy portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work. Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer. I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed. Listening, reading, writing and interaction should be reviewed separately. A transcript cannot establish every aspect of spoken performance. An external reviewer should know what help was permitted.\n\nA disagreement between reviewers calls for examination of the evidence. I would treat an uneven profile as information for planning. The next plan should target recurring weaknesses rather than every possible error. A delayed task with new material helps check retention and transfer. Course completion is not an official proficiency certificate. Any score estimate should state its evidence and uncertainty."},"sourceB":{"title":"A review separates three kinds of progress","text":"An adult-learning programme evaluated its graduates through a portfolio and unfamiliar tasks. One learner had completed every lesson and produced polished revised essays, but struggled to respond when an interviewer changed the question. Another had gaps in the completion record yet handled unfamiliar reading confidently. These cases showed why participation, supported practice and independent performance should not be collapsed into one number.\n\nThe reviewers recorded separate evidence for listening, reading, writing and spoken interaction. They noted when a task had been repeated or prepared with assistance. A strong revised essay remained useful evidence of learning and editing, but it was not labelled an unaided first attempt. Reviewers also avoided judging speaking solely from a transcript, since timing, intelligibility and interaction were not fully represented in the written words.\n\nWhere two reviewers disagreed substantially, they discussed the criteria and requested another independent sample rather than averaging away the uncertainty. Learners received a practical follow-up plan: maintain strengths, practise identified weak areas and repeat selected tasks later using new material. No conversion from course points to a guaranteed examination band was offered. The programme could describe progress toward its internal objectives, while an external examination remained a separate assessment. Its final report was more informative because it acknowledged uneven profiles and missing evidence, rather than forcing every learner into a confident single-level declaration."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement accurately preserves the briefing's first main point?","answer":"My portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work.","options":["My portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work.","It is not true that my portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“My portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement agrees with the next point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer.","options":["Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer.","It is not true that familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which statement correctly reflects the third point in the teaching briefing?","answer":"I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed.","options":["I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed.","It is not true that i need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed.","The briefing says that no evidence or qualification is necessary."],"explanation":"原文明确写道：“I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed.”。否定该句或取消证据要求都会改变原意。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why keep completion separate from independent performance?","answer":"They can show different patterns of achievement","options":["They can show different patterns of achievement","Completion proves every skill equally","Unfamiliar tasks are always invalid"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A review separates three kinds of progress”支持“They can show different patterns of achievement”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Why is a transcript insufficient for speaking review?","answer":"It omits important timing and interaction evidence","options":["It omits important timing and interaction evidence","It contains no words","It automatically proves pronunciation quality"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A review separates three kinds of progress”支持“It omits important timing and interaction evidence”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"How are substantial reviewer disagreements handled?","answer":"Discuss criteria and request another sample","options":["Discuss criteria and request another sample","Hide the disagreement","Convert course points directly to an exam band"],"explanation":"独立阅读文章“A review separates three kinds of progress”支持“Discuss criteria and request another sample”。应依据段落中的限定条件作答，而非套用教学文章。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"1. 首次作答","en":"My portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work.","zh":"我的档案区分首次作答和修改作品。","word":"first attempts","meaning":"首次作答","tip":"此句用“first attempts”（首次作答）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"2. 迁移","en":"Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer.","zh":"熟悉材料能展示学习，却不能证明普遍迁移。","word":"transfer","meaning":"迁移","tip":"此句用“transfer”（迁移）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"3. 排练","en":"I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed.","zh":"我需要未经排练任务的证据。","word":"rehearse","meaning":"排练","tip":"此句用“rehearse”（排练）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"4. 分别地","en":"Listening, reading, writing and interaction should be reviewed separately.","zh":"听力、阅读、写作和互动应分别评估。","word":"separately","meaning":"分别地","tip":"此句用“separately”（分别地）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"5. 转写文本","en":"A transcript cannot establish every aspect of spoken performance.","zh":"转写文本不能证明口语表现的每个方面。","word":"transcript","meaning":"转写文本","tip":"此句用“transcript”（转写文本）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"6. 允许的","en":"An external reviewer should know what help was permitted.","zh":"外部评阅者应了解允许了哪些帮助。","word":"permitted","meaning":"允许的","tip":"此句用“permitted”（允许的）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"7. 分歧","en":"A disagreement between reviewers calls for examination of the evidence.","zh":"评阅者分歧需要检验证据。","word":"disagreement","meaning":"分歧","tip":"此句用“disagreement”（分歧）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"8. 不均衡能力分布","en":"I would treat an uneven profile as information for planning.","zh":"我会把不均衡能力作为规划信息。","word":"uneven profile","meaning":"不均衡能力分布","tip":"此句用“uneven profile”（不均衡能力分布）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"9. 反复出现的","en":"The next plan should target recurring weaknesses rather than every possible error.","zh":"下一阶段计划应针对反复弱点，而非所有可能错误。","word":"recurring","meaning":"反复出现的","tip":"此句用“recurring”（反复出现的）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"10. 保持","en":"A delayed task with new material helps check retention and transfer.","zh":"使用新材料的延迟任务有助于检查保持和迁移。","word":"retention","meaning":"保持","tip":"此句用“retention”（保持）说明具体判断或事实，让论证有可追踪的信息。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"11. 熟练程度","en":"Course completion is not an official proficiency certificate.","zh":"课程完成不等于官方能力证书。","word":"proficiency","meaning":"熟练程度","tip":"此句用“proficiency”（熟练程度）限定主张的范围，避免把一个情况扩大成普遍结论。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"},{"title":"12. 不确定性","en":"Any score estimate should state its evidence and uncertainty.","zh":"任何分数估计都应说明证据及不确定性。","word":"uncertainty","meaning":"不确定性","tip":"此句用“uncertainty”（不确定性）提出建议或要求，并说明行动方向。结合本章案例，解释这句话对下一步决定有什么影响。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (首次作答): My portfolio distinguishes ___ from revised work.","answers":["first attempts"],"explanation":"原句用“first attempts”表达“首次作答”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the original sentence with the exact missing word or phrase (允许的): An external reviewer should know what help was ___.","answers":["permitted"],"explanation":"原句用“permitted”表达“允许的”，注意搭配和完整词组。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"Use the correct form of PROFICIENT: A completion badge is not an official ___ certificate.","answers":["proficiency"],"explanation":"空格需要“proficiency”这一词形；根据该句语法和意义，而非直接照抄给定词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Rewrite the sentence exactly with the supplied opening and preserve its meaning. Original: A transcript alone cannot establish every aspect of speaking. Required opening: Not every aspect of speaking …","answers":["Not every aspect of speaking can be established from a transcript alone."],"explanation":"参考答案：Not every aspect of speaking can be established from a transcript alone. 这是一项限定表达练习；自由改写可保留文本交由人工复核。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a learning-evidence report. Distinguish completed study from independent performance, identify missing evidence in each skill and propose a realistic follow-up assessment plan.","minimumWords":200,"sample":"My evidence report will separate participation, supported learning and independent performance. This distinction is necessary because a completed course and polished familiar work do not establish every aspect of language ability. A reviewer needs enough information to distinguish a stable ability from a successful rehearsed performance. The learner should therefore label assistance, revisions and repeated tasks honestly. The resulting profile can guide further practice even when the evidence is too limited to support a single confident level for all four skills.\n\nMy portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work. Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer. I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed. Listening, reading, writing and interaction should be reviewed separately. A transcript cannot establish every aspect of spoken performance. An external reviewer should know what help was permitted.\n\nA disagreement between reviewers calls for examination of the evidence. I would treat an uneven profile as information for planning. The next plan should target recurring weaknesses rather than every possible error. A delayed task with new material helps check retention and transfer. Course completion is not an official proficiency certificate. Any score estimate should state its evidence and uncertainty.\n\nThe follow-up plan should target recurring weaknesses and use delayed new tasks. Where the sample is incomplete, the report should say so instead of converting course points into a confident examination prediction."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Building a defensible language portfolio”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text.","followups":["What additional information could change your view, and why?","How would you respond to someone who challenged the strongest part of your explanation?"],"sample":"My portfolio would be useful only if a reviewer could see what I did independently and what help or rehearsal supported the finished work. My portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work. Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer. I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed. Listening, reading, writing and interaction should be reviewed separately. I would use unfamiliar tasks and keep separate evidence for each skill. The result can guide further practice even if it is too limited to justify one confident level or an examination prediction."},"project":"完成“Building a defensible language portfolio”的独立成品：先做笔记，再无范例完成写作和口头解释；记录帮助使用情况，接受一位同伴追问并提交修改说明。完成后再做保留的B阅读与独立听力测验，不以熟悉材料代替新任务。","reviewChecklist":["能在未见材料中保持重要限定条件，不夸大结论。","能用连贯理由支持观点，并准确区分证据与推断。","能回应追问、澄清和修正，而非重复背诵。","口语需真人互动及人工量表复核；本章完成不自动授予C1或雅思分数。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"Your language review is booked for Thursday at two in the afternoon. Please arrive ten minutes early with identification and your portfolio index. Do not bring prepared answers to the speaking prompts, because the reviewer will select unfamiliar topics. The first session includes listening and reading; you will then have a short break before writing. Spoken interaction will take place with a second participant later that afternoon. If you need an access adjustment, contact the coordinator before Tuesday rather than waiting until you arrive. The report will describe evidence in each skill and any limits in the sample. It will not be an official examination certificate. You may request clarification of a comment after receiving the report, but a new performance sample will require a separate appointment. Keep copies of your work for planning subsequent practice.","questions":[{"prompt":"What should the learner bring?","answer":"Identification and a portfolio index","options":["Identification and a portfolio index","Prepared speaking answers","An official certificate already issued"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Identification and a portfolio index”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"When should access needs be raised?","answer":"Before Tuesday","options":["Before Tuesday","Only on arrival","After the report is printed"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Before Tuesday”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"},{"prompt":"What will the report provide?","answer":"Per-skill evidence and sample limitations","options":["Per-skill evidence and sample limitations","An official examination certificate","A guaranteed future score"],"explanation":"请仅依据本次独立录音。正确内容为“Per-skill evidence and sample limitations”，其他选项改变了对象、时间、条件或责任。"}]}},{"chapter":65,"title":"词义精度与证据边界","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Distinguish plausible, probable and established claims","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第60章综合评估；能独立完成C1范围长篇理解与表达，或由评阅者确认可进入。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a neighbourhood water laboratory","text":"At a neighbourhood water laboratory, participants considered whether to describe its new filter as effective under the conditions tested. The background was specific: twelve household samples became clearer after filtration, although the trial did not measure dissolved contaminants or long term maintenance. However, clear water was being mistaken for safe water in the public demonstration. The proposed response was to publish the measured outcomes beside the unmeasured risks and remove the unqualified word safe.\n\nThe public demonstration creates a particular responsibility. Spectators can see a change in appearance immediately, whereas the untested chemical properties remain invisible. The institution should not rely on a small disclaimer to undo a striking visual suggestion of safety. I would place the measured outcome beside the demonstration itself and explain which tests were not conducted. The laboratory could then decide whether additional testing is feasible. If it is not, the narrower claim can still be useful. People may value a filter that removes visible particles, provided they do not confuse that benefit with protection from every contaminant. Responsible communication makes the boundary part of the main message rather than an inconvenient technical footnote. A plausible account is not necessarily the most probable one. The filter reduced visible particles without establishing that the water was safe. We should qualify the claim before it acquires a life of its own. The result holds under the conditions specified in the report. Absence of detected harm does not amount to evidence of harmlessness. Her wording conveys confidence without implying that the question is settled. The observation substantiates a narrower claim than the advertisement makes."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a community seed library","text":"This independent fictional case introduces a different setting. Its purpose is to test transfer, so the conclusion must be recovered from the new details rather than carried over from an earlier example. The participants have a practical decision to make, and an attractive first impression is not enough.\n\nAt a community seed library, the discussion concerned whether to label a tomato variety resilient rather than drought proof. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: plants survived two unusually dry weeks in sheltered gardens, but almost every gardener watered them during the hottest afternoons. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: survival under assisted conditions did not establish independence from irrigation. The suggested response was to retain the survival record while explicitly naming the assistance and the narrow observation period.\n\nThe label matters because a gardener buying seed may treat a description as advice about future care. A plant that survives with afternoon watering has demonstrated something useful, but not the ability to thrive without that support. Removing all mention of resilience would throw away the observation; retaining an absolute promise would turn assistance into an invisible condition. The library can give gardeners a more informative record by naming the weather, the shelter and the watering. That record would also make later comparisons possible. A subsequent trial in an exposed bed might produce a different result without showing that the first gardeners had lied. Different conditions would simply answer a different question.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Twelve household samples became clearer after filtration, although the trial did not measure dissolved contaminants or long term maintenance.","options":["Twelve household samples became clearer after filtration, although the trial did not measure dissolved contaminants or long term maintenance.","The trial measured dissolved chemicals as well as visible particles.","The households compared several years of filter maintenance."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Clear water was being mistaken for safe water in the public demonstration.","options":["The visible improvement was too small for spectators to notice.","The laboratory had already established chemical safety but explained it badly.","Clear water was being mistaken for safe water in the public demonstration."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Publish the measured outcomes beside the unmeasured risks and remove the unqualified word safe.","options":["Remove the clarity result until every possible contaminant has been tested.","Publish the measured outcomes beside the unmeasured risks and remove the unqualified word safe.","Retain the safety claim and add instructions about replacing the filter."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Plants survived two unusually dry weeks in sheltered gardens, but almost every gardener watered them during the hottest afternoons.","options":["Plants survived two unusually dry weeks in sheltered gardens, but almost every gardener watered them during the hottest afternoons.","The plants survived without watering in exposed gardens.","The gardeners compared several tomato varieties under identical conditions."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Survival under assisted conditions did not establish independence from irrigation.","options":["The observation concerned appearance rather than whether the plants survived.","The main uncertainty was whether gardeners preferred this variety to others.","Survival under assisted conditions did not establish independence from irrigation."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Retain the survival record while explicitly naming the assistance and the narrow observation period.","options":["Delete the survival observation because assistance makes it entirely uninformative.","Retain the survival record while explicitly naming the assistance and the narrow observation period.","Describe the variety as drought proof because survival was recorded."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 1","en":"A plausible account is not necessarily the most probable one.","zh":"似乎合理的解释不一定就是最可能的解释。","word":"plausible","meaning":"似乎合理的","tip":"关注“plausible”在这句话中如何表达“似乎合理的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 2","en":"The filter reduced visible particles without establishing that the water was safe.","zh":"过滤器减少了可见颗粒，却没有证明水可以安全饮用。","word":"establishing","meaning":"证明","tip":"关注“establishing”在这句话中如何表达“证明”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 3","en":"We should qualify the claim before it acquires a life of its own.","zh":"我们应在这一说法自行传播之前加上限定。","word":"qualify the claim","meaning":"给论断加上限定","tip":"关注“qualify the claim”在这句话中如何表达“给论断加上限定”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 4","en":"The result holds under the conditions specified in the report.","zh":"这一结果在报告明确的条件下成立。","word":"holds","meaning":"成立","tip":"关注“holds”在这句话中如何表达“成立”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 5","en":"Absence of detected harm does not amount to evidence of harmlessness.","zh":"未检测到危害不等于存在无害的证据。","word":"amount to","meaning":"等同于","tip":"关注“amount to”在这句话中如何表达“等同于”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 6","en":"Her wording conveys confidence without implying that the question is settled.","zh":"她的措辞表现出信心，却没有暗示问题已有定论。","word":"conveys","meaning":"传达","tip":"关注“conveys”在这句话中如何表达“传达”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 7","en":"The observation substantiates a narrower claim than the advertisement makes.","zh":"这一观察结果支持的论断比广告所作的更有限。","word":"substantiates","meaning":"用证据支持","tip":"关注“substantiates”在这句话中如何表达“用证据支持”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 8","en":"Effectiveness here refers to clarity rather than chemical purity.","zh":"这里的有效指的是清澈程度，而不是化学纯度。","word":"refers to","meaning":"指的是","tip":"关注“refers to”在这句话中如何表达“指的是”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 9","en":"I would reserve the word proven for a substantially stronger body of evidence.","zh":"我会把已证实这个词留给强得多的证据体系。","word":"reserve","meaning":"留作专用","tip":"关注“reserve”在这句话中如何表达“留作专用”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 10","en":"The distinction is consequential rather than merely terminological.","zh":"这个区别关系到实际后果，而不只是术语。","word":"consequential","meaning":"关系到实际后果的","tip":"关注“consequential”在这句话中如何表达“关系到实际后果的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 11","en":"Could you specify what would count as evidence against your interpretation?","zh":"你能明确什么样的证据会推翻你的解读吗？","word":"count as","meaning":"算作","tip":"关注“count as”在这句话中如何表达“算作”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"词义精度与证据边界 · 12","en":"The revised label is less dramatic but considerably more defensible.","zh":"修改后的标签没那么醒目，但有依据得多。","word":"defensible","meaning":"有依据的","tip":"关注“defensible”在这句话中如何表达“有依据的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The advertisement made a claim without adequate ___ (SUBSTANTIATE).","answers":["substantiation"],"explanation":"名词substantiation表示证据支持。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Use exactly one word: Although plausible, the explanation has not been established ___ doubt.","answers":["beyond"],"explanation":"固定搭配beyond doubt，表示无疑。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DOES NOT AMOUNT TO: Seeing clearer water is not the same as proving safety.","answers":["Seeing clearer water does not amount to proving safety."],"explanation":"does not amount to后接动名词。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: warranted / warranty / warranting. A categorical conclusion is not ___ by this limited test.","answers":["warranted"],"explanation":"被动结构is warranted by表示得到支持。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a neighbourhood water laboratory.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to publish the measured outcomes beside the unmeasured risks and remove the unqualified word safe. At a neighbourhood water laboratory, the initial suggestion was to describe its new filter as effective under the conditions tested. The relevant background is that twelve household samples became clearer after filtration, although the trial did not measure dissolved contaminants or long term maintenance. Nevertheless, clear water was being mistaken for safe water in the public demonstration. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe public demonstration creates a particular responsibility. Spectators can see a change in appearance immediately, whereas the untested chemical properties remain invisible. The institution should not rely on a small disclaimer to undo a striking visual suggestion of safety. I would place the measured outcome beside the demonstration itself and explain which tests were not conducted. The laboratory could then decide whether additional testing is feasible. If it is not, the narrower claim can still be useful. People may value a filter that removes visible particles, provided they do not confuse that benefit with protection from every contaminant. Responsible communication makes the boundary part of the main message rather than an inconvenient technical footnote.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a neighbourhood water laboratory. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to publish the measured outcomes beside the unmeasured risks and remove the unqualified word safe?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to describe its new filter as effective under the conditions tested. The relevant detail is that twelve household samples became clearer after filtration, although the trial did not measure dissolved contaminants or long term maintenance. However, clear water was being mistaken for safe water in the public demonstration. For that reason, I would publish the measured outcomes beside the unmeasured risks and remove the unqualified word safe. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“词义精度与证据边界”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释词义精度与证据边界的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a neighbourhood insulation trial. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that six households reported warmer bedrooms. However, the trial recorded perceptions but did not log room temperatures. We therefore needed to describe the improvement as reported rather than measured. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to install temperature loggers before the next winter comparison. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The trial recorded perceptions but did not log room temperatures.","options":["The trial recorded perceptions but did not log room temperatures.","The trial measured temperatures but not household opinions.","Only one household reported any change in warmth."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Describe the improvement as reported rather than measured.","options":["Treat perceptions as direct proof of a temperature increase.","Discard the households experiences because they were subjective.","Describe the improvement as reported rather than measured."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Install temperature loggers before the next winter comparison.","options":["Repeat the survey but omit temperature logging again.","Install temperature loggers before the next winter comparison.","Extend the claim to measured efficiency without collecting temperatures."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":66,"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Interpret idioms in context without treating them literally","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第65章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a volunteer theatre committee","text":"At a volunteer theatre committee, participants considered whether to use a plain summary beside an informal rehearsal discussion. The background was specific: a director said the opening scene was on thin ice, then asked for another rehearsal rather than cancelling the performance. However, two new volunteers understood the warning as an immediate withdrawal of permission. The proposed response was to explain that the metaphor signals vulnerability and invite the director to state the practical consequence.\n\nA rehearsal room benefits from informal language: it allows people to express doubt without turning every comment into an official notice. The solution is therefore not to ban metaphors. It is to clarify the action whenever a phrase could reasonably be mistaken for a decision. The director might say that the scene needs more work and name the next rehearsal. Volunteers would then know both the concern and what they should do about it. This preserves the collaborative tone while preventing a figurative warning from becoming an accidental cancellation. The committee should also make formal changes through a separate, recognisable channel so that participants do not have to infer administrative consequences from colourful conversation. The opening scene is on thin ice, so another rehearsal would help. I took the warning figuratively rather than as a literal instruction. Before we pull the plug, let us establish what can still be repaired. She was testing the waters rather than committing the committee to a decision. The joke fell flat because its cultural reference was unfamiliar. An idiom can soften criticism while making its practical meaning less explicit. When you say the scene is shaky, which change are you requesting?"},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a bicycle repair cooperative","text":"The next original fictional account presents a decision that looks straightforward until the relevant qualification is considered. There is no hidden correct opinion about the institution. The reading task is to establish what the account supports, what it leaves open, and what the proposed response actually changes.\n\nAt a bicycle repair cooperative, the discussion concerned whether to clarify an idiomatic budget warning before approving new equipment. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the treasurer said the group was robbing Peter to pay Paul when it delayed rent to replace worn tools. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: one member interpreted the remark as an accusation of theft rather than criticism of cash flow. The suggested response was to separate the figurative criticism from the accounting decision and record the actual transfers.\n\nThe accounting issue survives even when the metaphor is removed. Money needed for one obligation has been diverted to another, leaving a gap that will eventually require an answer. Yet a financial gap is not the same as an allegation that an individual has stolen funds. The members need to know the amount, the repayment date and the consequence of delaying rent. Discussing the implied offence instead would divert attention from that practical problem. A written record should therefore use the actual transactions rather than the colourful phrase. Members may still disagree about whether the purchase was justified, but they would at least be disagreeing about the same decision.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A director said the opening scene was on thin ice, then asked for another rehearsal rather than cancelling the performance.","options":["The director cancelled the opening scene before requesting another rehearsal.","The volunteers had been formally told that the venue was unsafe.","A director said the opening scene was on thin ice, then asked for another rehearsal rather than cancelling the performance."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Two new volunteers understood the warning as an immediate withdrawal of permission.","options":["The warning was ambiguous because the director had not chosen an opening scene.","Two new volunteers understood the warning as an immediate withdrawal of permission.","The volunteers understood the metaphor but disagreed with the artistic judgement."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Explain that the metaphor signals vulnerability and invite the director to state the practical consequence.","options":["Explain that the metaphor signals vulnerability and invite the director to state the practical consequence.","Treat the remark as a cancellation and notify the audience immediately.","Remove informal expressions from every rehearsal discussion."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The treasurer said the group was robbing Peter to pay Paul when it delayed rent to replace worn tools.","options":["The treasurer reported that tools had been stolen from the workshop.","The cooperative delayed buying tools in order to pay rent early.","The treasurer said the group was robbing Peter to pay Paul when it delayed rent to replace worn tools."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"One member interpreted the remark as an accusation of theft rather than criticism of cash flow.","options":["The remark concealed a disagreement about who owned the purchased tools.","One member interpreted the remark as an accusation of theft rather than criticism of cash flow.","The actual transfers were clear but members disputed which tools were missing."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Separate the figurative criticism from the accounting decision and record the actual transfers.","options":["Separate the figurative criticism from the accounting decision and record the actual transfers.","Investigate theft before examining the rent payment.","Record the metaphor as the formal explanation of the transaction."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 1","en":"The opening scene is on thin ice, so another rehearsal would help.","zh":"开场这一幕还不稳妥，因此再排练一次会有帮助。","word":"on thin ice","meaning":"处于不稳妥的状态","tip":"关注“on thin ice”在这句话中如何表达“处于不稳妥的状态”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 2","en":"I took the warning figuratively rather than as a literal instruction.","zh":"我把这句警告理解为比喻，而非字面指令。","word":"figuratively","meaning":"以比喻方式","tip":"关注“figuratively”在这句话中如何表达“以比喻方式”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 3","en":"Before we pull the plug, let us establish what can still be repaired.","zh":"在终止项目之前，我们先确定还有哪些地方可以补救。","word":"pull the plug","meaning":"终止项目","tip":"关注“pull the plug”在这句话中如何表达“终止项目”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 4","en":"She was testing the waters rather than committing the committee to a decision.","zh":"她是在试探反应，而非让委员会承诺作出决定。","word":"testing the waters","meaning":"试探反应","tip":"关注“testing the waters”在这句话中如何表达“试探反应”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 5","en":"The joke fell flat because its cultural reference was unfamiliar.","zh":"这个笑话没有效果，因为其中的文化背景不为人熟悉。","word":"fell flat","meaning":"未能奏效","tip":"关注“fell flat”在这句话中如何表达“未能奏效”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 6","en":"An idiom can soften criticism while making its practical meaning less explicit.","zh":"习语可以缓和批评，同时让实际含义不那么明确。","word":"soften criticism","meaning":"缓和批评","tip":"关注“soften criticism”在这句话中如何表达“缓和批评”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 7","en":"When you say the scene is shaky, which change are you requesting?","zh":"你说这一幕不稳妥，具体希望作出什么修改？","word":"shaky","meaning":"不稳妥的","tip":"关注“shaky”在这句话中如何表达“不稳妥的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 8","en":"The metaphor implies vulnerability, not inevitable failure.","zh":"这个比喻暗示的是脆弱，而非必然失败。","word":"vulnerability","meaning":"脆弱性","tip":"关注“vulnerability”在这句话中如何表达“脆弱性”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 9","en":"I would avoid that expression until I knew how the audience understood it.","zh":"在了解听众如何理解之前，我会避免使用那个表达。","word":"avoid","meaning":"避免","tip":"关注“avoid”在这句话中如何表达“避免”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 10","en":"Her colourful phrasing should not be mistaken for a formal cancellation.","zh":"她生动的措辞不应被误认为正式取消。","word":"formal cancellation","meaning":"正式取消","tip":"关注“formal cancellation”在这句话中如何表达“正式取消”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 11","en":"The expression makes sense once the surrounding exchange is considered.","zh":"考虑前后的对话后，这个表达就说得通了。","word":"surrounding exchange","meaning":"前后的对话","tip":"关注“surrounding exchange”在这句话中如何表达“前后的对话”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"习语、隐喻与自然表达 · 12","en":"A short clarification preserved the humour and prevented a costly misunderstanding.","zh":"简短的澄清保留了幽默，也避免了代价高昂的误解。","word":"preserved","meaning":"保留了","tip":"关注“preserved”在这句话中如何表达“保留了”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the established idiom: Before cancelling, let us test the ___.","answers":["waters"],"explanation":"test the waters表示试探反应。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"The warning was intended ___ (FIGURE), not literally.","answers":["figuratively"],"explanation":"副词figuratively修饰was intended。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use RATHER THAN: She was exploring reactions; she was not making a commitment.","answers":["She was exploring reactions rather than making a commitment."],"explanation":"rather than并列两个动名词短语。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: literal / literate / literary. The volunteers mistakenly gave the idiom a ___ interpretation.","answers":["literal"],"explanation":"literal interpretation指字面理解。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a volunteer theatre committee.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to explain that the metaphor signals vulnerability and invite the director to state the practical consequence. At a volunteer theatre committee, the initial suggestion was to use a plain summary beside an informal rehearsal discussion. The relevant background is that a director said the opening scene was on thin ice, then asked for another rehearsal rather than cancelling the performance. Nevertheless, two new volunteers understood the warning as an immediate withdrawal of permission. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nA rehearsal room benefits from informal language: it allows people to express doubt without turning every comment into an official notice. The solution is therefore not to ban metaphors. It is to clarify the action whenever a phrase could reasonably be mistaken for a decision. The director might say that the scene needs more work and name the next rehearsal. Volunteers would then know both the concern and what they should do about it. This preserves the collaborative tone while preventing a figurative warning from becoming an accidental cancellation. The committee should also make formal changes through a separate, recognisable channel so that participants do not have to infer administrative consequences from colourful conversation.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a volunteer theatre committee. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to explain that the metaphor signals vulnerability and invite the director to state the practical consequence?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to use a plain summary beside an informal rehearsal discussion. The relevant detail is that a director said the opening scene was on thin ice, then asked for another rehearsal rather than cancelling the performance. However, two new volunteers understood the warning as an immediate withdrawal of permission. For that reason, I would explain that the metaphor signals vulnerability and invite the director to state the practical consequence. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“习语、隐喻与自然表达”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释习语、隐喻与自然表达的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a football clubs equipment meeting. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the coach said the old booking system had run its course. However, the remark concerned replacing the booking process, not closing the club. We therefore needed to check what practical change the metaphor referred to. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to replace the paper calendar while retaining the existing membership rules. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The remark concerned replacing the booking process, not closing the club.","options":["The metaphor referred to closing the football club.","The committee had already replaced its membership rules.","The remark concerned replacing the booking process, not closing the club."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Check what practical change the metaphor referred to.","options":["Treat the coachs wording as the complete implementation plan.","Check what practical change the metaphor referred to.","Interpret the phrase as an instruction to stop playing matches."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Replace the paper calendar while retaining the existing membership rules.","options":["Replace the paper calendar while retaining the existing membership rules.","Close the club while keeping the paper calendar.","Change membership requirements instead of the booking process."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":67,"title":"语域与关系管理","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Adjust formality while preserving the same substantive request","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第66章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a university archive","text":"At a university archive, participants considered whether to rewrite a request for missing catalogue records. The background was specific: the first message to an external donor read send the list today, whereas the revised message explained the deadline and asked whether delivery was feasible. However, a highly deferential rewrite could obscure the fact that the exhibition depended on those records. The proposed response was to state the consequence plainly while leaving the donor room to propose a workable time.\n\nThe archive depends on cooperation from somebody it cannot simply instruct as though they were a subordinate. Explaining the exhibition deadline gives the donor a reason to help and a basis for proposing an alternative. Yet politeness should not disguise the fact that missing records may affect the display. A useful message would identify the essential records, state the latest workable time and ask what can realistically be supplied. It might also offer a partial delivery if that would keep the exhibition moving. This approach treats courtesy as a means of making cooperation possible, rather than as an accumulation of apologies that leaves the recipient unsure what is actually being requested. Would it be feasible to send the records before Thursday afternoon? We need the list today, but please tell us if that deadline is impractical. The request sounds courteous without concealing its urgency. Familiarity permits brevity, but it does not excuse avoidable ambiguity. I would make the consequence explicit rather than bury it in apologies. The donor may welcome a clear explanation more than elaborate deference. Please let us know what timescale you could realistically accommodate."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：an apartment residents association","text":"A separate fictional case offers another opportunity to distinguish an observation from its interpretation. The people involved share enough common ground to continue working, but their initial description risks implying more than they have established. The difference becomes visible when the suggested action is compared with the available detail.\n\nAt an apartment residents association, the discussion concerned whether to adapt a reminder about shared storage to its audience. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: a familiar neighbour welcomed a brief informal note, while a newly arrived tenant needed the rule and the reason spelled out. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: using identical friendly jokes with both readers assumed a relationship that did not yet exist. The suggested response was to keep the rule consistent but adjust the greeting, explanation and degree of familiarity.\n\nEqual treatment does not require identical sentences. The established neighbour already knows where the storage room is and why access matters; the newcomer may know neither. A joke can create warmth when a relationship supports it, but can suggest exclusion when it presumes knowledge the reader lacks. Conversely, an unnecessarily legalistic notice might make a minor reminder sound like a threat. The association should preserve the same requirement while changing the amount of context and the interpersonal framing. The test is whether each reader can understand the obligation and respond without having to guess what kind of relationship the writer assumes. That purpose is more useful than a universal preference for either formality or friendliness.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The first message to an external donor read send the list today, whereas the revised message explained the deadline and asked whether delivery was feasible.","options":["The revised request removed any reference to the exhibition deadline.","The first message to an external donor read send the list today, whereas the revised message explained the deadline and asked whether delivery was feasible.","The donor had already agreed to deliver the list by Thursday."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A highly deferential rewrite could obscure the fact that the exhibition depended on those records.","options":["A highly deferential rewrite could obscure the fact that the exhibition depended on those records.","The original message supplied too much background for the donor to follow.","The polite revision was too brief to state any practical request."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"State the consequence plainly while leaving the donor room to propose a workable time.","options":["Send a more deferential message that omits the consequence of delay.","Keep the imperative because explanation would reduce urgency.","State the consequence plainly while leaving the donor room to propose a workable time."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A familiar neighbour welcomed a brief informal note, while a newly arrived tenant needed the rule and the reason spelled out.","options":["The newcomer had ignored several earlier notices about the room.","A familiar neighbour welcomed a brief informal note, while a newly arrived tenant needed the rule and the reason spelled out.","Both recipients had previously helped write the storage rule."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Using identical friendly jokes with both readers assumed a relationship that did not yet exist.","options":["Using identical friendly jokes with both readers assumed a relationship that did not yet exist.","The different recipients were being asked to follow different storage rules.","The association had already established the same rapport with both readers."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Keep the rule consistent but adjust the greeting, explanation and degree of familiarity.","options":["Use the established neighbours joke unchanged to demonstrate equal treatment.","Write a formal threat to both residents without explaining the reason.","Keep the rule consistent but adjust the greeting, explanation and degree of familiarity."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 1","en":"Would it be feasible to send the records before Thursday afternoon?","zh":"能否在周四下午之前发送这些记录？","word":"feasible","meaning":"可行的","tip":"关注“feasible”在这句话中如何表达“可行的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 2","en":"We need the list today, but please tell us if that deadline is impractical.","zh":"我们今天需要清单，但如果这个期限不现实，请告诉我们。","word":"impractical","meaning":"不现实的","tip":"关注“impractical”在这句话中如何表达“不现实的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 3","en":"The request sounds courteous without concealing its urgency.","zh":"这个请求听起来很有礼貌，也没有掩盖紧迫性。","word":"courteous","meaning":"有礼貌的","tip":"关注“courteous”在这句话中如何表达“有礼貌的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 4","en":"Familiarity permits brevity, but it does not excuse avoidable ambiguity.","zh":"熟悉的关系允许简短表达，却不能为可避免的歧义开脱。","word":"ambiguity","meaning":"歧义","tip":"关注“ambiguity”在这句话中如何表达“歧义”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 5","en":"I would make the consequence explicit rather than bury it in apologies.","zh":"我会明确说明后果，而不是将其埋在道歉之中。","word":"make the consequence explicit","meaning":"明确说明后果","tip":"关注“make the consequence explicit”在这句话中如何表达“明确说明后果”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 6","en":"The donor may welcome a clear explanation more than elaborate deference.","zh":"与繁复的谦辞相比，捐赠者或许更欢迎清楚的解释。","word":"deference","meaning":"尊重与谦让","tip":"关注“deference”在这句话中如何表达“尊重与谦让”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 7","en":"Please let us know what timescale you could realistically accommodate.","zh":"请告诉我们你实际能够配合的时间安排。","word":"accommodate","meaning":"配合安排","tip":"关注“accommodate”在这句话中如何表达“配合安排”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 8","en":"An informal greeting would imply a relationship we have not yet established.","zh":"随意的问候会暗示一种我们尚未建立的关系。","word":"established","meaning":"建立","tip":"关注“established”在这句话中如何表达“建立”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 9","en":"The substance of the request remains unchanged across the two versions.","zh":"这两个版本的请求实质保持不变。","word":"substance","meaning":"实质","tip":"关注“substance”在这句话中如何表达“实质”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 10","en":"That phrasing risks sounding entitled rather than merely efficient.","zh":"那种措辞可能显得自以为有权要求，而非仅仅高效。","word":"entitled","meaning":"自以为有权要求的","tip":"关注“entitled”在这句话中如何表达“自以为有权要求的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 11","en":"We can be direct about the deadline and flexible about the method.","zh":"我们可以明确说明期限，同时在方法上保持灵活。","word":"flexible","meaning":"灵活的","tip":"关注“flexible”在这句话中如何表达“灵活的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"语域与关系管理 · 12","en":"The appropriate register depends on audience, purpose and existing rapport.","zh":"适当的语域取决于受众、目的和已有关系。","word":"register","meaning":"语域","tip":"关注“register”在这句话中如何表达“语域”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"Excessive ___ (DEFER) can obscure an urgent request.","answers":["deference"],"explanation":"名词deference表示尊重或谦让。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: Would it be feasible ___ send the records today?","answers":["to"],"explanation":"it is feasible to do。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use PROVIDED THAT: We can accept Friday if you confirm it today.","answers":["We can accept Friday provided that you confirm it today."],"explanation":"provided that引导条件。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: blunt / bluntly / bluntness. The imperative sounded unnecessarily ___.","answers":["blunt"],"explanation":"系动词sounded后接形容词。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a university archive.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to state the consequence plainly while leaving the donor room to propose a workable time. At a university archive, the initial suggestion was to rewrite a request for missing catalogue records. The relevant background is that the first message to an external donor read send the list today, whereas the revised message explained the deadline and asked whether delivery was feasible. Nevertheless, a highly deferential rewrite could obscure the fact that the exhibition depended on those records. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe archive depends on cooperation from somebody it cannot simply instruct as though they were a subordinate. Explaining the exhibition deadline gives the donor a reason to help and a basis for proposing an alternative. Yet politeness should not disguise the fact that missing records may affect the display. A useful message would identify the essential records, state the latest workable time and ask what can realistically be supplied. It might also offer a partial delivery if that would keep the exhibition moving. This approach treats courtesy as a means of making cooperation possible, rather than as an accumulation of apologies that leaves the recipient unsure what is actually being requested.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a university archive. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to state the consequence plainly while leaving the donor room to propose a workable time?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to rewrite a request for missing catalogue records. The relevant detail is that the first message to an external donor read send the list today, whereas the revised message explained the deadline and asked whether delivery was feasible. However, a highly deferential rewrite could obscure the fact that the exhibition depended on those records. For that reason, I would state the consequence plainly while leaving the donor room to propose a workable time. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“语域与关系管理”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释语域与关系管理的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a gardening societies invitation. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that a draft began with a joke suitable for longstanding friends. However, the recipient was a new sponsor who had never met the writer. We therefore needed to explain the event before using familiar humour. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to send a courteous invitation with a clear date and purpose. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The recipient was a new sponsor who had never met the writer.","options":["The draft already supplied the date and purpose clearly.","The recipient was a new sponsor who had never met the writer.","The sponsor had a longstanding friendship with the writer."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Explain the event before using familiar humour.","options":["Explain the event before using familiar humour.","Use familiar humour to imply an established personal relationship.","Replace the invitation with a formal demand for payment."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Send a courteous invitation with a clear date and purpose.","options":["Keep the joke and remove the explanation of the event.","Send the invitation without a date to avoid sounding demanding.","Send a courteous invitation with a clear date and purpose."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":68,"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Control emphasis without overstating the underlying case","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第67章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a public transport forum","text":"At a public transport forum, participants considered whether to argue for late buses without dismissing other passengers. The background was specific: a speaker began it is the last connection that matters most to shift workers, then acknowledged that earlier services also needed repair. However, the emphasis could be heard as claiming that daytime journeys had no social value. The proposed response was to define the priority as a marginal funding decision rather than a ranking of passengers worth.\n\nThe forum is deciding how to allocate an additional service, not determining whose life matters most. That distinction should shape the argument. The speaker can show why the final connection has an unusually large effect on shift workers without implying that daytime passengers have trivial needs. An explicit comparison of the consequences would make the emphasis defensible. It would also allow opponents to challenge the cost or the predicted benefit rather than respond to a moral accusation nobody needs to make. The revised speech should retain its focus, because removing every emphatic construction would obscure the urgency. It should instead connect that focus to a limited decision and acknowledge the unmet needs outside it. It is the final connection that shift workers cannot afford to miss. That priority does not imply that earlier journeys are unimportant. Not once during the trial did the revised timetable require another vehicle. Compelling though the example is, it cannot settle the entire funding question. What matters here is the effect of the next small allocation. The emphasis is justified only if the qualification remains visible. Far from dismissing daytime users, the proposal addresses an additional gap."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a small museum board","text":"In this newly invented case, readers should attend to the relationship between a stated aim and the evidence used to defend it. The account does not invite a judgement about personalities. Instead, it supplies a limited situation in which a careful summary can preserve something that a confident headline loses.\n\nAt a small museum board, the discussion concerned whether to defend a quiet visiting hour while recognising staffing limits. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: a curator said not once had the trial required additional security, but the attendance log covered only three sessions. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: a striking negative construction made a small observation sound like a universal guarantee. The suggested response was to preserve the useful observation and restrict the conclusion to the short trial.\n\nThe curator can reasonably use the trial to answer a claim that extra security was needed on every occasion. It cannot answer every possible staffing question across a full year. Attendance, exhibitions and visitor needs may change. The board should therefore ask whether the proposed expansion resembles the circumstances already observed. If it does, the small trial is relevant; if not, more checking is necessary. The impressive rhythm of the negative construction should not obscure that comparison. Nor should caution become a reason to pretend that the three sessions tell the board nothing. A limited observation can justify a limited next step, provided the next step includes a way to notice when its assumptions fail.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A speaker began it is the last connection that matters most to shift workers, then acknowledged that earlier services also needed repair.","options":["A speaker began it is the last connection that matters most to shift workers, then acknowledged that earlier services also needed repair.","The speaker argued that daytime services had sufficient funding.","The forum was choosing whether all earlier buses should be withdrawn."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The emphasis could be heard as claiming that daytime journeys had no social value.","options":["The emphasis proved that daytime travel had less social value.","The speaker lacked any example of a journey affected by the last connection.","The emphasis could be heard as claiming that daytime journeys had no social value."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Define the priority as a marginal funding decision rather than a ranking of passengers worth.","options":["Remove the reference to shift workers and discuss funding only in aggregate.","Define the priority as a marginal funding decision rather than a ranking of passengers worth.","Rank passengers by social contribution before allocating the additional service."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A curator said not once had the trial required additional security, but the attendance log covered only three sessions.","options":["A curator said not once had the trial required additional security, but the attendance log covered only three sessions.","The quiet hour had been tested across all seasons of the year.","Every trial session required an additional security officer."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A striking negative construction made a small observation sound like a universal guarantee.","options":["The negative construction understated a well established long term staffing need.","The main problem was whether visitors understood the phrase quiet hour.","A striking negative construction made a small observation sound like a universal guarantee."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Preserve the useful observation and restrict the conclusion to the short trial.","options":["Discard the three sessions because short trials can never inform a next step.","Preserve the useful observation and restrict the conclusion to the short trial.","Expand permanently without checking whether later sessions resemble the trial."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 1","en":"It is the final connection that shift workers cannot afford to miss.","zh":"轮班工作者最不能错过的正是最后一班接驳车。","word":"cannot afford to miss","meaning":"最不能错过","tip":"关注“cannot afford to miss”在这句话中如何表达“最不能错过”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 2","en":"That priority does not imply that earlier journeys are unimportant.","zh":"这种优先安排并不意味着较早的行程不重要。","word":"imply","meaning":"意味着","tip":"关注“imply”在这句话中如何表达“意味着”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 3","en":"Not once during the trial did the revised timetable require another vehicle.","zh":"在试行期间，修改后的时刻表一次也没有需要增加车辆。","word":"Not once","meaning":"一次也没有","tip":"关注“Not once”在这句话中如何表达“一次也没有”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 4","en":"Compelling though the example is, it cannot settle the entire funding question.","zh":"这个例子虽有说服力，却不能解决整个拨款问题。","word":"Compelling though","meaning":"虽有说服力","tip":"关注“Compelling though”在这句话中如何表达“虽有说服力”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 5","en":"What matters here is the effect of the next small allocation.","zh":"这里重要的是下一笔小额拨款的作用。","word":"allocation","meaning":"拨款分配","tip":"关注“allocation”在这句话中如何表达“拨款分配”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 6","en":"The emphasis is justified only if the qualification remains visible.","zh":"只有限定条件仍然清楚可见，这种强调才合理。","word":"qualification","meaning":"限定条件","tip":"关注“qualification”在这句话中如何表达“限定条件”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 7","en":"Far from dismissing daytime users, the proposal addresses an additional gap.","zh":"这个提案非但没有忽视日间乘客，反而是在弥补另一处缺口。","word":"Far from","meaning":"远非","tip":"关注“Far from”在这句话中如何表达“远非”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 8","en":"We should resist turning a practical priority into a moral hierarchy.","zh":"我们应避免把实际优先事项变成人的价值排序。","word":"hierarchy","meaning":"等级排序","tip":"关注“hierarchy”在这句话中如何表达“等级排序”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 9","en":"The construction draws attention to the contrast without proving it.","zh":"这种句式突出了对比，但并不能证明对比成立。","word":"draws attention to","meaning":"突出","tip":"关注“draws attention to”在这句话中如何表达“突出”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 10","en":"Had the trial lasted longer, seasonal pressures might have emerged.","zh":"如果试行持续更久，季节性压力可能就会出现。","word":"Had the trial lasted","meaning":"假如试行曾持续","tip":"关注“Had the trial lasted”在这句话中如何表达“假如试行曾持续”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 11","en":"I accept the limitation; nevertheless, the immediate access problem remains.","zh":"我接受这个局限；不过，眼前的出行问题仍然存在。","word":"nevertheless","meaning":"不过","tip":"关注“nevertheless”在这句话中如何表达“不过”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"强调、让步与修辞分寸 · 12","en":"The rhetorical force of a sentence should not exceed its evidential support.","zh":"一句话的修辞力度不应超过其证据支持。","word":"exceed","meaning":"超过","tip":"关注“exceed”在这句话中如何表达“超过”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"transform","prompt":"Begin Not once: The trial did not require an extra bus on any occasion.","answers":["Not once did the trial require an extra bus."],"explanation":"否定状语前置需要助动词倒装。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete the cleft: It is the final connection ___ matters most here.","answers":["that"],"explanation":"强调结构it is...that。"},{"type":"formation","prompt":"The phrase creates ___ (EMPHASISE) without supplying evidence.","answers":["emphasis"],"explanation":"名词emphasis作宾语。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use THOUGH: The example is compelling, but it is not conclusive.","answers":["Compelling though the example is, it is not conclusive."],"explanation":"形容词前置的让步结构。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a public transport forum.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to define the priority as a marginal funding decision rather than a ranking of passengers worth. At a public transport forum, the initial suggestion was to argue for late buses without dismissing other passengers. The relevant background is that a speaker began it is the last connection that matters most to shift workers, then acknowledged that earlier services also needed repair. Nevertheless, the emphasis could be heard as claiming that daytime journeys had no social value. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe forum is deciding how to allocate an additional service, not determining whose life matters most. That distinction should shape the argument. The speaker can show why the final connection has an unusually large effect on shift workers without implying that daytime passengers have trivial needs. An explicit comparison of the consequences would make the emphasis defensible. It would also allow opponents to challenge the cost or the predicted benefit rather than respond to a moral accusation nobody needs to make. The revised speech should retain its focus, because removing every emphatic construction would obscure the urgency. It should instead connect that focus to a limited decision and acknowledge the unmet needs outside it.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a public transport forum. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to define the priority as a marginal funding decision rather than a ranking of passengers worth?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to argue for late buses without dismissing other passengers. The relevant detail is that a speaker began it is the last connection that matters most to shift workers, then acknowledged that earlier services also needed repair. However, the emphasis could be heard as claiming that daytime journeys had no social value. For that reason, I would define the priority as a marginal funding decision rather than a ranking of passengers worth. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“强调、让步与修辞分寸”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释强调、让步与修辞分寸的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a discussion about a village footbridge. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the chair stressed that it was the handrail that required immediate repair. However, the deck had also been inspected but was not judged urgently unsafe. We therefore needed to distinguish immediate priority from a claim that other work was unnecessary. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to repair the handrail first and schedule routine deck maintenance. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The deck had also been inspected but was not judged urgently unsafe.","options":["The deck had also been inspected but was not judged urgently unsafe.","The deck had been judged urgently unsafe too.","No inspection of the bridge deck had occurred."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Distinguish immediate priority from a claim that other work was unnecessary.","options":["Infer that deck maintenance would never be required.","Treat both components as needing identical immediate action.","Distinguish immediate priority from a claim that other work was unnecessary."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Repair the handrail first and schedule routine deck maintenance.","options":["Repair the handrail and cancel routine deck maintenance.","Repair the handrail first and schedule routine deck maintenance.","Repair the deck first and postpone the handrail."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":69,"title":"快速多人讨论","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Track competing proposals and unresolved decisions","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第68章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a local food festival planning meeting","text":"At a local food festival planning meeting, participants considered whether to choose between one large kitchen and several small stalls. The background was specific: Mira preferred a central kitchen for hygiene checks, Arun defended stalls for shorter queues, and Jo asked whether either option fitted the available electrical supply. However, rapid agreement about the event date was being treated as agreement about the kitchen design. The proposed response was to record each proposal separately and leave the power question open until an electrician checked the site.\n\nThe organiser needs a record of the changing discussion, not a transcript that merely captures every word. A useful summary would list the central kitchen, the separate stalls and the electrical constraint as distinct items. It would show which questions have been answered and which remain open. The group should not interpret momentum as consensus: people may agree that the event should happen while disagreeing about how it can operate safely. A brief confirmation round would cost little compared with redesigning the site later. It would also give quieter participants a clear chance to correct the summary. After the electrical check, the discussion can return to hygiene and queues with a more reliable account of what is feasible. Mira supported the kitchen plan, whereas Arun was still discussing queues. Was that agreement about the date or about the proposed layout? Jo has raised a condition that neither proposal yet satisfies. Let me separate the decisions we have made from those still outstanding. I lost the referent of that last yes during the overlap. We appear to agree on timing, but not necessarily on facilities."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a community orchestra rehearsal committee","text":"This independent fictional case introduces a different setting. Its purpose is to test transfer, so the conclusion must be recovered from the new details rather than carried over from an earlier example. The participants have a practical decision to make, and an attractive first impression is not enough.\n\nAt a community orchestra rehearsal committee, the discussion concerned whether to settle how to share a restricted practice hall. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the conductor wanted full ensemble sessions, the percussion leader requested sectional work, and the librarian suggested alternating weeks. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: a brief yes followed a question about keys, but a later summary attributed it to the whole timetable. The suggested response was to ask each speaker to confirm the proposal they supported rather than counting isolated agreement words.\n\nConversation does not arrive as a sequence of neatly labelled votes. Speakers answer the most recent question they heard, return to an earlier objection, or acknowledge a detail without accepting the proposal around it. The librarian therefore needs a summary that identifies both the plan and the person endorsing it. A head count of affirmative words would be unreliable even if every word were transcribed perfectly. The group can resolve the ambiguity by reading back the proposed timetable and inviting corrections. Silence should not automatically be counted as agreement, especially after a rapid exchange in which some members have not had a clear opportunity to speak. The final record should preserve any remaining objection.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Mira preferred a central kitchen for hygiene checks, Arun defended stalls for shorter queues, and Jo asked whether either option fitted the available electrical supply.","options":["All three speakers endorsed the central kitchen after the electrical check.","Jo proposed separate stalls as the only hygienic option.","Mira preferred a central kitchen for hygiene checks, Arun defended stalls for shorter queues, and Jo asked whether either option fitted the available electrical supply."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Rapid agreement about the event date was being treated as agreement about the kitchen design.","options":["The electrical supply had been checked and ruled out both options.","Rapid agreement about the event date was being treated as agreement about the kitchen design.","The participants disagreed about the event date but had settled the kitchen design."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Record each proposal separately and leave the power question open until an electrician checked the site.","options":["Record each proposal separately and leave the power question open until an electrician checked the site.","Count support for the date as support for the central kitchen.","Choose the shortest queue before asking whether the equipment can run."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The conductor wanted full ensemble sessions, the percussion leader requested sectional work, and the librarian suggested alternating weeks.","options":["The percussion leader endorsed the conductors full ensemble schedule.","The librarian suggested cancelling sectional rehearsals entirely.","The conductor wanted full ensemble sessions, the percussion leader requested sectional work, and the librarian suggested alternating weeks."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A brief yes followed a question about keys, but a later summary attributed it to the whole timetable.","options":["The group could not decide because nobody had offered an alternative schedule.","A brief yes followed a question about keys, but a later summary attributed it to the whole timetable.","The key discussion showed that the timetable had been formally approved."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Ask each speaker to confirm the proposal they supported rather than counting isolated agreement words.","options":["Ask each speaker to confirm the proposal they supported rather than counting isolated agreement words.","Record the isolated yes as endorsement of the whole timetable.","Divide the room equally without checking which proposal members supported."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 1","en":"Mira supported the kitchen plan, whereas Arun was still discussing queues.","zh":"米拉支持厨房方案，而阿伦仍在讨论排队问题。","word":"whereas","meaning":"而","tip":"关注“whereas”在这句话中如何表达“而”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 2","en":"Was that agreement about the date or about the proposed layout?","zh":"刚才的同意是针对日期，还是针对建议的布局？","word":"proposed layout","meaning":"建议的布局","tip":"关注“proposed layout”在这句话中如何表达“建议的布局”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 3","en":"Jo has raised a condition that neither proposal yet satisfies.","zh":"乔提出了一个两个方案都还未满足的条件。","word":"condition","meaning":"条件","tip":"关注“condition”在这句话中如何表达“条件”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 4","en":"Let me separate the decisions we have made from those still outstanding.","zh":"让我把已作出的决定和仍悬而未决的问题分开。","word":"outstanding","meaning":"尚未解决的","tip":"关注“outstanding”在这句话中如何表达“尚未解决的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 5","en":"I lost the referent of that last yes during the overlap.","zh":"多人同时说话时，我没听清最后那声同意针对什么。","word":"referent","meaning":"所指对象","tip":"关注“referent”在这句话中如何表达“所指对象”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 6","en":"We appear to agree on timing, but not necessarily on facilities.","zh":"我们似乎在时间上达成了一致，但设施方面未必如此。","word":"not necessarily","meaning":"未必","tip":"关注“not necessarily”在这句话中如何表达“未必”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 7","en":"Before we move on, could each person confirm their preferred option?","zh":"在继续之前，每个人能否确认自己倾向的方案？","word":"confirm","meaning":"确认","tip":"关注“confirm”在这句话中如何表达“确认”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 8","en":"The interruption introduced a new constraint rather than a competing proposal.","zh":"这次打断引入了新限制，而不是另一个竞争方案。","word":"competing proposal","meaning":"竞争方案","tip":"关注“competing proposal”在这句话中如何表达“竞争方案”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 9","en":"Arun has withdrawn his objection to the date, not to the kitchen.","zh":"阿伦撤回的是对日期的异议，而非对厨房的异议。","word":"withdrawn","meaning":"撤回了","tip":"关注“withdrawn”在这句话中如何表达“撤回了”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 10","en":"The summary omitted a reservation that materially affects the decision.","zh":"总结遗漏了一个实质影响决策的保留意见。","word":"materially","meaning":"实质性地","tip":"关注“materially”在这句话中如何表达“实质性地”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 11","en":"I would mark that conclusion as provisional until the power supply is checked.","zh":"在检查供电之前，我会把这个结论标为暂定。","word":"provisional","meaning":"暂定的","tip":"关注“provisional”在这句话中如何表达“暂定的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"快速多人讨论 · 12","en":"Following the discussion requires tracking changes, not merely recognising names.","zh":"跟上讨论需要追踪变化，而不只是认出人名。","word":"tracking changes","meaning":"追踪变化","tip":"关注“tracking changes”在这句话中如何表达“追踪变化”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The agreement remains ___ (PROVISION) until the supply is checked.","answers":["provisional"],"explanation":"形容词provisional表示暂定。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: referent / referral / referee. I lost track of the ___ of her yes.","answers":["referent"],"explanation":"referent指一个表达所指的对象。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use NEITHER: The first plan does not meet the condition; the second does not either.","answers":["Neither plan meets the condition."],"explanation":"neither修饰单数名词，谓语用单数。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: We agree ___ the date but disagree about the layout.","answers":["on"],"explanation":"agree on某项共同决定。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a local food festival planning meeting.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to record each proposal separately and leave the power question open until an electrician checked the site. At a local food festival planning meeting, the initial suggestion was to choose between one large kitchen and several small stalls. The relevant background is that Mira preferred a central kitchen for hygiene checks, Arun defended stalls for shorter queues, and Jo asked whether either option fitted the available electrical supply. Nevertheless, rapid agreement about the event date was being treated as agreement about the kitchen design. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe organiser needs a record of the changing discussion, not a transcript that merely captures every word. A useful summary would list the central kitchen, the separate stalls and the electrical constraint as distinct items. It would show which questions have been answered and which remain open. The group should not interpret momentum as consensus: people may agree that the event should happen while disagreeing about how it can operate safely. A brief confirmation round would cost little compared with redesigning the site later. It would also give quieter participants a clear chance to correct the summary. After the electrical check, the discussion can return to hygiene and queues with a more reliable account of what is feasible.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a local food festival planning meeting. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to record each proposal separately and leave the power question open until an electrician checked the site?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to choose between one large kitchen and several small stalls. The relevant detail is that Mira preferred a central kitchen for hygiene checks, Arun defended stalls for shorter queues, and Jo asked whether either option fitted the available electrical supply. However, rapid agreement about the event date was being treated as agreement about the kitchen design. For that reason, I would record each proposal separately and leave the power question open until an electrician checked the site. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“快速多人讨论”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释快速多人讨论的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a science club planning session. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that Lena proposed a telescope evening while Ben asked about transport. However, when Cara said yes she was answering a question about borrowing chairs. We therefore needed to avoid attributing the brief agreement to the whole event plan. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to confirm the event choice after the transport question is resolved. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"When Cara said yes she was answering a question about borrowing chairs.","options":["Cara agreed to the telescope evening when she said yes.","Transport had been settled before Lena spoke.","When Cara said yes she was answering a question about borrowing chairs."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Avoid attributing the brief agreement to the whole event plan.","options":["Treat the chair question as an objection to astronomy.","Avoid attributing the brief agreement to the whole event plan.","Count every affirmative word as a vote for the event."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Confirm the event choice after the transport question is resolved.","options":["Confirm the event choice after the transport question is resolved.","Confirm the telescope plan without settling transport.","Cancel the event because Cara declined to lend chairs."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":70,"title":"口音适应与听力边界","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Separate unfamiliar pronunciation from unsupported inference","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第69章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a regional oral history project","text":"At a regional oral history project, participants considered whether to train interviewers to request clarification respectfully. The background was specific: an interviewer recognised every written word in a transcript but repeatedly missed the speakers place names when hearing them without context. However, familiar vocabulary on a page concealed a genuine difficulty with connected speech and regional vowel patterns. The proposed response was to practise with consented recordings from several speakers and check place names rather than guessing them.\n\nThe project should treat comprehension as an outcome to be checked, not a quality attributed to a listener on the basis of confidence. Interviewers can prepare local place names, but preparation should not be confused with performance on an unfamiliar recording. Training should include several consenting speakers and opportunities to compare an unaided first hearing with a checked transcript afterwards. Clarification requests belong in the training as well. They allow the interviewer to protect the historical record without pretending to understand a name. A printed script may help vocabulary learning, yet cannot reproduce reduced syllables, timing or regional pronunciation. Progress should consequently be documented through new audio tasks as well as written exercises. I recognised the place name on paper but missed it in connected speech. Could you repeat the street name and then spell the final word? An unfamiliar vowel pattern does not imply unfamiliar vocabulary. I should verify the name instead of selecting the nearest familiar one. The written transcript cannot establish how much I understood unaided. Please correct me if I have joined two separate names together. The difficulty emerged when the speaker reduced several unstressed syllables."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a telephone repair booking service","text":"The next original fictional account presents a decision that looks straightforward until the relevant qualification is considered. There is no hidden correct opinion about the institution. The reading task is to establish what the account supports, what it leaves open, and what the proposed response actually changes.\n\nAt a telephone repair booking service, the discussion concerned whether to improve confirmation of addresses across diverse accents. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: staff correctly repeated most street names after a second hearing, yet house numbers were sometimes lost when callers corrected themselves mid sentence. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the errors reflected correction structure as well as pronunciation, so an accent label alone explained little. The suggested response was to repeat the final address in full and invite correction before closing the booking.\n\nThe service cannot infer comprehension merely because an operator sounds confident. The crucial outcome is whether the delivery reaches the intended address. Corrections are especially demanding because a speaker may repeat one part, replace another and leave the rest understood. Staff need to reconstruct the final version, not combine every number they heard into a plausible sequence. Repeating that final version gives the caller a chance to reject it. Written confirmation may provide additional protection where available, but should not be presented as proof that the earlier call was fully understood. Nor should training imply that one accent is inherently defective. The practical aim is shared understanding across variation and noise.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"An interviewer recognised every written word in a transcript but repeatedly missed the speakers place names when hearing them without context.","options":["The project tested only unfamiliar vocabulary and not regional pronunciation.","An interviewer recognised every written word in a transcript but repeatedly missed the speakers place names when hearing them without context.","Interviewers failed to recognise the place names even in the transcript."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Familiar vocabulary on a page concealed a genuine difficulty with connected speech and regional vowel patterns.","options":["Familiar vocabulary on a page concealed a genuine difficulty with connected speech and regional vowel patterns.","The written success established that the difficulty lay solely with memory.","The recorded speech contained no place names that required verification."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Practise with consented recordings from several speakers and check place names rather than guessing them.","options":["Use written recognition scores as the sole measure of listening progress.","Replace regional names with familiar guesses to maintain interview flow.","Practise with consented recordings from several speakers and check place names rather than guessing them."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Staff correctly repeated most street names after a second hearing, yet house numbers were sometimes lost when callers corrected themselves mid sentence.","options":["Staff could not recognise street names even after a second hearing.","Staff correctly repeated most street names after a second hearing, yet house numbers were sometimes lost when callers corrected themselves mid sentence.","Operators always lost addresses because callers spoke different languages."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The errors reflected correction structure as well as pronunciation, so an accent label alone explained little.","options":["The errors reflected correction structure as well as pronunciation, so an accent label alone explained little.","The account establishes accent as the only cause of booking errors.","The service had correct house numbers but no reliable street names."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Repeat the final address in full and invite correction before closing the booking.","options":["Repeat only the first number heard before the correction.","Label difficult accents instead of checking the callers final address.","Repeat the final address in full and invite correction before closing the booking."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 1","en":"I recognised the place name on paper but missed it in connected speech.","zh":"我在纸上认得那个地名，却在连续语流中没听出来。","word":"connected speech","meaning":"连续语流","tip":"关注“connected speech”在这句话中如何表达“连续语流”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 2","en":"Could you repeat the street name and then spell the final word?","zh":"你能重复街道名，再拼一下最后那个词吗？","word":"spell","meaning":"拼写","tip":"关注“spell”在这句话中如何表达“拼写”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 3","en":"An unfamiliar vowel pattern does not imply unfamiliar vocabulary.","zh":"不熟悉的元音模式不意味着词汇不熟悉。","word":"vowel pattern","meaning":"元音模式","tip":"关注“vowel pattern”在这句话中如何表达“元音模式”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 4","en":"I should verify the name instead of selecting the nearest familiar one.","zh":"我应该核实名称，而不是选择最接近的熟悉名称。","word":"verify","meaning":"核实","tip":"关注“verify”在这句话中如何表达“核实”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 5","en":"The written transcript cannot establish how much I understood unaided.","zh":"书面转录不能证明我在没有辅助时听懂了多少。","word":"unaided","meaning":"没有辅助的","tip":"关注“unaided”在这句话中如何表达“没有辅助的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 6","en":"Please correct me if I have joined two separate names together.","zh":"如果我把两个独立名称连在了一起，请纠正我。","word":"joined","meaning":"连接","tip":"关注“joined”在这句话中如何表达“连接”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 7","en":"The difficulty emerged when the speaker reduced several unstressed syllables.","zh":"说话者弱化了几个非重读音节时，困难就出现了。","word":"unstressed syllables","meaning":"非重读音节","tip":"关注“unstressed syllables”在这句话中如何表达“非重读音节”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 8","en":"Exposure to one voice is insufficient evidence of broad accent comprehension.","zh":"只接触一种声音不足以证明能广泛理解不同口音。","word":"insufficient evidence","meaning":"不充分的证据","tip":"关注“insufficient evidence”在这句话中如何表达“不充分的证据”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 9","en":"I understood the individual words after slowing down the recording.","zh":"放慢录音后，我听懂了各个单词。","word":"slowing down","meaning":"放慢","tip":"关注“slowing down”在这句话中如何表达“放慢”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 10","en":"Next time I will test the same skill with a different consenting speaker.","zh":"下次我会用另一位已同意录音的说话者检验同一技能。","word":"consenting","meaning":"已同意录音的","tip":"关注“consenting”在这句话中如何表达“已同意录音的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 11","en":"Listening competence needs actual audio tasks, not printed scripts alone.","zh":"听力能力需要真实音频任务，而不能只靠纸面脚本。","word":"actual audio tasks","meaning":"真实音频任务","tip":"关注“actual audio tasks”在这句话中如何表达“真实音频任务”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"口音适应与听力边界 · 12","en":"Clarification is a communication skill rather than an admission of failure.","zh":"澄清是一种沟通技能，而非承认失败。","word":"clarification","meaning":"澄清","tip":"关注“clarification”在这句话中如何表达“澄清”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"___ (INTELLIGIBLE) varies with listener familiarity as well as speech.","answers":["Intelligibility"],"explanation":"句首名词intelligibility。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: unaided / unarmed / unasked. The transcript cannot prove ___ comprehension of the recording.","answers":["unaided"],"explanation":"unaided表示没有辅助。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use RATHER THAN: Verify the name; do not guess it.","answers":["Verify the name rather than guess it."],"explanation":"rather than并列原形动词。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: Could you spell the name ___ me?","answers":["for"],"explanation":"spell something for someone。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a regional oral history project.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to practise with consented recordings from several speakers and check place names rather than guessing them. At a regional oral history project, the initial suggestion was to train interviewers to request clarification respectfully. The relevant background is that an interviewer recognised every written word in a transcript but repeatedly missed the speakers place names when hearing them without context. Nevertheless, familiar vocabulary on a page concealed a genuine difficulty with connected speech and regional vowel patterns. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe project should treat comprehension as an outcome to be checked, not a quality attributed to a listener on the basis of confidence. Interviewers can prepare local place names, but preparation should not be confused with performance on an unfamiliar recording. Training should include several consenting speakers and opportunities to compare an unaided first hearing with a checked transcript afterwards. Clarification requests belong in the training as well. They allow the interviewer to protect the historical record without pretending to understand a name. A printed script may help vocabulary learning, yet cannot reproduce reduced syllables, timing or regional pronunciation. Progress should consequently be documented through new audio tasks as well as written exercises.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a regional oral history project. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to practise with consented recordings from several speakers and check place names rather than guessing them?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to train interviewers to request clarification respectfully. The relevant detail is that an interviewer recognised every written word in a transcript but repeatedly missed the speakers place names when hearing them without context. However, familiar vocabulary on a page concealed a genuine difficulty with connected speech and regional vowel patterns. For that reason, I would practise with consented recordings from several speakers and check place names rather than guessing them. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“口音适应与听力边界”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释口音适应与听力边界的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a call arranging a furniture delivery. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the customer corrected the building name twice during a noisy connection. However, the operator recognised the written name later but had guessed during the call. We therefore needed to separate guessing from successful unaided comprehension. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to repeat the entire address and ask the customer to confirm it. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The operator recognised the written name later but had guessed during the call.","options":["The customer never corrected the building name.","The operator recognised the written name later but had guessed during the call.","The operator understood the corrected address unaided."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Separate guessing from successful unaided comprehension.","options":["Separate guessing from successful unaided comprehension.","Treat later written recognition as proof of earlier comprehension.","Focus on confidence rather than confirming the address."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Repeat the entire address and ask the customer to confirm it.","options":["Repeat the first building name and ignore later corrections.","Ask only for the delivery date before closing the call.","Repeat the entire address and ask the customer to confirm it."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":71,"title":"幽默、反讽与语境","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Infer a humorous stance cautiously from surrounding evidence","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第70章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a rain soaked outdoor concert","text":"At a rain soaked outdoor concert, participants considered whether to interpret a stage managers remark about perfect weather. The background was specific: she called the downpour ideal for our paper programmes, immediately moved the programmes under cover, and thanked the audience for waiting. However, the literal praise conflicted with both the practical action and the damaged materials. The proposed response was to read the remark as rueful humour while avoiding assumptions about how every audience member received it.\n\nThe immediate practical context supports a humorous reading: rain is damaging precisely the material praised as suited to the weather. The stage manager also acts to protect it, so the literal statement is not a reliable account of her judgement. Nevertheless, a reader should avoid claiming to know how every audience member felt. Some may recognise the joke, others may miss it, and others may understand it without enjoying it. In a live discussion, tone and shared expectations would add evidence. Since the case provides text, the commentary should explain the contradiction and leave those additional features unresolved. That is a stronger interpretation than confidently attaching a single emotional label to the speaker. Perfect weather for paper programmes, she said, as the rain intensified. The action following the remark makes a literal reading implausible. The humour appears directed at the situation rather than at the audience. I hear possible irony, but I would need the recording to judge delivery. Her deadpan expression may leave an unfamiliar listener uncertain. We should distinguish a supported interpretation from a claim about intention. The contrast between the words and the soaked programmes carries the joke."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a crowded office equipment room","text":"A separate fictional case offers another opportunity to distinguish an observation from its interpretation. The people involved share enough common ground to continue working, but their initial description risks implying more than they have established. The difference becomes visible when the suggested action is compared with the available detail.\n\nAt a crowded office equipment room, the discussion concerned whether to interpret a colleague praising an unreliable printer. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: after the fourth paper jam he announced that the machine clearly enjoyed a challenge, then quietly fetched the maintenance form. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the playful personification could signal frustration without directing hostility at the colleague who had loaded the paper. The suggested response was to distinguish the target of the joke from the person present and ask about the fault if the relationship is uncertain.\n\nThe remark attributes enjoyment to a machine that cannot experience it. That mismatch helps explain the humour, but the preceding breakdowns supply its emotional context. The speaker may be frustrated and still be making a friendly joke. Nothing in the account establishes that the person loading the paper is being blamed. A colleague uncertain about the relationship could respond to the repair problem rather than repeat the joke at someone elses expense. Hearing the original delivery might strengthen or weaken a judgement about tone; the printed account cannot reproduce it. The defensible conclusion concerns the contrast and the immediate situation, not a complete diagnosis of the speakers attitude towards every person in the room.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"She called the downpour ideal for our paper programmes, immediately moved the programmes under cover, and thanked the audience for waiting.","options":["She called the downpour ideal for our paper programmes, immediately moved the programmes under cover, and thanked the audience for waiting.","The manager praised the weather before any rain had begun.","The programmes were waterproof and unaffected by the downpour."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The literal praise conflicted with both the practical action and the damaged materials.","options":["The audience had explicitly asked the manager to make a joke.","The literal praise matched the managers protective actions.","The literal praise conflicted with both the practical action and the damaged materials."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Read the remark as rueful humour while avoiding assumptions about how every audience member received it.","options":["Treat the remark as evidence that the manager wanted the concert cancelled.","Read the remark as rueful humour while avoiding assumptions about how every audience member received it.","Report genuine praise for the weather because the concert continued."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"After the fourth paper jam he announced that the machine clearly enjoyed a challenge, then quietly fetched the maintenance form.","options":["After the fourth paper jam he announced that the machine clearly enjoyed a challenge, then quietly fetched the maintenance form.","The printer completed its work successfully after a challenging request.","The colleague loading the paper had already admitted damaging the machine."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The playful personification could signal frustration without directing hostility at the colleague who had loaded the paper.","options":["The joke directly established hostility towards the person present.","The maintenance form showed that no fault had occurred.","The playful personification could signal frustration without directing hostility at the colleague who had loaded the paper."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Distinguish the target of the joke from the person present and ask about the fault if the relationship is uncertain.","options":["Repeat the joke at the colleague instead of asking about the fault.","Distinguish the target of the joke from the person present and ask about the fault if the relationship is uncertain.","Treat the remark as a formal accusation against the colleague."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 1","en":"Perfect weather for paper programmes, she said, as the rain intensified.","zh":"雨越下越大，她却说这天气真适合纸质节目单。","word":"intensified","meaning":"加剧了","tip":"关注“intensified”在这句话中如何表达“加剧了”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 2","en":"The action following the remark makes a literal reading implausible.","zh":"这句话之后的行动使字面解读难以成立。","word":"implausible","meaning":"难以成立的","tip":"关注“implausible”在这句话中如何表达“难以成立的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 3","en":"The humour appears directed at the situation rather than at the audience.","zh":"幽默似乎针对处境，而不是观众。","word":"directed at","meaning":"针对","tip":"关注“directed at”在这句话中如何表达“针对”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 4","en":"I hear possible irony, but I would need the recording to judge delivery.","zh":"我觉得可能有反讽，但需要听录音才能判断说话方式。","word":"delivery","meaning":"说话方式","tip":"关注“delivery”在这句话中如何表达“说话方式”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 5","en":"Her deadpan expression may leave an unfamiliar listener uncertain.","zh":"她不动声色的表情可能让不熟悉的人拿不准。","word":"deadpan","meaning":"不动声色的","tip":"关注“deadpan”在这句话中如何表达“不动声色的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 6","en":"We should distinguish a supported interpretation from a claim about intention.","zh":"我们应区分有依据的解读和对意图的断言。","word":"intention","meaning":"意图","tip":"关注“intention”在这句话中如何表达“意图”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 7","en":"The contrast between the words and the soaked programmes carries the joke.","zh":"话语与湿透的节目单之间的反差构成了笑点。","word":"contrast","meaning":"反差","tip":"关注“contrast”在这句话中如何表达“反差”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 8","en":"Someone could understand the irony without finding the remark amusing.","zh":"一个人可以理解反讽，却不觉得这句话好笑。","word":"amusing","meaning":"好笑的","tip":"关注“amusing”在这句话中如何表达“好笑的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 9","en":"The same wording might sound hostile in a different relationship.","zh":"同样的措辞在另一种关系中可能显得有敌意。","word":"hostile","meaning":"有敌意的","tip":"关注“hostile”在这句话中如何表达“有敌意的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 10","en":"I would ask what she meant if the practical consequence remained unclear.","zh":"如果实际后果仍不清楚，我会询问她的意思。","word":"practical consequence","meaning":"实际后果","tip":"关注“practical consequence”在这句话中如何表达“实际后果”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 11","en":"The explanation should identify the incongruity instead of labelling everything sarcasm.","zh":"解释应指出不协调之处，而非把一切都叫作讽刺。","word":"incongruity","meaning":"不协调","tip":"关注“incongruity”在这句话中如何表达“不协调”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"幽默、反讽与语境 · 12","en":"The printed exchange suggests a reading; it does not reproduce vocal tone.","zh":"印出的对话提示一种解读，却不能再现语气。","word":"vocal tone","meaning":"语气","tip":"关注“vocal tone”在这句话中如何表达“语气”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The joke depends on the ___ (CONGRUOUS) between praise and damage.","answers":["incongruity"],"explanation":"incongruity表示不协调。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: deadpan / deadlock / deadline. She delivered the joke in a ___ manner, without changing expression.","answers":["deadpan"],"explanation":"deadpan描述面无表情的幽默表达。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DOES NOT NECESSARILY: Understanding irony is not proof that one finds it funny.","answers":["Understanding irony does not necessarily mean finding it funny."],"explanation":"否定必然联系。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: The humour is directed ___ the situation, not the audience.","answers":["at"],"explanation":"be directed at表示针对。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a rain soaked outdoor concert.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to read the remark as rueful humour while avoiding assumptions about how every audience member received it. At a rain soaked outdoor concert, the initial suggestion was to interpret a stage managers remark about perfect weather. The relevant background is that she called the downpour ideal for our paper programmes, immediately moved the programmes under cover, and thanked the audience for waiting. Nevertheless, the literal praise conflicted with both the practical action and the damaged materials. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe immediate practical context supports a humorous reading: rain is damaging precisely the material praised as suited to the weather. The stage manager also acts to protect it, so the literal statement is not a reliable account of her judgement. Nevertheless, a reader should avoid claiming to know how every audience member felt. Some may recognise the joke, others may miss it, and others may understand it without enjoying it. In a live discussion, tone and shared expectations would add evidence. Since the case provides text, the commentary should explain the contradiction and leave those additional features unresolved. That is a stronger interpretation than confidently attaching a single emotional label to the speaker.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a rain soaked outdoor concert. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to read the remark as rueful humour while avoiding assumptions about how every audience member received it?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to interpret a stage managers remark about perfect weather. The relevant detail is that she called the downpour ideal for our paper programmes, immediately moved the programmes under cover, and thanked the audience for waiting. However, the literal praise conflicted with both the practical action and the damaged materials. For that reason, I would read the remark as rueful humour while avoiding assumptions about how every audience member received it. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“幽默、反讽与语境”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释幽默、反讽与语境的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a rehearsal interrupted by a leaking roof. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that a musician called the bucket solo the highlight of the afternoon. However, the remark followed several drops landing in an empty metal container. We therefore needed to interpret the praise as humour about an unwelcome interruption. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to move the instruments before continuing the rehearsal. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The remark followed several drops landing in an empty metal container.","options":["The remark followed several drops landing in an empty metal container.","The bucket was an intended instrument in the performance.","The remark preceded the first sound of dripping water."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Interpret the praise as humour about an unwelcome interruption.","options":["Interpret the praise as a literal assessment of the planned concert.","Treat humour as proof that no practical disruption occurred.","Interpret the praise as humour about an unwelcome interruption."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Move the instruments before continuing the rehearsal.","options":["Remove the bucket but leave instruments under the leak.","Move the instruments before continuing the rehearsal.","Continue immediately because the musician liked the sound."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":72,"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Recover implied reservations without inventing intentions","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第71章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a design grant selection panel","text":"At a design grant selection panel, participants considered whether to interpret a cautious endorsement of an ambitious proposal. The background was specific: a reviewer praised the teams imagination and added that the timetable would certainly keep everyone occupied, while asking for a contingency plan. However, the sentence did not explicitly call the schedule unrealistic, but its placement after repeated deadline questions mattered. The proposed response was to report the reservation as an inference supported by context rather than as a direct quotation of rejection.\n\nThe panel can preserve a constructive relationship without treating praise as approval. Asking for a contingency plan gives the applicants something concrete to address, whereas debating whether the reviewer secretly disliked the team would achieve little. I would record the strength identified, the timetable concern inferred from the surrounding questions, and the revision needed before a decision. If the reviewer later clarified that no reservation was intended, the summary should change accordingly. That possibility does not make every inference equally speculative. Placement and practical consequence provide evidence, but they support a limited conclusion about the proposal rather than an account of the reviewers personality. The response should be equally specific. The compliment is followed by a reservation that changes its practical force. She did not reject the idea outright, but she withheld immediate approval. The timetable would keep everyone occupied may imply concern about feasibility. That interpretation is supported by the repeated requests for contingency plans. I would not attribute personal hostility on the basis of that sentence. Quoting the praise alone would misrepresent the reviewers overall stance. The hedge softens the judgement without necessarily weakening the reservation."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a local newspaper editorial meeting","text":"In this newly invented case, readers should attend to the relationship between a stated aim and the evidence used to defend it. The account does not invite a judgement about personalities. Instead, it supplies a limited situation in which a careful summary can preserve something that a confident headline loses.\n\nAt a local newspaper editorial meeting, the discussion concerned whether to decide whether a feature was ready for publication. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the editor described the interviews as lively and said the fact checking would make an excellent project for another week. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the compliment accompanied a postponement, so quoting only the positive adjective reversed the practical message. The suggested response was to state both the praise and the implied need for further verification while leaving personal motives unknown.\n\nA summary that reports only lively interviews would leave readers expecting publication. A summary that says the editor hated the feature would invent a judgement absent from the exchange. The postponement and the reference to fact checking are more informative about readiness than either extreme. They show that further work is expected, even though the editor has chosen a courteous formulation. The writer can act on that information by asking which facts need checking and when the work can be reviewed. Asking whether the editor really meant the compliment would not settle those practical questions. A careful interpretation therefore preserves both a positive judgement about one quality and a reservation about another.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A reviewer praised the teams imagination and added that the timetable would certainly keep everyone occupied, while asking for a contingency plan.","options":["The reviewer rejected the proposal explicitly on the grounds of poor imagination.","The panel had already accepted the timetable without requesting further details.","A reviewer praised the teams imagination and added that the timetable would certainly keep everyone occupied, while asking for a contingency plan."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The sentence did not explicitly call the schedule unrealistic, but its placement after repeated deadline questions mattered.","options":["The praise removed any need to interpret the later questions.","The sentence did not explicitly call the schedule unrealistic, but its placement after repeated deadline questions mattered.","The phrase proved a personal dislike of the applicants."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Report the reservation as an inference supported by context rather than as a direct quotation of rejection.","options":["Report the reservation as an inference supported by context rather than as a direct quotation of rejection.","Quote only the teams imagination as evidence of immediate approval.","Describe the proposal as formally rejected without qualification."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The editor described the interviews as lively and said the fact checking would make an excellent project for another week.","options":["The editor approved publication while suggesting optional future research.","The editor described the interviews as dull and unusable.","The editor described the interviews as lively and said the fact checking would make an excellent project for another week."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The compliment accompanied a postponement, so quoting only the positive adjective reversed the practical message.","options":["The positive adjective was stronger evidence of readiness than the delayed checking.","The compliment accompanied a postponement, so quoting only the positive adjective reversed the practical message.","The postponement showed that no part of the feature had value."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"State both the praise and the implied need for further verification while leaving personal motives unknown.","options":["State both the praise and the implied need for further verification while leaving personal motives unknown.","Report praise for the interviews without mentioning the postponement.","Infer that the editor disliked the writer personally."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 1","en":"The compliment is followed by a reservation that changes its practical force.","zh":"这句赞扬之后的保留意见改变了它的实际作用。","word":"reservation","meaning":"保留意见","tip":"关注“reservation”在这句话中如何表达“保留意见”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 2","en":"She did not reject the idea outright, but she withheld immediate approval.","zh":"她没有直接否决想法，但暂未立即批准。","word":"withheld","meaning":"暂未给予","tip":"关注“withheld”在这句话中如何表达“暂未给予”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 3","en":"The timetable would keep everyone occupied may imply concern about feasibility.","zh":"时间表会让大家忙个不停可能暗示对可行性的担忧。","word":"feasibility","meaning":"可行性","tip":"关注“feasibility”在这句话中如何表达“可行性”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 4","en":"That interpretation is supported by the repeated requests for contingency plans.","zh":"反复要求应急计划支持了这种解读。","word":"contingency plans","meaning":"应急计划","tip":"关注“contingency plans”在这句话中如何表达“应急计划”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 5","en":"I would not attribute personal hostility on the basis of that sentence.","zh":"我不会仅凭那句话就认定她有个人敌意。","word":"attribute","meaning":"归因于","tip":"关注“attribute”在这句话中如何表达“归因于”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 6","en":"Quoting the praise alone would misrepresent the reviewers overall stance.","zh":"只引用赞扬会歪曲评审者的整体立场。","word":"misrepresent","meaning":"歪曲","tip":"关注“misrepresent”在这句话中如何表达“歪曲”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 7","en":"The hedge softens the judgement without necessarily weakening the reservation.","zh":"缓和措辞弱化了判断的语气，却未必减轻保留意见。","word":"hedge","meaning":"缓和措辞","tip":"关注“hedge”在这句话中如何表达“缓和措辞”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 8","en":"We can infer caution while remaining uncertain about its precise cause.","zh":"我们可以推断存在谨慎态度，同时不确定其具体原因。","word":"caution","meaning":"谨慎","tip":"关注“caution”在这句话中如何表达“谨慎”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 9","en":"Where does the text stop and your interpretation begin?","zh":"文本到哪里结束，你的解读从哪里开始？","word":"text","meaning":"文本","tip":"关注“text”在这句话中如何表达“文本”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 10","en":"The proposed action gives stronger evidence of stance than the polite adjective.","zh":"建议采取的行动比礼貌的形容词更能体现立场。","word":"stance","meaning":"立场","tip":"关注“stance”在这句话中如何表达“立场”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 11","en":"A useful summary preserves both the endorsement and its limits.","zh":"有用的总结会同时保留支持意见及其限制。","word":"endorsement","meaning":"支持","tip":"关注“endorsement”在这句话中如何表达“支持”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"隐含立场与审慎推断 · 12","en":"I would revise that inference if a later passage explicitly contradicted it.","zh":"如果后文明确与之冲突，我会修改这个推断。","word":"contradicted","meaning":"与之冲突","tip":"关注“contradicted”在这句话中如何表达“与之冲突”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"That is an ___ (INFER), not a direct quotation.","answers":["inference"],"explanation":"名词inference表示推断。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: withheld / withdrew / withstood. She ___ approval pending a revised timetable.","answers":["withheld"],"explanation":"withhold approval表示暂不给予批准。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use BOTH: Preserve the compliment. Preserve the reservation too.","answers":["Preserve both the compliment and the reservation."],"explanation":"both...and并列宾语。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: The interpretation is consistent ___ the surrounding questions.","answers":["with"],"explanation":"consistent with表示相符。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a design grant selection panel.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to report the reservation as an inference supported by context rather than as a direct quotation of rejection. At a design grant selection panel, the initial suggestion was to interpret a cautious endorsement of an ambitious proposal. The relevant background is that a reviewer praised the teams imagination and added that the timetable would certainly keep everyone occupied, while asking for a contingency plan. Nevertheless, the sentence did not explicitly call the schedule unrealistic, but its placement after repeated deadline questions mattered. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe panel can preserve a constructive relationship without treating praise as approval. Asking for a contingency plan gives the applicants something concrete to address, whereas debating whether the reviewer secretly disliked the team would achieve little. I would record the strength identified, the timetable concern inferred from the surrounding questions, and the revision needed before a decision. If the reviewer later clarified that no reservation was intended, the summary should change accordingly. That possibility does not make every inference equally speculative. Placement and practical consequence provide evidence, but they support a limited conclusion about the proposal rather than an account of the reviewers personality. The response should be equally specific.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a design grant selection panel. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to report the reservation as an inference supported by context rather than as a direct quotation of rejection?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to interpret a cautious endorsement of an ambitious proposal. The relevant detail is that a reviewer praised the teams imagination and added that the timetable would certainly keep everyone occupied, while asking for a contingency plan. However, the sentence did not explicitly call the schedule unrealistic, but its placement after repeated deadline questions mattered. For that reason, I would report the reservation as an inference supported by context rather than as a direct quotation of rejection. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“隐含立场与审慎推断”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释隐含立场与审慎推断的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a panel reviewing a campsite proposal. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that a reviewer praised the drawings and asked where the floodwater would go. However, the praise did not amount to approval of the drainage plan. We therefore needed to preserve the reservation when summarising the panels response. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to request a revised drainage assessment before approving the site. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The praise did not amount to approval of the drainage plan.","options":["The reviewer had approved the drainage plan.","The panel criticised the drawings as poorly executed.","The praise did not amount to approval of the drainage plan."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Preserve the reservation when summarising the panels response.","options":["Report the flood question as rejection of the drawings.","Preserve the reservation when summarising the panels response.","Treat praise for drawings as sufficient site approval."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Request a revised drainage assessment before approving the site.","options":["Request a revised drainage assessment before approving the site.","Approve the site before checking the drainage assessment.","Revise the illustrations without addressing floodwater."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":73,"title":"批判性评论阅读","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Distinguish a works argument from a reviewers preferred argument","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第72章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a review of an exhibition about household objects","text":"At a review of an exhibition about household objects, participants considered whether to evaluate whether limited scale necessarily meant limited insight. The background was specific: the reviewer complained that no national production figures appeared, although the exhibition explicitly explored how three families repaired inherited objects. However, a fair demand for context could become an unfair demand that a small exhibition turn into a different kind of project. The proposed response was to judge the stated purpose first and then explain what broader context would add.\n\nA small exhibition can illuminate repair as a family practice precisely because it does not attempt to describe an entire national economy. That choice does not exempt it from criticism. The reviewer could ask whether the three families are introduced clearly, whether repaired objects support the claims made about them, or whether a little wider context would prevent misleading impressions. Those questions engage with the actual exhibition. Demanding a complete statistical account may instead replace its purpose. I would recommend a short contextual panel if it helped visitors locate the stories, while preserving the close attention that makes the project distinctive. Evaluation becomes more persuasive when it explains the relationship between an omission and a real limitation for the intended audience. The exhibition should first be judged against its stated purpose. Missing national statistics limit context without necessarily defeating the project. The reviewer appears to demand a different exhibition rather than a better version. That criticism identifies an omission but does not explain its significance. The domestic scale enables detail that a national survey might lose. I would distinguish restricted scope from inadequate execution."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a review of a documentary about a village ferry","text":"This independent fictional case introduces a different setting. Its purpose is to test transfer, so the conclusion must be recovered from the new details rather than carried over from an earlier example. The participants have a practical decision to make, and an attractive first impression is not enough.\n\nAt a review of a documentary about a village ferry, the discussion concerned whether to assess the relation between close observation and public relevance. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the film followed one operator through winter and included no interviews with transport ministers, which one critic treated as evidence of political innocence. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: a missing interview could limit policy explanation without erasing the films attention to work and dependence. The suggested response was to identify the specific unanswered policy question instead of substituting a general label for analysis.\n\nThe absence of ministers may matter to a viewer seeking a national policy explanation. It does not prevent the film from showing how one operators labour connects a village to essential services. The reviewer needs to specify which claim the documentary makes and whether its evidence is adequate for that claim. If the film offers a comprehensive explanation of transport policy, the omission becomes more serious. If it offers close observation of a working life, another standard is appropriate. Calling it innocent bypasses this distinction. The criticism would gain force by identifying a moment where the narrative itself requires a policy explanation that the film fails to provide, rather than demanding a different film altogether.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The reviewer complained that no national production figures appeared, although the exhibition explicitly explored how three families repaired inherited objects.","options":["The reviewer objected that too many production figures displaced the families.","The reviewer complained that no national production figures appeared, although the exhibition explicitly explored how three families repaired inherited objects.","The exhibition claimed to explain the whole national furniture industry."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A fair demand for context could become an unfair demand that a small exhibition turn into a different kind of project.","options":["A fair demand for context could become an unfair demand that a small exhibition turn into a different kind of project.","The exhibition failed because small scale projects cannot provide insight.","The reviewer had shown that the exhibited objects were incorrectly described."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Judge the stated purpose first and then explain what broader context would add.","options":["Replace the family accounts with a comprehensive statistical survey.","Dismiss every request for context as irrelevant to a small exhibition.","Judge the stated purpose first and then explain what broader context would add."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The film followed one operator through winter and included no interviews with transport ministers, which one critic treated as evidence of political innocence.","options":["The documentary promised a complete account of national transport policy.","The film followed one operator through winter and included no interviews with transport ministers, which one critic treated as evidence of political innocence.","The film interviewed ministers but omitted the ferry operator."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A missing interview could limit policy explanation without erasing the films attention to work and dependence.","options":["A missing interview could limit policy explanation without erasing the films attention to work and dependence.","The absence of ministers proved the operator had no political awareness.","The film could not reveal dependence because it followed only one worker."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Identify the specific unanswered policy question instead of substituting a general label for analysis.","options":["Judge the film solely by how many ministers it interviewed.","Assume close observation answers every possible policy question.","Identify the specific unanswered policy question instead of substituting a general label for analysis."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 1","en":"The exhibition should first be judged against its stated purpose.","zh":"首先应根据展览明确的目的来评价它。","word":"stated purpose","meaning":"明确的目的","tip":"关注“stated purpose”在这句话中如何表达“明确的目的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 2","en":"Missing national statistics limit context without necessarily defeating the project.","zh":"缺少全国数据限制了背景信息，却未必使项目失败。","word":"limit context","meaning":"限制背景信息","tip":"关注“limit context”在这句话中如何表达“限制背景信息”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 3","en":"The reviewer appears to demand a different exhibition rather than a better version.","zh":"评论者似乎要求的是另一种展览，而非改进后的同一展览。","word":"different exhibition","meaning":"另一种展览","tip":"关注“different exhibition”在这句话中如何表达“另一种展览”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 4","en":"That criticism identifies an omission but does not explain its significance.","zh":"那条批评指出了缺漏，却未解释其重要性。","word":"omission","meaning":"缺漏","tip":"关注“omission”在这句话中如何表达“缺漏”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 5","en":"The domestic scale enables detail that a national survey might lose.","zh":"家庭层面的规模保留了全国调查可能丢失的细节。","word":"domestic scale","meaning":"家庭层面的规模","tip":"关注“domestic scale”在这句话中如何表达“家庭层面的规模”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 6","en":"I would distinguish restricted scope from inadequate execution.","zh":"我会区分范围有限和执行不足。","word":"restricted scope","meaning":"有限范围","tip":"关注“restricted scope”在这句话中如何表达“有限范围”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 7","en":"The strongest objection concerns an unanswered question relevant to the exhibitions aim.","zh":"最有力的异议关乎一个与展览目标相关却未获解答的问题。","word":"unanswered question","meaning":"未获解答的问题","tip":"关注“unanswered question”在这句话中如何表达“未获解答的问题”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 8","en":"The comparison is unfair unless both works are assessed by compatible criteria.","zh":"除非用相容标准评价两件作品，否则比较不公平。","word":"compatible criteria","meaning":"相容的标准","tip":"关注“compatible criteria”在这句话中如何表达“相容的标准”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 9","en":"A persuasive review represents the work accurately before challenging it.","zh":"有说服力的评论会先准确呈现作品，再提出质疑。","word":"represents","meaning":"呈现","tip":"关注“represents”在这句话中如何表达“呈现”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 10","en":"The proposed context could enrich the exhibition without replacing its central focus.","zh":"建议加入的背景可以丰富展览，而不取代其核心。","word":"enrich","meaning":"丰富","tip":"关注“enrich”在这句话中如何表达“丰富”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 11","en":"I disagree with the verdict because its criterion remains unstated.","zh":"我不同意这个结论，因为它的标准未被说明。","word":"criterion","meaning":"标准","tip":"关注“criterion”在这句话中如何表达“标准”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"批判性评论阅读 · 12","en":"An engaging sentence is not a substitute for a proportionate judgement.","zh":"生动的句子不能替代适度的判断。","word":"proportionate judgement","meaning":"适度的判断","tip":"关注“proportionate judgement”在这句话中如何表达“适度的判断”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"By attributing an aim the exhibition explicitly rejects, the review risks ___ (REPRESENT) its stated purpose.","answers":["misrepresenting"],"explanation":"risk后接动名词；此处语境是歪曲。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: scope / scoop / slope. The criticism ignores the deliberately narrow ___ of the project.","answers":["scope"],"explanation":"scope表示范围。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use AGAINST: Its stated purpose should provide the standard for judging the exhibition.","answers":["The exhibition should be judged against its stated purpose."],"explanation":"judge against表示依据某标准评价。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: An omission matters only ___ relation to a relevant aim.","answers":["in"],"explanation":"in relation to固定结构。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a review of an exhibition about household objects.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to judge the stated purpose first and then explain what broader context would add. At a review of an exhibition about household objects, the initial suggestion was to evaluate whether limited scale necessarily meant limited insight. The relevant background is that the reviewer complained that no national production figures appeared, although the exhibition explicitly explored how three families repaired inherited objects. Nevertheless, a fair demand for context could become an unfair demand that a small exhibition turn into a different kind of project. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nA small exhibition can illuminate repair as a family practice precisely because it does not attempt to describe an entire national economy. That choice does not exempt it from criticism. The reviewer could ask whether the three families are introduced clearly, whether repaired objects support the claims made about them, or whether a little wider context would prevent misleading impressions. Those questions engage with the actual exhibition. Demanding a complete statistical account may instead replace its purpose. I would recommend a short contextual panel if it helped visitors locate the stories, while preserving the close attention that makes the project distinctive. Evaluation becomes more persuasive when it explains the relationship between an omission and a real limitation for the intended audience.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a review of an exhibition about household objects. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to judge the stated purpose first and then explain what broader context would add?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to evaluate whether limited scale necessarily meant limited insight. The relevant detail is that the reviewer complained that no national production figures appeared, although the exhibition explicitly explored how three families repaired inherited objects. However, a fair demand for context could become an unfair demand that a small exhibition turn into a different kind of project. For that reason, I would judge the stated purpose first and then explain what broader context would add. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“批判性评论阅读”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释批判性评论阅读的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a review of a small portrait exhibition. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the critic demanded a complete history of national painting. However, the exhibition had promised a close study of one artists family portraits. We therefore needed to judge the criticism against the stated scope. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to explain which limited historical context would improve the existing exhibition. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The exhibition had promised a close study of one artists family portraits.","options":["The critic demanded fewer family portraits and more technical labels.","The exhibition had promised a close study of one artists family portraits.","The exhibition promised a national survey of painting."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Judge the criticism against the stated scope.","options":["Judge the criticism against the stated scope.","Use the widest possible standard regardless of the stated aim.","Treat narrow scope as evidence that no criticism is legitimate."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Explain which limited historical context would improve the existing exhibition.","options":["Replace the exhibition with a complete national history.","Reject every suggestion of context as a change of purpose.","Explain which limited historical context would improve the existing exhibition."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":74,"title":"文学视角与多义理解","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Support interpretations with textual details and competing readings","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第73章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a fictional story about a woman returning a borrowed key","text":"At a fictional story about a woman returning a borrowed key, participants considered whether to interpret the narrators repeated attention to a quiet hallway. The background was specific: the key was described as warm in her hand, the door remained closed, and a final sentence said that nobody had asked her to hurry. However, these details could suggest relief, hesitation or an altered relationship, but none established a complete history of the household. The proposed response was to offer a principal interpretation and test it against an alternative supported by the same details.\n\nThe key and the closed door establish a pattern of contact and separation, but their emotional meaning is not fixed. The final reference to nobody asking the woman to hurry may suggest a new freedom, a painful absence of expectation, or both at once. A strong interpretation should explain why the chosen reading fits that sentence as well as the physical details. I would resist supplying an unseen argument or death merely to make the story unambiguous. Instead, I would compare two plausible readings and identify the phrase that each finds hardest to accommodate. Such a comparison treats uncertainty as a property of the narrative rather than a defect the reader must repair by inventing information. The warm key suggests attachment, although it does not establish affection. The closed door permits several readings rather than a single fixed symbolism. My interpretation depends on the final sentence as well as the repeated image. Relief and hesitation need not be mutually exclusive responses. We should not invent a family history to resolve the narratives ambiguity."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a fictional poem about an abandoned railway platform","text":"The next original fictional account presents a decision that looks straightforward until the relevant qualification is considered. There is no hidden correct opinion about the institution. The reading task is to establish what the account supports, what it leaves open, and what the proposed response actually changes.\n\nAt a fictional poem about an abandoned railway platform, the discussion concerned whether to interpret the contrast between a timetable and growing grass. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the timetable still promised departures while weeds covered the track, and the speaker addressed a traveller who never appeared. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the contrast supported a reading about failed promises but did not prove that the absent traveller had died. The suggested response was to keep emotional possibilities open and distinguish the speakers language from biographical claims about the writer.\n\nThe promised departures and unusable track create a tension between an official statement and the physical scene. That tension can support several emotional readings: abandonment, delayed hope, institutional forgetfulness or a refusal to accept change. The missing traveller intensifies the uncertainty without settling its cause. A reader who concludes that the traveller died needs a detail beyond absence alone, since many kinds of absence are possible. A reader who sees only scenery must still explain why the poem addresses someone who never arrives. Strong interpretation does not require eliminating ambiguity. It requires showing how a proposed reading accounts for the language while acknowledging the detail that leaves another reading available.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The key was described as warm in her hand, the door remained closed, and a final sentence said that nobody had asked her to hurry.","options":["The key was described as warm in her hand, the door remained closed, and a final sentence said that nobody had asked her to hurry.","The woman explained her relief directly in a conversation at the door.","Another character told the woman to return the key immediately."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"These details could suggest relief, hesitation or an altered relationship, but none established a complete history of the household.","options":["The warm key established an affectionate relationship with certainty.","The closed door proved that the household had been permanently abandoned.","These details could suggest relief, hesitation or an altered relationship, but none established a complete history of the household."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Offer a principal interpretation and test it against an alternative supported by the same details.","options":["Treat hesitation and relief as interpretations that cannot coexist.","Offer a principal interpretation and test it against an alternative supported by the same details.","Supply a definite family dispute to resolve the unanswered history."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The timetable still promised departures while weeds covered the track, and the speaker addressed a traveller who never appeared.","options":["The timetable still promised departures while weeds covered the track, and the speaker addressed a traveller who never appeared.","The traveller appeared at the platform and criticised the timetable.","The poem stated that the railway was still operating on schedule."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The contrast supported a reading about failed promises but did not prove that the absent traveller had died.","options":["The absent traveller was explicitly described as dead.","The weeds made the promised departures a literal description of current service.","The contrast supported a reading about failed promises but did not prove that the absent traveller had died."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Keep emotional possibilities open and distinguish the speakers language from biographical claims about the writer.","options":["Ignore the timetable and focus only on decorative scenery.","Keep emotional possibilities open and distinguish the speakers language from biographical claims about the writer.","Treat the writers biography as sufficient proof of the travellers fate."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 1","en":"The warm key suggests attachment, although it does not establish affection.","zh":"温热的钥匙暗示牵连，但不能证明有感情。","word":"attachment","meaning":"牵连","tip":"关注“attachment”在这句话中如何表达“牵连”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 2","en":"The closed door permits several readings rather than a single fixed symbolism.","zh":"紧闭的门允许多种解读，而非一种固定象征。","word":"readings","meaning":"解读","tip":"关注“readings”在这句话中如何表达“解读”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 3","en":"My interpretation depends on the final sentence as well as the repeated image.","zh":"我的解读既依赖最后一句，也依赖反复出现的意象。","word":"interpretation","meaning":"解读","tip":"关注“interpretation”在这句话中如何表达“解读”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 4","en":"Relief and hesitation need not be mutually exclusive responses.","zh":"如释重负与犹豫不必是互相排斥的反应。","word":"mutually exclusive","meaning":"互相排斥的","tip":"关注“mutually exclusive”在这句话中如何表达“互相排斥的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 5","en":"We should not invent a family history to resolve the narratives ambiguity.","zh":"我们不应编造家庭史来消除叙事的多义性。","word":"ambiguity","meaning":"多义性","tip":"关注“ambiguity”在这句话中如何表达“多义性”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 6","en":"The narrator notices the hallway but gives us limited access to other minds.","zh":"叙述者注意到走廊，却很少让我们了解他人的内心。","word":"access to","meaning":"了解的机会","tip":"关注“access to”在这句话中如何表达“了解的机会”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 7","en":"An alternative reading accounts for the same detail with fewer assumptions.","zh":"另一种解读用更少假设解释了同一细节。","word":"assumptions","meaning":"假设","tip":"关注“assumptions”在这句话中如何表达“假设”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 8","en":"The absence of dialogue makes the silence conspicuous rather than self explanatory.","zh":"没有对话让沉默变得醒目，而非不言自明。","word":"conspicuous","meaning":"醒目的","tip":"关注“conspicuous”在这句话中如何表达“醒目的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 9","en":"That claim concerns the narrator, not necessarily the author.","zh":"那个说法针对叙述者，不一定针对作者。","word":"narrator","meaning":"叙述者","tip":"关注“narrator”在这句话中如何表达“叙述者”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 10","en":"Which phrase would be hardest to explain under your preferred interpretation?","zh":"在你倾向的解读下，哪个短语最难解释？","word":"explain","meaning":"解释","tip":"关注“explain”在这句话中如何表达“解释”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 11","en":"The ending sustains uncertainty while changing its emotional weight.","zh":"结尾保留了不确定性，却改变了它的情感分量。","word":"sustains uncertainty","meaning":"保留不确定性","tip":"关注“sustains uncertainty”在这句话中如何表达“保留不确定性”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"文学视角与多义理解 · 12","en":"A convincing reading acknowledges the textual detail that resists it.","zh":"有说服力的解读会承认文本中不支持自己的细节。","word":"acknowledges","meaning":"承认","tip":"关注“acknowledges”在这句话中如何表达“承认”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The ending deliberately preserves ___ (AMBIGUOUS).","answers":["ambiguity"],"explanation":"名词ambiguity表示多义或不确定。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: narrator / narrative / narrate. The ___ notices the hallway but cannot know everyones thoughts.","answers":["narrator"],"explanation":"narrator表示叙述者。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use NEED NOT: Relief and hesitation are not necessarily incompatible.","answers":["Relief and hesitation need not be incompatible."],"explanation":"need not表示并非必然。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: This reading accounts ___ the repeated image.","answers":["for"],"explanation":"account for表示解释。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a fictional story about a woman returning a borrowed key.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to offer a principal interpretation and test it against an alternative supported by the same details. At a fictional story about a woman returning a borrowed key, the initial suggestion was to interpret the narrators repeated attention to a quiet hallway. The relevant background is that the key was described as warm in her hand, the door remained closed, and a final sentence said that nobody had asked her to hurry. Nevertheless, these details could suggest relief, hesitation or an altered relationship, but none established a complete history of the household. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe key and the closed door establish a pattern of contact and separation, but their emotional meaning is not fixed. The final reference to nobody asking the woman to hurry may suggest a new freedom, a painful absence of expectation, or both at once. A strong interpretation should explain why the chosen reading fits that sentence as well as the physical details. I would resist supplying an unseen argument or death merely to make the story unambiguous. Instead, I would compare two plausible readings and identify the phrase that each finds hardest to accommodate. Such a comparison treats uncertainty as a property of the narrative rather than a defect the reader must repair by inventing information.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a fictional story about a woman returning a borrowed key. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to offer a principal interpretation and test it against an alternative supported by the same details?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to interpret the narrators repeated attention to a quiet hallway. The relevant detail is that the key was described as warm in her hand, the door remained closed, and a final sentence said that nobody had asked her to hurry. However, these details could suggest relief, hesitation or an altered relationship, but none established a complete history of the household. For that reason, I would offer a principal interpretation and test it against an alternative supported by the same details. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“文学视角与多义理解”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释文学视角与多义理解的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a reading group discussing a fictional letter. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the writer folded an empty page and placed it in a drawer. However, the passage supplied no reason why the message remained unwritten. We therefore needed to offer competing interpretations without inventing a definitive backstory. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to support each reading with the gestures actually described. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The passage supplied no reason why the message remained unwritten.","options":["The passage supplied no reason why the message remained unwritten.","The letter explained why the message was unwritten.","The writer burned a completed letter after an argument."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Offer competing interpretations without inventing a definitive backstory.","options":["Treat the empty page as conclusive evidence of a particular loss.","Supply a backstory before considering the gestures.","Offer competing interpretations without inventing a definitive backstory."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Support each reading with the gestures actually described.","options":["Judge the writers biography instead of the described scene.","Support each reading with the gestures actually described.","Choose one history as fact and disregard alternatives."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":75,"title":"学术论证与因果审查","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Separate association, mechanism and causal identification","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第74章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a fictional study of library study rooms","text":"At a fictional study of library study rooms, participants considered whether to evaluate whether booking rooms improved examination results. The background was specific: students who reserved quiet rooms earned higher marks, but the report also noted that frequent users had stronger attendance before the rooms opened. However, the association could reflect prior motivation, and a plausible concentration mechanism did not remove that alternative explanation. The proposed response was to compare changes over time and seek a design that addresses selection rather than calling correlation proof.\n\nThe room users may genuinely benefit from a quiet environment, and that possibility deserves investigation. The problem is not that a concentration mechanism is absurd; it is that the observed comparison cannot isolate it. Stronger prior attendance suggests that users already differed from non users in a way relevant to examination marks. Collecting more detail about those differences could help, but it would not automatically make every adjustment credible. The evaluation should specify the causal question and explain why its comparison addresses selection. Meanwhile, the descriptive finding can still guide a modest decision about demand for rooms. It should not be advertised as a proven increase in grades caused by the booking system. Higher marks among room users establish an association rather than a causal effect. Prior attendance may influence both room use and examination performance. The proposed mechanism is credible but does not eliminate selection bias. We need a comparison that addresses differences already present before the change. The result is compatible with several explanations the design cannot separate. I would narrow the conclusion instead of dismissing the observation entirely."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a fictional evaluation of workplace mentoring","text":"A separate fictional case offers another opportunity to distinguish an observation from its interpretation. The people involved share enough common ground to continue working, but their initial description risks implying more than they have established. The difference becomes visible when the suggested action is compared with the available detail.\n\nAt a fictional evaluation of workplace mentoring, the discussion concerned whether to assess a claim that mentoring prevented staff departures. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: participants stayed longer than non participants, although supervisors had invited employees they already expected to promote. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: promotion prospects could influence both selection and retention, leaving the programmes independent effect uncertain. The suggested response was to separate the descriptive retention difference from the causal claim and propose a fair comparison group.\n\nA comparison between participants and non participants would be informative about observed retention. The difficulty begins when the report treats those groups as though participation were their only relevant difference. Supervisors expectations may already predict who is likely to stay. The programme might still help, but the observed gap cannot tell the evaluator how much help it provided. A better design would address the selection process before estimating the effect. That might require comparing otherwise similar employees under a defensible allocation procedure. Simply interviewing more participants about how much they enjoyed the mentoring would answer another useful question, not remove the causal uncertainty. The report should name both the finding and its boundary.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Students who reserved quiet rooms earned higher marks, but the report also noted that frequent users had stronger attendance before the rooms opened.","options":["Room users and non users had identical attendance before the trial.","The study randomly assigned access to quiet rooms.","Students who reserved quiet rooms earned higher marks, but the report also noted that frequent users had stronger attendance before the rooms opened."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The association could reflect prior motivation, and a plausible concentration mechanism did not remove that alternative explanation.","options":["The descriptive association was invalid simply because causation was uncertain.","The association could reflect prior motivation, and a plausible concentration mechanism did not remove that alternative explanation.","The concentration mechanism eliminated the possibility of prior motivation."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Compare changes over time and seek a design that addresses selection rather than calling correlation proof.","options":["Compare changes over time and seek a design that addresses selection rather than calling correlation proof.","Advertise the booking system as a demonstrated cause of higher marks.","Increase sample size without examining how users selected themselves."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Participants stayed longer than non participants, although supervisors had invited employees they already expected to promote.","options":["Employees were assigned to mentoring independently of promotion prospects.","Supervisors invited staff they believed were most likely to resign.","Participants stayed longer than non participants, although supervisors had invited employees they already expected to promote."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Promotion prospects could influence both selection and retention, leaving the programmes independent effect uncertain.","options":["Enjoyment interviews would remove uncertainty about causal selection.","Promotion prospects could influence both selection and retention, leaving the programmes independent effect uncertain.","The retention difference proved that mentoring had no possible benefit."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Separate the descriptive retention difference from the causal claim and propose a fair comparison group.","options":["Separate the descriptive retention difference from the causal claim and propose a fair comparison group.","Treat the retention gap as the programmes independent effect.","Estimate impact without considering why supervisors selected participants."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 1","en":"Higher marks among room users establish an association rather than a causal effect.","zh":"使用自习室者分数更高，表明关联而非因果效应。","word":"association","meaning":"关联","tip":"关注“association”在这句话中如何表达“关联”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 2","en":"Prior attendance may influence both room use and examination performance.","zh":"先前的出勤情况可能同时影响自习室使用和考试表现。","word":"Prior attendance","meaning":"先前出勤情况","tip":"关注“Prior attendance”在这句话中如何表达“先前出勤情况”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 3","en":"The proposed mechanism is credible but does not eliminate selection bias.","zh":"提出的机制可信，却不能消除选择偏差。","word":"selection bias","meaning":"选择偏差","tip":"关注“selection bias”在这句话中如何表达“选择偏差”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 4","en":"We need a comparison that addresses differences already present before the change.","zh":"我们需要一种能够处理变化前既有差异的比较。","word":"comparison","meaning":"比较","tip":"关注“comparison”在这句话中如何表达“比较”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 5","en":"The result is compatible with several explanations the design cannot separate.","zh":"这个结果与研究设计无法区分的多种解释相容。","word":"compatible with","meaning":"与之相容","tip":"关注“compatible with”在这句话中如何表达“与之相容”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 6","en":"I would narrow the conclusion instead of dismissing the observation entirely.","zh":"我会缩小结论范围，而不是完全否定观察结果。","word":"narrow the conclusion","meaning":"缩小结论范围","tip":"关注“narrow the conclusion”在这句话中如何表达“缩小结论范围”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 7","en":"Even a precise estimate can answer the wrong causal question.","zh":"即使估计很精确，也可能回答了错误的因果问题。","word":"precise estimate","meaning":"精确估计","tip":"关注“precise estimate”在这句话中如何表达“精确估计”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 8","en":"The report should distinguish measured outcomes from inferred mechanisms.","zh":"报告应区分测量到的结果和推断的机制。","word":"inferred mechanisms","meaning":"推断的机制","tip":"关注“inferred mechanisms”在这句话中如何表达“推断的机制”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 9","en":"What evidence would make the selection explanation less plausible?","zh":"什么证据会让选择因素的解释不那么可信？","word":"less plausible","meaning":"较不可信的","tip":"关注“less plausible”在这句话中如何表达“较不可信的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 10","en":"Comparing changes may help, provided the groups had credible earlier trends.","zh":"比较变化可能有帮助，前提是各组先前趋势可信。","word":"provided","meaning":"前提是","tip":"关注“provided”在这句话中如何表达“前提是”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 11","en":"The limitation concerns identification, not merely the size of the sample.","zh":"这个局限关乎因果识别，而不只是样本大小。","word":"identification","meaning":"因果识别","tip":"关注“identification”在这句话中如何表达“因果识别”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"学术论证与因果审查 · 12","en":"Uncertainty about causation does not make the descriptive finding worthless.","zh":"因果关系不确定，并不使描述性发现失去价值。","word":"descriptive finding","meaning":"描述性发现","tip":"关注“descriptive finding”在这句话中如何表达“描述性发现”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The design leaves ___ (CONFOUND) unresolved.","answers":["confounding"],"explanation":"confounding作名词指混杂问题。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: causal / casual / causally. The report establishes association, not a ___ effect.","answers":["causal"],"explanation":"causal形容因果效应。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DOES NOT RULE OUT: A credible mechanism leaves selection bias possible.","answers":["A credible mechanism does not rule out selection bias."],"explanation":"rule out排除。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: The estimate is precise, but precision is not the same ___ causal identification.","answers":["as"],"explanation":"the same as固定结构。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a fictional study of library study rooms.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to compare changes over time and seek a design that addresses selection rather than calling correlation proof. At a fictional study of library study rooms, the initial suggestion was to evaluate whether booking rooms improved examination results. The relevant background is that students who reserved quiet rooms earned higher marks, but the report also noted that frequent users had stronger attendance before the rooms opened. Nevertheless, the association could reflect prior motivation, and a plausible concentration mechanism did not remove that alternative explanation. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe room users may genuinely benefit from a quiet environment, and that possibility deserves investigation. The problem is not that a concentration mechanism is absurd; it is that the observed comparison cannot isolate it. Stronger prior attendance suggests that users already differed from non users in a way relevant to examination marks. Collecting more detail about those differences could help, but it would not automatically make every adjustment credible. The evaluation should specify the causal question and explain why its comparison addresses selection. Meanwhile, the descriptive finding can still guide a modest decision about demand for rooms. It should not be advertised as a proven increase in grades caused by the booking system.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a fictional study of library study rooms. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to compare changes over time and seek a design that addresses selection rather than calling correlation proof?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to evaluate whether booking rooms improved examination results. The relevant detail is that students who reserved quiet rooms earned higher marks, but the report also noted that frequent users had stronger attendance before the rooms opened. However, the association could reflect prior motivation, and a plausible concentration mechanism did not remove that alternative explanation. For that reason, I would compare changes over time and seek a design that addresses selection rather than calling correlation proof. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“学术论证与因果审查”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释学术论证与因果审查的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a report about office plants and staff absence. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that departments with more plants reported fewer sick days. However, those departments also had more flexible home working arrangements. We therefore needed to avoid claiming that plants alone caused the difference. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to investigate the work arrangements before drawing a causal conclusion. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"Those departments also had more flexible home working arrangements.","options":["Work arrangements were identical across departments.","Departments with fewer plants had more flexible home working.","Those departments also had more flexible home working arrangements."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Avoid claiming that plants alone caused the difference.","options":["Dismiss the observed difference because the cause is uncertain.","Avoid claiming that plants alone caused the difference.","Assume that a visible association identifies its cause."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Investigate the work arrangements before drawing a causal conclusion.","options":["Investigate the work arrangements before drawing a causal conclusion.","Add plants everywhere without investigating other differences.","Count plants again but leave work arrangements unexamined."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":76,"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Synthesize compatible findings without flattening disagreement","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第75章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：two fictional reports about an urban river path","text":"At two fictional reports about an urban river path, participants considered whether to combine access benefits with observations of uneven use. The background was specific: a transport survey counted more weekday cyclists after resurfacing, while interviews with older pedestrians described difficulty at two shared corners. However, the counts and interviews addressed different questions, so treating one as a refutation of the other discarded useful evidence. The proposed response was to retain the overall increase and specify where design changes might improve access for particular users.\n\nThe river path is not experienced by an average user who simultaneously rides a bicycle and walks cautiously around every corner. Different people can gain and lose from the same design. The two reports should therefore be combined in a way that preserves their units of observation. Counting cyclists answers one question; listening to older pedestrians answers another. The authority could inspect the identified corners, test clearer separation or reduced speeds, and monitor whether the improvement creates new barriers elsewhere. It need not reject resurfacing to acknowledge a design problem. Equally, a positive traffic count cannot settle the question of access for everyone. The recommendation should state whose experience each proposed measure is intended to improve. More cyclists and less comfortable corners can both be genuine findings. The reports address different outcomes rather than necessarily contradicting each other. Aggregating the results would conceal which users encountered the difficulty. We should preserve the disagreement about priorities instead of averaging it away. The interviews explain experiences that the traffic counts cannot capture. Neither source alone supports a conclusion about universal accessibility. The synthesis needs to identify where the evidence converges and where it diverges."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：two fictional accounts of a school meal change","text":"In this newly invented case, readers should attend to the relationship between a stated aim and the evidence used to defend it. The account does not invite a judgement about personalities. Instead, it supplies a limited situation in which a careful summary can preserve something that a confident headline loses.\n\nAt two fictional accounts of a school meal change, the discussion concerned whether to integrate reduced waste with complaints about choice. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: kitchen records showed fewer discarded portions, whereas pupil discussions revealed that some children brought food from home instead. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the apparently positive waste result did not reveal whether children ate more of the meal or avoided it. The suggested response was to join the operational and participation evidence before recommending expansion.\n\nFewer discarded portions could reflect better acceptance, smaller servings or lower participation. The kitchen record alone cannot distinguish these possibilities. The pupil discussions make one alternative visible, although they do not establish how many children chose it. Together the sources suggest a need to count participation as well as waste and to understand why some pupils opt out. They do not justify dismissing the operational improvement, because less discarded food remains relevant. They also do not justify treating it as evidence that all pupils prefer the new meals. A recommendation to expand should explain which outcome it prioritises and how it will detect a gain in efficiency that conceals a loss in access.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A transport survey counted more weekday cyclists after resurfacing, while interviews with older pedestrians described difficulty at two shared corners.","options":["The pedestrian interviews covered every resident using the path.","A transport survey counted more weekday cyclists after resurfacing, while interviews with older pedestrians described difficulty at two shared corners.","Cyclist counts fell after resurfacing the river path."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The counts and interviews addressed different questions, so treating one as a refutation of the other discarded useful evidence.","options":["The counts and interviews addressed different questions, so treating one as a refutation of the other discarded useful evidence.","The interviews directly refuted the increase in weekday cycling.","The overall increase established that every group gained equally."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Retain the overall increase and specify where design changes might improve access for particular users.","options":["Discard the interviews because they contain fewer numbers.","Reject resurfacing entirely without examining the two corners.","Retain the overall increase and specify where design changes might improve access for particular users."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Kitchen records showed fewer discarded portions, whereas pupil discussions revealed that some children brought food from home instead.","options":["Every pupil ate the new meal and reported preferring it.","Kitchen records showed fewer discarded portions, whereas pupil discussions revealed that some children brought food from home instead.","Waste increased even though more pupils bought school meals."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The apparently positive waste result did not reveal whether children ate more of the meal or avoided it.","options":["The apparently positive waste result did not reveal whether children ate more of the meal or avoided it.","Fewer discarded portions established that pupils ate more of the meal.","The interviews proved that no pupil benefited from the change."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Join the operational and participation evidence before recommending expansion.","options":["Expand on waste figures alone without measuring participation.","Dismiss the waste reduction solely because some pupils opted out.","Join the operational and participation evidence before recommending expansion."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 1","en":"More cyclists and less comfortable corners can both be genuine findings.","zh":"骑车者增加和转角舒适度下降可以同时是真实发现。","word":"genuine findings","meaning":"真实发现","tip":"关注“genuine findings”在这句话中如何表达“真实发现”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 2","en":"The reports address different outcomes rather than necessarily contradicting each other.","zh":"这些报告讨论不同结果，未必互相矛盾。","word":"contradicting","meaning":"与之矛盾","tip":"关注“contradicting”在这句话中如何表达“与之矛盾”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 3","en":"Aggregating the results would conceal which users encountered the difficulty.","zh":"把结果合并会掩盖哪些使用者遇到了困难。","word":"Aggregating","meaning":"合并","tip":"关注“Aggregating”在这句话中如何表达“合并”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 4","en":"We should preserve the disagreement about priorities instead of averaging it away.","zh":"我们应保留优先事项的分歧，而非通过取平均将其抹去。","word":"averaging it away","meaning":"通过取平均抹去","tip":"关注“averaging it away”在这句话中如何表达“通过取平均抹去”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 5","en":"The interviews explain experiences that the traffic counts cannot capture.","zh":"访谈解释了交通计数无法捕捉的体验。","word":"capture","meaning":"捕捉","tip":"关注“capture”在这句话中如何表达“捕捉”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 6","en":"Neither source alone supports a conclusion about universal accessibility.","zh":"任何一个来源都不能单独支持普遍可达性的结论。","word":"universal accessibility","meaning":"普遍可达性","tip":"关注“universal accessibility”在这句话中如何表达“普遍可达性”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 7","en":"The synthesis needs to identify where the evidence converges and where it diverges.","zh":"综合分析需指出证据在哪里一致、在哪里分歧。","word":"converges","meaning":"汇合一致","tip":"关注“converges”在这句话中如何表达“汇合一致”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 8","en":"A targeted corner redesign may retain the benefit while addressing the barrier.","zh":"有针对性的转角改造可能保留收益并解决障碍。","word":"targeted","meaning":"有针对性的","tip":"关注“targeted”在这句话中如何表达“有针对性的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 9","en":"Before combining the figures, check whether their observation periods match.","zh":"合并数字之前，要检查观察时段是否一致。","word":"observation periods","meaning":"观察时段","tip":"关注“observation periods”在这句话中如何表达“观察时段”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 10","en":"The source with more numbers is not automatically the more relevant one.","zh":"数字更多的来源并不自动更相关。","word":"relevant","meaning":"相关的","tip":"关注“relevant”在这句话中如何表达“相关的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 11","en":"An unresolved difference should be reported rather than silently harmonised.","zh":"尚未解决的差异应被报告，而非悄悄统一。","word":"harmonised","meaning":"统一","tip":"关注“harmonised”在这句话中如何表达“统一”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"跨来源综合与冲突证据 · 12","en":"The recommendation follows from the combined evidence, not from one preferred headline.","zh":"建议来自综合证据，而非某个偏爱的标题。","word":"combined evidence","meaning":"综合证据","tip":"关注“combined evidence”在这句话中如何表达“综合证据”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The findings are compatible rather than ___ (CONTRADICT).","answers":["contradictory"],"explanation":"形容词contradictory。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: disaggregate / aggregate / aggravate. To see which users struggle, we should ___ the results by age group.","answers":["disaggregate"],"explanation":"disaggregate表示按组拆分。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use WHILE: Cycling increased, and some pedestrians became less comfortable.","answers":["Cycling increased while some pedestrians became less comfortable."],"explanation":"while在此保留同时存在的对比。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: One report does not automatically take precedence ___ the other.","answers":["over"],"explanation":"take precedence over优先于。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at two fictional reports about an urban river path.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to retain the overall increase and specify where design changes might improve access for particular users. At two fictional reports about an urban river path, the initial suggestion was to combine access benefits with observations of uneven use. The relevant background is that a transport survey counted more weekday cyclists after resurfacing, while interviews with older pedestrians described difficulty at two shared corners. Nevertheless, the counts and interviews addressed different questions, so treating one as a refutation of the other discarded useful evidence. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe river path is not experienced by an average user who simultaneously rides a bicycle and walks cautiously around every corner. Different people can gain and lose from the same design. The two reports should therefore be combined in a way that preserves their units of observation. Counting cyclists answers one question; listening to older pedestrians answers another. The authority could inspect the identified corners, test clearer separation or reduced speeds, and monitor whether the improvement creates new barriers elsewhere. It need not reject resurfacing to acknowledge a design problem. Equally, a positive traffic count cannot settle the question of access for everyone. The recommendation should state whose experience each proposed measure is intended to improve.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at two fictional reports about an urban river path. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to retain the overall increase and specify where design changes might improve access for particular users?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to combine access benefits with observations of uneven use. The relevant detail is that a transport survey counted more weekday cyclists after resurfacing, while interviews with older pedestrians described difficulty at two shared corners. However, the counts and interviews addressed different questions, so treating one as a refutation of the other discarded useful evidence. For that reason, I would retain the overall increase and specify where design changes might improve access for particular users. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“跨来源综合与冲突证据”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释跨来源综合与冲突证据的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during two accounts of a new market timetable. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that stallholders reported higher morning sales while cleaners described later finishing times. However, the accounts measured different consequences of the same change. We therefore needed to retain both results rather than treating one as a refutation. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to compare financial benefits with the additional staffing burden. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The accounts measured different consequences of the same change.","options":["Sales fell while the cleaning burden increased.","The accounts measured different consequences of the same change.","The cleaners reported shorter finishing times."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Retain both results rather than treating one as a refutation.","options":["Retain both results rather than treating one as a refutation.","Treat the accounts as incompatible descriptions of the same outcome.","Report higher sales as proof that nobody faced an extra burden."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Compare financial benefits with the additional staffing burden.","options":["Expand on sales figures without considering staffing costs.","Reverse the timetable without comparing the financial benefit.","Compare financial benefits with the additional staffing burden."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":77,"title":"高压追问与边界说明","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Answer a difficult question directly and qualify only what requires qualification","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第76章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a public hearing on a community workshop","text":"At a public hearing on a community workshop, participants considered whether to respond to a demand for an absolute safety guarantee. The background was specific: a resident asked whether an accident could ever happen, and the coordinator described inspections, training and the remaining limits of supervision. However, reciting procedures without answering the guarantee question sounded evasive even though the procedures were relevant. The proposed response was to say plainly that zero risk cannot be promised, then explain the controls and the conditions for stopping an activity.\n\nA promise that no accident could ever occur would offer reassurance unsupported by any realistic supervision arrangement. The coordinator should answer that point before describing training and inspections. Otherwise, even accurate procedural information may sound like an attempt to avoid the question. The next step is to explain who checks equipment, who can stop an activity and what happens when a control fails. Residents then have criteria they can question rather than a general invitation to trust the organisers. The coordinator should also distinguish a documented hazard from an unknown frequency. Such distinctions make the remaining risk visible without presenting uncertainty as either a reason for panic or permission to ignore a preventable problem. No, we cannot guarantee that an accident will never happen. What we can explain is how risks are reduced and who can stop the activity. The question deserves a direct answer before a list of procedures. I do not have evidence for that precise figure, so I will not invent one. Let me distinguish the risk we have measured from the risk we have not."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a charity funding interview","text":"This independent fictional case introduces a different setting. Its purpose is to test transfer, so the conclusion must be recovered from the new details rather than carried over from an earlier example. The participants have a practical decision to make, and an attractive first impression is not enough.\n\nAt a charity funding interview, the discussion concerned whether to answer criticism of an uncertain impact estimate. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: a panel member demanded an exact number of people whose lives had changed, but the charity held attendance records and follow up interviews rather than a causal evaluation. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: inventing a precise impact total would turn incomplete evidence into false certainty. The suggested response was to state what can be counted, what remains an estimate, and what further evaluation would resolve.\n\nAttendance is a count of participation, while a life changed is an interpretation requiring a definition and stronger evidence. Follow up interviews may reveal valuable experiences, but a striking story cannot establish how every participant would have fared without the charity. The funding panel deserves this distinction before it evaluates the proposal. The organisation can still describe its activities, the needs it addresses and the experiences reported by participants. It should also say whether it can track outcomes over time. Refusing an invented total is not an admission that the work is useless. It is a commitment to make claims whose strength matches the evaluation actually undertaken, while planning how to answer the harder question.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A resident asked whether an accident could ever happen, and the coordinator described inspections, training and the remaining limits of supervision.","options":["A resident asked whether an accident could ever happen, and the coordinator described inspections, training and the remaining limits of supervision.","The coordinator promised zero risk before describing inspections.","The resident asked only whether a particular inspection had occurred."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Reciting procedures without answering the guarantee question sounded evasive even though the procedures were relevant.","options":["Relevant procedures could logically establish that accidents were impossible.","Admitting residual risk would mean that supervision had no value.","Reciting procedures without answering the guarantee question sounded evasive even though the procedures were relevant."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Say plainly that zero risk cannot be promised, then explain the controls and the conditions for stopping an activity.","options":["Promise zero risk provided residents sign the participation form.","Say plainly that zero risk cannot be promised, then explain the controls and the conditions for stopping an activity.","Answer with inspection details while leaving the guarantee question open."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A panel member demanded an exact number of people whose lives had changed, but the charity held attendance records and follow up interviews rather than a causal evaluation.","options":["A panel member demanded an exact number of people whose lives had changed, but the charity held attendance records and follow up interviews rather than a causal evaluation.","The charity had a causal evaluation isolating its impact.","The panel asked for the number of recorded attendances only."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Inventing a precise impact total would turn incomplete evidence into false certainty.","options":["Attendance records established precisely how many lives changed.","Follow up stories had no relevance because they could not establish a causal total.","Inventing a precise impact total would turn incomplete evidence into false certainty."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"State what can be counted, what remains an estimate, and what further evaluation would resolve.","options":["Avoid describing any outcome because independent effects are uncertain.","State what can be counted, what remains an estimate, and what further evaluation would resolve.","Convert attendance into a precise causal impact number."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 1","en":"No, we cannot guarantee that an accident will never happen.","zh":"不能，我们无法保证事故永远不会发生。","word":"guarantee","meaning":"保证","tip":"关注“guarantee”在这句话中如何表达“保证”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 2","en":"What we can explain is how risks are reduced and who can stop the activity.","zh":"我们能解释的是如何降低风险，以及谁有权停止活动。","word":"risks are reduced","meaning":"风险得到降低","tip":"关注“risks are reduced”在这句话中如何表达“风险得到降低”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 3","en":"The question deserves a direct answer before a list of procedures.","zh":"在列举程序之前，这个问题值得一个直接回答。","word":"direct answer","meaning":"直接回答","tip":"关注“direct answer”在这句话中如何表达“直接回答”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 4","en":"I do not have evidence for that precise figure, so I will not invent one.","zh":"我没有证据支持那个精确数字，因此不会编造。","word":"precise figure","meaning":"精确数字","tip":"关注“precise figure”在这句话中如何表达“精确数字”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 5","en":"Let me distinguish the risk we have measured from the risk we have not.","zh":"让我区分已测量的风险和未测量的风险。","word":"measured","meaning":"测量了","tip":"关注“measured”在这句话中如何表达“测量了”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 6","en":"That is a fair challenge to the conclusion, although not to every observation.","zh":"这是对结论的合理质疑，尽管并非针对每一项观察。","word":"fair challenge","meaning":"合理质疑","tip":"关注“fair challenge”在这句话中如何表达“合理质疑”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 7","en":"I would need to check the inspection record before answering that detail.","zh":"在回答那个细节之前，我需要核查检查记录。","word":"inspection record","meaning":"检查记录","tip":"关注“inspection record”在这句话中如何表达“检查记录”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 8","en":"The control reduces exposure without eliminating every possibility of harm.","zh":"这项控制降低了暴露风险，却没有消除一切伤害可能。","word":"exposure","meaning":"暴露风险","tip":"关注“exposure”在这句话中如何表达“暴露风险”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 9","en":"If the equipment fails that check, the session will not proceed.","zh":"如果设备未通过那项检查，活动将不再继续。","word":"proceed","meaning":"继续进行","tip":"关注“proceed”在这句话中如何表达“继续进行”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 10","en":"My uncertainty concerns the frequency, not the existence of the hazard.","zh":"我不确定的是发生频率，而不是危险是否存在。","word":"frequency","meaning":"频率","tip":"关注“frequency”在这句话中如何表达“频率”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 11","en":"We should publish the decision rule rather than merely ask for trust.","zh":"我们应公布决策规则，而非仅仅要求信任。","word":"decision rule","meaning":"决策规则","tip":"关注“decision rule”在这句话中如何表达“决策规则”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"高压追问与边界说明 · 12","en":"An honest limitation can strengthen accountability instead of weakening it.","zh":"诚实说明局限可以加强问责，而非削弱它。","word":"accountability","meaning":"问责","tip":"关注“accountability”在这句话中如何表达“问责”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"A candid answer improves ___ (ACCOUNTABLE).","answers":["accountability"],"explanation":"名词accountability表示问责与责任说明。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: residual / residential / resolute. The controls reduce risk but leave some ___ risk.","answers":["residual"],"explanation":"residual表示剩余的。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use CANNOT: A guarantee of zero risk is beyond what we can offer.","answers":["We cannot guarantee zero risk."],"explanation":"直接回答无法保证。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: I would need to check the record before commenting ___ that detail.","answers":["on"],"explanation":"comment on固定结构。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a public hearing on a community workshop.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to say plainly that zero risk cannot be promised, then explain the controls and the conditions for stopping an activity. At a public hearing on a community workshop, the initial suggestion was to respond to a demand for an absolute safety guarantee. The relevant background is that a resident asked whether an accident could ever happen, and the coordinator described inspections, training and the remaining limits of supervision. Nevertheless, reciting procedures without answering the guarantee question sounded evasive even though the procedures were relevant. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nA promise that no accident could ever occur would offer reassurance unsupported by any realistic supervision arrangement. The coordinator should answer that point before describing training and inspections. Otherwise, even accurate procedural information may sound like an attempt to avoid the question. The next step is to explain who checks equipment, who can stop an activity and what happens when a control fails. Residents then have criteria they can question rather than a general invitation to trust the organisers. The coordinator should also distinguish a documented hazard from an unknown frequency. Such distinctions make the remaining risk visible without presenting uncertainty as either a reason for panic or permission to ignore a preventable problem.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a public hearing on a community workshop. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to say plainly that zero risk cannot be promised, then explain the controls and the conditions for stopping an activity?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to respond to a demand for an absolute safety guarantee. The relevant detail is that a resident asked whether an accident could ever happen, and the coordinator described inspections, training and the remaining limits of supervision. However, reciting procedures without answering the guarantee question sounded evasive even though the procedures were relevant. For that reason, I would say plainly that zero risk cannot be promised, then explain the controls and the conditions for stopping an activity. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“高压追问与边界说明”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释高压追问与边界说明的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a school board question about a new climbing wall. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that a parent asked whether injuries could be completely ruled out. However, the organiser had inspection records but could not eliminate every risk. We therefore needed to answer the guarantee question directly before describing safeguards. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to explain supervision rules and the conditions that would stop a session. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The organiser had inspection records but could not eliminate every risk.","options":["The organiser had inspection records but could not eliminate every risk.","The inspection records ruled out every possibility of injury.","The parent asked whether the wall had any supervision at all."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Answer the guarantee question directly before describing safeguards.","options":["Answer the guarantee question only by listing procedures.","Treat residual risk as proof that inspection is pointless.","Answer the guarantee question directly before describing safeguards."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Explain supervision rules and the conditions that would stop a session.","options":["Discuss supervision but omit the conditions for stopping.","Explain supervision rules and the conditions that would stop a session.","Promise zero injuries if all participants obey instructions."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":78,"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Challenge an argument in its strongest reasonable form","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第77章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a debate about keeping a public swimming pool open later","text":"At a debate about keeping a public swimming pool open later, participants considered whether to weigh access for shift workers against staffing costs. The background was specific: supporters argued that current hours excluded people with irregular jobs, while opponents cited the cost of lifeguards rather than hostility to those users. However, portraying opponents as indifferent to health avoided the actual resource objection. The proposed response was to acknowledge the strongest cost argument and compare a limited trial with the present schedule.\n\nThe staffing objection deserves an answer in financial and operational terms. Accusing opponents of indifference would leave that objection intact while making cooperation harder. A limited late opening trial could provide demand information and a realistic record of staffing needs, provided its costs and review criteria were agreed in advance. Supporters would then need to accept the possibility that demand was lower than expected; opponents would need to accept evidence of substantial unmet need. This creates a disagreement that experience can inform. The argument should compare the trial with the current arrangement rather than contrast perfect access with unlimited expense. Both sides can care about public health while assigning different weights to the available options. The strongest objection concerns staffing costs, not indifference to swimmers. I accept that concern but question whether it rules out a limited trial. Could we state the opposing argument in terms its supporters would recognise? The example challenges a universal claim without settling the whole policy. Your conclusion follows only if no cheaper staffing arrangement is feasible. We agree on the access problem but differ over the proportional response."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a debate over pedestrianising a market street","text":"The next original fictional account presents a decision that looks straightforward until the relevant qualification is considered. There is no hidden correct opinion about the institution. The reading task is to establish what the account supports, what it leaves open, and what the proposed response actually changes.\n\nAt a debate over pedestrianising a market street, the discussion concerned whether to distinguish accessibility objections from opposition to cleaner air. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: shopkeepers worried about deliveries and residents with limited mobility, even though most accepted that through traffic made the street unpleasant. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: responding only with pollution statistics left the specific access problem unanswered. The suggested response was to test timed deliveries and accessible pickup points while examining whether traffic would move elsewhere.\n\nCleaner air is relevant, but it does not tell a person with limited mobility how to reach a shop. Equally, an access concern does not establish that the present traffic pattern is the only possible arrangement. The debate becomes productive when these interests are separated from the initial positions. Timed delivery windows and accessible collection points may address some objections, though their practicality needs testing. Traffic displacement also matters because a cleaner market street could shift burdens to neighbouring roads. An honest comparison should therefore consider the whole arrangement rather than its most appealing photograph. Participants can agree on the harm caused by traffic while disagreeing about whether this design distributes the benefits and costs fairly.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Supporters argued that current hours excluded people with irregular jobs, while opponents cited the cost of lifeguards rather than hostility to those users.","options":["Opponents denied that irregular workers needed access to exercise.","Supporters and opponents had agreed the late service would be cost free.","Supporters argued that current hours excluded people with irregular jobs, while opponents cited the cost of lifeguards rather than hostility to those users."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Portraying opponents as indifferent to health avoided the actual resource objection.","options":["The debate required choosing between unlimited opening and closure.","Portraying opponents as indifferent to health avoided the actual resource objection.","The staffing objection was answered by showing that health matters."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Acknowledge the strongest cost argument and compare a limited trial with the present schedule.","options":["Acknowledge the strongest cost argument and compare a limited trial with the present schedule.","Dismiss the cost objection as hostility to shift workers.","Make late opening permanent before estimating staffing needs."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Shopkeepers worried about deliveries and residents with limited mobility, even though most accepted that through traffic made the street unpleasant.","options":["All shopkeepers opposed cleaner air as an objective.","The proposal already resolved delivery and accessible pickup arrangements.","Shopkeepers worried about deliveries and residents with limited mobility, even though most accepted that through traffic made the street unpleasant."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Responding only with pollution statistics left the specific access problem unanswered.","options":["Mobility concerns proved the existing traffic pattern was the only option.","Responding only with pollution statistics left the specific access problem unanswered.","Pollution figures answered the specific mobility problem."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Test timed deliveries and accessible pickup points while examining whether traffic would move elsewhere.","options":["Test timed deliveries and accessible pickup points while examining whether traffic would move elsewhere.","Use pollution statistics instead of examining deliveries.","Reject any trial without considering timed access or nearby streets."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 1","en":"The strongest objection concerns staffing costs, not indifference to swimmers.","zh":"最有力的异议关乎人员成本，而非对游泳者漠不关心。","word":"staffing costs","meaning":"人员成本","tip":"关注“staffing costs”在这句话中如何表达“人员成本”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 2","en":"I accept that concern but question whether it rules out a limited trial.","zh":"我接受这个担忧，但质疑它是否排除了有限试行。","word":"rules out","meaning":"排除","tip":"关注“rules out”在这句话中如何表达“排除”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 3","en":"Could we state the opposing argument in terms its supporters would recognise?","zh":"我们能否用支持者认可的方式陈述对方论点？","word":"supporters would recognise","meaning":"支持者会认可","tip":"关注“supporters would recognise”在这句话中如何表达“支持者会认可”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 4","en":"The example challenges a universal claim without settling the whole policy.","zh":"这个例子质疑了普遍论断，却没有解决整个政策问题。","word":"universal claim","meaning":"普遍论断","tip":"关注“universal claim”在这句话中如何表达“普遍论断”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 5","en":"Your conclusion follows only if no cheaper staffing arrangement is feasible.","zh":"只有不存在可行的更低成本排班时，你的结论才成立。","word":"only if","meaning":"只有在某条件下","tip":"关注“only if”在这句话中如何表达“只有在某条件下”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 6","en":"We agree on the access problem but differ over the proportional response.","zh":"我们认同出行问题，却对适度回应存在分歧。","word":"proportional response","meaning":"适度回应","tip":"关注“proportional response”在这句话中如何表达“适度回应”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 7","en":"That rebuttal addresses the slogan rather than the underlying argument.","zh":"那项反驳针对口号，而不是背后的论证。","word":"rebuttal","meaning":"反驳","tip":"关注“rebuttal”在这句话中如何表达“反驳”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 8","en":"The choice involves competing benefits, not a contest between care and neglect.","zh":"选择涉及不同收益，而非关心与漠视的较量。","word":"competing benefits","meaning":"不同的收益","tip":"关注“competing benefits”在这句话中如何表达“不同的收益”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 9","en":"A trial could test demand without committing the budget indefinitely.","zh":"试行可以检验需求，而不无限期承诺预算。","word":"indefinitely","meaning":"无限期地","tip":"关注“indefinitely”在这句话中如何表达“无限期地”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 10","en":"What result would persuade you to revise your present position?","zh":"什么结果会促使你修改目前立场？","word":"revise","meaning":"修改","tip":"关注“revise”在这句话中如何表达“修改”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 11","en":"I would withdraw that objection if the cost assumption proved mistaken.","zh":"如果成本假设被证明有误，我会撤回那项异议。","word":"withdraw","meaning":"撤回","tip":"关注“withdraw”在这句话中如何表达“撤回”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"辩论、反驳与钢人论证 · 12","en":"Acknowledging a strong counterargument makes the remaining disagreement clearer.","zh":"承认有力反论点会让剩余分歧更清楚。","word":"counterargument","meaning":"反论点","tip":"关注“counterargument”在这句话中如何表达“反论点”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The reply is a ___ (REBUT), not a description of motives.","answers":["rebuttal"],"explanation":"名词rebuttal表示反驳。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: charitable / charity / charitably. A ___ reading presents the strongest reasonable opposing case.","answers":["charitable"],"explanation":"charitable reading指善意且公平的解读。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use ONLY IF: A trial is acceptable on the condition that staffing can be funded.","answers":["A trial is acceptable only if staffing can be funded."],"explanation":"only if引导必要条件。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: We differ ___ the response, although we agree on the problem.","answers":["over"],"explanation":"differ over表示在某问题上意见不同。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a debate about keeping a public swimming pool open later.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to acknowledge the strongest cost argument and compare a limited trial with the present schedule. At a debate about keeping a public swimming pool open later, the initial suggestion was to weigh access for shift workers against staffing costs. The relevant background is that supporters argued that current hours excluded people with irregular jobs, while opponents cited the cost of lifeguards rather than hostility to those users. Nevertheless, portraying opponents as indifferent to health avoided the actual resource objection. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe staffing objection deserves an answer in financial and operational terms. Accusing opponents of indifference would leave that objection intact while making cooperation harder. A limited late opening trial could provide demand information and a realistic record of staffing needs, provided its costs and review criteria were agreed in advance. Supporters would then need to accept the possibility that demand was lower than expected; opponents would need to accept evidence of substantial unmet need. This creates a disagreement that experience can inform. The argument should compare the trial with the current arrangement rather than contrast perfect access with unlimited expense. Both sides can care about public health while assigning different weights to the available options.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a debate about keeping a public swimming pool open later. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to acknowledge the strongest cost argument and compare a limited trial with the present schedule?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to weigh access for shift workers against staffing costs. The relevant detail is that supporters argued that current hours excluded people with irregular jobs, while opponents cited the cost of lifeguards rather than hostility to those users. However, portraying opponents as indifferent to health avoided the actual resource objection. For that reason, I would acknowledge the strongest cost argument and compare a limited trial with the present schedule. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“辩论、反驳与钢人论证”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释辩论、反驳与钢人论证的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a debate about extending library hours. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that opponents cited staffing costs rather than hostility to evening readers. However, a supporter replied by accusing them of disliking education. We therefore needed to address the strongest actual objection instead of an invented motive. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to cost a limited evening trial and evaluate demand before permanent expansion. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"A supporter replied by accusing them of disliking education.","options":["Opponents said they disliked education.","Staffing costs had already been resolved by funding.","A supporter replied by accusing them of disliking education."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Address the strongest actual objection instead of an invented motive.","options":["Treat evening demand as irrelevant to the decision.","Address the strongest actual objection instead of an invented motive.","Respond to motives instead of the specific cost objection."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Cost a limited evening trial and evaluate demand before permanent expansion.","options":["Cost a limited evening trial and evaluate demand before permanent expansion.","Extend permanently before costing staffing.","Reject evening access without testing a limited service."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":79,"title":"谈判与条件交换","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Identify interests, constraints and genuinely conditional concessions","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第78章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a negotiation over rehearsal space in a school hall","text":"At a negotiation over rehearsal space in a school hall, participants considered whether to agree a schedule between a drama group and a youth club. The background was specific: the drama group needed uninterrupted evenings before its performance, while the youth club needed predictable access rather than a particular weekday. However, splitting every evening equally would satisfy neither need and create repeated setup costs. The proposed response was to offer a short performance preparation block in exchange for a longer guaranteed youth club booking afterwards.\n\nThe groups initial positions make the room look impossible to share, but the reasons behind those positions reveal an exchange. Continuous preparation is especially valuable before a performance, while predictability may matter more than a particular weekday to the youth club. A negotiated block can therefore be fair without dividing each evening in half. The arrangement should specify dates, setup responsibilities and what happens if the performance moves. Confirmation matters because each group is being asked to rely on a later benefit. I would also schedule a short review after the first exchange. That gives both parties a way to correct practical problems without treating every request for adjustment as a breach of goodwill. Our priority is uninterrupted rehearsal time rather than any particular weekday. Would a guaranteed later booking compensate for the temporary loss of access? We can concede those evenings provided the later arrangement is confirmed. That is a conditional offer, not an agreement to surrender the slot. Let us compare the costs of setup before dividing the hours equally. The positions conflict, but the underlying interests may be compatible."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a negotiation between a bakery and a community cafe","text":"A separate fictional case offers another opportunity to distinguish an observation from its interpretation. The people involved share enough common ground to continue working, but their initial description risks implying more than they have established. The difference becomes visible when the suggested action is compared with the available detail.\n\nAt a negotiation between a bakery and a community cafe, the discussion concerned whether to revise a supply arrangement after a price increase. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the bakery needed orders earlier to reduce waste, while the cafe needed predictable weekly spending rather than the cheapest possible loaf. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: an immediate demand for a price cut concealed an opportunity to exchange reliable volume for a stable rate. The suggested response was to link the price concession to a minimum order and set a review date both sides can meet.\n\nThe price discussion initially appears to be a contest over how much each side pays. Looking at waste and ordering reveals another dimension. Predictable volume helps the bakery plan production, and a stable rate helps the cafe plan its budget. A concession can therefore be tied to an action the other party values rather than offered without a return. The resulting agreement should define the minimum order, the notice required for changes and what happens when demand falls unexpectedly. A vague promise to be reasonable would leave the central risks untouched. Neither side needs to disclose every internal cost, but each needs enough information to judge whether the proposed exchange is reliable and acceptable.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The drama group needed uninterrupted evenings before its performance, while the youth club needed predictable access rather than a particular weekday.","options":["The youth club preferred unpredictable access spread across evenings.","The drama group needed uninterrupted evenings before its performance, while the youth club needed predictable access rather than a particular weekday.","Both groups required the same weekday for identical reasons."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Splitting every evening equally would satisfy neither need and create repeated setup costs.","options":["Splitting every evening equally would satisfy neither need and create repeated setup costs.","Equal hours on every evening would minimise setup costs.","The drama group needed permanent control rather than a temporary block."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Offer a short performance preparation block in exchange for a longer guaranteed youth club booking afterwards.","options":["Divide every evening equally regardless of setup needs.","Promise future access without specifying dates or confirmation.","Offer a short performance preparation block in exchange for a longer guaranteed youth club booking afterwards."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The bakery needed orders earlier to reduce waste, while the cafe needed predictable weekly spending rather than the cheapest possible loaf.","options":["The cafe was concerned only with obtaining the cheapest possible loaf.","The bakery needed orders earlier to reduce waste, while the cafe needed predictable weekly spending rather than the cheapest possible loaf.","The bakery needed the cafe to place orders later each day."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"An immediate demand for a price cut concealed an opportunity to exchange reliable volume for a stable rate.","options":["An immediate demand for a price cut concealed an opportunity to exchange reliable volume for a stable rate.","Price was the only dimension over which either party could negotiate.","Reliable volume would matter to the cafe but not the bakery."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Link the price concession to a minimum order and set a review date both sides can meet.","options":["Demand a lower price without offering an ordering commitment.","Accept a stable rate without specifying minimum volume or a review date.","Link the price concession to a minimum order and set a review date both sides can meet."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 1","en":"Our priority is uninterrupted rehearsal time rather than any particular weekday.","zh":"我们的优先需求是连续排练时间，而非某个特定星期几。","word":"uninterrupted","meaning":"连续不被打断的","tip":"关注“uninterrupted”在这句话中如何表达“连续不被打断的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 2","en":"Would a guaranteed later booking compensate for the temporary loss of access?","zh":"保证之后的预约能否补偿暂时失去使用权？","word":"compensate for","meaning":"补偿","tip":"关注“compensate for”在这句话中如何表达“补偿”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 3","en":"We can concede those evenings provided the later arrangement is confirmed.","zh":"只要后续安排得到确认，我们可以让出那些晚间时段。","word":"provided","meaning":"只要","tip":"关注“provided”在这句话中如何表达“只要”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 4","en":"That is a conditional offer, not an agreement to surrender the slot.","zh":"那是一项有条件提议，而不是同意放弃时段。","word":"conditional offer","meaning":"有条件提议","tip":"关注“conditional offer”在这句话中如何表达“有条件提议”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 5","en":"Let us compare the costs of setup before dividing the hours equally.","zh":"在平均分配时段之前，我们先比较布置成本。","word":"setup","meaning":"布置","tip":"关注“setup”在这句话中如何表达“布置”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 6","en":"The positions conflict, but the underlying interests may be compatible.","zh":"立场相冲突，但背后的实际诉求可能相容。","word":"underlying interests","meaning":"背后的实际诉求","tip":"关注“underlying interests”在这句话中如何表达“背后的实际诉求”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 7","en":"I need to consult the group before committing to that exchange.","zh":"在承诺这项交换之前，我需要征求小组意见。","word":"committing","meaning":"作出承诺","tip":"关注“committing”在这句话中如何表达“作出承诺”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 8","en":"If either condition cannot be met, we should reopen the whole package.","zh":"如果任一条件无法满足，我们应重新讨论整套方案。","word":"reopen","meaning":"重新讨论","tip":"关注“reopen”在这句话中如何表达“重新讨论”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 9","en":"The written agreement should specify dates rather than rely on goodwill.","zh":"书面协议应明确日期，而非依靠善意。","word":"goodwill","meaning":"善意","tip":"关注“goodwill”在这句话中如何表达“善意”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 10","en":"What is the least predictable part of the current arrangement for you?","zh":"对你们来说，目前安排中最不确定的是什么？","word":"predictable","meaning":"可预见的","tip":"关注“predictable”在这句话中如何表达“可预见的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 11","en":"A reciprocal concession should address a real constraint on each side.","zh":"相互让步应解决双方各自的实际限制。","word":"reciprocal concession","meaning":"相互让步","tip":"关注“reciprocal concession”在这句话中如何表达“相互让步”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"谈判与条件交换 · 12","en":"We have an outline agreement, subject to confirmation of the hall booking.","zh":"我们达成了框架协议，但须待礼堂预约确认。","word":"subject to","meaning":"以某条件为前提","tip":"关注“subject to”在这句话中如何表达“以某条件为前提”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The offer is ___ (CONTINGENCY) on written confirmation.","answers":["contingent"],"explanation":"be contingent on表示取决于。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: reciprocal / reciprocate / reciprocity. Both sides make a ___ concession.","answers":["reciprocal"],"explanation":"形容词reciprocal表示相互的。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use SUBJECT TO: The agreement is valid if the booking is confirmed.","answers":["The agreement is subject to confirmation of the booking."],"explanation":"subject to表示以某条件为前提。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: We can exchange early access ___ guaranteed later dates.","answers":["for"],"explanation":"exchange A for B。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a negotiation over rehearsal space in a school hall.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to offer a short performance preparation block in exchange for a longer guaranteed youth club booking afterwards. At a negotiation over rehearsal space in a school hall, the initial suggestion was to agree a schedule between a drama group and a youth club. The relevant background is that the drama group needed uninterrupted evenings before its performance, while the youth club needed predictable access rather than a particular weekday. Nevertheless, splitting every evening equally would satisfy neither need and create repeated setup costs. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe groups initial positions make the room look impossible to share, but the reasons behind those positions reveal an exchange. Continuous preparation is especially valuable before a performance, while predictability may matter more than a particular weekday to the youth club. A negotiated block can therefore be fair without dividing each evening in half. The arrangement should specify dates, setup responsibilities and what happens if the performance moves. Confirmation matters because each group is being asked to rely on a later benefit. I would also schedule a short review after the first exchange. That gives both parties a way to correct practical problems without treating every request for adjustment as a breach of goodwill.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a negotiation over rehearsal space in a school hall. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to offer a short performance preparation block in exchange for a longer guaranteed youth club booking afterwards?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to agree a schedule between a drama group and a youth club. The relevant detail is that the drama group needed uninterrupted evenings before its performance, while the youth club needed predictable access rather than a particular weekday. However, splitting every evening equally would satisfy neither need and create repeated setup costs. For that reason, I would offer a short performance preparation block in exchange for a longer guaranteed youth club booking afterwards. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“谈判与条件交换”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释谈判与条件交换的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a negotiation about a shared exhibition room. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that one group needed a full installation week while another needed fixed weekend access. However, an equal daily split would create repeated setup work for both. We therefore needed to exchange blocks of time according to underlying needs. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to confirm one installation week in return for guaranteed later weekends. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"An equal daily split would create repeated setup work for both.","options":["Setup took no time and could be repeated without cost.","An equal daily split would create repeated setup work for both.","Both groups needed identical daily access."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Exchange blocks of time according to underlying needs.","options":["Exchange blocks of time according to underlying needs.","Divide each day equally because fairness requires identical hours.","Treat positions as fixed without examining needs."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Confirm one installation week in return for guaranteed later weekends.","options":["Split every day and repeat installation for each group.","Offer later weekends without confirming any dates.","Confirm one installation week in return for guaranteed later weekends."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":80,"title":"即时改述与概念解释","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Reformulate accurately for a different audience under time pressure","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第79章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a workshop explaining digital privacy to older residents","text":"At a workshop explaining digital privacy to older residents, participants considered whether to explain metadata without treating it as the content of a message. The background was specific: the instructor compared a messages content with the outside of an envelope, then explained that digital records can reveal repeated contact patterns. However, the envelope analogy made the concept accessible but could conceal how detailed modern tracking becomes when records are combined. The proposed response was to use the analogy as a starting point and explicitly name where it stops being accurate.\n\nThe envelope analogy offers a useful entry point because it separates message content from information surrounding the message. It becomes misleading if listeners assume that digital metadata is limited to one sender and one address on one object. Combined records can reveal contact patterns at a scale the ordinary envelope does not suggest. The instructor should therefore name that limit immediately and show a second example involving several records over time. Asking participants to explain a new case would reveal more than asking whether the analogy was clear. The aim is not to make a technical term memorable at any cost. It is to leave listeners with a distinction they can use accurately after the convenient comparison has been set aside. Think of metadata as information about a message rather than its contents. The envelope analogy helps initially, but it understates the scale of tracking. Let me put that another way without changing the central distinction. Several harmless looking records can reveal a pattern when combined. The analogy stops being accurate at precisely that point."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a science volunteer explaining an uncertainty interval","text":"In this newly invented case, readers should attend to the relationship between a stated aim and the evidence used to defend it. The account does not invite a judgement about personalities. Instead, it supplies a limited situation in which a careful summary can preserve something that a confident headline loses.\n\nAt a science volunteer explaining an uncertainty interval, the discussion concerned whether to clarify what a range around an estimate does and does not mean. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the volunteer first called it a margin of doubt, then distinguished uncertainty in the estimate from differences between individual people. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: a vivid phrase helped listeners begin but could make all forms of variation sound interchangeable. The suggested response was to restate the idea using the actual question and check the listeners understanding with a fresh example.\n\nUncertainty about an estimated average does not describe the range within which every individual observation must fall. The convenient phrase margin of doubt risks blurring these concepts. An example using repeated samples can reveal why the estimate changes, while a separate example can show differences between people. The volunteer should check understanding by asking listeners to apply the distinction, rather than asking whether the explanation sounded clear. If listeners confuse the two again, the reformulation has not yet achieved its purpose. A successful analogy is not one the audience remembers as a slogan; it is one that helps them reason accurately when the same idea appears in an unfamiliar form.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The instructor compared a messages content with the outside of an envelope, then explained that digital records can reveal repeated contact patterns.","options":["The instructor compared a messages content with the outside of an envelope, then explained that digital records can reveal repeated contact patterns.","The instructor equated metadata with the words inside a message.","The workshop showed that isolated records reveal no contact information."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The envelope analogy made the concept accessible but could conceal how detailed modern tracking becomes when records are combined.","options":["The envelope analogy fully represented combined digital tracking.","The main difficulty was that listeners did not know what an envelope was.","The envelope analogy made the concept accessible but could conceal how detailed modern tracking becomes when records are combined."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Use the analogy as a starting point and explicitly name where it stops being accurate.","options":["Abandon every analogy in favour of technical vocabulary alone.","Use the analogy as a starting point and explicitly name where it stops being accurate.","Keep the analogy but omit its limit to avoid complexity."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The volunteer first called it a margin of doubt, then distinguished uncertainty in the estimate from differences between individual people.","options":["The volunteer first called it a margin of doubt, then distinguished uncertainty in the estimate from differences between individual people.","The volunteer defined uncertainty as variation between all individual people.","The first phrase clearly distinguished every relevant kind of variation."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A vivid phrase helped listeners begin but could make all forms of variation sound interchangeable.","options":["A memorable phrase established successful transfer without checking.","The only issue was whether listeners liked the phrase margin of doubt.","A vivid phrase helped listeners begin but could make all forms of variation sound interchangeable."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Restate the idea using the actual question and check the listeners understanding with a fresh example.","options":["Use one range to describe both an estimate and every individuals variation.","Restate the idea using the actual question and check the listeners understanding with a fresh example.","Ask whether the wording sounded clear without testing a new example."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 1","en":"Think of metadata as information about a message rather than its contents.","zh":"可以把元数据理解为有关消息的信息，而非消息内容。","word":"metadata","meaning":"元数据","tip":"关注“metadata”在这句话中如何表达“元数据”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 2","en":"The envelope analogy helps initially, but it understates the scale of tracking.","zh":"信封类比起初有帮助，却低估了追踪规模。","word":"understates","meaning":"低估","tip":"关注“understates”在这句话中如何表达“低估”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 3","en":"Let me put that another way without changing the central distinction.","zh":"让我换一种说法，同时保留核心区别。","word":"distinction","meaning":"区别","tip":"关注“distinction”在这句话中如何表达“区别”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 4","en":"Several harmless looking records can reveal a pattern when combined.","zh":"几个看似无害的记录结合起来可能揭示一种模式。","word":"combined","meaning":"合并在一起","tip":"关注“combined”在这句话中如何表达“合并在一起”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 5","en":"The analogy stops being accurate at precisely that point.","zh":"类比恰恰从那一点开始不再准确。","word":"stops being accurate","meaning":"不再准确","tip":"关注“stops being accurate”在这句话中如何表达“不再准确”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 6","en":"Could you explain the difference using a message you sent yesterday?","zh":"你能用昨天发过的一条消息解释这个区别吗？","word":"explain","meaning":"解释","tip":"关注“explain”在这句话中如何表达“解释”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 7","en":"I may have simplified that too far; one qualification matters here.","zh":"我可能简化过头了；这里有一个重要限定。","word":"qualification","meaning":"限定","tip":"关注“qualification”在这句话中如何表达“限定”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 8","en":"Rephrasing should reduce the linguistic burden without removing the conceptual difficulty.","zh":"改述应降低语言负担，而不删除概念难点。","word":"linguistic burden","meaning":"语言负担","tip":"关注“linguistic burden”在这句话中如何表达“语言负担”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 9","en":"The term refers to the record of contact, not necessarily its purpose.","zh":"这个术语指联系记录，而不一定指联系目的。","word":"purpose","meaning":"目的","tip":"关注“purpose”在这句话中如何表达“目的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 10","en":"I will use a fresh example to check whether my explanation worked.","zh":"我会用一个新例子检查解释是否有效。","word":"fresh example","meaning":"新例子","tip":"关注“fresh example”在这句话中如何表达“新例子”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 11","en":"That summary preserves the main idea but loses a consequential exception.","zh":"那个总结保留了主旨，却丢失了一个重要例外。","word":"consequential exception","meaning":"关系重大的例外","tip":"关注“consequential exception”在这句话中如何表达“关系重大的例外”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"即时改述与概念解释 · 12","en":"A fluent explanation is useful only if the listener can reconstruct the distinction.","zh":"只有听者能够还原区别，流畅的解释才有用。","word":"reconstruct","meaning":"还原","tip":"关注“reconstruct”在这句话中如何表达“还原”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"A useful analogy avoids unnecessary ___ (DISTORT).","answers":["distortion"],"explanation":"名词distortion表示失真。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: accessible / access / accessibility. The explanation should be ___ to a new audience.","answers":["accessible"],"explanation":"形容词accessible表示易懂的。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use WITHOUT: Simplify the language, but preserve the distinction.","answers":["Simplify the language without losing the distinction."],"explanation":"without后接动名词。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: Let me put that ___ another way.","answers":["in"],"explanation":"put something in another way。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a workshop explaining digital privacy to older residents.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to use the analogy as a starting point and explicitly name where it stops being accurate. At a workshop explaining digital privacy to older residents, the initial suggestion was to explain metadata without treating it as the content of a message. The relevant background is that the instructor compared a messages content with the outside of an envelope, then explained that digital records can reveal repeated contact patterns. Nevertheless, the envelope analogy made the concept accessible but could conceal how detailed modern tracking becomes when records are combined. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe envelope analogy offers a useful entry point because it separates message content from information surrounding the message. It becomes misleading if listeners assume that digital metadata is limited to one sender and one address on one object. Combined records can reveal contact patterns at a scale the ordinary envelope does not suggest. The instructor should therefore name that limit immediately and show a second example involving several records over time. Asking participants to explain a new case would reveal more than asking whether the analogy was clear. The aim is not to make a technical term memorable at any cost. It is to leave listeners with a distinction they can use accurately after the convenient comparison has been set aside.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a workshop explaining digital privacy to older residents. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to use the analogy as a starting point and explicitly name where it stops being accurate?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to explain metadata without treating it as the content of a message. The relevant detail is that the instructor compared a messages content with the outside of an envelope, then explained that digital records can reveal repeated contact patterns. However, the envelope analogy made the concept accessible but could conceal how detailed modern tracking becomes when records are combined. For that reason, I would use the analogy as a starting point and explicitly name where it stops being accurate. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“即时改述与概念解释”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释即时改述与概念解释的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a workshop explaining a computer backup. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the instructor compared a backup with a spare key. However, the analogy omitted the fact that a backup must be updated when files change. We therefore needed to keep the useful comparison while stating where it fails. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to show how an old backup can restore an outdated version of a document. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The analogy omitted the fact that a backup must be updated when files change.","options":["The analogy omitted the fact that a backup must be updated when files change.","The analogy already explained the need for updates.","The backup remained current even when files changed."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Keep the useful comparison while stating where it fails.","options":["Use the memorable analogy as a complete technical explanation.","Abandon the backup because it could become outdated.","Keep the useful comparison while stating where it fails."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Show how an old backup can restore an outdated version of a document.","options":["Show only an up to date copy and omit the outdated version.","Show how an old backup can restore an outdated version of a document.","Keep the spare key comparison without naming its limit."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":81,"title":"综合评论与公平评价","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Evaluate achievement, limitations and audience suitability together","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第80章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a review of a practical guide to repairing furniture","text":"At a review of a practical guide to repairing furniture, participants considered whether to assess usefulness without demanding specialist completeness. The background was specific: the guide explained loose joints clearly and showed inexpensive tools, but its section on antique finishes offered only a brief caution. However, a beginner could benefit greatly while still needing expert help with valuable or fragile objects. The proposed response was to recommend the book to its intended readers and identify the specific tasks that exceed its scope.\n\nA useful review helps readers decide whether a book suits their likely tasks. Here, ordinary furniture repairs and valuable antique finishes involve different levels of risk. The reviewer can praise the clear sequence and inexpensive tools while advising readers to seek specialist help in the latter case. That boundary should be specific enough to guide a purchase and a practical decision. A vague statement that the book is not comprehensive would add little, because no introductory guide can be comprehensive. Nor would an unqualified recommendation protect beginners who cannot recognise when a finish is fragile. The fairest verdict identifies what the guide enables, which audience benefits, and which jobs require support beyond its pages. The guide succeeds as an introduction without replacing specialist advice. Its clearest strength is the sequence of repairs a beginner can follow. The brief treatment of antique finishes limits its suitability for valuable objects. That limitation warrants a qualification rather than an outright dismissal. I would recommend it to beginners working on ordinary furniture. The review should explain whom the recommendation is intended to help. The illustrations carry practical information rather than merely decorating the page."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a review of a public history podcast","text":"This independent fictional case introduces a different setting. Its purpose is to test transfer, so the conclusion must be recovered from the new details rather than carried over from an earlier example. The participants have a practical decision to make, and an attractive first impression is not enough.\n\nAt a review of a public history podcast, the discussion concerned whether to balance compelling storytelling with transparent sourcing. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the episodes used vivid reconstructions and a separate source list, yet listeners could not always tell which spoken lines came from documents. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the programme could be engaging and well researched while still blurring evidence and dramatic invention in particular moments. The suggested response was to praise the accessible narrative and request clearer signals around reconstructed speech.\n\nA separate source list is valuable, but it does not tell a listener at each moment whether an actor is reading documented speech or an invented reconstruction. That distinction affects how a vivid line should be remembered and repeated. The reviewer can acknowledge the programme makers research while identifying a weakness in the presentation of evidence. A short introduction or change in delivery might make the boundary clearer without destroying narrative momentum. The recommendation should not imply that dramatic reconstruction is automatically dishonest. Its fairness depends on what the audience is led to believe. The issue is therefore the transparency of the transition, not whether a history programme is permitted to be engaging.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The guide explained loose joints clearly and showed inexpensive tools, but its section on antique finishes offered only a brief caution.","options":["The guide provided specialist restoration advice for every antique finish.","The diagrams required tools unavailable to beginners.","The guide explained loose joints clearly and showed inexpensive tools, but its section on antique finishes offered only a brief caution."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A beginner could benefit greatly while still needing expert help with valuable or fragile objects.","options":["Attractive illustrations established that every instruction was safe.","A beginner could benefit greatly while still needing expert help with valuable or fragile objects.","The missing specialist detail made the book useless for ordinary repairs."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Recommend the book to its intended readers and identify the specific tasks that exceed its scope.","options":["Recommend the book to its intended readers and identify the specific tasks that exceed its scope.","Recommend the guide for valuable antiques without qualification.","Reject the guide because introductory books should be comprehensive."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The episodes used vivid reconstructions and a separate source list, yet listeners could not always tell which spoken lines came from documents.","options":["Listeners could always identify documented and reconstructed speech.","The podcast contained no source list and claimed no historical research.","The episodes used vivid reconstructions and a separate source list, yet listeners could not always tell which spoken lines came from documents."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The programme could be engaging and well researched while still blurring evidence and dramatic invention in particular moments.","options":["A separate bibliography made distinctions within the spoken narrative unnecessary.","The programme could be engaging and well researched while still blurring evidence and dramatic invention in particular moments.","The reconstruction proved the makers had done no research."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Praise the accessible narrative and request clearer signals around reconstructed speech.","options":["Praise the accessible narrative and request clearer signals around reconstructed speech.","Ban dramatic reconstruction regardless of how it is signalled.","Praise the source list without noting unclear boundaries in the audio."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 1","en":"The guide succeeds as an introduction without replacing specialist advice.","zh":"这本指南成功地提供入门指导，却不能替代专家建议。","word":"specialist advice","meaning":"专家建议","tip":"关注“specialist advice”在这句话中如何表达“专家建议”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 2","en":"Its clearest strength is the sequence of repairs a beginner can follow.","zh":"它最明显的优势是初学者能照着操作的修理步骤。","word":"sequence","meaning":"步骤顺序","tip":"关注“sequence”在这句话中如何表达“步骤顺序”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 3","en":"The brief treatment of antique finishes limits its suitability for valuable objects.","zh":"对古董涂饰处理过于简略，限制了它对贵重物件的适用性。","word":"antique finishes","meaning":"古董表面涂饰","tip":"关注“antique finishes”在这句话中如何表达“古董表面涂饰”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 4","en":"That limitation warrants a qualification rather than an outright dismissal.","zh":"那个局限需要加以限定，而非全盘否定。","word":"outright dismissal","meaning":"全盘否定","tip":"关注“outright dismissal”在这句话中如何表达“全盘否定”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 5","en":"I would recommend it to beginners working on ordinary furniture.","zh":"我会把它推荐给修理普通家具的初学者。","word":"ordinary furniture","meaning":"普通家具","tip":"关注“ordinary furniture”在这句话中如何表达“普通家具”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 6","en":"The review should explain whom the recommendation is intended to help.","zh":"评论应解释推荐旨在帮助哪些人。","word":"recommendation","meaning":"推荐意见","tip":"关注“recommendation”在这句话中如何表达“推荐意见”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 7","en":"The illustrations carry practical information rather than merely decorating the page.","zh":"插图承载实际信息，而非仅仅装饰页面。","word":"carry practical information","meaning":"承载实际信息","tip":"关注“carry practical information”在这句话中如何表达“承载实际信息”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 8","en":"An expert may find omissions that are appropriate to an introductory book.","zh":"专家可能发现一些对于入门书而言合理的省略。","word":"omissions","meaning":"省略","tip":"关注“omissions”在这句话中如何表达“省略”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 9","en":"The judgement becomes unfair when the intended audience disappears from view.","zh":"忽视目标读者时，判断就会失去公平。","word":"intended audience","meaning":"目标读者","tip":"关注“intended audience”在这句话中如何表达“目标读者”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 10","en":"The strongest criticism identifies a task the reader might attempt unsafely.","zh":"最有力的批评指出读者可能不安全地尝试的任务。","word":"unsafely","meaning":"不安全地","tip":"关注“unsafely”在这句话中如何表达“不安全地”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 11","en":"Praise and reservation can coexist in a clear recommendation.","zh":"清晰的推荐可以同时包含赞扬与保留。","word":"coexist","meaning":"同时存在","tip":"关注“coexist”在这句话中如何表达“同时存在”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"综合评论与公平评价 · 12","en":"I would distinguish attractive presentation from genuinely usable instruction.","zh":"我会区分吸引人的呈现和真正可用的指导。","word":"usable instruction","meaning":"可用的指导","tip":"关注“usable instruction”在这句话中如何表达“可用的指导”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The recommendation must be ___ (PROPORTION) to the evidence.","answers":["proportionate"],"explanation":"形容词proportionate表示适度相称。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: suitability / suitable / suitably. The omission limits the books ___ for specialist tasks.","answers":["suitability"],"explanation":"所有格之后此处需要名词。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DESPITE: The guide has limits, but it remains useful for beginners.","answers":["Despite its limits, the guide remains useful for beginners."],"explanation":"despite后接名词短语。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: I would recommend the guide ___ beginners.","answers":["to"],"explanation":"recommend something to someone。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a review of a practical guide to repairing furniture.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to recommend the book to its intended readers and identify the specific tasks that exceed its scope. At a review of a practical guide to repairing furniture, the initial suggestion was to assess usefulness without demanding specialist completeness. The relevant background is that the guide explained loose joints clearly and showed inexpensive tools, but its section on antique finishes offered only a brief caution. Nevertheless, a beginner could benefit greatly while still needing expert help with valuable or fragile objects. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nA useful review helps readers decide whether a book suits their likely tasks. Here, ordinary furniture repairs and valuable antique finishes involve different levels of risk. The reviewer can praise the clear sequence and inexpensive tools while advising readers to seek specialist help in the latter case. That boundary should be specific enough to guide a purchase and a practical decision. A vague statement that the book is not comprehensive would add little, because no introductory guide can be comprehensive. Nor would an unqualified recommendation protect beginners who cannot recognise when a finish is fragile. The fairest verdict identifies what the guide enables, which audience benefits, and which jobs require support beyond its pages.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a review of a practical guide to repairing furniture. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to recommend the book to its intended readers and identify the specific tasks that exceed its scope?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to assess usefulness without demanding specialist completeness. The relevant detail is that the guide explained loose joints clearly and showed inexpensive tools, but its section on antique finishes offered only a brief caution. However, a beginner could benefit greatly while still needing expert help with valuable or fragile objects. For that reason, I would recommend the book to its intended readers and identify the specific tasks that exceed its scope. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“综合评论与公平评价”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释综合评论与公平评价的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a review of a beginners bird guide. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the guide offered clear drawings but omitted several rare seasonal visitors. However, the intended readers were local beginners rather than specialist researchers. We therefore needed to assess the omission in relation to the intended audience. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to recommend the guide for common species while naming its specialist limits. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The intended readers were local beginners rather than specialist researchers.","options":["The book was written primarily for rare bird researchers.","The common local species had no usable illustrations.","The intended readers were local beginners rather than specialist researchers."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Assess the omission in relation to the intended audience.","options":["Treat a beginner audience as a reason to ignore accuracy.","Assess the omission in relation to the intended audience.","Judge every omission independently of audience and purpose."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Recommend the guide for common species while naming its specialist limits.","options":["Recommend the guide for common species while naming its specialist limits.","Recommend the guide as complete specialist coverage.","Reject its common species guidance because rare visitors are missing."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":82,"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Repair reasoning rather than merely replacing simple vocabulary","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第81章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a draft proposal for a weekend bus route","text":"At a draft proposal for a weekend bus route, participants considered whether to revise an argument that jumped from one example to a universal conclusion. The background was specific: the draft cited one crowded Saturday bus and declared that every weekend service would pay for itself, although no cost calculation appeared. However, stronger adjectives would not repair the missing link between observed demand and financial viability. The proposed response was to narrow the demand claim and add information about operating costs, frequency and variation across weekends.\n\nThe crowded journey is a reason to investigate demand, not a financial forecast. To reach the latter, the proposal needs an estimate of how often similar demand occurs, what the service would cost and how fares or subsidy would meet that cost. A holiday trip might be unusual, so the next observations should include ordinary weekends. The revised paragraph can retain the vivid example as an introduction while changing the conclusion to a case for testing. This is a more important improvement than replacing everyday words with impressive alternatives. Readers should be able to see the missing link and judge the information supplied to bridge it. A polished leap remains a leap even when its vocabulary sounds authoritative. One crowded bus does not establish that every weekend service will be viable. The missing warrant links observed demand to the financial conclusion. Replacing weak adjectives will not repair that gap in reasoning. The claim should be narrowed to what the observation actually supports. We need operating costs as well as an estimate of passenger demand. The revised paragraph separates an encouraging example from a general forecast."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a draft article opposing a new school timetable","text":"The next original fictional account presents a decision that looks straightforward until the relevant qualification is considered. There is no hidden correct opinion about the institution. The reading task is to establish what the account supports, what it leaves open, and what the proposed response actually changes.\n\nAt a draft article opposing a new school timetable, the discussion concerned whether to remove a false choice between tradition and complete disruption. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the writer presented the current timetable and a radically longer day as the only possibilities, despite several moderate proposals from teachers. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the persuasive contrast depended on excluding realistic alternatives from consideration. The suggested response was to restore the intermediate options and evaluate them using the same criteria.\n\nThe article can criticise a longer day without pretending that every alternative is equally long. By excluding moderate proposals, it makes the present arrangement appear to be the only protection against disruption. Restoring those proposals changes the argumentative task: the writer must explain why the current timetable is preferable to each plausible option, using comparable criteria. That may require evidence about travel, staff workload and pupils concentration. The revision is substantial because it alters the choice readers are asked to make. Merely inserting a sentence that admits other views would be insufficient if the conclusion still relied on the original false contrast. The options must be represented accurately throughout the argument.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The draft cited one crowded Saturday bus and declared that every weekend service would pay for itself, although no cost calculation appeared.","options":["The crowded bus was evidence of ordinary demand throughout the year.","The draft cited one crowded Saturday bus and declared that every weekend service would pay for itself, although no cost calculation appeared.","The proposal calculated costs across a representative set of weekends."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Stronger adjectives would not repair the missing link between observed demand and financial viability.","options":["Stronger adjectives would not repair the missing link between observed demand and financial viability.","An example could establish financial viability without operating costs.","The problem could be repaired by using more sophisticated adjectives alone."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Narrow the demand claim and add information about operating costs, frequency and variation across weekends.","options":["Keep the universal forecast and add confident language.","Remove the useful observation rather than narrowing its implication.","Narrow the demand claim and add information about operating costs, frequency and variation across weekends."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The writer presented the current timetable and a radically longer day as the only possibilities, despite several moderate proposals from teachers.","options":["Teachers had proposed only a radically longer day.","The writer presented the current timetable and a radically longer day as the only possibilities, despite several moderate proposals from teachers.","The article examined several moderate timetables under common criteria."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The persuasive contrast depended on excluding realistic alternatives from consideration.","options":["The persuasive contrast depended on excluding realistic alternatives from consideration.","Acknowledging alternatives in a footnote would repair the false contrast throughout.","The current timetable was proved best because disruption had costs."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Restore the intermediate options and evaluate them using the same criteria.","options":["Keep the two extreme options as the only relevant choices.","Restore alternatives but assess them by different criteria.","Restore the intermediate options and evaluate them using the same criteria."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 1","en":"One crowded bus does not establish that every weekend service will be viable.","zh":"一辆拥挤的公交车不能证明每趟周末服务都能维持运营。","word":"viable","meaning":"能维持运营的","tip":"关注“viable”在这句话中如何表达“能维持运营的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 2","en":"The missing warrant links observed demand to the financial conclusion.","zh":"缺失的论证依据连接着观察到的需求和财务结论。","word":"warrant","meaning":"论证依据","tip":"关注“warrant”在这句话中如何表达“论证依据”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 3","en":"Replacing weak adjectives will not repair that gap in reasoning.","zh":"替换无力的形容词无法修补这个推理缺口。","word":"gap in reasoning","meaning":"推理缺口","tip":"关注“gap in reasoning”在这句话中如何表达“推理缺口”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 4","en":"The claim should be narrowed to what the observation actually supports.","zh":"论断应缩小到观察实际支持的范围。","word":"narrowed","meaning":"缩小了范围","tip":"关注“narrowed”在这句话中如何表达“缩小了范围”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 5","en":"We need operating costs as well as an estimate of passenger demand.","zh":"我们既需要乘客需求估计，也需要运营成本。","word":"operating costs","meaning":"运营成本","tip":"关注“operating costs”在这句话中如何表达“运营成本”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 6","en":"The revised paragraph separates an encouraging example from a general forecast.","zh":"修改后的段落区分了积极例子与一般预测。","word":"forecast","meaning":"预测","tip":"关注“forecast”在这句话中如何表达“预测”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 7","en":"That transition implies a causal connection the previous sentence has not established.","zh":"那个过渡暗示了前一句尚未确立的因果联系。","word":"causal connection","meaning":"因果联系","tip":"关注“causal connection”在这句话中如何表达“因果联系”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 8","en":"An alternative explanation belongs in the argument, not only in a footnote.","zh":"另一种解释应进入论证，而不只出现在脚注里。","word":"alternative explanation","meaning":"另一种解释","tip":"关注“alternative explanation”在这句话中如何表达“另一种解释”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 9","en":"If the conclusion survives a narrower premise, the argument becomes stronger.","zh":"如果结论在更有限的前提下仍成立，论证就更有力。","word":"premise","meaning":"前提","tip":"关注“premise”在这句话中如何表达“前提”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 10","en":"The question is whether the evidence is sufficient, not whether the prose sounds confident.","zh":"问题是证据是否充分，而不是文字是否自信。","word":"sufficient","meaning":"充分的","tip":"关注“sufficient”在这句话中如何表达“充分的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 11","en":"I would remove the universal quantifier before adding more examples.","zh":"我会先删去全称限定，再增加更多例子。","word":"universal quantifier","meaning":"全称限定","tip":"关注“universal quantifier”在这句话中如何表达“全称限定”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"论证修改与逻辑修复 · 12","en":"Substantive revision changes the reasoning as well as its presentation.","zh":"实质修改既改变表达，也改变推理。","word":"Substantive revision","meaning":"实质修改","tip":"关注“Substantive revision”在这句话中如何表达“实质修改”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The draft makes an unsupported ___ (GENERALISE).","answers":["generalisation"],"explanation":"英式名词generalisation。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: warrant / warranty / warranted. The missing ___ connects the observation to the conclusion.","answers":["warrant"],"explanation":"论证中的warrant表示从证据到结论的依据。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use SUFFICIENT: The observation does not provide enough evidence for that claim.","answers":["The observation does not provide sufficient evidence for that claim."],"explanation":"sufficient修饰不可数名词evidence。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: Revision should address reasoning as well ___ style.","answers":["as"],"explanation":"as well as表示也。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a draft proposal for a weekend bus route.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to narrow the demand claim and add information about operating costs, frequency and variation across weekends. At a draft proposal for a weekend bus route, the initial suggestion was to revise an argument that jumped from one example to a universal conclusion. The relevant background is that the draft cited one crowded Saturday bus and declared that every weekend service would pay for itself, although no cost calculation appeared. Nevertheless, stronger adjectives would not repair the missing link between observed demand and financial viability. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe crowded journey is a reason to investigate demand, not a financial forecast. To reach the latter, the proposal needs an estimate of how often similar demand occurs, what the service would cost and how fares or subsidy would meet that cost. A holiday trip might be unusual, so the next observations should include ordinary weekends. The revised paragraph can retain the vivid example as an introduction while changing the conclusion to a case for testing. This is a more important improvement than replacing everyday words with impressive alternatives. Readers should be able to see the missing link and judge the information supplied to bridge it. A polished leap remains a leap even when its vocabulary sounds authoritative.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a draft proposal for a weekend bus route. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to narrow the demand claim and add information about operating costs, frequency and variation across weekends?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to revise an argument that jumped from one example to a universal conclusion. The relevant detail is that the draft cited one crowded Saturday bus and declared that every weekend service would pay for itself, although no cost calculation appeared. However, stronger adjectives would not repair the missing link between observed demand and financial viability. For that reason, I would narrow the demand claim and add information about operating costs, frequency and variation across weekends. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“论证修改与逻辑修复”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释论证修改与逻辑修复的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a proposal for a town shuttle. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the writer predicted universal demand from one crowded holiday trip. However, ordinary weekday demand and operating costs had not been measured. We therefore needed to repair the unsupported generalisation before polishing the language. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to collect representative demand data and prepare a cost comparison. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"Ordinary weekday demand and operating costs had not been measured.","options":["The holiday trip had been almost empty.","Ordinary weekday demand and operating costs had not been measured.","Ordinary weekday demand was measured across a full year."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Repair the unsupported generalisation before polishing the language.","options":["Repair the unsupported generalisation before polishing the language.","Infer universal demand from a single unusual journey.","Improve vocabulary before repairing the unsupported forecast."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Collect representative demand data and prepare a cost comparison.","options":["Forecast costs from the holiday crowd alone.","Collect more holiday anecdotes without measuring ordinary demand.","Collect representative demand data and prepare a cost comparison."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":83,"title":"专业报告与可执行建议","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Connect findings, uncertainty and practical decisions","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第82章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a report on a volunteer transport service","text":"At a report on a volunteer transport service, participants considered whether to recommend changes to booking procedures. The background was specific: drivers reported repeated route changes and passengers described uncertainty about pickup times, while the booking log contained no record of when changes were communicated. However, without that timing information the report could identify a coordination problem but not assign responsibility fairly. The proposed response was to introduce a time stamped confirmation process and review missed pickups after a defined trial.\n\nPassengers and drivers both describe an experience of uncertainty, but their accounts do not yet establish when a change was made or communicated. The report should resist assigning responsibility to whichever group tells the most compelling story. A simple log could record the original booking, each revision, its confirmation and the eventual pickup. That would support later investigation while immediately improving coordination. The recommendation should name who maintains the log, how volunteers can access the current version and when results will be reviewed. It should also avoid creating unnecessary work that makes the procedure unlikely to survive. A modest system used reliably is more valuable than an elaborate recommendation that nobody has the time or authority to implement. The records reveal a coordination problem without identifying who caused it. The recommendation should name an owner, a deadline and a review criterion. Time stamped confirmations would make later route changes traceable. We should not assign blame where the necessary sequence is undocumented. The proposed trial addresses an observable process rather than a presumed attitude. An actionable finding connects the evidence with a decision someone can implement."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a report on energy use in a small arts centre","text":"A separate fictional case offers another opportunity to distinguish an observation from its interpretation. The people involved share enough common ground to continue working, but their initial description risks implying more than they have established. The difference becomes visible when the suggested action is compared with the available detail.\n\nAt a report on energy use in a small arts centre, the discussion concerned whether to prioritise savings without overstating an incomplete audit. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: meter readings suggested heavy overnight consumption, but the building contained refrigeration equipment that had to remain on. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: calling all night use waste would confuse necessary operation with avoidable consumption. The suggested response was to measure individual circuits before changing schedules and identify who can authorise each adjustment.\n\nOvernight electricity use is a promising place to investigate, not a category that can automatically be eliminated. Refrigeration protects supplies that would otherwise spoil, and switching it off could produce costs greater than the electricity saved. The audit needs to distinguish essential loads from equipment that can safely follow a schedule. Measuring individual circuits would make that distinction more reliable. The report should then connect each proposed change to a responsible person, a safety check and an expected saving. A recommendation to be more efficient would sound agreeable while leaving nobody with an implementable task. Precision here concerns the decision as much as the number of units reported on a meter.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Drivers reported repeated route changes and passengers described uncertainty about pickup times, while the booking log contained no record of when changes were communicated.","options":["Drivers reported repeated route changes and passengers described uncertainty about pickup times, while the booking log contained no record of when changes were communicated.","The booking log recorded exactly when every change was communicated.","Drivers and passengers agreed which person caused each missed pickup."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Without that timing information the report could identify a coordination problem but not assign responsibility fairly.","options":["The accounts established responsibility as well as a coordination problem.","The absence of timing records showed that the passengers caused the changes.","Without that timing information the report could identify a coordination problem but not assign responsibility fairly."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Introduce a time stamped confirmation process and review missed pickups after a defined trial.","options":["Recommend better coordination without specifying a process or owner.","Introduce a time stamped confirmation process and review missed pickups after a defined trial.","Assign blame using the most vivid interview account."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Meter readings suggested heavy overnight consumption, but the building contained refrigeration equipment that had to remain on.","options":["Meter readings suggested heavy overnight consumption, but the building contained refrigeration equipment that had to remain on.","The audit established that no equipment needed to run overnight.","Individual circuits had already been measured during the full year."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Calling all night use waste would confuse necessary operation with avoidable consumption.","options":["All night consumption could be removed without affecting refrigeration.","A promising aggregate reading identified which device wasted energy.","Calling all night use waste would confuse necessary operation with avoidable consumption."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Measure individual circuits before changing schedules and identify who can authorise each adjustment.","options":["Recommend efficiency without naming authorisation or safety checks.","Measure individual circuits before changing schedules and identify who can authorise each adjustment.","Switch off all equipment overnight before measuring separate loads."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 1","en":"The records reveal a coordination problem without identifying who caused it.","zh":"记录揭示了协调问题，却没有确定由谁造成。","word":"coordination problem","meaning":"协调问题","tip":"关注“coordination problem”在这句话中如何表达“协调问题”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 2","en":"The recommendation should name an owner, a deadline and a review criterion.","zh":"建议应明确负责人、期限和复核标准。","word":"review criterion","meaning":"复核标准","tip":"关注“review criterion”在这句话中如何表达“复核标准”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 3","en":"Time stamped confirmations would make later route changes traceable.","zh":"带时间标记的确认可以让后续路线变化可追溯。","word":"traceable","meaning":"可追溯的","tip":"关注“traceable”在这句话中如何表达“可追溯的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 4","en":"We should not assign blame where the necessary sequence is undocumented.","zh":"在必要的事件顺序未记录时，我们不应归责。","word":"undocumented","meaning":"未被记录的","tip":"关注“undocumented”在这句话中如何表达“未被记录的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 5","en":"The proposed trial addresses an observable process rather than a presumed attitude.","zh":"建议的试行针对可观察流程，而非假定态度。","word":"observable process","meaning":"可观察流程","tip":"关注“observable process”在这句话中如何表达“可观察流程”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 6","en":"An actionable finding connects the evidence with a decision someone can implement.","zh":"可执行的发现把证据与某人能够实施的决定连接起来。","word":"actionable finding","meaning":"可执行的发现","tip":"关注“actionable finding”在这句话中如何表达“可执行的发现”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 7","en":"The report must distinguish an immediate fix from a longer term investigation.","zh":"报告必须区分即时补救和长期调查。","word":"longer term investigation","meaning":"长期调查","tip":"关注“longer term investigation”在这句话中如何表达“长期调查”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 8","en":"Who will check whether the new procedure reduces missed pickups?","zh":"谁来检查新流程是否减少接送遗漏？","word":"missed pickups","meaning":"接送遗漏","tip":"关注“missed pickups”在这句话中如何表达“接送遗漏”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 9","en":"The cost estimate excludes volunteer time and should say so explicitly.","zh":"成本估计未计志愿者时间，应明确说明这一点。","word":"excludes","meaning":"未计入","tip":"关注“excludes”在这句话中如何表达“未计入”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 10","en":"If the trial fails the agreed criterion, the procedure should be revised.","zh":"如果试行未达约定标准，就应修改流程。","word":"agreed criterion","meaning":"约定标准","tip":"关注“agreed criterion”在这句话中如何表达“约定标准”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 11","en":"The executive summary needs the reservation as well as the recommendation.","zh":"执行摘要既需要建议，也需要保留说明。","word":"executive summary","meaning":"执行摘要","tip":"关注“executive summary”在这句话中如何表达“执行摘要”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"专业报告与可执行建议 · 12","en":"A report is incomplete if its practical implications cannot be followed.","zh":"如果实际含义无法落实，报告就不完整。","word":"practical implications","meaning":"实际含义","tip":"关注“practical implications”在这句话中如何表达“实际含义”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"Each action needs a named owner for ___ (IMPLEMENT).","answers":["implementation"],"explanation":"名词implementation表示实施。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: actionable / action / actively. The report should make its findings ___.","answers":["actionable"],"explanation":"make宾语形容词补语。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use UNTIL: We must document the sequence before assigning responsibility.","answers":["We must not assign responsibility until the sequence is documented."],"explanation":"not...until表示直到才。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: The recommendation follows ___ the documented coordination problem.","answers":["from"],"explanation":"follow from表示由此得出。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a report on a volunteer transport service.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to introduce a time stamped confirmation process and review missed pickups after a defined trial. At a report on a volunteer transport service, the initial suggestion was to recommend changes to booking procedures. The relevant background is that drivers reported repeated route changes and passengers described uncertainty about pickup times, while the booking log contained no record of when changes were communicated. Nevertheless, without that timing information the report could identify a coordination problem but not assign responsibility fairly. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nPassengers and drivers both describe an experience of uncertainty, but their accounts do not yet establish when a change was made or communicated. The report should resist assigning responsibility to whichever group tells the most compelling story. A simple log could record the original booking, each revision, its confirmation and the eventual pickup. That would support later investigation while immediately improving coordination. The recommendation should name who maintains the log, how volunteers can access the current version and when results will be reviewed. It should also avoid creating unnecessary work that makes the procedure unlikely to survive. A modest system used reliably is more valuable than an elaborate recommendation that nobody has the time or authority to implement.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a report on a volunteer transport service. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to introduce a time stamped confirmation process and review missed pickups after a defined trial?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to recommend changes to booking procedures. The relevant detail is that drivers reported repeated route changes and passengers described uncertainty about pickup times, while the booking log contained no record of when changes were communicated. However, without that timing information the report could identify a coordination problem but not assign responsibility fairly. For that reason, I would introduce a time stamped confirmation process and review missed pickups after a defined trial. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“专业报告与可执行建议”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释专业报告与可执行建议的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a report on a community pantry. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that volunteers described frequent stock shortages but recorded no delivery times. However, the evidence showed a problem without identifying its precise cause. We therefore needed to avoid assigning blame before the sequence can be reconstructed. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to introduce a delivery log with named responsibilities and a review date. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The evidence showed a problem without identifying its precise cause.","options":["The evidence showed a problem without identifying its precise cause.","Delivery times were recorded in a complete sequence.","The volunteers had identified exactly who caused each shortage."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Avoid assigning blame before the sequence can be reconstructed.","options":["Assign blame before establishing the order of events.","Dismiss shortages because the log was incomplete.","Avoid assigning blame before the sequence can be reconstructed."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Introduce a delivery log with named responsibilities and a review date.","options":["Keep informal reports without adding timed records.","Introduce a delivery log with named responsibilities and a review date.","Name a responsible person from the most vivid complaint."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":84,"title":"风格编辑与作者声音","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Improve clarity while preserving intentional voice","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第83章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：an editor working on a personal essay about migration","text":"At an editor working on a personal essay about migration, participants considered whether to reduce ambiguity without smoothing away the narrators rhythm. The background was specific: short repeated sentences conveyed the speakers anxiety, while a pronoun in the third paragraph made it unclear which family member had left. However, treating every repetition as a fault would remove an intentional effect, whereas leaving the pronoun unresolved would preserve accidental confusion. The proposed response was to clarify the reference and discuss rhythm with the author before rewriting the repeated sentences.\n\nThe narrators short repeated sentences have an identifiable function: they make anxiety audible in the prose. Changing them merely because variety is usually recommended would impose an abstract rule at the expense of the essay. The pronoun problem is different. It prevents readers from knowing which family member acted and does not contribute a comparable effect. I would clarify that reference, then discuss any broader rhythm changes with the author. The editorial note should explain the distinction so that the writer can accept or reject a change intelligently. Preserving voice does not require leaving every difficulty untouched. It requires understanding which difficulties are doing intentional work and which are obstructing the intended experience. The repeated short sentences create a rhythm that serves the narrators anxiety. The ambiguous pronoun produces confusion rather than productive uncertainty. I would clarify the reference while preserving the deliberate repetition. An editor should be able to explain what each substantial change achieves. The smoother version is not automatically the more faithful one. Could this repetition be intentional rather than an oversight? We should ask the author before replacing the distinctive sentence pattern."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：an editor revising a natural history column","text":"In this newly invented case, readers should attend to the relationship between a stated aim and the evidence used to defend it. The account does not invite a judgement about personalities. Instead, it supplies a limited situation in which a careful summary can preserve something that a confident headline loses.\n\nAt an editor revising a natural history column, the discussion concerned whether to distinguish decorative excess from useful imagery. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: a comparison between moth wings and folded maps helped explain a pattern, but three additional metaphors competed with it in the same sentence. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: removing every image would sacrifice an effective explanation along with the distracting ones. The suggested response was to retain the image that does explanatory work and simplify the surrounding sentence.\n\nThe first image gives readers a way to visualise a pattern they may not have seen. The later images compete for attention without adding a comparable explanation. An editor who removes them selectively can improve the column while preserving its accessible style. An editor who replaces the whole passage with technical description may solve one problem by creating another for the intended audience. The author should be able to see why one comparison remains and another goes. That explanation also helps future drafts. The relevant standard is not a ban on figurative language but a judgement about what each image contributes, whether it misleads, and how much work the surrounding sentence asks of readers.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"Short repeated sentences conveyed the speakers anxiety, while a pronoun in the third paragraph made it unclear which family member had left.","options":["The repeated sentences occurred accidentally in an otherwise neutral account.","The pronoun deliberately left every family member equally mysterious.","Short repeated sentences conveyed the speakers anxiety, while a pronoun in the third paragraph made it unclear which family member had left."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Treating every repetition as a fault would remove an intentional effect, whereas leaving the pronoun unresolved would preserve accidental confusion.","options":["Preserving voice required keeping every confusing reference unchanged.","Treating every repetition as a fault would remove an intentional effect, whereas leaving the pronoun unresolved would preserve accidental confusion.","A smoother rhythm would necessarily preserve the narrators anxious voice."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Clarify the reference and discuss rhythm with the author before rewriting the repeated sentences.","options":["Clarify the reference and discuss rhythm with the author before rewriting the repeated sentences.","Vary every sentence length before consulting the author.","Leave the accidental reference unclear to preserve all uncertainty."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A comparison between moth wings and folded maps helped explain a pattern, but three additional metaphors competed with it in the same sentence.","options":["Every metaphor added a separate explanation of a different feature.","The first image obscured the pattern rather than explaining it.","A comparison between moth wings and folded maps helped explain a pattern, but three additional metaphors competed with it in the same sentence."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Removing every image would sacrifice an effective explanation along with the distracting ones.","options":["Removing all imagery would preserve exactly the same accessibility.","Removing every image would sacrifice an effective explanation along with the distracting ones.","The distracting images showed that figurative language never helps beginners."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Retain the image that does explanatory work and simplify the surrounding sentence.","options":["Retain the image that does explanatory work and simplify the surrounding sentence.","Replace every image with technical description.","Keep the competing images because vividness alone justifies them."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 1","en":"The repeated short sentences create a rhythm that serves the narrators anxiety.","zh":"反复的短句形成了体现叙述者焦虑的节奏。","word":"rhythm","meaning":"节奏","tip":"关注“rhythm”在这句话中如何表达“节奏”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 2","en":"The ambiguous pronoun produces confusion rather than productive uncertainty.","zh":"有歧义的代词造成的是混乱，而非有意义的不确定性。","word":"productive uncertainty","meaning":"有意义的不确定性","tip":"关注“productive uncertainty”在这句话中如何表达“有意义的不确定性”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 3","en":"I would clarify the reference while preserving the deliberate repetition.","zh":"我会澄清指代，同时保留有意的重复。","word":"deliberate repetition","meaning":"有意的重复","tip":"关注“deliberate repetition”在这句话中如何表达“有意的重复”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 4","en":"An editor should be able to explain what each substantial change achieves.","zh":"编辑应能解释每一处实质改动达到了什么效果。","word":"substantial change","meaning":"实质改动","tip":"关注“substantial change”在这句话中如何表达“实质改动”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 5","en":"The smoother version is not automatically the more faithful one.","zh":"更顺畅的版本未必更忠实。","word":"faithful","meaning":"忠实的","tip":"关注“faithful”在这句话中如何表达“忠实的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 6","en":"Could this repetition be intentional rather than an oversight?","zh":"这个重复是否可能是有意的，而非疏忽？","word":"oversight","meaning":"疏忽","tip":"关注“oversight”在这句话中如何表达“疏忽”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 7","en":"We should ask the author before replacing the distinctive sentence pattern.","zh":"在替换独特句式之前，我们应询问作者。","word":"distinctive","meaning":"独特的","tip":"关注“distinctive”在这句话中如何表达“独特的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 8","en":"The paragraph loses its emotional progression when every sentence has equal length.","zh":"当每句长度相同时，段落失去了情感推进。","word":"emotional progression","meaning":"情感推进","tip":"关注“emotional progression”在这句话中如何表达“情感推进”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 9","en":"This change improves cohesion without altering the narrators attitude.","zh":"这个改动改善了衔接，同时没有改变叙述者态度。","word":"cohesion","meaning":"衔接","tip":"关注“cohesion”在这句话中如何表达“衔接”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 10","en":"The revision removes an accidental obstacle to the intended effect.","zh":"修改移除了阻碍预期效果的偶然障碍。","word":"intended effect","meaning":"预期效果","tip":"关注“intended effect”在这句话中如何表达“预期效果”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 11","en":"A useful style note records the decision and its rationale.","zh":"有用的风格说明会记录决定及其理由。","word":"rationale","meaning":"理由","tip":"关注“rationale”在这句话中如何表达“理由”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"风格编辑与作者声音 · 12","en":"Editorial restraint can require as much judgement as extensive rewriting.","zh":"编辑上的克制可能与大幅改写同样需要判断力。","word":"Editorial restraint","meaning":"编辑上的克制","tip":"关注“Editorial restraint”在这句话中如何表达“编辑上的克制”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The repetition appears ___ (INTENTION), not accidental.","answers":["intentional"],"explanation":"形容词intentional。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: cohesion / cohesive / cohesively. Clarifying the pronoun improves textual ___.","answers":["cohesion"],"explanation":"名词cohesion表示衔接。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use WHILE: Clarify the reference and preserve the rhythm.","answers":["Clarify the reference while preserving the rhythm."],"explanation":"while后接分词表达同时发生。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: Consult the author before substituting your voice ___ theirs.","answers":["for"],"explanation":"substitute A for B意为以A替代B。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at an editor working on a personal essay about migration.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to clarify the reference and discuss rhythm with the author before rewriting the repeated sentences. At an editor working on a personal essay about migration, the initial suggestion was to reduce ambiguity without smoothing away the narrators rhythm. The relevant background is that short repeated sentences conveyed the speakers anxiety, while a pronoun in the third paragraph made it unclear which family member had left. Nevertheless, treating every repetition as a fault would remove an intentional effect, whereas leaving the pronoun unresolved would preserve accidental confusion. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe narrators short repeated sentences have an identifiable function: they make anxiety audible in the prose. Changing them merely because variety is usually recommended would impose an abstract rule at the expense of the essay. The pronoun problem is different. It prevents readers from knowing which family member acted and does not contribute a comparable effect. I would clarify that reference, then discuss any broader rhythm changes with the author. The editorial note should explain the distinction so that the writer can accept or reject a change intelligently. Preserving voice does not require leaving every difficulty untouched. It requires understanding which difficulties are doing intentional work and which are obstructing the intended experience.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at an editor working on a personal essay about migration. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to clarify the reference and discuss rhythm with the author before rewriting the repeated sentences?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to reduce ambiguity without smoothing away the narrators rhythm. The relevant detail is that short repeated sentences conveyed the speakers anxiety, while a pronoun in the third paragraph made it unclear which family member had left. However, treating every repetition as a fault would remove an intentional effect, whereas leaving the pronoun unresolved would preserve accidental confusion. For that reason, I would clarify the reference and discuss rhythm with the author before rewriting the repeated sentences. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“风格编辑与作者声音”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释风格编辑与作者声音的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during an editor reviewing a travel memoir. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that repeated short clauses reproduced the narrators breathless arrival. However, a separate sentence confused the station with the neighbouring hotel. We therefore needed to preserve deliberate rhythm while removing accidental ambiguity. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to clarify the location reference and consult the author about larger stylistic changes. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"A separate sentence confused the station with the neighbouring hotel.","options":["The repeated clauses were accidental typing errors.","The unclear location was a deliberate part of the rhythm.","A separate sentence confused the station with the neighbouring hotel."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Preserve deliberate rhythm while removing accidental ambiguity.","options":["Preserve confusion because voice requires no intervention.","Preserve deliberate rhythm while removing accidental ambiguity.","Smooth every sentence regardless of its narrative function."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Clarify the location reference and consult the author about larger stylistic changes.","options":["Clarify the location reference and consult the author about larger stylistic changes.","Replace the entire rhythm before discussing the authors intention.","Keep the hotel and station reference ambiguous."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":85,"title":"独立研究与证据综合","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Define an answerable question and document a transparent inquiry","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第84章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a learner research project on access to a neighbourhood park","text":"At a learner research project on access to a neighbourhood park, participants considered whether to investigate how different entrances affect practical access. The background was specific: the researcher mapped steps, opening hours and bus stops, interviewed consenting users, and recorded the time of each observation. However, a small voluntary interview sample could reveal barriers without representing every resident or establishing how common each barrier was. The proposed response was to separate direct observation from testimony and explain the limits before proposing improvements.\n\nThe investigation should begin with a question the learner can answer responsibly: which observable entrance features make access easier or harder under specified conditions? Mapping steps and opening hours provides direct information, while interviews add experiences the map cannot show. Those forms of evidence should be labelled separately. A handful of volunteers may identify a serious barrier without establishing its prevalence across the neighbourhood. The learner should record consent, avoid unnecessary personal details and preserve conflicting accounts rather than select only convenient quotations. A recommendation can then address a documented obstacle while acknowledging what remains unknown. The quality of the project lies in the traceable link between question, method and conclusion, not the breadth of an unsupported final claim. The question must be narrow enough for the available evidence to answer. An interview can reveal a barrier without showing how widespread it is. I recorded the time and conditions of every direct observation. Participants should understand how their comments will be used before agreeing. The report separates what I observed from what residents told me. Convenient access to interviewees does not make the sample representative."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a learner investigation of food packaging in a school canteen","text":"This independent fictional case introduces a different setting. Its purpose is to test transfer, so the conclusion must be recovered from the new details rather than carried over from an earlier example. The participants have a practical decision to make, and an attractive first impression is not enough.\n\nAt a learner investigation of food packaging in a school canteen, the discussion concerned whether to study which changes might reduce unnecessary packaging. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the learner counted wrappers on five days and asked staff about hygiene requirements, but examinations changed attendance during two observations. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the counts offered useful examples but could not simply be multiplied into a reliable annual total. The suggested response was to repeat observations under ordinary attendance and distinguish essential packaging from replaceable items.\n\nMultiplying a small count by the number of school days produces a precise looking annual figure. It does not make the sampled days typical. Examination attendance offers a specific reason to question the calculation rather than a vague warning that all data are unreliable. A better investigation would record ordinary and unusual days separately, note changes in meal participation and ask which wrappers serve necessary functions. It could then compare achievable alternatives on the same basis. The learner should seek permission before photographing people or recording comments and should avoid including identifying details unnecessarily. A transparent small project can support a modest recommendation without pretending to be a complete audit of the school year.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The researcher mapped steps, opening hours and bus stops, interviewed consenting users, and recorded the time of each observation.","options":["The map recorded opinions but no observable entrance features.","The researcher mapped steps, opening hours and bus stops, interviewed consenting users, and recorded the time of each observation.","The interviews were randomly sampled from all neighbourhood residents."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A small voluntary interview sample could reveal barriers without representing every resident or establishing how common each barrier was.","options":["A small voluntary interview sample could reveal barriers without representing every resident or establishing how common each barrier was.","A small interview sample could establish the proportion of every resident affected.","Direct observation and testimony were interchangeable forms of evidence."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Separate direct observation from testimony and explain the limits before proposing improvements.","options":["Combine testimony with observations without identifying their sources.","Claim representative prevalence from the willing interviewees.","Separate direct observation from testimony and explain the limits before proposing improvements."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The learner counted wrappers on five days and asked staff about hygiene requirements, but examinations changed attendance during two observations.","options":["Hygiene requirements were omitted from the staff discussion.","The learner counted wrappers on five days and asked staff about hygiene requirements, but examinations changed attendance during two observations.","All five observations occurred under ordinary attendance conditions."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The counts offered useful examples but could not simply be multiplied into a reliable annual total.","options":["The counts offered useful examples but could not simply be multiplied into a reliable annual total.","Multiplication could turn unusual days into a reliable annual total.","The examination attendance difference made every observation worthless."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Repeat observations under ordinary attendance and distinguish essential packaging from replaceable items.","options":["Scale the five day count directly to the whole year.","Remove all packaging without distinguishing its functions.","Repeat observations under ordinary attendance and distinguish essential packaging from replaceable items."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 1","en":"The question must be narrow enough for the available evidence to answer.","zh":"问题必须足够具体，让现有证据能够回答。","word":"available evidence","meaning":"现有证据","tip":"关注“available evidence”在这句话中如何表达“现有证据”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 2","en":"An interview can reveal a barrier without showing how widespread it is.","zh":"访谈可以揭示障碍，却不能说明它有多普遍。","word":"widespread","meaning":"普遍的","tip":"关注“widespread”在这句话中如何表达“普遍的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 3","en":"I recorded the time and conditions of every direct observation.","zh":"我记录了每次直接观察的时间与条件。","word":"direct observation","meaning":"直接观察","tip":"关注“direct observation”在这句话中如何表达“直接观察”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 4","en":"Participants should understand how their comments will be used before agreeing.","zh":"参与者应在同意之前理解其评论将如何使用。","word":"agreeing","meaning":"同意","tip":"关注“agreeing”在这句话中如何表达“同意”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 5","en":"The report separates what I observed from what residents told me.","zh":"报告区分了我观察到的内容和居民告诉我的内容。","word":"separates","meaning":"区分","tip":"关注“separates”在这句话中如何表达“区分”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 6","en":"Convenient access to interviewees does not make the sample representative.","zh":"方便接触受访者并不使样本具有代表性。","word":"representative","meaning":"有代表性的","tip":"关注“representative”在这句话中如何表达“有代表性的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 7","en":"I would retain the contradictory account rather than remove it as inconvenient.","zh":"我会保留矛盾陈述，而非因其不方便就删去。","word":"contradictory account","meaning":"矛盾陈述","tip":"关注“contradictory account”在这句话中如何表达“矛盾陈述”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 8","en":"The recommendation is provisional because the evening entrance was not observed.","zh":"建议是暂定的，因为尚未观察夜间入口情况。","word":"provisional","meaning":"暂定的","tip":"关注“provisional”在这句话中如何表达“暂定的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 9","en":"Each factual claim should be traceable to a recorded observation or source.","zh":"每项事实论断都应能追溯到记录的观察或来源。","word":"traceable","meaning":"可追溯的","tip":"关注“traceable”在这句话中如何表达“可追溯的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 10","en":"An unanswered question can become a justified priority for further investigation.","zh":"未解答的问题可以成为进一步调查的合理优先事项。","word":"investigation","meaning":"调查","tip":"关注“investigation”在这句话中如何表达“调查”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 11","en":"The method should permit another person to understand how the conclusion was reached.","zh":"方法应让他人理解结论是如何得出的。","word":"permit","meaning":"使得能够","tip":"关注“permit”在这句话中如何表达“使得能够”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"独立研究与证据综合 · 12","en":"A transparent limitation is more informative than an implausibly comprehensive claim.","zh":"透明的局限比不可信的全面论断更有信息价值。","word":"transparent limitation","meaning":"透明的局限","tip":"关注“transparent limitation”在这句话中如何表达“透明的局限”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"Convenience does not ensure sample ___ (REPRESENT).","answers":["representativeness"],"explanation":"representativeness表示代表性。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: traceable / trace / tracing. Every claim should be ___ to a source.","answers":["traceable"],"explanation":"be traceable to可追溯到。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DISTINGUISH: Keep direct observations separate from testimony.","answers":["Distinguish direct observations from testimony."],"explanation":"distinguish A from B。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: Participants agreed ___ the stated use of their comments.","answers":["to"],"explanation":"agree to表示同意某安排。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a learner research project on access to a neighbourhood park.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to separate direct observation from testimony and explain the limits before proposing improvements. At a learner research project on access to a neighbourhood park, the initial suggestion was to investigate how different entrances affect practical access. The relevant background is that the researcher mapped steps, opening hours and bus stops, interviewed consenting users, and recorded the time of each observation. Nevertheless, a small voluntary interview sample could reveal barriers without representing every resident or establishing how common each barrier was. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe investigation should begin with a question the learner can answer responsibly: which observable entrance features make access easier or harder under specified conditions? Mapping steps and opening hours provides direct information, while interviews add experiences the map cannot show. Those forms of evidence should be labelled separately. A handful of volunteers may identify a serious barrier without establishing its prevalence across the neighbourhood. The learner should record consent, avoid unnecessary personal details and preserve conflicting accounts rather than select only convenient quotations. A recommendation can then address a documented obstacle while acknowledging what remains unknown. The quality of the project lies in the traceable link between question, method and conclusion, not the breadth of an unsupported final claim.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a learner research project on access to a neighbourhood park. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to separate direct observation from testimony and explain the limits before proposing improvements?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to investigate how different entrances affect practical access. The relevant detail is that the researcher mapped steps, opening hours and bus stops, interviewed consenting users, and recorded the time of each observation. However, a small voluntary interview sample could reveal barriers without representing every resident or establishing how common each barrier was. For that reason, I would separate direct observation from testimony and explain the limits before proposing improvements. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“独立研究与证据综合”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释独立研究与证据综合的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a student investigation of street lighting. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the student photographed four routes and interviewed willing passers by. However, the voluntary interviews did not represent every resident in the district. We therefore needed to report observed barriers without estimating unsupported population percentages. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to repeat observations at different times and document the sampling limits. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The voluntary interviews did not represent every resident in the district.","options":["The photographs covered every route under every lighting condition.","The voluntary interviews did not represent every resident in the district.","The interviews represented every resident in the district."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Report observed barriers without estimating unsupported population percentages.","options":["Report observed barriers without estimating unsupported population percentages.","Convert willing interviewees into a representative population percentage.","Treat direct observations and opinions as the same evidence."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Repeat observations at different times and document the sampling limits.","options":["Publish unsupported district wide percentages from the volunteers.","Repeat only the same route at the same time without noting limits.","Repeat observations at different times and document the sampling limits."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":86,"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Defend a conclusion while revising it when evidence warrants","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第85章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a presentation proposing a shared tool library","text":"At a presentation proposing a shared tool library, participants considered whether to answer questions about demand and maintenance. The background was specific: the presenter had survey responses from interested residents and a repair budget based on quotations, but no evidence about how often tools would return late. However, a confident presentation could make an untested operating assumption look like an established result. The proposed response was to defend the observed interest while proposing a small pilot to test return times and repair needs.\n\nThe presenter should not defend return times as though survey enthusiasm had measured them. Interest, venue costs, repair quotations and actual borrowing behaviour are different forms of information. The first three can justify trying a small service, while a pilot is needed for the fourth. Answering the question directly would make that distinction visible: the plan assumes timely returns, and that assumption has not been tested. The pilot should record delays and maintenance needs before the group invests in a larger collection. This response protects the documented parts of the proposal without treating the whole presentation as invulnerable. An audience can have more confidence in a plan that knows how to learn than in one whose author can only repeat assurances. The survey supports interest in the library, not a forecast of reliable returns. That question exposes an assumption I have not yet tested. I can defend the demand evidence while revising the operating plan. Let me answer the specific challenge before returning to the wider proposal. The repair quotation is documented, whereas the return rate remains uncertain. A small pilot would test the assumption more credibly than another opinion survey."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a presentation about converting a car park into a garden","text":"The next original fictional account presents a decision that looks straightforward until the relevant qualification is considered. There is no hidden correct opinion about the institution. The reading task is to establish what the account supports, what it leaves open, and what the proposed response actually changes.\n\nAt a presentation about converting a car park into a garden, the discussion concerned whether to respond to a question exposing an omitted accessibility issue. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the design included paths and raised beds, but the presenter had not checked the distance from accessible parking after the proposed conversion. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: defending every detail would protect the presenters pride at the expense of the projects stated purpose. The suggested response was to acknowledge the omission, revise the access plan and explain which parts of the evidence remain useful.\n\nThe access question does not erase the benefits of the garden. It reveals a design requirement that the present plan has not satisfied. The presenter should therefore distinguish the environmental and community arguments from the assumption about how everyone reaches the site. A revised drawing can test whether accessible parking, paths and entrance distances work together. The response should not promise a solution before checking it, but can name the review needed and who should contribute. This preserves momentum without treating a questioner as an adversary. A defensible presentation is one whose claims can survive correction, not one whose author refuses every change to protect the appearance of certainty.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The presenter had survey responses from interested residents and a repair budget based on quotations, but no evidence about how often tools would return late.","options":["The presenter had survey responses from interested residents and a repair budget based on quotations, but no evidence about how often tools would return late.","The tool survey measured how often borrowed items returned late.","The repair budget was based solely on residents guesses."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A confident presentation could make an untested operating assumption look like an established result.","options":["Interest responses established all operating assumptions.","Uncertain return rates invalidated the documented repair quotations.","A confident presentation could make an untested operating assumption look like an established result."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Defend the observed interest while proposing a small pilot to test return times and repair needs.","options":["Abandon the whole proposal because one operating assumption is untested.","Defend the observed interest while proposing a small pilot to test return times and repair needs.","Defend return reliability as though the survey had measured it."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The design included paths and raised beds, but the presenter had not checked the distance from accessible parking after the proposed conversion.","options":["The design included paths and raised beds, but the presenter had not checked the distance from accessible parking after the proposed conversion.","The design had already checked the replacement accessible parking distance.","The questioner challenged the existence of any benefit from gardens."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"Defending every detail would protect the presenters pride at the expense of the projects stated purpose.","options":["The access omission erased every environmental argument.","Refusing revision would protect the plans stated purpose.","Defending every detail would protect the presenters pride at the expense of the projects stated purpose."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Acknowledge the omission, revise the access plan and explain which parts of the evidence remain useful.","options":["Promise a solution before checking the revised access route.","Acknowledge the omission, revise the access plan and explain which parts of the evidence remain useful.","Treat the question as hostility and defend every detail."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 1","en":"The survey supports interest in the library, not a forecast of reliable returns.","zh":"调查支持大家对工具库有兴趣，而非保证可靠归还的预测。","word":"reliable returns","meaning":"可靠归还","tip":"关注“reliable returns”在这句话中如何表达“可靠归还”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 2","en":"That question exposes an assumption I have not yet tested.","zh":"那个问题揭示了一个我尚未检验的假设。","word":"assumption","meaning":"假设","tip":"关注“assumption”在这句话中如何表达“假设”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 3","en":"I can defend the demand evidence while revising the operating plan.","zh":"我可以维护需求证据，同时修改运营计划。","word":"operating plan","meaning":"运营计划","tip":"关注“operating plan”在这句话中如何表达“运营计划”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 4","en":"Let me answer the specific challenge before returning to the wider proposal.","zh":"让我先回应具体质疑，再回到整体提案。","word":"specific challenge","meaning":"具体质疑","tip":"关注“specific challenge”在这句话中如何表达“具体质疑”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 5","en":"The repair quotation is documented, whereas the return rate remains uncertain.","zh":"维修报价有记录，而归还率仍不确定。","word":"documented","meaning":"有记录的","tip":"关注“documented”在这句话中如何表达“有记录的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 6","en":"A small pilot would test the assumption more credibly than another opinion survey.","zh":"小规模试行会比另一项意见调查更可信地检验假设。","word":"pilot","meaning":"试行","tip":"关注“pilot”在这句话中如何表达“试行”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 7","en":"I would change that recommendation if the pilot revealed persistent losses.","zh":"如果试行显示持续丢失，我会修改那项建议。","word":"revealed","meaning":"显示","tip":"关注“revealed”在这句话中如何表达“显示”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 8","en":"Acknowledging an omission does not invalidate every part of the project.","zh":"承认一个遗漏并不会使项目每个部分都失效。","word":"omission","meaning":"遗漏","tip":"关注“omission”在这句话中如何表达“遗漏”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 9","en":"Could you clarify whether your objection concerns cost or practical access?","zh":"你能澄清异议关乎成本还是实际可达性吗？","word":"objection","meaning":"异议","tip":"关注“objection”在这句话中如何表达“异议”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 10","en":"My earlier answer was too broad; the evidence supports a narrower one.","zh":"我先前的回答太宽泛；证据支持的是更有限的回答。","word":"narrower","meaning":"更有限的","tip":"关注“narrower”在这句话中如何表达“更有限的”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 11","en":"The revised slide should distinguish measured quantities from planning assumptions.","zh":"修改后的幻灯片应区分测量数量与规划假设。","word":"planning assumptions","meaning":"规划假设","tip":"关注“planning assumptions”在这句话中如何表达“规划假设”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"演讲答辩与即时澄清 · 12","en":"Composure means engaging with the challenge, not pretending it never arose.","zh":"镇定意味着认真处理质疑，而非假装它未曾出现。","word":"Composure","meaning":"镇定","tip":"关注“Composure”在这句话中如何表达“镇定”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The question exposes an ___ (ASSUME) rather than a measured fact.","answers":["assumption"],"explanation":"名词assumption。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: composure / composed / compose. The presenter retained ___ under sustained questioning.","answers":["composure"],"explanation":"retain composure保持镇定。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use WARRANTS: New evidence gives us reason to revise the recommendation.","answers":["New evidence warrants revising the recommendation."],"explanation":"warrant后可接动名词。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: That answer responds ___ the specific objection.","answers":["to"],"explanation":"respond to回应。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a presentation proposing a shared tool library.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to defend the observed interest while proposing a small pilot to test return times and repair needs. At a presentation proposing a shared tool library, the initial suggestion was to answer questions about demand and maintenance. The relevant background is that the presenter had survey responses from interested residents and a repair budget based on quotations, but no evidence about how often tools would return late. Nevertheless, a confident presentation could make an untested operating assumption look like an established result. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe presenter should not defend return times as though survey enthusiasm had measured them. Interest, venue costs, repair quotations and actual borrowing behaviour are different forms of information. The first three can justify trying a small service, while a pilot is needed for the fourth. Answering the question directly would make that distinction visible: the plan assumes timely returns, and that assumption has not been tested. The pilot should record delays and maintenance needs before the group invests in a larger collection. This response protects the documented parts of the proposal without treating the whole presentation as invulnerable. An audience can have more confidence in a plan that knows how to learn than in one whose author can only repeat assurances.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a presentation proposing a shared tool library. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to defend the observed interest while proposing a small pilot to test return times and repair needs?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to answer questions about demand and maintenance. The relevant detail is that the presenter had survey responses from interested residents and a repair budget based on quotations, but no evidence about how often tools would return late. However, a confident presentation could make an untested operating assumption look like an established result. For that reason, I would defend the observed interest while proposing a small pilot to test return times and repair needs. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“演讲答辩与即时澄清”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释演讲答辩与即时澄清的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a presentation about a neighbourhood repair event. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the presenter had reliable venue costs but no estimate of repair time. However, a questioner asked whether long queues would make the event impractical. We therefore needed to acknowledge the untested assumption without discarding the documented costs. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to run a small pilot and record waiting times before enlarging the event. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"A questioner asked whether long queues would make the event impractical.","options":["A questioner asked whether long queues would make the event impractical.","Repair times were known as reliably as venue costs.","The questioner disputed the existence of the documented venue costs."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Acknowledge the untested assumption without discarding the documented costs.","options":["Treat uncertainty about repair time as a reason to reject every figure.","Defend waiting times as already established.","Acknowledge the untested assumption without discarding the documented costs."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Run a small pilot and record waiting times before enlarging the event.","options":["Repeat the cost slide instead of testing repair duration.","Run a small pilot and record waiting times before enlarging the event.","Enlarge the event before collecting waiting time data."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":87,"title":"作品集与能力证据","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Select evidence that reveals independent performance and development","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第86章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a learner preparing a language portfolio","text":"At a learner preparing a language portfolio, participants considered whether to show progress without hiding the amount of assistance received. The background was specific: the portfolio included an initial report, reviewer comments and a revised version, with a record of dictionaries, translation tools and human support used. However, a polished final text alone could not show what the learner could produce independently under unfamiliar conditions. The proposed response was to retain the revision history and add a fresh timed task completed with clearly stated conditions.\n\nA portfolio can demonstrate learning precisely because it reveals how a text changed. Removing the initial draft would conceal the decisions and assistance that produced the final version. I would include selected reviewer comments, the learners response to them and an account of any substantial rewriting by others. A fresh independent task should sit alongside the polished work, with its time limit and permitted tools stated. The two products answer different questions and should not be collapsed into one flattering score. Reflection is strongest when it identifies a transferable method the learner can apply again, not merely gratitude for corrections. Reviewers can then distinguish a developed revision skill from an unsupported claim of equal performance in every unfamiliar situation. The final essay shows the quality achieved with feedback, not necessarily unaided performance. I have included the first draft so that the revision process remains visible. The assistance record distinguishes dictionary use from substantial human rewriting. A fresh timed task provides evidence that the polished portfolio cannot supply. The reflection should identify a change in method rather than merely express satisfaction."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：a mentor reviewing a speaking portfolio","text":"A separate fictional case offers another opportunity to distinguish an observation from its interpretation. The people involved share enough common ground to continue working, but their initial description risks implying more than they have established. The difference becomes visible when the suggested action is compared with the available detail.\n\nAt a mentor reviewing a speaking portfolio, the discussion concerned whether to distinguish rehearsed fluency from spontaneous interaction. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: one recording showed an elegant prepared talk, while another captured an unplanned discussion with interruptions and requests for clarification. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the prepared talk demonstrated organisation but could not establish how the speaker handled unexpected contributions. The suggested response was to include both kinds of performance and describe the different abilities each makes visible.\n\nPrepared performance and spontaneous interaction involve overlapping but different demands. A rehearsed talk allows the speaker to choose vocabulary, organise the sequence and practise difficult sounds in advance. An unscripted exchange requires attention to another persons contribution and sometimes a reformulation that was never planned. Neither recording should be discarded. Together they provide a richer picture, provided the preparation conditions are visible. The mentor should also note whether the listener repeatedly rescued the speaker or whether clarification was successfully initiated and resolved. A single smooth recording may be encouraging, but it cannot justify a broad claim about performance across unfamiliar situations. The portfolio should reveal that boundary rather than hide it.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The portfolio included an initial report, reviewer comments and a revised version, with a record of dictionaries, translation tools and human support used.","options":["The final report was submitted without any feedback or revision.","The portfolio omitted all records of translation and human assistance.","The portfolio included an initial report, reviewer comments and a revised version, with a record of dictionaries, translation tools and human support used."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A polished final text alone could not show what the learner could produce independently under unfamiliar conditions.","options":["Recording assistance made the final text worthless as evidence of learning.","A polished final text alone could not show what the learner could produce independently under unfamiliar conditions.","A polished final text alone established unfamiliar unaided performance."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Retain the revision history and add a fresh timed task completed with clearly stated conditions.","options":["Retain the revision history and add a fresh timed task completed with clearly stated conditions.","Hide the first draft so that only the best version is assessed.","Treat feedback assisted and timed independent tasks as identical evidence."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"One recording showed an elegant prepared talk, while another captured an unplanned discussion with interruptions and requests for clarification.","options":["Both recordings captured unprepared discussion with interruptions.","The prepared talk showed no organisation or pronunciation practice.","One recording showed an elegant prepared talk, while another captured an unplanned discussion with interruptions and requests for clarification."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The prepared talk demonstrated organisation but could not establish how the speaker handled unexpected contributions.","options":["Only spontaneous recordings had any possible assessment value.","The prepared talk demonstrated organisation but could not establish how the speaker handled unexpected contributions.","A rehearsed talk established how unexpected turns would be handled."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Include both kinds of performance and describe the different abilities each makes visible.","options":["Include both kinds of performance and describe the different abilities each makes visible.","Discard the unplanned exchange because it was less polished.","Use the elegant talk as sufficient evidence of every speaking skill."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 1","en":"The final essay shows the quality achieved with feedback, not necessarily unaided performance.","zh":"最终文章展示了经反馈达到的质量，而未必是独立表现。","word":"unaided performance","meaning":"独立无辅助的表现","tip":"关注“unaided performance”在这句话中如何表达“独立无辅助的表现”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 2","en":"I have included the first draft so that the revision process remains visible.","zh":"我纳入了初稿，让修改过程保持可见。","word":"revision process","meaning":"修改过程","tip":"关注“revision process”在这句话中如何表达“修改过程”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 3","en":"The assistance record distinguishes dictionary use from substantial human rewriting.","zh":"辅助记录区分查词典和大量人工改写。","word":"assistance record","meaning":"辅助记录","tip":"关注“assistance record”在这句话中如何表达“辅助记录”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 4","en":"A fresh timed task provides evidence that the polished portfolio cannot supply.","zh":"新计时任务提供了精修作品集无法提供的证据。","word":"fresh timed task","meaning":"新计时任务","tip":"关注“fresh timed task”在这句话中如何表达“新计时任务”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 5","en":"The reflection should identify a change in method rather than merely express satisfaction.","zh":"反思应指出方法变化，而非仅仅表达满意。","word":"reflection","meaning":"反思","tip":"关注“reflection”在这句话中如何表达“反思”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 6","en":"I can explain why I accepted one suggestion and rejected another.","zh":"我能解释为什么接受一项建议而拒绝另一项。","word":"rejected","meaning":"拒绝了","tip":"关注“rejected”在这句话中如何表达“拒绝了”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 7","en":"The collection needs an unsuccessful attempt as well as exemplary work.","zh":"作品集既需要优秀作品，也需要不成功的尝试。","word":"exemplary work","meaning":"优秀作品","tip":"关注“exemplary work”在这句话中如何表达“优秀作品”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 8","en":"Repeated practice on the same prompt may conceal dependence on memorised structure.","zh":"反复练同一题目可能掩盖对背诵结构的依赖。","word":"memorised structure","meaning":"背诵的结构","tip":"关注“memorised structure”在这句话中如何表达“背诵的结构”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 9","en":"The reviewer should know the time limit and resources available for each task.","zh":"评阅者应知道每项任务的时限和可用资源。","word":"resources","meaning":"资源","tip":"关注“resources”在这句话中如何表达“资源”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 10","en":"Progress is clearer when comparable tasks are assessed under documented conditions.","zh":"在记录明确的条件下评阅可比任务，进步会更清楚。","word":"comparable tasks","meaning":"可比任务","tip":"关注“comparable tasks”在这句话中如何表达“可比任务”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 11","en":"My claim concerns this demonstrated skill rather than my overall language level.","zh":"我的论断关乎这项已展示技能，而非整体语言等级。","word":"demonstrated skill","meaning":"已展示技能","tip":"关注“demonstrated skill”在这句话中如何表达“已展示技能”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"作品集与能力证据 · 12","en":"A portfolio supports an assessment; it does not replace independent verification.","zh":"作品集支持评估，却不能替代独立验证。","word":"independent verification","meaning":"独立验证","tip":"关注“independent verification”在这句话中如何表达“独立验证”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The record makes the extent of ___ (ASSIST) transparent.","answers":["assistance"],"explanation":"不可数名词assistance。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: unaided / unneeded / unrelated. The fresh task tests ___ performance without tutor support.","answers":["unaided"],"explanation":"unaided表示独立无辅助。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use RATHER THAN: The essay demonstrates revision, not necessarily independent production.","answers":["The essay demonstrates revision rather than necessarily independent production."],"explanation":"rather than保留两项证据范围的区别。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: The portfolio provides evidence ___ development.","answers":["of"],"explanation":"evidence of表示某方面的证据。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a learner preparing a language portfolio.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to retain the revision history and add a fresh timed task completed with clearly stated conditions. At a learner preparing a language portfolio, the initial suggestion was to show progress without hiding the amount of assistance received. The relevant background is that the portfolio included an initial report, reviewer comments and a revised version, with a record of dictionaries, translation tools and human support used. Nevertheless, a polished final text alone could not show what the learner could produce independently under unfamiliar conditions. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nA portfolio can demonstrate learning precisely because it reveals how a text changed. Removing the initial draft would conceal the decisions and assistance that produced the final version. I would include selected reviewer comments, the learners response to them and an account of any substantial rewriting by others. A fresh independent task should sit alongside the polished work, with its time limit and permitted tools stated. The two products answer different questions and should not be collapsed into one flattering score. Reflection is strongest when it identifies a transferable method the learner can apply again, not merely gratitude for corrections. Reviewers can then distinguish a developed revision skill from an unsupported claim of equal performance in every unfamiliar situation.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a learner preparing a language portfolio. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to retain the revision history and add a fresh timed task completed with clearly stated conditions?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to show progress without hiding the amount of assistance received. The relevant detail is that the portfolio included an initial report, reviewer comments and a revised version, with a record of dictionaries, translation tools and human support used. However, a polished final text alone could not show what the learner could produce independently under unfamiliar conditions. For that reason, I would retain the revision history and add a fresh timed task completed with clearly stated conditions. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“作品集与能力证据”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释作品集与能力证据的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during a learner assembling a writing portfolio. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that the most fluent essay had been extensively rewritten by a tutor. However, an earlier draft and a fresh independent task were also available. We therefore needed to document the assistance rather than present every text as unaided work. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to include the revision history and assess the independent task separately. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"An earlier draft and a fresh independent task were also available.","options":["The fluent essay had been produced entirely unaided.","The portfolio contained neither an initial draft nor a fresh task.","An earlier draft and a fresh independent task were also available."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Document the assistance rather than present every text as unaided work.","options":["Discard every assisted text as incapable of showing development.","Document the assistance rather than present every text as unaided work.","Treat tutor rewriting as independent performance."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Include the revision history and assess the independent task separately.","options":["Include the revision history and assess the independent task separately.","Submit only the polished essay without assistance notes.","Combine the tutor revised and independent tasks without distinction."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}},{"chapter":88,"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移","target":"C2","contentStatus":"C2目标训练；原创教学案例，未经外部等级认证","focus":"Test unfamiliar performance and report uncertainty honestly","grammar":"精确限定、篇章衔接、语域控制、复杂论证与独立即兴表达","prerequisite":"完成第87章任务；无需仅凭通关宣称已达C2。","sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a fictional final course review","text":"At a fictional final course review, participants considered whether to separate completion from demonstrated advanced competence. The background was specific: a learner completed every exercise but performed unevenly on an unfamiliar reading, a live discussion and an independently assessed essay several weeks later. However, a single percentage could conceal a strong reading result alongside unresolved speaking difficulties. The proposed response was to report the four skills separately, preserve assessor comments and plan targeted practice before reassessment.\n\nThe completion record deserves recognition, but the unfamiliar tasks provide the more relevant evidence about transfer. If reading is strong and spontaneous speaking remains uncertain, averaging them into one reassuring figure would obscure the next learning need. Each skill should be reported with conditions, assessor comments and the limits of the judgement. Disagreement between reviewers should lead to examination of the performance and criteria, not an unexplained compromise. The learner can then practise the weak area and return to a new task after an interval. This approach may produce a narrower conclusion than the learner hoped for, but it gives a credible direction for improvement. A course badge should record what has actually been established rather than borrow authority from an ambitious target label. Completing every exercise is evidence of persistence rather than proof of C2 proficiency. The unfamiliar task revealed a weakness hidden by repeated course items. We should report the four skills separately before considering any overall judgement. The delayed assessment tests retention and transfer rather than immediate recall alone. Two reviewers disagreed, so the result requires discussion rather than automatic averaging. The badge currently represents course completion, with proficiency still to be verified."},"sourceB":{"title":"独立测验：an external review of an advanced language programme","text":"In this newly invented case, readers should attend to the relationship between a stated aim and the evidence used to defend it. The account does not invite a judgement about personalities. Instead, it supplies a limited situation in which a careful summary can preserve something that a confident headline loses.\n\nAt an external review of an advanced language programme, the discussion concerned whether to evaluate whether its final badge represented transferable ability. There was a reason the idea initially appeared attractive: the review found high scores on repeated course items but no comparable results on new tasks assessed after a delay by independent reviewers. That account nevertheless contained an important reservation: the programme had evidence of practice success rather than proof that its advertised proficiency had been reached. The suggested response was to commission independent tasks, document conditions and describe the badge as course completion until validation is available.\n\nRepeated items can show that learners remember practised material. They cannot reveal how much the same learners will understand when vocabulary, speaker and task change. The review therefore needs assessments protected from rehearsal, with conditions recorded and productive work evaluated independently. Different skills may show different results, so one average should not conceal a persistent weakness. Disagreement between assessors should trigger examination of evidence and criteria rather than an unexplained arithmetic compromise. Until such validation exists, a completion badge remains useful as a record of participation and effort. It becomes misleading only when the programme presents it as a proficiency judgement the available evidence has not established.\n\nThe conclusion remains specific to the conditions described. Another interpretation should account for the same details, identify any additional assumptions, and explain what evidence would change the judgement."},"questions":[{"source":"A","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"A learner completed every exercise but performed unevenly on an unfamiliar reading, a live discussion and an independently assessed essay several weeks later.","options":["The final assessment repeated only exercises from the course.","A learner completed every exercise but performed unevenly on an unfamiliar reading, a live discussion and an independently assessed essay several weeks later.","The learner performed evenly across every unfamiliar skill task."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"A single percentage could conceal a strong reading result alongside unresolved speaking difficulties.","options":["A single percentage could conceal a strong reading result alongside unresolved speaking difficulties.","One average would fully explain the different skill results.","Completing the exercises established proficiency despite conflicting new performance."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"A","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Report the four skills separately, preserve assessor comments and plan targeted practice before reassessment.","options":["Award a C2 judgement solely from complete participation.","Repeat the same familiar items instead of assessing the weak skill.","Report the four skills separately, preserve assessor comments and plan targeted practice before reassessment."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which observation provides the stated basis for the discussion?","answer":"The review found high scores on repeated course items but no comparable results on new tasks assessed after a delay by independent reviewers.","options":["The repeated quiz scores were low although transfer scores were uniformly high.","The review found high scores on repeated course items but no comparable results on new tasks assessed after a delay by independent reviewers.","The review included comparable delayed tasks from independent assessors."],"explanation":"依据该来源明确陈述的背景证据；注意条件、观察时段和研究对象是否被选项改变。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which reservation most directly limits the initial interpretation?","answer":"The programme had evidence of practice success rather than proof that its advertised proficiency had been reached.","options":["The programme had evidence of practice success rather than proof that its advertised proficiency had been reached.","Success on rehearsed items established performance across unfamiliar conditions.","The lack of external validation made course effort irrelevant."],"explanation":"保留文本中的具体限定，区分文本支持的推断与选项增加的假设。"},{"source":"B","prompt":"Which next step best matches the position developed in this account?","answer":"Commission independent tasks, document conditions and describe the badge as course completion until validation is available.","options":["Retain the proficiency claim while seeking only more practice quiz scores.","Withdraw every record of completion instead of qualifying the badge.","Commission independent tasks, document conditions and describe the badge as course completion until validation is available."],"explanation":"正确行动针对该来源所指出的具体缺口；看似相关的行动可能遗漏关键条件。"}],"lessons":[{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 1","en":"Completing every exercise is evidence of persistence rather than proof of C2 proficiency.","zh":"完成全部练习证明坚持，而非证明达到C2水平。","word":"persistence","meaning":"坚持","tip":"关注“persistence”在这句话中如何表达“坚持”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 2","en":"The unfamiliar task revealed a weakness hidden by repeated course items.","zh":"陌生任务揭示了被重复课程题掩盖的弱项。","word":"unfamiliar task","meaning":"陌生任务","tip":"关注“unfamiliar task”在这句话中如何表达“陌生任务”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 3","en":"We should report the four skills separately before considering any overall judgement.","zh":"考虑整体判断之前，应分别报告四项技能。","word":"overall judgement","meaning":"整体判断","tip":"关注“overall judgement”在这句话中如何表达“整体判断”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 4","en":"The delayed assessment tests retention and transfer rather than immediate recall alone.","zh":"延迟评估检验保持与迁移，而不只是即时回忆。","word":"retention and transfer","meaning":"保持与迁移","tip":"关注“retention and transfer”在这句话中如何表达“保持与迁移”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 5","en":"Two reviewers disagreed, so the result requires discussion rather than automatic averaging.","zh":"两位评阅者意见不一，因此需要讨论，而非自动取平均。","word":"averaging","meaning":"取平均","tip":"关注“averaging”在这句话中如何表达“取平均”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 6","en":"The badge currently represents course completion, with proficiency still to be verified.","zh":"徽章目前代表完成课程，能力水平仍待验证。","word":"verified","meaning":"验证","tip":"关注“verified”在这句话中如何表达“验证”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 7","en":"An assessment claim is stronger when the conditions and limitations are documented.","zh":"当条件与局限被记录时，评估论断更有力。","word":"documented","meaning":"记录","tip":"关注“documented”在这句话中如何表达“记录”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 8","en":"Printed listening scripts cannot establish comprehension of unfamiliar spoken language.","zh":"书面听力脚本不能证明能理解陌生口语。","word":"comprehension","meaning":"理解","tip":"关注“comprehension”在这句话中如何表达“理解”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 9","en":"I would prioritise spontaneous interaction before repeating familiar vocabulary tests.","zh":"我会优先练自发互动，而非重复熟悉的词汇测验。","word":"spontaneous interaction","meaning":"自发互动","tip":"关注“spontaneous interaction”在这句话中如何表达“自发互动”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 10","en":"No single local recognition score can establish pronunciation, fluency and overall competence.","zh":"单一本地识别分数不能证明发音、流利度及整体能力。","word":"recognition score","meaning":"识别分数","tip":"关注“recognition score”在这句话中如何表达“识别分数”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 11","en":"The reassessment should use new material after a meaningful interval.","zh":"复评应在有意义的间隔后使用新材料。","word":"reassessment","meaning":"复评","tip":"关注“reassessment”在这句话中如何表达“复评”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"},{"title":"外部评估与延迟迁移 · 12","en":"A defensible conclusion may be narrower than the learners hoped for.","zh":"有依据的结论可能比学习者期望的更有限。","word":"defensible conclusion","meaning":"有依据的结论","tip":"关注“defensible conclusion”在这句话中如何表达“有依据的结论”。先用中文说明句意，再保留这一关键区别换一个场景表达。"}],"drills":[{"type":"formation","prompt":"The badge requires independent ___ (VALIDATE).","answers":["validation"],"explanation":"名词validation表示验证。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Choose only: transfer / translate / transit. New tasks after a delay test retention and ___.","answers":["transfer"],"explanation":"transfer在此表示技能迁移。"},{"type":"transform","prompt":"Use DOES NOT ESTABLISH: Course completion is not proof of proficiency.","answers":["Course completion does not establish proficiency."],"explanation":"establish表示确立或证明。"},{"type":"cloze","prompt":"Complete exactly: Report uncertainty instead ___ concealing it behind a single score.","answers":["of"],"explanation":"instead of后接动名词。"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an advisory commentary about the teaching case, using Source A only. Evaluate the initial proposal, explain the most important qualification, recommend a proportionate response, and identify what could change your judgement. Do not copy the model. Use an appropriate register for the people responsible at a fictional final course review.","minimumWords":240,"sample":"The immediate objective should be to report the four skills separately, preserve assessor comments and plan targeted practice before reassessment. At a fictional final course review, the initial suggestion was to separate completion from demonstrated advanced competence. The relevant background is that a learner completed every exercise but performed unevenly on an unfamiliar reading, a live discussion and an independently assessed essay several weeks later. Nevertheless, a single percentage could conceal a strong reading result alongside unresolved speaking difficulties. A defensible response must preserve the distinction this creates rather than resolve the difficulty by ignoring part of the account.\n\nThe completion record deserves recognition, but the unfamiliar tasks provide the more relevant evidence about transfer. If reading is strong and spontaneous speaking remains uncertain, averaging them into one reassuring figure would obscure the next learning need. Each skill should be reported with conditions, assessor comments and the limits of the judgement. Disagreement between reviewers should lead to examination of the performance and criteria, not an unexplained compromise. The learner can then practise the weak area and return to a new task after an interval. This approach may produce a narrower conclusion than the learner hoped for, but it gives a credible direction for improvement. A course badge should record what has actually been established rather than borrow authority from an ambitious target label.\n\nI would record the recommendation together with its conditions and the information that could make it necessary to reconsider. The strongest objection should be answered in its actual form, not replaced by a more convenient one. If later evidence changes the position, the revision should be explained openly. The point is to make a proportionate decision that remains accountable to the details, rather than adopt a more impressive conclusion whose certainty the case cannot support."},"speaking":{"prompt":"Without reading a script, explain your response to the teaching case at a fictional final course review. Speak for two to three minutes, distinguish evidence from inference, and respond to a partner who disagrees.","followups":["Which detail would most strongly justify changing your recommendation to report the four skills separately, preserve assessor comments and plan targeted practice before reassessment?","Restate the opposing view in its strongest reasonable form, then explain the remaining disagreement."],"sample":"I would separate the original idea from the qualification that changes its meaning. The proposal was to separate completion from demonstrated advanced competence. The relevant detail is that a learner completed every exercise but performed unevenly on an unfamiliar reading, a live discussion and an independently assessed essay several weeks later. However, a single percentage could conceal a strong reading result alongside unresolved speaking difficulties. For that reason, I would report the four skills separately, preserve assessor comments and plan targeted practice before reassessment. This does not dismiss every contribution; it makes the decision more precise. If challenged, I would distinguish the stated details from my interpretation. I would also explain what information could change my mind, rather than defend the same conclusion regardless of the evidence."},"project":"围绕“外部评估与延迟迁移”完成一次陌生场景迁移：自选新案例，准备一页证据与限定说明，提交独立初稿、评阅意见和修订稿；再与真人进行5分钟追问。保留时间、辅助工具与评阅者记录。本地识别只辅助转写，不认证C2；涉及听力时需另用获授权的陌生真人音频。","reviewChecklist":["能在新场景中解释外部评估与延迟迁移的关键区别，而非复述原案例。","能明确区分文本事实、合理推断和仍缺少的证据。","能回应真人追问并根据反证修正立场，保留独立表现记录。","经外部评阅并在间隔数周后用新材料复测；课程完成不自动等于C2。"],"assessmentListening":{"text":"I want to explain a decision we reached during an advanced course review. It would be easy to report only the most memorable phrase and miss why the group changed its approach. The relevant background was that learners scored highly on repeated quizzes but unevenly on delayed new tasks. However, the final badge had been advertised as proof of a proficiency level. We therefore needed to distinguish course success from independently demonstrated transferable ability. That distinction mattered more than making the final summary sound confident. After discussing the alternatives, the group decided to revise the badge wording and obtain external assessments using unfamiliar material. The decision was not a claim that the initial observation had no value. It was a way to preserve what remained useful without carrying forward an implication the available detail could not justify. If someone later summarises our discussion, I would want both the reason for the concern and the agreed action to remain visible. Otherwise, a listener might reasonably understand a much stronger conclusion than we intended.","delivery":"Original fictional script. TTS or printed text does not substitute for independent assessment with unfamiliar human speech.","questions":[{"prompt":"What qualification explains the change of approach?","answer":"The final badge had been advertised as proof of a proficiency level.","options":["The course review found no success on repeated quizzes.","The final badge had been advertised as proof of a proficiency level.","Independent delayed tasks produced uniformly strong results."],"explanation":"关注However之后的具体限定；区分实际听到的信息与选项改变的条件。"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the group need to preserve?","answer":"Distinguish course success from independently demonstrated transferable ability.","options":["Distinguish course success from independently demonstrated transferable ability.","Equate repeated item scores with unfamiliar task competence.","Treat course completion as having no value whatsoever."],"explanation":"关注We therefore needed to之后说明的判断任务，保留证据与结论之间的区别。"},{"prompt":"What action was actually agreed?","answer":"Revise the badge wording and obtain external assessments using unfamiliar material.","options":["Keep the proficiency claim while adding more familiar quizzes.","Obtain reviews of rehearsed material only.","Revise the badge wording and obtain external assessments using unfamiliar material."],"explanation":"关注After discussing the alternatives之后的行动；选项可能只保留了行动的一部分。"}]}}];